/* the Under pressure nook: series, steps, marks first used, practice (built by dbtredraft/site/nookdata.py) */
window.LADDER_NOOK={"series":[{"id":"G","page":"dbtg","name":"general","footings":15,"steps":[{"id":"G1","title":"the frame: states are maps of categories, skills are functions","marks":[["P₁","P₁, P₂, …: the statements of a footing, numbered; each is written once in words and once in marks"],["≔","is defined as"],["∧","and: both sides hold","r01c.html#f8b6cf","1c · And, and the Ors"],["∀","for all: what follows holds of every such thing","r02c.html#b4f71d","2c · Every and Some"],["𝔰","a state of mind: a map of categories 𝐖 → 𝐗"],["w","a situation"],[":","such that; after a name, what kind of thing it is (h : w → w′ reads «h goes from w to w′»)"],["∃!","for exactly one: there is one such thing and no second"],["x","a response"],["(","f(x) is f applied to x; brackets also group what belongs together"],["=","is the same as"],["h","a person, the other; in h : w → w′, a happening that leads from one situation to the next"],["→","from the left to the right: an arrow, a change, or a step"],["′","w′ (read «w prime»): another thing of the same kind as w"],["ς","a skill"],["_","the mark after _ sits below the line and says which one: ς_w is the ς that belongs to w; a longer one is held in braces, as in ς_{w′}"],["∘","after: g ∘ f does f first and then g, so it reads right to left","r09b.html#333979","9b · Composing, and Brackets Don't Matter"],["¬","not: what follows is false","r01b.html#c19f8b","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["⇒","if … then: when the left side holds, the right side holds","r01d.html#2f2e8d","1d · If, and Exactly When"],["◇","possibly; can be"],["□","necessarily; must be"],["𝐖","the category of situations: things are situations w, arrows are happenings h : w → w′"],["𝐗","the category of responses: things are x = (t, b, e, u, a), arrows are changes of response"],["id","the arrow that changes nothing","r09b.html#250552","9b · Composing, and Brackets Don't Matter"],["t","a time; in 𝐗, a thought"],["b","a body state; in G13, the problem act"],["e","an emotion; e₀ is calm"],["u","urge: an emotion’s readied act"],["a","an act"],["∈","is in: is a member of the set","r02e.html#9d86c6","2e · Membership and Same Members"],["T","thoughts"],["×","paired with: A × B is every pair of one thing from A and one from B","r02i.html#50591a","2i · Pairs in Order"],["B","body"],["E","emotions"],["U","urges"],["A","actions"],["↦","is turned into"],["{","{ … } is a set: the things listed, or { x : … } every x such that …"],["W","the world: situations"],["⊆","is inside: every member of the left is a member of the right","r02e.html#b78709","2e · Membership and Same Members"],["⋯","and so on in the same way","r01b.html","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["⋃","the union of all"],["⇔","exactly when: each side holds when the other does","r01d.html#982a95","1d · If, and Exactly When"]],"practice":[],"u":"A state of mind reads every situation as a response. What, then, does a skill act on? Why does a skill work the same whichever way the situation was reached?","tree":{"t":"Zoe gets a C on a chemistry quiz and reads it as \"I will never get into medicine\". What does she do?","o":[["She works out which questions she lost marks on and books a study slot for each topic.",["G1","G13","G15"],4,"Her state of mind read the C as \"never\". A skill does not act on the C. It acts on the reading, and it works the same whichever way the C came about. Problem solving turns \"never\" into a list of topics (ER12), and each slot is one trial she can record (G15). \"Never\" was a claim stronger than the facts (IE21), and a list of topics is a claim that fits them."],["She turns the reading into \"one quiz is one quiz\" and lets the panic settle.",["G1","M1","DT19"],6,"The panic has two causes: the C, and what she made of it. She can't change the first tonight, and she can change the second. \"Never\" is a thought, and a thought can be watched without being obeyed (DT19). Checked against the facts (ER7), one quiz is a small share of one course. The emotion changes because one part of it changed (ER5)."],["She lets the disappointment sit, and goes to the next lecture anyway.",["DT13","ER22"],1,"The C is on the page, and it stays there. Accepting it leaves the disappointment and drops the fight with it (DT13). The disappointment fits: she wanted more and got less (ER8). It rises and falls like a wave if she neither blocks it nor feeds it (ER22). The feeling and the lecture can both happen, and this is the middle between feeling it all and disowning it (G10)."],["She keeps telling herself medicine is over, and stops revising chemistry.",["G4","G6"],6,"This makes sense: if the road is closed, why walk it. But the reading now makes itself true, because unrevised chemistry gives the next C. That is the feedback of the transaction (G8), a thought moving an act that moves the thought. It is still one of the four options, chosen at this moment, and the next moment is a new choice (G4)."]],"e":[["The study slots are booked and kept. The second quiz comes back a C too, and her lab partner, who never seems to revise, gets an A.",[["She runs a chain on the second quiz to find which link the study slots missed.",["G13","G15"],"Two trials are still a short record (G15). The chain asks what happened between the slot and the quiz, link by link, and finds where a skill would have changed the result (G13). The partner's A is no link in her chain. A rating on someone else's scale tells her nothing about her own."],["She checks the facts: two Cs are two quizzes, still a small share of the course.",["ER7","G1"],"The reading \"never\" is back, with new evidence it likes. The check asks the same questions again: what happened, what is she adding to it, how likely is the worst, and could she cope if it came (ER7). The skill works the same at the second C as at the first, and that sameness is what practice adds (G1)."],["She keeps the slots and adds one: a question session with the tutor.",["IE5","G2"],"Readiness is a count of conditions, and one more condition never lowers it (G2). Asking the tutor takes a clear ask (IE5), and it goes against \"cope alone\", the rule of a world that does not validate (G11). She is doing the best she can and she needs to do more, and both are true (G6)."]]],["The panic settles. Then a friend hears the grade and says, \"a C in chemistry, that's medicine over then\".",[["She notices the friend's words as a thought she can let pass, not a verdict.",["DT19","M1"],"The friend has handed her old reading back to her in another voice. A thought is a sensation of the brain, hers or anyone's, and it comes and goes (DT19). She does not have to push it away or take it in. That is the mind held and not handed over (M1)."],["She tells the friend, kindly and plainly, that one quiz is one quiz.",["IE5","IE10"],"She worked for this reading, and FAST keeps it: fair, no apology for her view, truthful (IE10). Said gently, it keeps the friend too (IE7). The friend's remark was invalidating, and the friend may have no better way to talk about a bad grade (G7)."],["She lets the sting be there, and goes back to her reading.",["ER22","DT13"],"The panic can rise again and fall again, and a second wave does not mean the first skill failed. She does not have to win the argument to keep studying. Accepting that the friend thinks this, and may go on thinking it, costs her nothing she needs (DT13)."]]],["She goes to the next lecture. It covers the exact topic she got wrong, the lecturer says \"you should all know this by now\", and she feels it all again.",[["She stays, and takes notes on exactly that topic.",["ER10","G15"],"Shame's urge is to leave the room (ER11). Staying with her pen moving is opposite action, done all the way (ER10). It also turns the lecture into the study she needed, one more trial on the record (G15). \"You should all know\" is a claim about everyone, and one student who does not yet know it breaks it (IE3)."],["She rides the wave of disappointment through the hour.",["ER22","DT11"],"The wave is higher this time because the topic is the one she got wrong. It is still a wave (ER22). One thing in the moment, this slide and then the next, is how the hour gets survived (DT11). She was not asked to like it."],["She half-smiles and keeps her hands open on the desk.",["DT18"],"The body tells the brain. Open hands say \"I am not fighting this\" before her mind can agree (DT18), and that is willingness in a small place (DT17). In a full lecture hall, it is a skill no one else has to see."]]]]}},{"id":"G2","title":"degree as a cardinality: one measure for states and skills","marks":[["∃","for some: there is at least one such thing","r02c.html#b4f71d","2c · Every and Some"],["|","|X| is how many are in X, or how strong x is; a | b offers a or b"],["…","and so on, up to the last one named"],["≤","is at most","r01c.html#01c421","1c · And, and the Ors"],["≥","is at least","r04h.html","4h · Order"],[";","ends one claim and starts the next on the same line; both are meant"],["^","to the power of"],["𝔴","wise mind"],["Σ","the sum of everything named below it","r04k.html","4k · Counting Choices"],["ω","a weight; in M3, wise mind’s combining map"],["ν","vulnerability"],["ι","invalidate (in IE11, intensity)"],["Z","what gets in the way"],["⊖","the opposite (of an urge)"],["🙂","happy; pleasant"],["Θ","attention: where it is at each time"],["+","added to; taken together with"]],"practice":[],"u":"If a degree is how many of a fixed set of conditions hold, why can one more condition never lower it? What do \"even a little\", \"all the way\" and \"one at a time\" each count?","tree":{"t":"Mike's readiness for a calculus final is low: of past papers, formulas, sleep and notes, only formulas hold. What does he do?","o":[["He adds one condition a day: a past paper tonight, notes tomorrow, sleep before the exam.",["G2","G15"],3,"Readiness is a count: how many of the four conditions hold. One more condition never lowers it, so each evening's step can only help (G2). This is building mastery, one hard-but-possible step at a time (ER18), and sleep is on the list because the body is part of readiness (ER19). \"Unready\" as a verdict gave him nothing to do, and the count gives him three things."],["He counts what holds, even a little, and stops calling himself completely unready.",["G2","M5"],7,"\"Completely unready\" says zero of four, and the count is one. \"Even a little\" means at least one, and it is true. Counting is describing, and describing is not judging (M5). The feeling eases because \"completely\" was stronger than the facts (IE21), and a weaker claim leaves room to act (IE20)."],["He accepts going in at two of four, and does the best he can on the day.",["G2","DT13"],1,"Time fixes some limits, and no skill adds days. Accepting the count is not approving of it (DT14). It stops the panic from costing him a third condition, the sleep. He is doing the best he can, and next term he needs to start sooner: both hold together (G6)."],["He decides it is hopeless and does nothing more before the final.",["G4"],6,"Hopelessness makes sense at one of four. But doing nothing is the fourth option, and it has a result like the other three: the count stays at one. It is helplessness about a problem that has pieces, one end of the second line (G10). The other three options stay open until the exam starts."]],"e":[["He does the past paper and scores worse than he expected. His study partner looks at the mark and says, \"maybe aim for a pass\".",[["He marks which questions cost him, and makes them tomorrow's notes.",["G2","G13"],"The paper did its job. It was a measure, and a low measure taken early shows where the weak links are (G13). \"Aim for a pass\" is the partner's forecast. His own next condition is now better aimed than it was this morning (G2)."],["He records the score as one trial, and keeps the plan.",["G15"],"One trial with a bad result does not show the plan fails, because a skill claims \"often\" and not \"always\" (G15). He needs several papers before he can conclude anything. The partner's comment is a rating on the partner's scale, and the record stays on his own."],["He notices the urge to give up, and runs STOP before deciding anything.",["DT4"],"Discouragement comes with an urge, and the urge is to close the book. STOP puts a gap between the urge and the act (DT4). In the gap he can see that deciding now would be emotion mind deciding. The decision can wait for wise mind (M3), and the next condition can't wait."]]],["He counts what holds. His roommate, who finished revising last week, looks over and says, \"you're not ready at all\".",[["He describes what holds, plainly: \"formulas and one past paper\".",["M4","G2"],"A description can't be argued with, and a judgment can (M4). \"Not ready at all\" says zero, and the count says two. He does not need the roommate to agree with a count."],["He validates himself: worry before a final makes sense.",["IE24","IE22"],"The roommate dismissed his work, and he can supply the validation that was missing (IE24). Worry before a final fits the facts. \"Not at all\" does not. Validation is given only to what is valid (IE22), and here that is the worry and the work, not the verdict."],["He lets the roommate have his opinion, and gets back to the notes.",["DT13","M5"],"That the roommate thinks this is now a fact, and it can be accepted like any other (DT13). Judging the roommate back would cost the evening. Doing what works (M5) means the notes, because the notes are what change the count."]]],["He accepts two of four. On the day, the first question is on a topic he never covered, and all around him pens start moving.",[["He does the questions he can first, then attempts the new one.",["DT11","ER12"],"One thing in the moment (DT11): the question he is on is the only one that exists for now. That is also a solution to the real problem, which is marks, and marks come from what he can do (ER12). The other pens tell him nothing about his own paper."],["He breathes out longer than in for a minute, then reads the question again.",["DT6"],"The panic is in his body first, and his body can be reached directly. Paced breathing slows the heart within a minute (DT6). It moves the body part of the emotion, and the rest follows (ER5). The minute is repaid by reading the question properly."],["He accepts this question will cost him, and does the rest well.",["DT13","DT16"],"He accepted two of four last night, and now it has a face. Acceptance is chosen again at each fork (DT16), and this is a fork. Refusing the lost question would spread its cost over all the others."]]]]}},{"id":"G3","title":"goals: each lack down, each skill up","marks":[["↓","goes down","r11econ.html","11·econ · Why Would Oranges Be Left to Rot?"],["↑","goes up","r05b.html","5b · Integers as Pairs"],["𝐌","the mindfulness series"],["∅","the empty set: nothing is in it","r02f.html","2f · Sifting, and a Set to Start From"],["∨","or: at least one side holds","r01c.html#01c421","1c · And, and the Ors"],["J","judgments"],["≠","is not the same as","r01b.html#c19f8b","1b · Not, and the Totally False"],["☉","the self"],["⊢","holds that; says"],["𝐈𝐄","the interpersonal effectiveness series"],["R","relationship"],["♡","wish well; like; love"],["𝐄𝐑","the regulating emotion series"],["/","per; divided by"],["𝐃𝐓","the distress tolerance series"],["F","facts"],["⛓","the addictive act: the substance or repeated act a person is hooked on (never medicine)"],["ℙ","probability"],["🧰","the toolbox: the skills held"]],"practice":[],"u":"The goal lowers four lacks and raises four skill sets. Pair each lack with the series that works on it.","tree":{"t":"Randy studies alone, knows no one in his lecture, and skips problem sets when his mood is low. What does he do?","o":[["He asks two classmates to work through problem set 3 with him on Thursday.",["G3","IE16","IE5"],4,"He has two lacks, and each feeds the other: alone, his mood drops, and when his mood is low he skips, and skipping keeps him alone. That is the loop of the transaction in small (G8). One clear ask (IE5) to people who sit near him and study the same thing (IE16) cuts both arrows at once. He did not cause being new here, and he is the one who can change it."],["He notices the low mood, names it, and does just the first problem.",["G3","ER1","M2"],5,"\"Mood decides the act\" is the lack itself. Noticing the mood on purpose (M2) and naming it (ER6) puts a gap between the mood and the act. One problem done while low is opposite action in a small dose (ER10). It has to be learned at the desk on a low evening, because that is the setting that needs it."],["He lets the loneliness be there while he works, without making it the reason to stop.",["G3","DT13"],1,"The loneliness fits the facts: he knows no one yet (ER8). So it is not there to be argued away. It can be accepted and carried (DT13), feeling and work side by side. He does not disown it, and he does not let it run the evening (G10)."],["He skips the set again and stays away from the lecture.",["G4"],6,"Skipping gives relief tonight, and relief pays, which is why it repeats (IE25). Both lacks stay up, and next week starts further back. This choice has causes too, and none of them is a flaw in him."]],"e":[["He asks two classmates. One says yes. The other says, \"we've already got a group\", and turns back to her friends.",[["He meets the one who said yes, and asks a third person next week.",["G3","IE16","IE13"],"A no to one ask is not a no to every ask. Finding people takes being near, being alike, and trying more than once (IE16), and one yes is already a study group of two. He can ask again at the same height without softening it (IE13)."],["He walks the obstacles: was the ask clear, was the time good for them?",["IE15"],"Before he decides the no was about him, he checks in order what else it could be (IE15). \"We've already got a group\" names its own cause, and the cause is in her timetable. Finding causes works better than judging, himself included (G6)."],["He lets the sting of the no be, and still goes on Thursday.",["ER22","IE17"],"He can't observe why she said no, so a guess about it is held loosely (IE17). The sting is a wave (ER22). If he lets it decide Thursday, mood is deciding the act again, and that was the lack he set out to lower (G3)."]]],["He does the first problem and gets it wrong. The low mood sinks lower, and it says, \"see, no point\".",[["He does one pleasant thing for twenty minutes, then the second problem.",["ER16","DT10"],"A small pleasant event is a skill, with a time limit and a plan to return (ER16). It makes him less vulnerable to the mood (ER14) without handing the evening over. Being gentle with himself and then going on is the whole of self-soothing (DT10)."],["He checks his body: has he eaten or slept?",["ER19","ER14"],"Low mood often has a body under it. PLEASE asks about illness, food, sleep and exercise before it asks about willpower (ER19). If the cause is a missed dinner, the fix is dinner. A lecture to himself would blame the wrong link (G13)."],["He notes the mood and keeps a record of it next to the work.",["G15","ER6"],"\"No point\" is a conclusion from one trial. Mood rated before and after, written beside what he did, is the record that can test it (G15). Naming the mood exactly (ER6) also puts him a step outside it."]]],["He works with the loneliness there. Halfway through, a message comes from home: \"your cousin's made the dean's list, are you keeping up?\"",[["He answers the message later, and finishes the problem first.",["DT9","DT11"],"Putting the message away for an hour is distraction used once, with a plan to come back (DT9). One thing in the moment, and the thing is the problem (DT11). The message will be the same in an hour, and he will be a problem further on."],["He calls someone he trusts and tells them.",["IE5","IE16"],"The message compares him with a cousin, and a comparison invalidates what he is carrying (G11). Loneliness is the problem here, and reaching a person is the skill that fits it. One who listens is enough to start with (IE16)."],["He lets both feelings be there, and does one more line.",["ER22","DT13"],"There are two waves now, loneliness and the sting from home. Neither has to go before the next line is written (ER22). Accepting that home sees him this way for now (DT13) is not agreeing that home is right (DT14)."]]]]}},{"id":"G4","title":"four options: move the thing, move the map, keep both, or nothing","marks":[["p","a problem"],["κ","change the situation"],["ϵ","change the emotional response"],["τ","tolerate"],["μ","stay miserable; in a myth list, a myth"],["σ","suffering"],["∞","without end","r08g.html","8g · Inequalities"],["π","pain"],["🤲","accept; radical acceptance"],["∉","is not in the set","r02e.html","2e · Membership and Same Members"],["K","consequences, K = K⁺ + K⁻ (wanted and unwanted)"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why are there only four ways to meet a painful problem? Why does it follow that you are already carrying induction into action, even when you use no skill?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's group-project partner has done nothing, and the report is due Friday. What does she do?","o":[["She messages the partner and the tutor today, with a plan for who writes what by Wednesday.",["G4","IE5"],4,"Changing the situation is the first of the four options, and here the situation is a person, so the tools are interpersonal. DEAR MAN describes the facts, asks for one thing, and stays on it (IE5). She weighs her three aims first (IE4): the report matters most, and the partnership has to last until Friday. She did not cause the gap, and she is the one who can close it."],["She tells herself the mark is shared, and lets go of the resentment so she can work.",["G4","ER9"],7,"The second option changes her response and leaves the partner as he is. The anger fits the facts, because a goal of hers is blocked (ER8). But acting on it tonight would not help the report, and when acting on a fitting emotion does not help, opposite action is the way through (ER9). Finding what caused the gap will serve her better than blame."],["She writes her part well and accepts that the partner may not deliver.",["G4","DT13"],1,"The third option keeps both: the problem as it is, and her feeling as it is. Acceptance drops only the fight, \"he should have done it\", which changes nothing by Friday (DT13). Accepting is not approving of him (DT14). It gives her evenings back to the part she controls."],["She stews all week and hands in nothing extra.",["G4"],6,"Stewing is rumination, and rumination keeps the anger firing without solving anything (ER3). It is the fourth option, and it is a choice even when it does not feel like one. It makes sense: someone treated unfairly waits for fairness. The report and the week both get worse while she waits."]],"e":[["She messages them both. The tutor replies within the hour. The partner reads the message and posts photos from a party that night.",[["She writes the partner's part as a list of tasks and asks the tutor to divide the mark.",["IE5","IE14"],"The pair could not settle it, so she takes it to the one who can decide and by whose rule (IE14). The ask is in DEAR MAN form: facts, one request, what is in it for the tutor (IE5). The photos are not evidence she needs, and leaving them out keeps her fair (IE10)."],["She checks: is the plan clear, is it fair, is she asking the right person?",["IE15","IE11"],"Before she pushes harder, she troubleshoots in order (IE15). Then she runs the ten checks to see how firm she may be (IE11). Most of them fall her way: it is her right, the timing is his own deadline, and she has done her homework. So the next ask can be firmer (IE12)."],["She stops waiting, and writes enough for the report to pass.",["G4","ER12"],"The first solution failed, so the loop returns to the start with another (ER12). Changing the situation is still the option. Only the lever has changed, from his hands to hers (G4). She did not cause it, and she is solving it."]]],["She lets go of the resentment. On Thursday the partner sends his section: one page, copied from the lecture slides, with \"hope that's ok!\"",[["She checks the facts: does the resentment fit now? It does, and it is the right size.",["ER7","ER8"],"Letting go on Monday was right for Monday's facts. Thursday's facts are new, and an emotion is a message to be checked (ER2). The check says the anger fits and at this size (ER8). A fitting emotion is not a failure of the earlier skill. It is information."],["She uses the anger for one clear message: \"I need your section rewritten by noon\".",["IE5","ER13"],"When an emotion fits and acting on it helps, she acts on its urge in a useful form (ER13). Anger is there to get past what blocks a goal, and here that takes one firm sentence. The broken record keeps her on the ask if he jokes it away (IE5)."],["She rides the wave of anger, then works.",["ER22"],"Anger that fits is still a wave. If she feeds it with replays, it lasts all night (ER3). If she blocks it, it comes back. Watched, it peaks and falls (ER22), and the report gets the hours."]]],["She accepts it and writes her part well. Then the tutor announces that the mark will be shared equally, \"to teach teamwork\".",[["She asks the tutor to look again, with the evidence of who wrote what.",["IE5","IE10"],"Acceptance was for what could not be changed, and a tutor's decision can sometimes be changed (DT12). Self-respect is an aim of its own (IE4), and FAST keeps it: fair to the tutor, no apology for asking, truthful about the split (IE10)."],["She accepts this too, and turns the mind each time it comes back.",["DT16","DT13"],"\"To teach teamwork\" will come back to her many times, and each return is a fork (DT16). The tutor's reason is a cause like any other, and it does not have to be fair to be real (DT13). She is not approving of it. She is refusing to pay for it twice."],["She notices the urge to complain to everyone, and does not act on it.",["DT4","DT25"],"The urge is to rebel, loudly. STOP holds it for a moment (DT4). Alternate rebellion lets it out with no cost (DT25): she can write the angriest email of her life and not send it. Her standing with the tutor and the class stays whole for the next ask."]]]]}},{"id":"G5","title":"being present: four absences in a row is a drift","marks":[["⇏","does not give"],["✓","validate; valid"],["∴","therefore","r05a.html","5a · Equations Revisited"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is one who drifts out of their own life still in it? Why does a drift call for a fresh commitment, and not for leaving?","tree":{"t":"Terry has gone to no lectures and opened no coursework for four days running. What does he do?","o":[["He makes a fresh commitment: tomorrow's 9 a.m. lecture, and one hour on the backlog.",["G5"],2,"Four days running is a drift, and a drift calls for a fresh commitment. It does not call for a verdict. One who drifts out of his own life is still in it, so there is nothing to be let back into. The commitment is small and has a time and a place, which is a value brought down to one step (ER17). He wants to improve, and tomorrow at nine is where that goes."],["He reminds himself that drifting does not take him out of his own life, and lets the shame ease.",["G5","M5"],6,"Shame says he has left and can't come back. But he broke no rule of his own on purpose: the drift had causes, and each day made the next easier to miss. So the shame does not fit, and shame that does not fit is answered by not hiding (ER11). Seeing the four days without judging them (M5) lowers the wall he would have to climb to return."],["He accepts that the backlog is real and goes to one lecture with it still undone.",["G5","DT13"],1,"He wants to return with the backlog cleared, as if the four days had not happened. They happened. Accepting the backlog as it stands (DT13) lets him be present before he is caught up. Presence comes first. This is willingness: taking part in the term he has, and not the one he wishes he had (DT17)."],["He stays in his room a fifth day.",["G4","G5"],6,"Each day away makes the next one easier to skip and harder to explain, so the fifth has causes. Avoiding the lecture relieves the dread for a morning, and the relief pays (IE25). The drift grows, and so does the commitment he will need. He is still in his life on the fifth day."]],"e":[["He goes to the 9 a.m. lecture. That night he studies late to catch up, sleeps through two alarms, and misses the next one.",[["He makes a fresh commitment for tomorrow, without punishing himself.",["G5","DT16"],"One missed day after a return is not the drift come back. Four in a row is the mark, and he is at one (G5). A commitment is made again at every fork (DT16). Punishing himself would add shame, and shame is what kept him in his room."],["He sets two alarms and puts the phone across the room.",["G14","DT24"],"He knew what was needed and he was willing. What failed was the fourth question: sleep got in the way (G14). The fix belongs at that link. Putting the phone out of reach burns the bridge to the snooze button (DT24), and going to bed on time lowers what made him vulnerable (ER19)."],["He runs a chain: what led to oversleeping?",["G13"],"The chain shows that the late night was a skill pushed too far: catching up, paid for with sleep. The key link is \"one more chapter\" at 1 a.m. (G13). The repair is to succeed tomorrow. Self-blame is not repair."]]],["The shame eases. Then an email arrives from his tutor, copied to the year head: he is at risk of failing the term.",[["He replies today, with a plan for the backlog.",["IE5","ER12"],"The urge is to leave it unread until he has good news. Answering early with a plan is the skill (IE5), and the plan comes out of the problem-solving loop (ER12). An email that named a risk gets back a reply that names steps. Asking the tutor for help goes against \"cope alone\" (G11)."],["He checks the facts: at risk is not failed.",["ER7"],"The fear fits, because the threat is real (ER8). The check is about its size. \"At risk\" means some outcomes are bad and some are not, and the ones that are not run through this week's work (ER7). Fear at the right size moves him, and that is what fear is for (ER2)."],["He lets the fear come, and does one hour of the backlog.",["ER22","ER18"],"The fear is a wave, and it does not have to pass before he starts (ER22). One hour is hard but possible, and mastery is built in steps of that size (ER18). The fear goes on beside the work: acceptance and change together (∎)."]]],["He goes to the lecture with the backlog undone. The lecturer calls on him for last week's result, and the row behind him laughs when he can't give it.",[["He says plainly, \"I missed last week, I'll catch up\".",["IE10","M4"],"A plain description is all the moment needs (M4). FAST keeps his self-respect: truthful, and no apology beyond what is due (IE10). Seeming fine when he is not is the taught end of the second line (G10), and one true sentence steps off it."],["He lets the embarrassment rise and fall while the lecture goes on.",["ER22"],"The laugh lasts three seconds, and the embarrassment lasts longer, but it is a wave (ER22). Its urge is to leave and not come back, which is the drift again. He stays in his seat and the wave passes under him."],["He asks the lecturer afterwards what to read first.",["IE5","G5"],"Being present means turning up again and asking. The ask is small and clear (IE5). It also changes what the lecturer knows: a student who asks is read differently from one who vanished, and the environment answers what it sees (G9)."]]]]}},{"id":"G6","title":"the axioms: laid down, held in trust","marks":[["ℓ","a link in a chain"],["K_","K_☉ x: ☉ knows x"]],"practice":[],"u":"\"People are doing the best they can\" and \"they still need to do better\" are both laid down. Why do these two not contradict each other?","tree":{"t":"Claire has failed the same statistics midterm twice. What does she do?","o":[["She asks the tutor which topics cost her most, and plans her third attempt around them.",["G6","G13"],3,"Two assumptions hold at once: she is doing the best she can, and she needs to do better. That is a dialectic. Both sides are true, and the plan is their synthesis (IE19). A chain on the second failure finds which topics cost her the marks (G13), and mastery is built on those (ER18). \"Try harder\" alone tells her nothing. \"Try at these topics\" does."],["She drops \"I'm hopeless at statistics\" and says \"I haven't learned this yet\".",["G6","M5"],7,"\"Hopeless\" is a judgment of her whole self, drawn from two exams. \"Not yet\" is a description with time in it, and things change (IE19). The judgment had no cause in it to work on, and the description has one. Finding causes works better than blame, and that applies to her own case as it would to anyone's."],["She accepts that she has to resit, and that this is hard.",["G6","DT13"],4,"She may not have caused all of it: the course moved fast and nobody checked on her. She still has to solve it. Accepting the resit drops \"it's not fair that I have to\", which was costing her the energy the resit needs (DT13). The second half matters as much: it is hard, and saying so is validating herself (IE24)."],["She decides she is stupid and stops going to the class.",["G4","G6"],6,"After two failures \"stupid\" makes sense as a conclusion. It is also what a world says when it calls a struggle a fault (G11), now in her own voice. That is self-invalidation, one end of the first line (G10). The assumptions say the failures were caused, and \"stupid\" names no cause. Stopping closes the road the other three options keep open."]],"e":[["She asks the tutor. The tutor's list covers almost the whole course, and ends, \"honestly, have you thought about a different module?\"",[["She breaks the list into weeks, one topic a day.",["ER18","DT11"],"The whole list is too much to look at, and one day's topic is not. Mastery needs steps that are hard but possible (ER18), and only today's step exists today (DT11). The tutor's question can wait until the record of a few weeks can answer it (G15)."],["She asks the tutor which three to start with.",["IE5"],"The tutor answered a different question from the one she asked. She asks again, and narrower: which three first (IE5). She does not have to take up the idea of another module to get her answer. Staying on her ask is the broken record."],["She reminds herself she needs to try harder, and that trying is not failing.",["G6","M5"],"The tutor's remark sounds like a verdict. The assumptions do not give verdicts. They say she is doing her best and that more is needed (G6). She holds both without judging either (M5). The tutor is doing the best they can as well, with a long list and a short office hour."]]],["She says \"I haven't learned this yet\" aloud in the study room. A classmate laughs: \"third time lucky, yeah?\"",[["She validates herself, exactly as she would a friend.",["IE24"],"If a friend had failed twice and come back for a third try, she would call it stubborn in the good sense. She says the same to herself (IE24). The laugh was invalidating. Her own validation does not wait for his."],["She keeps the words, and notices the laugh as a sting that will pass.",["ER22","DT19"],"The sting is real, and it is a wave (ER22). \"Third time lucky\" is his thought, and repeating it to herself would make it hers (DT19). The sentence she chose is still true after the laugh."],["She tells the classmate it is not funny to her.",["IE10","IE5"],"One fair, truthful sentence with no apology in it keeps her self-respect (IE10). She describes and does not attack, so he is told once and plainly what the joke did (IE5). Whether he takes it in is his part."]]],["She accepts the resit. Then the date is posted: it is the Saturday of her sister's wedding, and her mother says, \"you can't seriously be choosing an exam\".",[["She asks the department about another date, with the facts.",["IE5","ER12"],"A clash is a new problem, so the loop starts again (ER12). Before she chooses between the two, she finds out whether she has to. One clear ask with the facts attached (IE5) may dissolve the dilemma."],["She makes pros and cons for each choice.",["DT5"],"Her mother's sentence is pressure, and pressure makes fast decisions. Four boxes for each choice, written down, slow it (DT5). They are checked against her own goals, long term above this week. Her mother's wish goes in the boxes as one entry among others."],["She accepts that she will miss one, and chooses in wise mind.",["M3","DT13"],"Reason says the exam and emotion says the wedding. Wise mind reads both and can act before every doubt is gone (M3). Whichever she chooses, something is lost, and acceptance carries the loss (DT13). Her mother's hurt is valid too, and she can say so without changing her choice (IE22)."]]]]}},{"id":"G7","title":"biosocial: biology is the base case, the transaction is the step","marks":[["min","the least of","r08b.html#0ef1ef","8b · Any Two Reals Compare"],["ε","the emotional reading of a situation"],["t½","how long until it halves"],["🔥","crisis; in IE, the extreme signal"],["Δ","the change in","r13b.html#23f506","13b · A Slope Means No Gap"],["⇐","follows from: ⇒ read backwards, the left side holds when the right side does","r02j.html","2j · Sets by Hand"],["δ","a small step of time or size"],[">","is more than"],["lim","what it settles toward in the long run","r10a.html#c5f5eb","10a · The Challenge and the Answer"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does the tulip in a rose garden show a poor fit with no one at fault? Which of the two sides, the born or the round-by-round, could you still change?","tree":{"t":"Jean was born sensitive, and her school mocked anyone who cried over a grade. A bad grade arrives. What does she do?","o":[["She finds one place where feelings about grades are allowed, a friend or a study group, and goes there with it.",["G7","IE16"],4,"The biosocial view has two sides: what she was born with, and what the world around her does with it. Her sensitivity is the base case, given at birth, and no skill changes it. The step can be broken, though: one signal that is heard stops the series of louder signals and harder disbelief. So the solving goes on the surrounding side. She finds people near her and like her (IE16), and one setting that hears her is enough. She is a tulip in a rose garden, and she is looking for a better bed."],["She tells herself her sensitivity is a starting point, not a fault, and the shame eases.",["G7","IE24"],7,"The school taught her that the feeling was wrong, and she learned to say it to herself. That is self-invalidation, the taught end of the first line (G10). The biosocial view names causes on both sides and blames neither: a poor fit, with no one at fault. Validating herself (IE24) means saying what is true: she feels strongly, and the grade mattered. The shame eases because it was built on \"something is wrong with me\", and that claim does not hold."],["She lets the tears come in private, then goes to class.",["G7","ER22"],1,"She feels things strongly and comes back slowly, and those are traits, not choices. Tolerating means letting the wave run its own length (ER22) in a place that is safe for it, without disowning it and without letting it decide the day. That is the middle of the first line (G10). Then class. She is doing the best she can with the body she has."],["She hides the feeling and tells everyone she doesn't care.",["G7","G10"],6,"This is what the school rewarded, so it makes sense that she does it: in that world the one who gives up her signal is told \"good\". It is one of the two ends the series can run to, with the outburst as the other. \"I don't care\" works for a day. The feeling is still there, unheard now even by her, and the line swings back."]],"e":[["She takes it to a study group. One of them shrugs: \"it's only a grade\". The others nod and open their books.",[["She says what she needs: \"I just want to say it, not be fixed\".",["IE5","IE23"],"\"It's only a grade\" is the old world in a new room (G11). The step in the series is the same as before: not heard, so say it louder or stop saying it. She takes a third way and asks plainly for the kind of validation she wants (IE5). Listening is the first of the levels (IE23), and most people can give it once they are asked."],["She notices it as invalidation and validates herself.",["IE24","G11"],"She can name what happened: her feeling was called too big for its cause. Knowing the pattern (G11) keeps her from running its next step, which is to agree with them. The one who shrugged is doing his best too, and may only know how to fix. She says to herself what a friend would say (IE24)."],["She lets it go and finds one person there who listens.",["IE16","DT13"],"The group as a whole is not the garden she hoped for, and she accepts that (DT13). A group is made of people, though, and \"all of them\" was more than the facts showed (IE20). One person who stays after and asks how she is doing is where a friendship starts (IE16)."]]],["The shame eases. At dinner she mentions the grade, and a parent says, \"you're too sensitive, you always have been\".",[["She describes what happened without arguing: \"I got a bad grade and I'm upset\".",["M4","IE10"],"\"Too sensitive\" is a judgment of her. \"I got a bad grade and I'm upset\" is a description of the evening (M4). She does not have to win the judgment to keep the description. Said without apology, it keeps her self-respect (IE10)."],["She remembers the biosocial view: sensitivity is a starting point, the setting is the other half.",["G7","G9"],"The parent has named one half and called it the whole. \"Always have been\" is true: it is the base case, and it came with her. The other half is the dialogue between a person and the world around her (G9), and the dinner table is part of that world. A parent who says this may fear that taking the feeling seriously will make it bigger (G7)."],["She lets the hurt be there, and does something soothing.",["DT10","ER22"],"Being called too sensitive hurts a sensitive person more than most, and that is the transaction in a single sentence. The hurt is a wave (ER22). Soothing is being gentle with herself (DT10), and it does for her what the table did not."]]],["She lets the tears come in private and goes to class. Twenty minutes in they start again, and the girl beside her glances over.",[["She steps out for two minutes and splashes cold water on her face.",["DT6"],"She is slow to come back to baseline, and the body can be helped. Cold water on the face slows the heart quickly (DT6). It is a crisis skill, because crying in a room that mocks it is close to a crisis for her (DT2). Two minutes later she is back in her seat."],["She focuses on one thing: writing down what the lecturer says.",["DT9","M5"],"One thing in the moment (M5). Attention put fully on the writing leaves less of it for the wave, and that is distraction used with wise mind (DT9). She is not pretending the feeling away, which would be the old end of the line. It is still there, under the pen."],["She lets them come quietly, and stays.",["ER22","DT13"],"The school said tears were a disaster. She tests that: a few tears, one glance, and the lecture goes on. A wave that is not blocked passes sooner (ER22). She accepts that she is someone who cries (DT13), and that this is allowed in more places than she was taught."]]]]}},{"id":"G8","title":"the transaction drawn: one dysfunction, five instabilities, fed back","marks":[["⇄","each moves the other"],["Λ","instability: Λ_e emotion, Λ_a acts, Λ_h relationships, Λ_☉ self, Λ_T thinking"]],"practice":[],"u":"Pick one instability and trace how it feeds back into the pair it came from.","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps changing her major and dropping study groups. What does she do?","o":[["She picks one study group and stays for the whole term, whatever her mood.",["G8","G15"],3,"The transaction is a loop. Emotional instability moves, and is moved by, instability of acts, of relationships, of the self, and of thinking. Dropping groups is the relationship arrow, and changing majors is the self arrow. Any arrow that is cut can break the loop. So she holds one thing steady, a group, and keeps a record of it week by week (G15). Staying whatever her mood means the mood no longer decides the act (G3)."],["She notices the dropping as part of a loop, not a flaw in her, and feels less ashamed.",["G8","G6"],6,"\"I can't stick to anything\" is a judgment of her self. The loop says something different: each instability is an effect of the last round and a cause of the next. Every act is caused (G6). Shame was one of the things turning the loop, because shame after dropping one group made the next group harder to join. Seeing the loop takes that push away."],["She accepts that her choices have been unstable, and keeps her current major for now.",["G8","DT13"],1,"The past changes are facts. Fighting them with \"I should have stayed in the first one\" keeps her looking backward, and it is one more reason to jump again. Accepting them (DT13) lets the present major be a choice and not an escape. \"For now\" asks less than forever (DT25), and it is enough to stop one turn of the loop."],["She switches major again next week.",["G8"],6,"The switch relieves something at once: the dread of the hard module, and the feeling of being in the wrong place. That relief is why it repeats (IE25). The loop turns once more, and each instability feeds the others. It is a choice with causes, and the same four arrows will be there in the new major."]],"e":[["She stays with one group. By week five two of them have paired off as friends, she feels like the spare, and she wants out.",[["She makes pros and cons of quitting, before the urge decides.",["DT5"],"The urge to leave is the loop asking for its next turn. Four boxes, written now and checked against her own goal for the term (DT5), put the long run beside the relief. She decides from wise mind and not from the feeling of being spare."],["She notices it is the old loop, and commits to week six.",["G8","G5"],"She has been here before: a feeling about the group, then the exit. Naming it as the loop (G8) puts a gap in it. A fresh commitment is made one week at a time (G5). Week six is all she has to promise."],["She tells the group she is finding it hard, and asks for one change.",["IE5","IE7"],"Leaving in silence was the old act. Saying what is happening and asking for one change, such as rotating who works with whom (IE5), is the new one. GIVE keeps it gentle (IE7). She can't know that they meant to leave her out (IE17), and asking is how she finds out."]]],["She sees it as a loop. At home a parent says, \"you can't stick to anything, you never could\".",[["She validates herself: the loop has causes, and she is working on it.",["IE24","G6"],"The parent has said aloud the judgment she had just put down. She gives herself what the sentence leaves out (IE24): this has causes, and she is five weeks into changing one of them. The parent is in the first assumption too, doing the best they can, and worried (G6)."],["She weakens \"anything\" to \"some things\": she has kept this group for a month.",["IE20","IE21"],"\"Anything\" and \"never\" are claims about every case, and one case breaks them (IE21). She has one: this group, for a month. \"Some things, so far\" is the claim that fits the facts (IE20). It leaves room for the parent to be partly right and for her to be changing."],["She lets the comment pass, and keeps going to the group.",["DT19","DT13"],"The comment is a thought her parent had, said out loud. She can watch it go by without taking it in as a fact about her (DT19). She accepts that the parent sees her this way for now (DT13). The record of weeks will answer better than an argument could."]]],["She keeps the major. Then she fails a core module in it, and a friend says, \"maybe it's a sign\".",[["She asks her adviser whether the module can be retaken, and plans it.",["IE5","ER12"],"A failed module is a problem with solutions. It is not a verdict on the major. The loop would turn it into a reason to jump. She takes it to the adviser with one clear question (IE5) and works the problem from the top (ER12)."],["She checks the facts: one module, not the major.",["ER7","IE21"],"\"A sign\" is an interpretation, and the fact under it is one failed module (ER7). Reading one point as the whole line is a claim stronger than the facts (IE21). She checks how likely the worst is, and what she would do if it came."],["She accepts the fail, and keeps the major for now, as she decided.",["DT13","DT16"],"This is the fork her decision was made for. Accepting the fail (DT13) and turning the mind back to the choice she made (DT16) is the stable act the loop has not seen before. Sadness about the module can come along. It does not get to pick the major."]]]]}},{"id":"G9","title":"the environment–person system: a dialogue between two systems","marks":[["⇢","can lead to (possible, not necessary)"],["bg","background"]],"practice":[],"u":"The person is three systems, each moving the other two. Why does that make thoughts of dying possible but not fixed?","tree":{"t":"Mike's scholarship is cut. What does he do?","o":[["He sees the financial aid office this week and applies for two bursaries.",["G9","ER12"],4,"A person and the world around him are two systems in dialogue. The cut is a life change, on the world's side. He answers on that side: he defines the problem, lists solutions, picks one, acts, and checks (ER12). The aid office is also social support, which is where the world's side of the picture begins. He did not cause the cut, and he is the one who has to solve it."],["He notices the worry in his body and his thoughts, and slows his breathing before he decides anything.",["G9","DT6"],6,"The person is three systems: thinking, acting, and body with feeling. Each moves the other two. The cut reached his thinking first, and his body followed. Since each moves the others, he can come in at any of them, and the body is the quickest: paced breathing slows the heart within a minute (DT6). Calmer thinking follows the calmer body, and then he can decide well."],["He accepts that money will be tight this term and keeps studying.",["G9","DT13"],1,"The cut is a fact now. \"They shouldn't have\" keeps part of him arguing with a committee that is not listening. Accepting it (DT13) is not approving of it (DT14). It keeps the acting system, his study, from being dragged down by the thinking one. This is living a term that is not the one he wanted (DT12)."],["He stops eating properly and stops going to class.",["G9"],6,"Each system moves the others, so the cut spreads: worry cuts appetite, an empty stomach worsens mood, and low mood keeps him from class. The body's basics were his protection against vulnerability (ER19), and they have gone first. It makes sense as a response to a shock. It also turns one loss into three."]],"e":[["He sees the aid office. Both bursaries are closed for this year, and the officer says, \"you should have come in September\".",[["He asks what opens next term, and sets a reminder.",["IE5","G14"],"\"Should have\" is about September. His question is about January, and he stays on it (IE5). The reminder answers the third of the four questions ahead of time: the thought will come to mind when the fund opens (G14)."],["He looks for part-time work on campus that fits his timetable.",["ER12"],"The first solution failed, and the problem-solving loop has a return for that: go back to the list and take the next one (ER12). A closed fund says nothing about him. It moves him to the next line of the plan."],["He notices the worry in body and thought, and slows down before deciding.",["G9","DT6"],"The officer's remark stings, and the sting reaches all three systems at once (G9). A decision made in that state would be emotion mind deciding (M3). One minute of paced breathing first (DT6). The options will be the same afterwards, and he will see them better."]]],["He calms his breathing. That night he lies awake, and at 3 a.m. the rent has become \"I'll have to drop out\".",[["He writes a two-line plan for the rent, then lies down again.",["ER21","ER12"],"A worry in bed is sorted first: can it be solved? The rent can, so it gets two lines on paper, problem and first step (ER21). The full plan belongs to daylight (ER12). Once it is on paper his mind no longer has to hold it."],["He aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"Trying to sleep is an effort, and effort wakes him further. Rest is the aim he can reach (ER21). \"I'll have to drop out\" is a 3 a.m. thought, and the rule is to leave such thoughts unsolved until morning. Tired minds make every problem look worse."],["He uses paired relaxation, muscle by muscle.",["DT7"],"The worry sits in the thinking system, and the door to it at 3 a.m. is the body (G9). Tensing and letting go, one muscle group at a time, with one word on each breath out (DT7), gives his attention something slow to follow. He practised it on easier nights so that it would be there on this one."]]],["He accepts a tight term. Then his laptop dies, a week before two essays are due, and a classmate says, \"just buy another\".",[["He asks the library about a loan laptop.",["IE5","ER12"],"A new problem goes to the top of the loop (ER12). Asking an office for help is a skill (IE5), and it goes against the pull to manage alone and look fine, which is the second line of the three (G10). The library is social support too, of an official kind."],["He accepts this too, and does the next essay in the library.",["DT13","DT12"],"\"Just buy another\" comes from a different life. He accepts that his is the one with library hours in it (DT12). Acceptance leaves the pain of that and drops the fight (DT13). The essay does not care which machine it is written on."],["He tells a friend how hard it is, and asks to borrow one for a week.",["IE16","IE5"],"Telling one person the true state of things is the opposite of seeming to cope (G10). The ask is specific and has an end date (IE5). Friendships grow by this kind of exchange (IE16), and he can lend something back when he has it."]]]]}},{"id":"G10","title":"three dialectical dimensions: three lines a person swings along","marks":[["⟷","a line between two ends"]],"practice":[],"u":"On each of the three lines, why is the work a synthesis and not a jump to the other end?","tree":{"t":"Zoe panics in front of her teacher, then tells everyone the exam is no big deal. What does she do?","o":[["She tells her teacher she is anxious about the exam and asks for one practice paper.",["G10","IE5"],5,"She has swung along two lines in one afternoon. On the first she went from feeling everything to disowning it. On the second she went from visibly needing help to seeming fine. On each line one end rises from biology and the other from what her world taught. The work is a synthesis on each line and not a jump to the other end. \"I'm anxious, and I'd like one practice paper\" is a synthesis on both: the feeling is owned, and the need is turned into an ask (IE5). It has to be learned in the classroom, because that is where she swings."],["She owns the worry to herself: \"I care about this, and it scares me\".",["G10","ER6"],7,"The panic and \"no big deal\" are two ends of one line, and neither is a lie: she does feel that much, and she did learn to wave it away. The middle is a sentence that keeps both, and naming the emotion exactly is how she finds it (ER6). \"I care, and it scares me\" has the size of the feeling without the alarm, and the calm without the denial. Opposites held together are a dialectic (IE19). She blames herself for neither end, because both were caused."],["She lets herself feel the fear before the exam without hiding it or acting it out.",["G10","ER22"],1,"At one end the fear is acted out, and at the other it is hidden. Between them there is a third thing to do with a feeling, which is to have it. The fear rises and falls like a wave when she neither blocks it nor feeds it (ER22). It fits the facts, since an exam is a real test (ER8), so it is not there to be removed. It is there to be carried into the room."],["She keeps swinging: panic one day, \"don't care\" the next.",["G10"],6,"A person at one end of a line soon finds herself at the other, and each end sets up the next. The panic shames her, so she disowns it. The disowned fear builds up, so she panics. Both ends have causes, one in her and one in what she was taught. The swing goes on, and neither end revises for the exam."]],"e":[["She tells her teacher. The teacher smiles, says \"you'll be fine, you always are\", and turns to the next student.",[["She asks again, clearly: \"could I have one practice paper?\"",["IE5","IE13"],"\"You always are\" is the price of seeming to cope: people believe it (G10). The old move would be to agree and leave. She says the ask again at the same height, calm, without softening it (IE13). That is the broken record of DEAR MAN (IE5). The teacher did not refuse. The teacher had not heard an ask yet."],["She validates her own worry, since the teacher didn't.",["IE24","G10"],"The teacher meant kindness and delivered invalidation: the worry was waved away. She holds both of those as true (IE19). Then she gives herself what was missing (IE24): this worry makes sense before this exam. Agreeing with \"you'll be fine\" would be the disowning end of the line again."],["She finds a practice paper online herself.",["ER12"],"One route closed, and the problem is still there, so she takes the next solution on the list (ER12). This is not seeming to cope. She has said the need out loud, and now she is meeting it. Her calm on the outside and the facts on the inside match this time."]]],["She owns the worry. As the exam gets closer it grows, and the night before, her friends are all saying they haven't revised and don't care.",[["She copes ahead: she rehearses the exam morning in her mind.",["ER18"],"A fear that grows as the day nears can be met before the day. She imagines the morning in detail, with the fear in it, and rehearses what she will do (ER18). The rehearsal includes the thing she fears most, a blank on the first question, and her skilled answer to it."],["She checks the facts about the exam: what it covers, how much it counts.",["ER7"],"Her friends offer the \"don't care\" end, and it is tempting. The check asks what is true (ER7): the exam counts for this much, covers these topics, and she has done this many. The fear fits, and now it has a size (ER8). Fear at its right size is useful, because it gets her to revise."],["She rides each wave of worry without acting on it.",["ER22"],"The worry comes in waves through the evening. Each one carries an urge, to panic or to join the friends in not caring. She watches each wave rise and fall and does neither (ER22). She has the feeling and stays off both ends of the line (G10)."]]],["She lets herself feel the fear openly. Outside the exam hall a classmate says, loudly, \"calm down, you're stressing everyone out\".",[["She says, \"I'm nervous, that's all, I'll be okay\".",["IE10","M4"],"One truthful sentence, with no apology for the feeling (IE10). It describes and does not defend (M4). It is neither end of the line: she does not act the fear out at him, and she does not deny it. He has been told the plain fact."],["She lets the comment pass and keeps feeling what she feels.",["G10","ER22"],"He is asking her to go back to the taught end, to hide it for the comfort of others. That request is how the taught end got taught (G11). She declines without a word, by going on feeling it quietly (ER22). Owning an emotion does not need the room's permission."],["She does paced breathing before going in.",["DT6"],"Having the fear is not the same as leaving it at any height. Regulating works both ways, like a thermostat (ER1), and turning it down a little before the exam is her own choice, not his. Breathing out longer than in slows the heart (DT6). She goes in with the fear smaller and still hers."]]]]}},{"id":"G11","title":"genesis: worlds in which emotion is not validated","marks":[["⊨","fits; is true in"],["♮","name the emotion"],["⊣","blocks, or works against"],["dom","what the thing applies to: its range of use","r09a.html#1128ad","9a · A Function Is Its Pairs"]],"practice":[],"u":"Choose two of the worlds that do not validate emotion. What does each one teach a vulnerable person about their feelings?","tree":{"t":"Randy's family told him to sort out his grades on his own. At university, a module goes badly. What does he do?","o":[["He books a meeting with his personal tutor, which his family never did.",["G11","IE5"],5,"He grew up in one of the worlds where feelings are to be coped with alone. That world taught him a rule, and the rule came with him to a place where it is false: here there are tutors whose work is to be asked. Adults find such worlds too, so he will meet the rule again. A behaviour has to be learned in each setting that needs it, and booking the meeting is the first trial in this one. The ask itself is DEAR MAN (IE5)."],["He reminds himself that needing help is normal, not weak.",["G11","IE3"],7,"\"Sort it out yourself\" hardened in him into \"asking is weakness\". That is a myth, and a myth claims every case, so one case breaks it (IE3): the strongest student he knows goes to office hours. The model names where the belief came from without blaming his family for it. No one teaches perfectly, and they may have been taught the same. With the cause found, the shame has less to stand on."],["He accepts that asking feels strange and asks anyway, feeling awkward.",["G11","DT13"],1,"The awkwardness is what an old rule feels like when it is broken. It does not mean he is doing something wrong. He does not wait for it to go. He accepts it as the cost of the new act (DT13) and acts with it there, which is willingness (DT17). It also counts as opposite action for shame that does not fit, which means not hiding (ER11)."],["He tries to fix it alone and tells no one.",["G11"],6,"This is what he was taught, and he does it well: to everyone else he looks fine. That is seeming to cope when he is not, the taught end of the second line (G10). It has a cause and a cost. The module stays unsolved for longer, and no one knows to help."]],"e":[["He books the tutor meeting. An hour before it, the tutor cancels with one line and no new time.",[["He rebooks the same day, and asks for a time this week.",["IE5","G11"],"The old rule has a reading ready: \"see, no one helps, sort it out yourself\". That is the world he came from speaking (G11). He treats the cancellation as a cancellation and asks again, with a time attached (IE5). He was never shown how to ask twice, so he is learning it now."],["He checks what he can do before then: the module page, the office hours.",["ER12"],"One solution is delayed, and the list has others (ER12). This is different from fixing it alone and telling no one. The meeting is still booked and the need is still said. He is working on it while he waits."],["He lets the disappointment be, without reading it as a sign.",["ER22","IE17"],"He can't observe the tutor's reasons, so he does not invent them (IE17). The disappointment is real and it passes like a wave (ER22). Reading it as a sign would make one cancelled hour into a rule about people. That would be a claim stronger than the facts (IE21)."]]],["He tells himself needing help is normal. On the phone a parent asks, \"why can't you manage on your own? Your brother did\".",[["He validates himself: asking for help is how university works.",["IE24","G11"],"This is the family rule with a comparison added. He knows where it comes from, and he does not have to take it on (G11). He says the true thing to himself (IE24). The parent is likely passing on what they were given, and is doing the best they can with it (G6)."],["He tells the parent what he is doing, without apologizing.",["IE10"],"FAST: fair to the parent, no apology for seeing a tutor, sticking to what he values, truthful about the module (IE10). He does not need the parent's agreement to keep the appointment. He does need his own self-respect on the way to it."],["He weakens \"I should manage alone\" to \"sometimes I need help\".",["IE20"],"\"Should manage alone\" covers every case, always. \"Sometimes I manage alone and sometimes I need help\" fits the facts (IE20). The weaker claim keeps what was good in his upbringing, his independence, and drops only the \"always\". Both sides stay whole, and that is the middle path (M9)."]]],["He asks anyway. The tutor answers while looking at the clock, and sighs when he asks a second question.",[["He keeps to his question, calmly, until it is answered.",["IE5","IE10"],"The sigh invites him to shrink the question or drop it. He stays on it, and that is the broken record (IE5). He does not apologize for needing the answer (IE10). An impatient answer is still an answer, and it is more than a silent student gets."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt: the tutor may be busy.",["IE17"],"A sigh and a clock can be observed. \"He thinks I'm a nuisance\" can't (IE17). The old rule would fill in the worst reading. Tutors are overstretched like everyone who teaches (G11), and that is a cause that has nothing to do with him."],["He notices the urge to apologize and leave, and stays.",["ER10","DT4"],"The urge is shame's: get out, take up less room. STOP gives him a second to see it as an urge (DT4). The shame does not fit, because asking a tutor a question breaks no value of his. So he does the opposite and stays for the answer (ER10)."]]]]}},{"id":"G12","title":"two analyses: what was done that should not be, what was not done that should be","marks":[["G","goals"],["⋆","a⋆: the best choice"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do something ineffective done and something effective not done call for two different analyses?","tree":{"t":"Sarah snapped at a teaching assistant, and also never asked for the extension she needed. What does she do?","o":[["She runs a chain on the snap and a missing-links check on the extension.",["G12","G13","G14"],7,"There are two kinds of problem and one analysis for each. Something done that should not have been gets a chain. Something needed and not done gets the four questions. The snap was done, so she writes its chain: what made her vulnerable (a night of four hours), what started it (the TA's \"this is late\"), the links (heat in her face, \"he thinks I'm lazy\", the urge to hit back), the act, and what followed. The extension was not done, so she asks in order: did she know she needed it, yes. Was she willing to ask, no. She stops at that first no. Each problem now has its own fix."],["She stops calling the week a disaster and sees two separate problems.",["G12","M5"],6,"\"A disaster\" is one judgment laid over two different events, and it gives her nothing to work on. Sorting comes first. One thing was done and one thing was not done, each with its own causes, and the tool for each is different. Once they are told apart, each is smaller than the week. The judgment drops (M5), and what is left is two descriptions: one sharp sentence to a TA, and one email not sent."],["She accepts that both happened, and lives with the missed extension and the awkwardness with the TA.",["G12","DT13"],1,"Neither analysis reaches backward. A chain mends the harm and plans for next time, and the four questions fix the next ask, but the snap was said and the deadline has passed. That part is for acceptance (DT13). An analysis is best done below the breakdown point (ER23), so accepting first and analysing when she is ready is the right order (∎). She was doing the best she could that week."],["She goes over both in her head all week and does nothing.",["G4"],6,"Going over it feels like analysis, and it is not one. A chain is written down, in order, and ends in a plan and a repair. Rumination circles the same two moments and ends where it began (ER3). She is treating both problems as one lump, so neither gets its tool. Both are left ready to happen again."]],"e":[["She runs both analyses. The chain shows the snap came on four hours of sleep. She writes chains for two older snaps, and the same link is in each.",[["She plans a cut at the tired link: no hard conversations after 10 p.m.",["G13","ER19"],"One chain shows one event. Several chains for the same act show the pattern, the links that keep coming back, and hers is tiredness. A link that appears in every chain is a key link by the usual test: imagine it gone, and the snaps go with it (G13). So the plan goes there. The rule about 10 p.m. prevents the starting event, and sleep lowers the vulnerability (ER19)."],["She works on sleep first.",["ER19","ER14"],"A chain ends with a plan to lower the vulnerability, and hers has now been named three times. Sleep is one of the body's basics (ER19), and seeing to it lowers the whole hill of emotion (ER14). She is fixing the first link of the chain, where one fix covers every later night."],["She apologizes to the TA, and says what she will do differently.",["IE10","IE7"],"The last step of a chain is repair: mend the harm itself, then overcorrect. The harm was to the TA, so the mending goes to the TA. An apology with the plan attached is the overcorrection. FAST keeps it to one apology and no grovelling (IE10), and GIVE keeps the tie (IE7)."]]],["She sees two problems. In the next seminar the TA answers her questions in one word and does not meet her eye.",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt and greets the TA normally.",["IE17","IE7"],"She can observe one-word answers. She can't observe what the TA thinks of her (IE17). The coolness goes into the chain under what followed, as harm to a relationship, and that box still awaits its repair. Meanwhile an easy manner keeps the door open (IE7)."],["She apologizes once, briefly.",["IE10","G12"],"Of her two problems this is the done one, and a done thing that caused harm calls for repair (G13). One clear apology for the one sharp sentence, with no speech about her week. FAST says no needless apologies (IE10), and that leaves the needed one, said once."],["She lets the awkwardness be, and keeps going to the seminar.",["ER22","DT13"],"The awkwardness is a consequence, and it is the right size for what happened (ER8). Its urge is to skip the seminar. That would add a new not-done thing to the list (G12). She stays, and it fades as she keeps turning up (ER22)."]]],["She accepts both. Then the late penalty appears on her transcript, and a friend says, \"why on earth didn't you just ask?\"",[["She asks whether there is an appeal route for the penalty.",["IE5","ER12"],"Accepting the past does not stop her solving the present. The penalty is a new fact and may have a remedy (ER12). She asks plainly (IE5), and this time she asks in time. It is also a trial of the very act she missed (G15)."],["She runs the four questions, so the next extension is asked for in time.",["G14"],"Her friend's \"why didn't you\" is the right question asked the wrong way. The four questions ask it properly. Did she know an extension was needed? Yes. Was she willing to ask? No: she thought asking would look weak. She stops there. The fixes for the second question are willingness, pros and cons, or opposite action (DT17, DT5, ER10). A calendar alarm would not have helped, because memory was not what failed."],["She accepts the penalty, and turns the mind again when it stings.",["DT16"],"The penalty will be on the transcript each time she opens it, and each time is a fork (DT16). Her friend's question stings because it is fair. She accepts the mark and the sting. The analysis is for the next deadline, and acceptance is for this one."]]]]}},{"id":"G13","title":"chain analysis: a path through 𝐖 and 𝐗, cut at its key links","marks":[["⟹","sets the stage for"],["S","a prompting event"],["k","what follows an act"],["<","is less than"],["″","x″ (read «x double prime»): a third thing of the same kind, after x and x′","r13d.html#de837e","13d · The Power Technique"],["∖","without: the left set with the members of the right set taken out","r02e.html","2e · Membership and Same Members"],["≪","is much smaller than"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why describe the problem act so that an actor could play it? Why look for the links where a skill would have broken the chain?","tree":{"t":"Terry stayed up all night gaming before an exam. What does he do?","o":[["He writes the chain from start to finish and plans a cut at its key link, \"one more game\", with his console off by 11 p.m. the night before.",["G13"],7,"His chain, in order. The act, described exactly: he played from 9 p.m. to 5 a.m. and sat the exam on two hours of sleep. What made him vulnerable: four short nights and no dinner. What started it, out in the world: at 9 a classmate posted \"this paper is going to be brutal\". The links: the thought \"I'm not ready\", a tight stomach, the urge to get away from it, \"one game to calm down\", a win, \"one more\", and at 1 a.m. \"too late to revise now\". What followed: relief at once, and a failed paper later. Then the test for a key link, which is to imagine it gone: without \"one more\" the night ends at ten. So that link gets the skill, a console already switched off (DT24), and the vulnerability gets a plan of its own (ER19)."],["He drops \"I'm so lazy\" and describes the night so an actor could play it.",["G13","M4"],6,"The first step of a chain is to describe the act exactly: what he did, said, thought and felt, and how much. \"Lazy\" fails that test, because no actor could play it. \"At 9 he read the post, his stomach tightened, he picked up the controller\" can be played. Each link is one of five kinds: an act, a body sense, a thought, an emotion, or something that happened (M4). Written this way the night is a row of small caused things, and at each one he can ask whether another person might have thought or done otherwise there. The shame eases because nothing in the row is a character."],["He accepts the exam went badly, and lets the disappointment be without calling himself lazy.",["G13","DT13","M5"],1,"A chain has a box for what followed: for him and for others, soon and later. The exam goes in that box, and nothing in the analysis changes what is written there. That part is for acceptance (DT13). A chain is not written to undo the night. It is written for the next one, and it has to be written from below the breakdown point (ER23). So letting the disappointment rise and fall first (ER22) is part of doing the analysis well. He was doing the best he could with the links he could see."],["He decides he will never change and games again tonight.",["G13","G4"],6,"This is the same chain run again, and it can be read that way. What makes him vulnerable tonight: shame about the exam, and still no sleep. What starts it: the mark. The links: \"I never change\", a heavy feeling, the urge to get away from it, the controller. One chain shows one night. Two chains side by side show the pattern, the links that keep coming back, and here it is \"a feeling I want to get away from, then the game\" (G8). That repeated link is the one to cut, whenever he is ready to write it down."]],"e":[["The console goes off at 11 for three nights. On the fourth, the night before his next exam, his friends message at 10:40: the team needs a fourth player, and \"you always carry us\".",[["He adds the message to the chain as a new starting event and plans for it: he says no now, and offers a time after the exam.",["IE13","IE7"],"A chain always starts with something in the world, and tonight it is a different something. The repair rule covers it: prevent the starting event where he can, and where he can't, cope ahead (ER18). He can't stop friends from messaging, so he has the no ready. It is said at a chosen height (IE13), with GIVE to keep the tie (IE7), and with a date, so the friendship has somewhere to go."],["He burns the bridge: he unplugs the console and gives the controller to his roommate.",["DT24"],"The first chain put the key link at \"one more\". Tonight shows a link earlier than that: the controller within reach at 10:40. Refining a chain means splitting links until none splits further, and the earliest key link is the cheapest to cut. With the controller in his roommate's hands (DT24), the choice is made in the afternoon, when choosing is easy (DT5)."],["He makes pros and cons before he answers.",["DT5"],"A chain marks each link as moving him toward the problem act or away from it. \"You always carry us\" moves him toward it, because praise pays (IE25). Pros and cons written before he answers is a link that moves him away (DT5). He fills all four boxes against his own goal and answers from wise mind (M3). The chain now has a working link in the place where the old one ran straight through."]]],["He writes the night out link by link. At \"told my mother I was revising\" the shame comes up hard and he wants to tear the page out.",[["He keeps going, through to the repair.",["G13","ER10"],"The lie belongs in the box for what followed, under harm to others, and a chain does not end at that box. Its last steps are repair. Repair mends the harm itself and not something beside it, so the mending is telling his mother the truth. Then he overcorrects: he tells her the plan as well. Shame's urge was to hide the page (ER11), and finishing the chain through to repair is the opposite action (ER10)."],["He takes a break and does something soothing, then finishes.",["DT10"],"A chain needs a clear head, because he has to recall small things in the right order. Past the breakdown point that can't be done (ER23). A soothing break with a set return (DT10) keeps the analysis accurate. He marks the line where he stopped, and writes the shame down there as a link of its own, because an emotion is a link too."],["He drops the judgments that crept back in.",["M5"],"\"Told my mother I was revising\" is a link. \"Because I'm a liar\" is not a link, since it did not happen at any moment of the night. The test for each line is whether it could be filmed or felt at a time on the clock. Judgments fail that test and come out (M5). What stays is exact enough to replay, and the key links only show in a chain that exact."]]],["He accepts the bad exam. Then the results come: he has failed the module, and his housemates, who all passed, go quiet when he walks in.",[["He asks about the resit and plans it.",["ER12","IE5"],"The box for what followed now has its later entry, so the repair step has something to work on. A failure is mended by succeeding at the thing failed, and not by self-blame. That means the resit, asked about clearly (IE5) and planned (ER12). The plan carries the chain's findings: console off the night before, and sleep seen to in the week before (ER19)."],["He checks the facts: a failed module, a resit, not a failed degree.",["ER7"],"A chain keeps what happened apart from what he made of it. What happened: a failed module, and a quiet room. What he made of it: \"they think I'm finished\". The second is a thought link, and a thought link can be checked (ER7). He can't observe their thoughts (IE17). Left unchecked, that thought would be the first link of the next chain."],["He accepts this too, without calling himself lazy.",["DT13","M5"],"He has already run this ending once: disappointment, then \"lazy\", then the urge to get away. This time he lets the sadness come as sadness (DT13) and leaves the judgment off (M5). The link where a feeling turned into a verdict was a key link, and accepting the feeling is the skill that goes there. The more painful the fact, the more turns of the mind it takes (DT16)."]]]]}},{"id":"G14","title":"missing links: the first question that fails","marks":[["🤲̂","willingness"],["j⋆","the first check that fails"],["∪","joined with: everything in either set","r02e.html","2e · Membership and Same Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why stop at the first no among the four questions, and not answer all four?","tree":{"t":"Claire knew she should email her professor about a missed lab, and was willing to, but it never came to mind. What does she do?","o":[["She asks the four questions in order, stops at the third, and sets a phone reminder for 9 a.m. tomorrow.",["G14"],5,"Missing links is four questions, always in the same order. One: did she know what was needed? Yes, an email to the professor. Two: was she willing? Yes. Three: did the thought of doing it come to mind? No. She stops there, at the first no, and does not go on to the fourth. Each question has its own fixes, and for the third they are cues: a calendar entry, an alarm, a note by the bed, or rehearsing the moment ahead (ER18). She picks one and sets it where her morning really happens. Working on willingness (DT17) would have mended a link that held."],["She stops calling herself careless, because the analysis found a cause and not a character flaw.",["G14","G6"],7,"The order of the questions matters, because each one rules something out. She knew, so this was not ignorance. She was willing, so this was not wilfulness or low spirits. Only then comes the question that failed: it never came to mind. \"Careless\" would be an answer at the second question, and she passed the second. So the word is wrong on the evidence, and the shame built on it does not fit (ER8). Forgetting is a cause (G6), and causes have fixes."],["She accepts that the lab mark may be lost, and emails anyway.",["G14","DT13"],1,"Missing links repairs the next time. It does not reach back to the lab she missed, and what it leaves untouched is for acceptance (DT13): the mark may be gone. The analysis still has one thing to say about today. The needed act was the email, the email is still possible, and all four questions now have a yes. So she sends it with the outcome unknown, which is willingness (DT17). Accepting the loss is not giving up the act (DT14)."],["She decides it is too late and never sends the email.",["G14","G4"],6,"Ask the four questions about today, and the answers have changed. Does she know what is needed? Yes. Is she willing? Not now: \"it's too late\" is the second question failing, and the reason is that she has lost heart. The fixes for the second question are different ones: willingness, pros and cons, or opposite action (DT17, DT5, ER10). A reminder would do nothing here. The missing link has moved from memory to will, and it stays missing until that is the question she answers."]],"e":[["The reminder goes off at 9 a.m., in the middle of a lecture, and she swipes it away. By evening the email is three days late, and her lab partner mentions that the professor asked where she was.",[["She runs the four questions again and moves the reminder to straight after the lecture.",["G14"],"Known: yes. Willing: yes. Came to mind: yes this time, at 9. Did anything get in the way of doing it right then? Yes, she was in a lecture. The first no has moved from the third question to the fourth, and that is progress, because the cue worked. The fix now belongs at the fourth: the cue moves to a time when the act is possible. One missed trial does not break a skill (G15)."],["She writes the email in the lecture break.",["DT11"],"At the fourth question, what gets in the way comes from a short list: putting it off, not being in the mood, forgetting how, or \"no one would care\". Hers is putting it off, because three days have turned the email into an essay of apology. One thing in the moment shrinks it: two lines, sent in the break (DT11). FAST keeps it to one sentence of sorry and then the ask (IE10)."],["She asks a friend to remind her at lunch.",["IE5","IE16"],"The fix at each question is chosen from many, and a person can be a cue. Hearing that the professor asked after her brings shame, and shame says tell nobody (ER11). Asking a friend to prompt her at lunch (IE5) answers the third question and goes against \"cope alone\" (G11) in one move. A friend is harder to swipe away than a phone."]]],["She stops calling herself careless and sends the email. The professor's reply: \"you should have been more careful\".",[["She validates herself: forgetting has a cause, and she has fixed it.",["IE24","G6"],"She has the analysis to check the professor's word against. Careful about what? She knew, and she was willing. The gap was at the third question, a cue that was not there. So the valid part is small and exact (IE22): she needed a reminder and had none. She says that to herself as she would to a friend (IE24). The professor sees only the missed lab, and is doing the best they can with that (G6)."],["She replies briefly, thanks the professor, and states her fix.",["IE10","IE7"],"A good analysis ends in a fix, and a fix can be said in one line: \"I've set reminders for every lab date.\" That line is truthful and does not grovel (IE10), and it takes the professor's concern seriously (IE7). She gives the result of the analysis and not a defence, and that keeps her self-respect and the tie together (IE4)."],["She lets the sting pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"\"Should have been more careful\" is now a thought in her head in the professor's voice. It is not one of the four questions and it has no fix attached, so there is nothing to do with it but watch it go (DT19). The sting under it is a wave (ER22). The analysis is finished: cause found, cue set. Rumination would reopen it every evening without adding a link (ER3)."]]],["She emails anyway. The reply is kind and final: the lab can't be made up, and the mark is lost.",[["She asks whether another lab can count instead.",["IE5","ER12"],"The needed act was done, and the answer was still no. That is a new problem and not a missing link, so the tool changes from the four questions to the problem-solving loop (ER12): another solution, asked for clearly and once (IE5). She checks first whether to ask at all (IE11). Knowing which analysis a moment calls for is part of the skill (G12)."],["She accepts the lost mark, and keeps the reminder system.",["DT13","G15"],"Two things could get mixed up: the mark, which is gone, and the method, which worked. Acceptance is for the mark (DT13). For the method she writes the trial down (G15): which question failed, which fix she chose, what she did. The next missing link will be found faster, because she will have this one to read."],["She notices the urge to give up on the module, and doesn't.",["DT4","DT5"],"\"Why bother with the module now\" is the second question starting to fail for every lab still to come. She catches it early. STOP holds the urge (DT4), and pros and cons are one of the fixes that belong to that question (DT5). The four boxes show the trade: every lab ahead, given up for one already gone."]]]]}},{"id":"G15","title":"practice: a trial, a measure, a record, and induction over trials","marks":[["[","[a, b] is every value from a up to b"],["−","less; taken away"],["⊇","holds all of: every member of the right is a member of the left","r02h.html","2h · All the Subsets"],["∩","what both sets share","r02e.html","2e · Membership and Same Members"],["𝐆","the general series"],["☼","each day"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why rate the state before and after each trial? What lets you conclude, from many trials, where a skill works for you?","tree":{"t":"Jean tries a timed study block on three evenings before her history exam. What does she do with what she found?","o":[["She records each trial, with her focus rated before and after, and keeps the block if two out of three helped.",["G15"],3,"Each practice is a trial with the same fields: the situation, the skill, what she did, how long it took, what she felt during, and what she feels now. She rates the state the skill is meant to move, here her focus, before and after. Three trials are a short record. A skill claims \"often\", not \"always\", so one flat evening does not break it, and two good ones do not prove it. She keeps the block and keeps counting. Trying harder, done properly, means measuring."],["She notices that her focus went up even a little, and gives herself credit for it.",["G15","G2"],2,"The record asks what changed, even a little. \"Even a little\" means at least one thing (G2), and on two evenings her focus rating rose. That counts. Credit for it is a reward given straight after a small step, and that is how a behaviour is shaped (IE25). She wants to improve, and here is evidence on paper that she is."],["She accepts that one evening did not work, and keeps going.",["G15","DT13"],1,"One trial where the rating did not move is one trial. She writes it in the record like the others, with its situation: tired, late, hungry. Accepting it (DT13) keeps it as a piece of data and stops it becoming a verdict. Induction needs the bad evenings written down as well, or the conclusion is worth nothing."],["She stops after the first bad evening and decides the method doesn't work.",["G15","G4"],6,"It makes sense to drop what did not help. But \"doesn't work\" is a claim about every evening, drawn from one. That is how a myth is tested, by one case, and it is the wrong test for a skill. Stopping leaves her with no record at all, and so the next method she tries will be judged on one evening too."]],"e":[["She keeps the block. On the fourth evening her focus does not move at all, and her flatmate says, \"told you those timers are a gimmick\".",[["She adds it to the record and keeps going: three of four still helped.",["G15","G2"],"The fourth trial goes in with the same fields as the rest. The count is three of four. \"A gimmick\" is the flatmate's rating, and a rating means something only on its rater's own scale. Her record is on her scale. One more good evening can only raise the count (G2)."],["She checks what was different that evening: sleep, food, mood.",["ER19","ER14"],"Each trial records its situation so that a flat one can be explained. That evening she had slept five hours and skipped lunch. The cause may be her vulnerability and not the block (ER14). So the fix is to the body's basics (ER19), and the trial after it will show whether that was the cause."],["She troubleshoots the block: was it the same length, the same place?",["ER24"],"When a skill does not work, she checks in order and fixes the first thing that failed (ER24). Did she use the skill as written, with the same length and the same place? The record shows the fourth block was in the kitchen, with the flatmate there. That is the first failed check, and it gets fixed before anything else."]]],["She gives herself credit. Then the exam mark comes back lower than she hoped, and her mother says, \"so all that timing was for nothing\".",[["She keeps the record going into the next term, and adds a past paper each week.",["G15","ER18"],"The record measured focus, and focus rose. The exam measured something further on. So the record needs a field nearer the exam, which is past papers, added one a week (ER18). The journal is where mastery is built a step at a time. She changes what is measured and keeps the method."],["She separates the block from the exam: the block raised focus, the exam had other causes.",["G13","G6"],"\"For nothing\" joins two things with one arrow. A chain on the exam would list its own links (G13): which topics came up, how she slept, how she read the questions. The block is one link among them, and by the record it moved her toward the result she wanted. Finding causes works better than blaming the skill."],["She lets the disappointment be.",["ER22","DT13"],"The mark is the mark, and she wanted more. The disappointment fits (ER8) and passes like a wave (ER22). Her mother's sentence hurts because it dismisses the effort (G11). She accepts both the mark and the sentence as facts (DT13), and the record stays open on her desk."]]],["She keeps going. A second evening does not work either, and now it is two of five.",[["She changes one thing (the time of day) and tries again.",["G15","ER24"],"A record lets her run a fair test: change one thing and keep the rest the same. Both bad evenings were late ones, so she moves the block to the afternoon. If she changed three things at once she would not know which one mattered. Each skill is practised at least twice, in different situations, and this is a different situation."],["She accepts two bad evenings, and keeps the plan for one more week.",["DT16","DT13"],"Two of five is not a conclusion yet. The urge is to decide now, and she turns the mind back to the plan (DT16). She accepts the two flat evenings as they are (DT13). One more week of trials will say more than tonight's mood can."],["She asks a classmate what works for them.",["IE5","IE16"],"Other people's trials are data too, though their ratings are on their own scale. A plain question (IE5) to someone studying the same exam (IE16) may give her a variation to test. Whatever the classmate says goes into her record as a new trial. She tests it before she believes it."]]]]}}]},{"id":"M","page":"dbtm","name":"mindfulness","footings":10,"steps":[{"id":"M1","title":"goals: less suffering, the mind held, reality as it is","marks":[["𝕋","times; t₀ is now"],["~","is joined to, by some chain of touches"],["J_eval","a judgment that rates good or bad"],["💭","thoughts"]],"practice":[],"u":"To hold the mind is to hold attention. What two things about attention do you hold? Why does seeing a thought as a thought give you time before you react?","tree":{"t":"During a physics exam, Evita thinks \"I'm going to fail\". What does she do?","o":[["She starts with the question she knows best, so marks go on the board while the thought plays out.",["M1","DT19"],5,"To hold the mind is to hold attention: what it rests on, and for how long. The thought wants her attention, and so does question four. She can't stop the thought from arriving, and she can choose where attention goes next. Putting it on the question she knows best is problem solving inside the exam (ER12): marks are the problem, and they come from writing. Holding attention has to be learned in the hall itself, because a skill is learned in each setting that needs it."],["She notes \"that's a thought, not a fact\" and lets the fear drop a notch.",["M1"],6,"\"I'm going to fail\" arrived looking like a forecast. It is an event in her mind, with causes: a hard first question, and a short night. A thought seen as a thought loses the authority of a fact (DT19). The fear was following the thought, so when the thought is put in its place the fear comes down a notch. One part of the emotion changed, and the rest moved with it (ER5)."],["She lets the thought stay in the background while she works.",["M1","DT19"],1,"She does not argue with the thought, and she does not push it away. Pushing a thought away is a way of holding on to it. It stays in the room like the hum of the lights. This is the mind held and not handed over: the thought is present, and it is not in charge. Meeting the exam as it is includes meeting her own mind as it is this morning."],["She believes the thought and stops writing.",["M1","G4"],6,"Believing it makes sense, because the thought came with fear, and fear feels like evidence. But this is emotion mind, where mood rules and facts do not count (M3). The forecast now makes itself true, since a blank page fails. The thought holds the mind, and the paper pays for it. The pen is still in her hand."]],"e":[["She starts with the question she knows best. Halfway through she sees she has misread it, and the invigilator calls, \"thirty minutes left\".",[["She notes \"that's a thought\" about the rising panic, and rereads the question slowly.",["M1","DT19"],"The misreading is a fact. \"So I really will fail\" is the old thought using it as proof (DT19). She sorts the two, and then gives the question her whole attention, one line at a time. Taking the time she needs before reacting is one of the goals of the skill (M1). Thirty minutes is enough for a slow reread."],["She fixes what she can in the time left, then moves on.",["DT11","ER12"],"The problem has changed, so she solves the new one: which marks can still be saved in thirty minutes (ER12). One thing in the moment (DT11), the part of the answer that still stands. Wishing she had read it properly is attention spent on the past, and the past is over (M5)."],["She runs STOP: pen down, one breath, then decides.",["DT4"],"The urge is to scribble faster, and it is panic's urge. Stopping costs five seconds (DT4). Stepping back and observing shows her the real situation: one question half wrong, three untouched, thirty minutes. Then she proceeds mindfully, which here means choosing the order on purpose."]]],["The fear drops a notch. At the next hard question the thought comes back louder, and this time it adds, \"and everyone will know\".",[["She notices it again, and lets it pass again.",["M1","DT16"],"The skill was never to make the thought stop. It was to see it for what it is each time it comes, and the second time is one more time. Each return is a fork, and she turns the same way (DT16). \"Everyone will know\" is a new thought, and she notices it the same way."],["She does paced breathing for three breaths.",["DT6"],"The louder thought has reached her body: fast heart, tight chest. Three slow breaths, out longer than in, bring the body down (DT6). The body is a part of the emotion she can reach directly (ER5). With her body calmer the thought is easier to see as a thought."],["She skips the hard question for now and comes back to it.",["DT11","M5"],"Doing what works means playing the exam she is in, and that exam allows any order (M5). She moves to a question she can do and takes the hard one later, with whatever time and calm are left (DT11). She has not given up on it. She will meet the hard question again on better terms."]]],["She works with the thought in the background. Then the student beside her closes his paper, stretches, and walks out with forty minutes to go.",[["She brings her attention back to her own paper.",["M2","M5"],"Her attention followed him to the door, and that is what attention does. Mindfulness is the coming back: on purpose, to the present, without judging the drift (M2). One thing at a time, and the thing is her own question (M5)."],["She checks the facts: finishing early says nothing about marks.",["ER7"],"What she saw: a student left early. What she added: \"he found it easy, so I am behind\". She can't observe his paper. Finishing early fits a strong student and it fits one who gave up (ER7). The jolt of fear was built on something she added."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall, and keeps writing.",["ER22"],"The jolt is a small wave and it lasts a few seconds if she leaves it alone (ER22). The background thought tries to ride it forward. She keeps the pen moving, the wave passes under the work, and the thought goes back to being a hum."]]]]}},{"id":"M2","title":"what mindfulness is: on purpose, now, no judging, no clinging","marks":[["⟨","⟨ … ⟩ is a list kept in order","r19d.html","19d · A Part’s Size Divides the Whole’s"],["⟩","closes a list kept in order","r19d.html","19d · A Part’s Size Divides the Whole’s"]],"practice":["meditation ≔ 𝐌  for a set  Δt ,   sitting ,  standing  or  lying ;   focus  or  open","movement :   yoga ,  martial arts ,  dance ,  walking ,  hiking   Θ on the moving body","every form :   drift  ⇒  return ;   then carry it into the day"],"u":"Mindfulness at a moment needs all four conditions at once. Which one fails when you are fully attending to a memory you cling to?","tree":{"t":"Randy rereads the same paragraph of his set text while replaying yesterday's bad seminar. What does he do?","o":[["He notices the drift, writes \"seminar\" on a note to deal with later, and rereads with his finger on the line.",["M2","M5"],5,"Mindfulness has four conditions: attention on purpose, on the present, not judging, not clinging. He is failing the second and the fourth, because the seminar is the past and he is holding on to it. The note gives the seminar a place, and that lets rumination rest (ER3). The finger on the line gives attention one thing to do, in one place (M5). He solves the drift without scolding himself, so the third condition holds too."],["He stops judging himself for drifting and simply comes back.",["M2","M5"],7,"There are two drifts here. One goes to the seminar, and the other goes to \"what's wrong with me, I can't even read\". The second is a judgment, and it takes more attention than the first. When he judges, the rule is not to judge the judging (M5): he notices, and he comes back. Minds drift, and that is a cause and not a fault (G6). Once the scolding stops, the tension it brought goes with it."],["He lets the seminar memory come and go while he keeps reading.",["M2"],1,"Not clinging does not mean throwing the memory out. Pushed away, a memory pushes back. He lets it arrive, sees it as a thought about the past happening now (M5), and lets it leave when it leaves. The reading goes on underneath, slower than he would like. This is accepting his own mind as it is this evening (DT13)."],["He keeps replaying the seminar for the rest of the evening.",["M2","G4"],6,"Replaying makes sense: something went wrong, and his mind is trying to fix it. But replaying is not fixing. It is rumination, and it keeps the shame firing all evening (ER3). The seminar does not change, and the paragraph goes unread. Tomorrow's seminar now starts with a text he has not read."]],"e":[["He writes the note and rereads. The memory pushes back in, with the exact face the tutor made when he gave his answer.",[["He sets a ten-minute slot after reading to think about the seminar on purpose.",["M2","ER21"],"The memory keeps coming because it has been told \"later\" with no time attached. He gives it an appointment (ER21). Thinking about it on purpose, at a set time, is mindful, and that is different from being dragged there (M2). If the ten minutes show a real problem, it goes to problem solving (ER12)."],["He reads one sentence at a time, finger on the line.",["M5"],"A paragraph is too big a unit for a mind that keeps getting pulled away. One sentence is small enough to hold (M5). When he drifts he has lost one sentence and not the page, and coming back is cheap. Doing what works means reading the way tonight allows."],["He notices clinging to the memory, and lets go of it again.",["M2","M6"],"The tutor's face is vivid because he keeps reaching for it. He notices the reaching. In a mind with no walls, things come and go on their own (M6), and the face can pass through like anything else. He may have to let go of it twenty times, and each time counts as the skill."]]],["He comes back without judging. Ten minutes later he finds he has turned two pages and taken in nothing.",[["He comes back again, as many times as it takes.",["M2","DT16"],"Two lost pages are a longer drift, and the move is the same. The skill is in the coming back, and it does not depend on never leaving (M2). Each return is a turn made again at a fork (DT16). Counting the returns as failures would bring the judging back in."],["He takes a five-minute walk, then starts the page again.",["ER14","DT9"],"A mind this sticky may be a tired body. A short walk lowers his vulnerability (ER14), and it is a break with a set return (DT9). He goes back to the page he lost. That page is where the reading really stands."],["He describes what he is doing: \"reading, drifting, coming back\".",["M4"],"He steps back to observe and describe when something is hard, then steps back in (M4). Put into words, the evening is three plain actions. \"Failing to read\" is not one of them, since nobody can observe a failing. The description is accurate, and it is kinder than the story he had."]]],["He lets the memory come and go. Then it comes with a wave of shame, and the thought \"they all saw I hadn't understood it\".",[["He checks whether the shame fits: was anything he did against his values?",["ER7","ER8"],"Shame fits when he has broken his own values or would be rejected by people he cares about if they knew (ER8). He gave a wrong answer in a room built for trying answers. That is no breach of his values, and the facts show no rejection. \"They all saw\" is a guess, because he can't observe their thoughts (IE17). The shame does not fit."],["He rides the wave, and goes on reading.",["ER22"],"Fitting or not, the shame is here, and it moves like a wave (ER22). Its urge is to hide, which tonight means closing the book and skipping tomorrow. He keeps his eyes on the line while the wave peaks and falls."],["He half-smiles, unseen, and opens his hands.",["DT18"],"Shame curls the body in: head down, hands closed. He changes the body first, with a slight half-smile and his hands open on the desk (DT18). The body tells the brain that he is not fighting this. No one in the library sees a thing."]]]]}},{"id":"M3","title":"wise mind: the pair of reason and emotion, read together","marks":[["𝔢","emotion mind"],["φ","feel: the emotion made from a thought and the body"],["f","the facts of a situation"],["𝐅","facts"],["𝐄","emotions"],["𝔯","reasonable mind"],["ρ","reason’s map to action"],["η","emotion’s map to action"],["≺","comes before; ranks below"],["🫁","breath"]],"practice":["ways in :   🫁 settles to the centre  ;   a flake of stone sinking in a lake  ;   steps down an inner spiral stair\nΘ ↦ the pause between breaths  ;   «wise» on the in-breath ,  «mind» on the out-breath\nask(q) in ,  listen out  ;   ask «is this 𝔴?»","r(τ) ≔ how centred in 𝔴 :   0  could not do it   …   5  centred","r(τ) ≠ calm(τ)   it rates getting into 𝔴 ,  not feeling better","eyes open :   learned with eyes shut  ⇏  there with eyes open","where :   the belly ,  the centre of the head ,  or between the eyes","widen :   Θ = centre  ↦  centre ∪ the room around ,   centre kept   focused ,  yet aware of where things are","breath focus is offered ,  never required ,  when it cannot be borne"],"u":"Why can reason not beat emotion mind? Why is a strong emotion not the same as emotion mind?","tree":{"t":"Sarah has to choose between a safe course and the one she loves. What does she do?","o":[["She lists the facts (entry grades, jobs) and her values side by side, and picks what both support.",["M3","DT5"],3,"Reasonable mind is ruled by facts, and values do not count in it. Emotion mind is ruled by mood, and facts do not count in it. Reasonable mind alone would pick the safe course, and emotion mind alone the loved one. Neither can beat the other, so the two have to be brought together. Two columns on one page do that (DT5). Wise mind is the pair read together, and that is a synthesis of the two (M9). It is not a compromise. Doing the weighing is the harder and better work."],["She steps back until the choice feels clear and without inner conflict.",["M3"],1,"Wise mind knows in five ways at once: by watching, by logic, by the body, by doing, and by intuition. She has been using one of them and getting louder. Finding wise mind is like tuning a radio, static first and then a clearer signal. The mark of it is that the act comes without inner conflict, even when it is hard. Everyone has wise mind, and she is doing the best she can to hear hers."],["She accepts that either choice costs something, and chooses.",["M3","DT13"],4,"Part of what keeps her stuck is the hope of a choice that costs nothing. There isn't one. Accepting that (DT13) ends the search for it. Wise mind can act before every doubt is gone. Wanting it to be otherwise and accepting it as it is can both be held (M9). She did not set up the dilemma, and she is the one who has to settle it."],["She lets the deadline pass without choosing.",["M3","G4"],6,"Not choosing makes sense when both pull hard, since any choice feels like a loss. This is the helpless end of the second line, waiting for the problem to be solved from outside (G10). And it is solved from outside, by the calendar. Not choosing was one of the four options, and it chose for her."]],"e":[["She fills both columns. Every fact points to the safe course and every value to the loved one, and her father says, \"well, the facts are the facts\".",[["She asks wise mind: which choice brings a sense of peace?",["M3"],"Her father has spoken for reasonable mind, where values do not count. When the columns split, adding more rows does not help. She asks the question that belongs to wise mind and listens with the body and intuition as well as with logic. A passing feeling can pass for depth, like rain water on a trap door passing for the ocean. So she asks again tomorrow and checks whether the answer is the same."],["She writes pros and cons for both, long term above short term.",["DT5"],"The two columns compared the courses. Pros and cons compare the consequences of choosing each, in four boxes, with the long term weighed above the short (DT5). \"Safe\" is a short-term word. Ten years on, the picture may look different, and the boxes make her look."],["She talks it through with someone she trusts.",["IE16","IE5"],"Saying it aloud to someone who is not her father lets her hear which course her own voice lifts for. She asks for listening and not for a verdict (IE5). Her father's view is valid as his view (IE22). The choice is hers, by her own rule (IE14)."]]],["She waits for the choice to feel clear. Two days before the deadline it still doesn't, and a friend says, \"just pick one, it's not that deep\".",[["She chooses with the clearest sense she has, and accepts the doubt.",["M3","DT13"],"The static has thinned without clearing. Wise mind does not need every doubt gone. It needs the clearest signal available by the time the choice is due. She takes that, and accepts that doubt comes along (DT13). No one is in wise mind all the time, and that includes deadline week."],["She asks for a short extension on the deadline.",["IE5"],"The deadline is a rule made by an office, and offices can be asked (IE5). It is a real solution if what she needs is a few more quiet days, and she is honest with herself about whether that is the case. An extension asked for in order to avoid the choice would be the same waiting with a new name."],["She notices the urgency without letting it decide.",["DT4","M4"],"Urgency is a body state: tight chest, fast thoughts. Her friend's \"just pick\" feeds it. She stops and observes it as urgency (DT4, M4). A choice made to end the discomfort of choosing is emotion mind with a calm face. The deadline is real, and the panic is optional."]]],["She chooses. The next morning she wakes with regret, and sees a photo of the other course's welcome day.",[["She checks the facts: is the regret about the course, or about losing the other?",["ER7"],"Regret after a real choice is nearly certain, because something was given up. The check sorts two things apart (ER7): evidence that the course is wrong, and grief for the one not taken. One morning and one photo are the second. Grief for the other course does not show that her choice was wrong."],["She turns the mind again: she chose, and she accepts the cost.",["DT16"],"She accepted the cost when she chose, and here it is, so this is a fork (DT16). Acceptance is chosen again each time the photo, or the thought of it, comes round. One turn does not make the next. The loss still gets felt, as sadness (G10)."],["She asks whether a switch is still possible, without committing to it.",["IE5","ER12"],"Knowing the door's real position is information, and asking costs little (IE5). If a switch is open for a month, the regret has time to be tested against the course itself (ER12). If it is closed, that is a fact to accept. She is better off knowing either way."]]]]}},{"id":"M4","title":"the what skills: observe, describe, participate, one at a time","marks":[["Ω","what is observed"],["N","words"],["∌","does not hold"],["i","interpret"],["⊋","holds everything in, and more than"],["⊊","is inside, and is smaller than","r38m.html","38m · Truth Cannot Be Defined Inside"],["👁","what is observed; sight"],["👂","hearing"],["👃","smell"],["👅","taste"],["✋","hold back; touch"],["🌊","urges"]],"practice":["𝗈 :   one sense at a time :   👁 without following ,  👂 ,  👃 ,  👅 ,  ✋ ,   then 💭 and 🌊 as they pass","𝗉 :   sing ,  dance ,  play ,  run ,  join a talk ;   become the count of the breath ,   become a word said over and over","𝗈 :   threes :  Θ = {🫁} → {🫁, ✋} → {🫁, ✋, 👂} ,   three breaths each ,  restart when one drops","𝖽 :   thoughts sorted into boxes as they pass :  planning ,  worry ,  the past ,  …","𝗉 :   held by the chair ,  the floor ,  the walls :  the kindness of ordinary things","r(τ) ≔ focus ,   wise mind ,   before and after"],"u":"Why is \"a sound\" a description and \"a bird\" not yet one? Why can no one describe another's intent?","tree":{"t":"Terry's lab partner rolls her eyes at his result. What does he do?","o":[["He describes only what he saw (\"you rolled your eyes\") and asks what she thinks of the result.",["M4","IE17"],7,"Describing puts words on what was observed, and only on that. No one senses another person's thought, intent or feeling. So \"you rolled your eyes\" is a description, and \"you think I'm useless\" is not. The form for saying it is \"when you do X, I feel Y, and I think Z\". Asking is the only way to learn what he can't observe (IE17), and it finds the cause, which works better than blaming her for a cause he guessed."],["He separates \"she rolled her eyes\" from \"she thinks I'm stupid\", and the hurt shrinks.",["M4"],6,"A sound is a sound, not yet a bird. An eye-roll is an eye-roll, not yet contempt. The hurt came from the second sentence, and the second sentence was his own. Checked against the facts (ER7), the eye-roll fits a dozen causes: the result, the equipment, her own morning. When he drops what he added, the emotion that rested on it gets smaller (ER5)."],["He notices the sting and keeps working on the lab.",["M4","ER22"],1,"He observes inside as well as outside: there is a sting in his chest. Observing means noticing it without pushing it away and without holding on to it. The sting is a small wave (ER22). He does one thing at a time, and the thing is the lab. He does not have to settle what she meant before he takes the next reading."],["He decides she despises him and stops talking to her.",["M4","G4"],6,"He treats a thought he never observed as a fact, and then acts on it. It makes sense if he comes from a world where a look did mean contempt (G11). But silence now changes how she acts toward him, and that will seem to prove him right. That is the transaction: each side moves the other (G7). The lab needs two people who talk."]],"e":[["He describes what he saw and asks. She shrugs, says \"nothing\", and looks at her phone.",[["He asks once more, plainly, and lets it be if she still says nothing.",["IE5","IE13"],"One more ask at the same height (IE13), with no edge on it: \"I'd like to know if the result looks off to you\" (IE5). Then he stops. He weighs how hard to push (IE11), and a shrug from a lab partner does not call for a third ask. He can accept that she won't say."],["He describes only her shrug, and does not fill in the rest.",["M4","IE17"],"Now he has two observations, an eye-roll and a shrug, and still no thought of hers (IE17). The pull to fill the gap is stronger, because \"nothing\" sounds like something. He holds to what can be sensed (M4). A gap he leaves empty hurts less than one he fills with the worst guess."],["He goes on with the lab and checks the result with the demonstrator.",["ER12"],"Under the eye-roll there may be a real question: is the result wrong? That question can be solved without her (ER12). The demonstrator can look at the numbers. He sorts the interpersonal problem from the lab problem and solves the one that has an answer today."]]],["He keeps the facts separate. At his next result she rolls her eyes again, and this time the pair at the next bench see it and smirk.",[["He keeps the facts separate, again.",["M4","DT16"],"Twice is still an observation, made twice. The smirk is a third. None of the three is a thought he can see (M4). The story has more to feed on now, and telling facts from story is a turn he makes again (DT16). It is harder the second time, and that is why the skill gets practised."],["He says how it lands: \"when you roll your eyes, I'm not sure what you mean\".",["IE6","IE5"],"The problem is no longer the result. It is what is going on between them, so he describes that and asks for one change (IE6). The sentence has the taught form: when you do X, I think Z (M4). It accuses her of nothing, and that leaves her room to answer."],["He lets the sting rise and fall.",["ER22"],"With an audience, the sting is embarrassment. It rises faster and it falls the same way (ER22). Its urge is to say something sharp or to go quiet for the week. He does neither, and takes the next reading while it passes."]]],["He keeps working. Then the demonstrator checks the result, and it is wrong. His partner says, \"I knew it\".",[["He finds where it went wrong and reruns the step.",["ER12","G13"],"A wrong result is a done thing, and it has a chain (G13): which step, which reading, what he was attending to at the time. The key link is the step that, redone, gives a different number. He reruns from there (ER12). Finding the cause works better than blaming, and that includes blaming himself."],["He drops \"she was right, I'm hopeless\".",["M5","IE21"],"\"She was right\" may be a fact about one result. \"I'm hopeless\" is a rating of a whole person, drawn from one number (IE21). He keeps the judgment that tells things apart, that this result is wrong, and drops the one that rates him bad (M5)."],["He accepts the mistake and tells his partner what he changed.",["DT13","IE10"],"The result was wrong, and he accepts that with no \"but\" (DT13). He tells her the fix, truthfully and without grovelling (IE10). Her \"I knew it\" also tells him that the eye-roll may have been about the number all along. One of the dozen causes turned out to be the real one, and it was not contempt."]]]]}},{"id":"M5","title":"the how skills: not judging, one thing, what works, all at once","marks":[["J_disc","a judgment that tells things apart"],["≟","is it the same as?"],["a⋆","the act that works"],["g","how far a consequence meets a goal"],["max","the greatest of","r08b.html#0ef1ef","8b · Any Two Reals Compare"],["Wish_","Wish_☉ φ: ☉ wishes that φ"],["≻","ranks above"]],"practice":["𝗇 :   say «a judging thought arose»  →  count them  →  replace each with a fact\n𝗈( judging face ,  posture ,  voice )  →  change them","n_J(☼)  ↓  over the days   the count is the measure","n_J(d)  reset each day ;   the steps taken easiest first ,  one a week","judging word or tone  ⇒  start the sentence over","𝟣 :   one word ,  «one» ,  on each breath ;   𝗈( wanting to move )  ∧  ¬move ;   go a little past wanting to stop","𝖾 :   angry ,  wilful  or  wanting to be right   ⇒   ask «does this work?» ;   willing hands"],"u":"Not judging is not approving. What do you say in place of \"bad\", and why does that keep what follows?","tree":{"t":"Claire calls her own essay \"rubbish\". What does she do?","o":[["She lists what is weak (the argument in section 2, no counterexample) and fixes those.",["M5"],3,"The rule is to keep the judgments that tell things apart and drop the ones that rate them good or bad. \"Rubbish\" rates the essay. \"Section 2 has no counterexample\" tells one part from another, and it can be worked on. She says what is, and what follows from it: the argument does not hold yet. Then she does one thing at a time. Trying harder needs a target, and the rating gave her none."],["She swaps \"rubbish\" for \"it is a first draft, and I am disappointed\".",["M5"],7,"The rule is not to swap \"bad\" for \"good\". Calling the essay brilliant would be a second judgment and no truer than the first. She says what is, a first draft, and how she feels about it, disappointed. Not judging is not hiding what she wants: she wanted it to be better, and she can say so. The feeling stays and is named (ER6), and the verdict goes. The verdict was the part that hurt."],["She accepts it is not her best, and submits it on time.",["M5","DT13"],1,"Doing what works means acting in the situation she is in, not the one she wishes for. She wishes for another week, and she has until five o'clock. Not judging is not approving, so she can see that the essay falls short and still hand it in. She accepts the gap between this draft and her best (DT13). An essay in on time gets a mark, and it shows her what to work on next."],["She deletes the draft and starts again the night before.",["M5","G4"],6,"\"Rubbish\" was a rating, and now it has turned into an act. It makes sense if she learned that only perfect work is safe to show, which is what perfectionist teaching leaves behind (G11). The judgment costs her the three good paragraphs along with the weak ones. The next draft will be written tired, and it will be judged by the same voice."]],"e":[["She fixes section 2. Reading on, section 3 now looks weak too, then the introduction, and it is midnight.",[["She lists what is weak in section 3 and fixes one thing at a time.",["M5","ER18"],"Fixing one section sharpened her eye, so the next one looks worse than it did. The essay did not get worse. Her eye got better. The method stays the same: describe the weakness, then one thing at a time (M5). Each fix is a hard-but-possible step, and that is how mastery is built (ER18)."],["She asks a friend to read it and say what works.",["IE5","IE16"],"At midnight her eye sees only faults. That is a mood, and it is not a measurement. A second reader gives her a description from outside. She asks for something specific, what works (IE5), because she already has a list of what doesn't."],["She sets a limit: two fixes, then submit.",["M5","DT11"],"Doing what works needs a goal she knows, and the goal is an essay handed in. It is not an essay with no faults (M5). The limit turns an endless task into two steps (DT11). Giving up being right includes giving up being perfect."]]],["She swaps the words. The disappointment stays strong, and her flatmate says, \"you always say that and then get a first\".",[["She lets the disappointment be.",["ER22","DT13"],"Dropping the judgment was never meant to remove the feeling. The disappointment fits, because she wanted more from the draft (ER8). She lets it rise and fall (ER22). Her flatmate means to comfort, and what arrives is \"your feeling is not warranted\" (G11). She can accept the feeling without her flatmate's agreement."],["She does one pleasant thing before going back to it.",["ER16"],"A small pleasant event, done with her whole attention, lowers how much the disappointment weighs (ER16). She is not running from the essay, because it comes with a return. She goes back less raw, and the same draft reads differently."],["She describes the disappointment as a wave, and watches it.",["M4","ER22"],"She steps back to observe and describe: heavy in the chest, a pull to close the laptop (M4). Described, it becomes something she is having. It stops being all of her. She watches the wave move, and it does move (ER22)."]]],["She submits on time. The mark is lower than she hoped, and the feedback begins, \"a disappointing effort from a capable student\".",[["She reads the feedback without judging it, and notes two things to change.",["M5","IE24"],"The first line of the feedback is a rating, and the rest is description. She does to the feedback what she did to the essay: she drops the rating and keeps the lines that tell things apart (M5). \"Disappointing effort\" misses how hard she worked, and she can validate that herself (IE24). Two notes go into the next essay."],["She accepts the mark.",["DT13"],"The mark is recorded and will not change. \"It should have been higher\" is the fight (DT13). Letting go of \"should\" leaves two true sentences: she wanted higher, and this mark had causes (M5). Both can be said without suffering added on top."],["She asks the tutor which change would help most.",["IE5"],"A clear, small ask (IE5). It turns a verdict into a direction, and it shows the tutor a student who is working on it. The tutor's \"capable\" was sincere, and she can take that part as it was meant."]]]]}},{"id":"M6","title":"mindfulness from a spiritual side: boundaries let go","marks":[["∂","the boundary of; in a rate, a partial change"]],"practice":[],"u":"How can freedom be wanting what you lack and still finding a life worth living without it?","tree":{"t":"Mike did not get into his first-choice university. What does he do?","o":[["He looks at transfer routes and second-year entry at his first choice.",["M6","ER12"],4,"Freedom, on this footing, is wanting what you lack and finding a life worth living without it. It does not ask him to stop wanting. The want can drive a plan: he defines the problem, lists the routes, picks one, and works it (ER12). What matters is how he holds it. A plan held lightly is problem solving. A plan he can't live without is clinging, and it would make every day at his real university a day in the wrong place."],["He finds what is worth living for at the university he did get into.",["M6"],2,"A spacious mind has no walls, and everything in it comes and goes, the wish included. He lets the wish be there and looks at what is in front of him: a course, a city, people he has not met. This is accumulating positives where he stands (ER16) and finding which of his values can be lived here (ER17). The feeling changes because his attention is no longer fixed on the one thing that is missing."],["He lets himself be sad about it, and goes to the open day anyway.",["M6","DT13"],1,"He worked for that place and did not get it. The sadness fits (ER8), and a loss wants grieving. Held in, the grief would wait for him, which is one end of the third line (G10). He lets it come and go in a mind with room for it. Then he goes to the open day with it. Accepting the rejection (DT13) does not mean he likes it."],["He refuses to accept the offer and sulks.",["M6","G4"],6,"Sulking makes sense: he worked hard for that place, and turning the other one down feels like loyalty to the dream. This is wilfulness, refusing to take part in what is (DT17). Clinging to the lost place keeps him out of the one he has. The offer has a deadline, and the sulk doesn't."]],"e":[["He looks at transfer routes. They need a first-year average well above what he has ever managed, and a cousin says, \"so that's that, then\".",[["He makes a plan to raise his average this year.",["ER12","ER17"],"The route is narrow, and it has a number on it. A number can be planned toward (ER12). He brings it down to a step he can take this week (ER17). The plan is worth having even if the transfer fails, because a higher average serves him wherever he ends up."],["He holds the wish lightly while he works where he is.",["M6"],"\"That's that\" and \"I must\" are the two ends. In the middle he works toward it and lets go of needing it (M8). The wish stays as something he would like, in a mind that has room for other things. If the average comes, good. If it doesn't, he has spent a year living his life and has not spent it waiting."],["He asks the admissions office what else counts.",["IE5"],"His cousin read the requirement as a wall. An office can say whether it is one (IE5): what else is weighed, and how many transfer each year. He asks about the facts before he accepts a closed door or throws himself at it."]]],["He finds things worth living for. Then a school friend posts photos from the first-choice campus, captioned \"living the dream\".",[["He notices the envy and lets it pass.",["ER6","ER22"],"He names it exactly: envy, because someone else has what he wanted (ER6). It fits the facts, so there is nothing to be ashamed of (ER8). It is also a wave (ER22). Named and watched, it passes without becoming a story about his whole life."],["He mutes the posts for a while.",["DT24"],"Each photo is a cue that restarts the wanting. Removing the cue for a while is a small burned bridge (DT24). He is not hiding from reality, since he knows where his friend is. He is choosing not to be shown it ten times a day while the new life is still young."],["He sends his friend a kind message.",["M7","IE7"],"Envy's urge is to pull away or to run the other person down. A sincere good wish is the opposite act (ER10), and it is loving kindness one step outward (M7). It keeps the friendship (IE7). The feeling often follows the act."]]],["He goes to the open day. It rains, the halls are shabby, and the guide says, \"most people here had us as their backup\".",[["He describes what he sees without judging: \"old building, wet day\".",["M4","M5"],"The sadness is offering him a verdict: this is where failures go. He describes what can be sensed, a building and the weather (M4), and leaves the rating off (M5). Even the guide's sentence is a description: many people came here second, and they are here."],["He finds one thing he liked: the library, the people.",["M6","DT11"],"One thing in the moment (DT11). A life worth living without what he lacks is built from particular things, and it starts with one (M6). The library is warm and open late. He does not have to love the place today. He only has to find one thing that is true and good about it."],["He lets the sadness come back, and stays for the afternoon.",["ER22","DT13"],"The rain and the remark bring the loss back. It is a wave, and it does not mean he was wrong to come (ER22). Its urge is to leave early and decide the place is hopeless. He stays, sad, and sees the rest. He is accepting the afternoon as it is (DT13)."]]]]}},{"id":"M7","title":"loving kindness: wishing well, outward by steps","marks":[],"practice":["O ⊆ { warmth ,  love ,  compassion ,  connection ,  wisdom ,  🙂 ,  ✓_☉(☉) }   toward h ,   even a little","palms open ,  breathe slowly ;   after each round ,  let go of tension ;   drift  ⇒  Wish_☉(happy(☉)) ,  then return ;   each day"],"u":"Why does loving kindness move outward by steps, and why choose only someone you want to feel kindly toward?","tree":{"t":"Zoe resents a classmate who got the part she wanted in the school play. What does she do?","o":[["She congratulates the classmate, and asks to understudy.",["M7","IE7"],2,"Loving kindness is a wish: may this one be happy, at peace, healthy and safe. A wish can be acted before it is felt. Congratulating is the wish said aloud, with GIVE to carry it (IE7). Asking to understudy solves her own problem, which is being out of the play. The two acts work together, because the first makes the second easy to grant. She still wants the part, and this is how she stays near it."],["She works out from herself to the classmate, a few sincere words at each step.",["M7"],5,"The practice has an order. It begins with herself or someone she already loves, and moves out through friends to those she is angry with. Starting with the classmate would be too far a reach, and the words would ring false. She goes by steps, four or five words she means, and begins again when she loses them. The resentment fits in kind, since she lost something she wanted, and it does not help her. So this is opposite action (ER10), practised for this one person."],["She lets the resentment be there without feeding it.",["M7","ER22"],1,"The practice says to choose only someone she wants to feel kindly toward. Today she is not there yet. The middle between forcing kindness and feeding the grudge is to let the resentment be what it is (ER22). She does not replay the casting list, and that keeps it from growing (ER3). She does not pretend it away either. She is doing the best she can until the wish becomes possible."],["She keeps the grudge all term.",["M7","G4"],6,"The grudge makes sense: she wanted the part, and the grudge feels like loyalty to that. But a grudge is rumination with a face on it, and it keeps the anger firing at every rehearsal (ER3). The classmate does not feel it. Zoe does, three afternoons a week."]],"e":[["She congratulates the classmate and gets the understudy role. At the first rehearsal the classmate says, in front of everyone, \"don't worry, I never get ill\".",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt and keeps to her part as understudy.",["IE17","M7"],"She can observe the sentence. She can't observe whether it was a joke, nerves, or a jab (IE17). The kind wish does not depend on which. She chose to wish this person well, and a clumsy remark is the first test of whether the wish holds (M7)."],["She lets the resentment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"The remark was public, so the resentment comes with embarrassment on top of it. Two waves rise together, and both fall (ER22). Neither one needs a reply in front of the cast."],["She focuses on learning the lines.",["ER18","M5"],"The lines are what she controls. Learning them is mastery, one scene at a time (ER18), done with her whole attention (M5). An understudy who knows the part is ready whatever the classmate's health does. It also gives the resentment less of her mind to live in."]]],["She works outward with kind words. When she gets to the classmate, \"may she be happy\" feels fake, and she thinks, \"I'm a hypocrite\".",[["She keeps going, starting closer to home.",["M7"],"The practice expects this. When the words go hollow she returns to a step where they are sincere, herself or a friend, and comes out again more slowly. The wish is built by repetition, and nobody finds it ready-made. Being three steps short of the classmate is a normal place to be in the practice."],["She acts as if she meant them, and lets the feeling follow.",["ER10"],"Opposite action works on acts, body, words and thoughts, all the way, and the emotion changes afterwards (ER10). Saying the words before the feeling arrives is how the method works, so it is not hypocrisy. A hypocrite hides the gap from other people. She knows the gap is there and is working to close it."],["She notices the judgment \"fake\" and drops it.",["M5"],"\"Fake\" and \"hypocrite\" are ratings. What is actually so: she said the words and did not feel them yet. When she judges, she does not judge the judging (M5). She notes that a judgment came and goes back to the words."]]],["She lets the resentment be. Then the classmate asks her, of all people, to help run lines after school.",[["She says yes for one session, with GIVE.",["IE7","IE4"],"She weighs the three aims (IE4): she wants little from this, the tie matters for a term of shared rehearsals, and her self-respect is safe either way. One session is a size of yes she can mean. GIVE makes it gentle and interested (IE7). It may also be the first step where the kind wish becomes sincere."],["She says no kindly, high on the ladder.",["IE13","IE10"],"A no is as valid as a yes. She can say it firmly and stay warm (IE13), with no invented excuse, because FAST is truthful (IE10). \"Not this week\" is true. It protects the wish, too, because a yes she resents would feed the grudge."],["She makes pros and cons before she answers.",["DT5"],"Two urges arrive together: to refuse out of spite, and to agree so as to look gracious. Neither is wise mind. Four boxes written before she replies (DT5) show what each answer costs and gives over the whole term."]]]]}},{"id":"M8","title":"doing mind and being mind, balanced in wise mind","marks":[["𝔡","doing mind"],["𝔟","being mind"],["λ","a mixing weight between 0 and 1"],["🫀","body sensations"],["❤","emotions"]],"practice":["reminders :   a written line ,  a set alarm ,  a note left where you will see it","read a line that makes you want to practise ;   «just this one moment ,  just this one task» ≔  Θ = one dish","resting :   stay aware of what needs doing ;   asked to do it :  do it ,  willing ,  whole","∀ ☼ :   one everyday act done one moment at a time","pleasant event :   aware of 🙂 while it happened ,  true or false   🫀 ,  ❤ ,  💭  during","unpleasant event :   aware of it while it happened ,  true or false   🫀 ,  ❤ ,  💭  during"],"u":"What does each mind get wrong when there is too much of it? Why does wise mind balance them instead of choosing one?","tree":{"t":"Jean is so driven by her grade average that she stops noticing her own day. What does she do?","o":[["She keeps her study plan and adds one hour a day with no goal in it.",["M8"],5,"Doing mind takes thoughts as facts and drives at goals. Being mind takes thoughts as sensations and meets each moment. She has too much of the first: it runs on autopilot, and it misses the moment. The fix does not take anything away from doing. It adds some being, in a measured amount: one hour, daily, in the plan like any other task (ER16). Balance has to be practised in the week she really has."],["She lets go of needing to reach the average, while still working toward it.",["M8"],2,"Work toward a goal, and let go of needing to reach it. These sound opposed, and they are held together, which makes it a dialectic (IE19). The work stays the same. What changes is the grip. The fear under the drive, that she is nothing without the number, comes from taking a thought as a fact. With a looser grip she can see her day again, and it does not make her lazy."],["She accepts that some weeks will lean to doing, and notices one moment each day.",["M8","DT13"],1,"Wise mind balances the two over time. It does not balance them in every hour. An exam week leans toward doing, and she accepts that (DT13). One moment noticed each day, tea or the walk to the library, keeps being mind alive at the smallest cost. \"Even a little\" counts (G2)."],["She keeps grinding and stops noticing anything else.",["M8","G4"],6,"The grind makes sense if good marks were the main thing her world answered warmly. A setting like that teaches that she is her achievements (G11). Too little being means missing her own life. The average may rise. She will not have been there for the term it took."]],"e":[["She adds the free hour. She spends the whole of it thinking about what she should be revising, and a friend texts, \"wish I had time to do nothing\".",[["She brings her attention to one sense at a time: taste, sound, touch.",["M8","DT10"],"Being mind meets the moment through the senses. Her doing mind has no senses in it, only plans. She gives attention a place to rest: the taste of the tea, then the sounds in the room (DT10). She has gone from too little being to a little, and that is the direction she wanted."],["She lets the worry come and go, and stays in the hour.",["M2","DT19"],"\"I should be revising\" is a thought, and it will come twenty times in the hour (DT19). She does not have to obey it or silence it. Each time, she notices it and comes back (M2). The hour has not failed because the worry visited."],["She plans the hour as a walk, so it has something in it.",["ER16"],"An empty hour is hard for a mind trained on goals. A pleasant event with a shape to it, a walk on a route she likes, gives the hour a body without giving it a target (ER16). She puts her attention on the walk and not on whether she is relaxing correctly."]]],["She lets go of needing the average. Her next mark drops four points, and her mother asks, \"what happened? You used to be so driven\".",[["She checks the facts: one mark, still within range.",["ER7"],"The fear says that letting go caused the drop and the slide has begun. The facts (ER7): one mark, four points, inside her usual spread. Her mother's question treats being driven as her worth, and that is a view she can decline to take on (G11)."],["She keeps working toward the average, without needing it.",["M8","M3"],"The test of the skill is this moment. Needing would mean a panic, and then the grind again. Not caring would mean a shrug. Wise mind does neither (M3). She looks at what the mark asks for, does that work, and leaves her worth out of it."],["She accepts the dip, and keeps the plan.",["DT13","G15"],"One mark is one trial, and one trial does not show that a skill fails (G15). She accepts the mark as it is (DT13) and writes it into the record with the rest of the term. The plan gets judged on the term."]]],["She notices one moment each day. Then deadlines come, and a week later she realizes she has not noticed anything since Monday.",[["She sets a cue: one moment at each meal.",["G14"],"She runs the four questions. She knew what to do, yes. She was willing, yes. Did it come to mind? No. That is the first no, and its fix is a cue (G14). Meals happen every day whatever the deadlines, so the moment is tied to them."],["She starts again without judging the week.",["M5","DT16"],"The drift is noticed, and the noticing is already the return. Judging the lost week would be doing mind grading her being mind (M5). She begins again today, and she may need to begin again many times (DT16)."],["She asks a friend to share one moment a day with her.",["IE16","IE5"],"A practice kept with another person is harder to drop. She asks plainly (IE5): one message a day, one thing each of them noticed. It also adds a little connection, and the grind had been squeezing that out too (IE16)."]]]]}},{"id":"M9","title":"the middle path: a synthesis of opposites","marks":[["x̄","the opposite pole of x"],["½","one half","r05f.html#3281a5","5f · Between Any Two, Another; Yet Holes"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is the middle path not half of each side, nor the point halfway between?","tree":{"t":"Evita wants to be top of the year and wants to enjoy university. What does she do?","o":[["She sets a study schedule that leaves two evenings a week free, and keeps both.",["M9"],3,"The middle path is not half of each, and it is not the point halfway between. Half-studying and half-enjoying would lose both. It is a synthesis that keeps each side whole: full work in the work hours, full evenings in the free ones. Do what is needed, and meet each moment fully. Keeping the schedule is the harder part, and that is where the trying goes."],["She drops \"either top or happy\" for \"both, in my own balance\".",["M9","IE20"],7,"\"Either–or\" becomes \"both–and\". The either–or was a claim that the two can never go together, and it was stronger than the facts (IE21). Balance is not always half and half. It is her own centre, and it need not be anyone else's. Once the two wants stop being enemies, the guilt she felt in each one for neglecting the other has no cause left."],["She accepts that some weeks will tip one way, and comes back to balance.",["M9"],1,"The middle path can go to an extreme and come back. A week of deadlines tips toward work, and a week with a festival tips the other way. Neither week is a failure of balance. Wanting it to be otherwise and accepting it as it is are themselves one of the pairs. She tolerates the tipped weeks, and the coming back is what makes it a path."],["She gives up enjoying anything until graduation.",["M9","G4"],6,"It makes sense: one goal is simpler than two, and putting off joy looks like discipline. But it is a jump to one end, and a person at one end of a line tends to swing to the other (G10). Three years with no pleasant events also raises vulnerability (ER14), and that is poor ground for coming top."]],"e":[["She keeps two evenings free. Then a big assignment is set, due the morning after one of them, and her study group plans to work that night.",[["She swaps the free evening to another day that week.",["M9","ER12"],"The synthesis was two free evenings. It was never Tuesday in particular. She solves the clash and keeps both sides whole (ER12). Rigid balance is another extreme. The path bends with the week and does not break (M9)."],["She asks for a short extension.",["IE5"],"Whether to ask depends on the checks (IE11): is it within the tutor's power, and is her reason one she would stand behind? If it is, a clear ask (IE5) is a fair way to keep both wants. If the honest answer is no, she has learned that too."],["She works that evening, and plans the swing back.",["M10","DT13"],"This week tips toward work, and she accepts it (DT13). What stops a tip from becoming a slide is naming the return ahead of time: which evening next week comes back to her (M10). An extreme with a planned return is still the middle path."]]],["She says \"both\". Her friends from the top study group call her a try-hard, and her other friends say she's \"always working\".",[["She validates herself: wanting both is fine.",["IE24"],"Each group lives at one end and sees her from there, so she looks extreme to both. Her centre is her own. She says what a friend would say (IE24): wanting to do well and wanting to live well are both sound, and so is holding them together."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"\"Try-hard\" and \"always working\" are other people's thoughts, said aloud (DT19). They contradict each other, and that tells her something about how much weight either carries. She watches them go by."],["She keeps one evening with those friends, as planned.",["M9","IE7"],"The plan already holds the answer. She turns up for the evening, fully there, with an easy manner (IE7). She does not argue the case for balance. She lives it where they can see it."]]],["She comes back to balance through the year. Then finals tip her far to one side for a whole month, and she feels she has lost it for good.",[["She accepts the month, and plans one small rest each day.",["M9","ER16"],"The middle path can go to an extreme and come back. A month of finals is that kind of extreme, and it has an end date. She accepts it and keeps a thread of the other side: one small pleasant thing a day (ER16). \"Lost for good\" is a claim about forever made from one month (IE21)."],["She checks her body: sleep and food.",["ER19"],"The feeling of having lost it may be tiredness. The body's basics are the first thing an extreme month wears down, and seeing to them lowers her vulnerability to every other feeling (ER19). She is not being indulgent. A top mark needs a working body."],["She plans the swing back for after finals.",["M10","ER18"],"A balanced life swings and comes back, and planning the swing keeps the pairs held (M10). She copes ahead (ER18): she pictures the first week after finals and what she will do in it. The return is in the diary before the month begins."]]]]}},{"id":"M10","title":"a balanced life: thirteen pairs held at once","marks":[["🖐","actions"],["≈","is about","r05.html","5 · Making Room to Undo"]],"practice":["∀ j :   ☉ marks a point x_☉ on the line  xⱼ ⟷ x̄ⱼ   where ☉ stands most of the time","x_☉ near an end   ⇒   that end is the one to work on","pull to xⱼ ,  pull to x̄ⱼ ,  and how the tension was held   a calendar of it","pick one pair :   too much ,  too little  ↦  do less ,  do more ;   check the facts and your own values ;   one or two exact steps next week"],"u":"Why does a balanced life hold all thirteen pairs at once, and not settle each one in turn?","tree":{"t":"A student cramming for finals has tipped toward the extremes. What does she do?","o":[["She picks two pairs, work and rest and need and want, and plans one move toward the middle in each.",["M10"],3,"A balanced life holds thirteen pairs at once, and thinking, feeling and acting all tend to the extremes. Thirteen can't be mended in finals week. The practice is to pick a pair, mark where she stands on it most of the time, and name whether she has too much or too little. Then she checks the facts and her own values and plans one or two exact steps for the week. Two pairs, one step each: a fixed bedtime, and one thing she wants and does not need. She can keep that up."],["She notices where she has tipped without judging it, and the strain eases.",["M10","M5"],7,"On each line she marks a point: where she stands most of the time. Marking is describing, and it rates nothing (M5). \"Far toward work, far toward self-denial\" is a position, and positions have causes: finals are a cause. Judging the tilt would be one more extreme, harsh where she could be accepting. Once the tilt is seen, it stops being a vague wrongness and becomes two points on two lines."],["She accepts that exam weeks lean one way, and plans the swing back for afterwards.",["M10"],1,"Several of the thirteen pairs are change against acceptance. This is one of them in practice: she accepts the lean and plans the change. Balance is held over time and does not have to hold in every hour. A lean with an end date and a planned return is tolerable (DT13). A lean taken for her character is not, so the planning matters."],["She stays at the extremes after finals too.",["M10","G4"],6,"The extremes had a cause, and the cause has ended. Habits outlast their causes. It makes sense that she carries on, because the cramming was rewarded with marks (IE25). Thinking, feeling and acting all drift to the ends unless she steers them. The lean lasts until she picks a pair and takes a step."]],"e":[["She plans two moves toward the middle. A week later she has made neither, and her flatmate says, \"balance is for people who've finished revising\".",[["She picks just one, and one small step in it.",["M10","ER18"],"Two steps were too many for this week. The practice allows one pair and one exact step (M10). Smaller is better here: mastery wants a step that is hard but possible (ER18), and it has to be possible in finals week. Lights out at midnight, three nights."],["She runs a missing-links check: did she forget, or was she unwilling?",["G14"],"The four questions, in order. Did she know the steps? Yes. Was she willing? She hesitates here. Her flatmate's line sounds true to part of her, so the first no is at the second question (G14). The fix for that one is pros and cons (DT5). A reminder would not have helped."],["She lets the week go, and begins again.",["DT16","M5"],"A week of not doing it is a fact. She does not rate it (M5). Beginning again is a turn made at this fork (DT16). Her flatmate's sentence is an either–or: revise or balance. The middle path does not grant that (M9)."]]],["She marks where she has tipped, and it is not two pairs but four. Looking at the page, she thinks, \"my whole life is off\".",[["She chooses one pair to start with, and leaves the rest.",["M10","DT11"],"Four marks on a page are information. The practice is one pair at a time, with one or two steps (M10). One thing in the moment (DT11). The other three will still be there next week, and they are easier to face from a place where one has moved."],["She does not judge the count.",["M5"],"\"My whole life is off\" rates everything on the strength of four points on four lines (M5). Four tipped pairs of thirteen is a count, and nine are in range. Degree is a number of conditions (G2), and hers can rise one at a time."],["She talks it through with someone who knows her.",["IE16","IE5"],"Balance is her own centre, and someone who knows her can help her see where that is. She asks for that plainly (IE5). Asking for help is itself on one of the lines, self against others, and it is likely one of the four she has tipped on."]]],["She plans the swing back. Finals end, and in place of relief she feels flat and empty, and her friends are all celebrating.",[["She plans one pleasant thing a day for a week.",["ER16"],"A month at one extreme has run her stock of pleasant events down to nothing, and flatness is what that feels like. The swing back does not happen by waiting for a mood. Small pleasant events, planned and done, add up (ER16). She does one a day whether or not she feels like it."],["She lets the flatness be, and checks her body.",["ER22","ER19"],"Flatness after long strain is a state that passes, and it is not a verdict on her life (ER22). She does not have to match her friends' mood tonight. She checks the basics, sleep, food and movement (ER19), because finals took those too."],["She picks one value to move toward next term.",["ER17"],"The emptiness has a cause: for a month, every hour had a purpose, and now none does. A value, brought down to one step she can take now, gives direction without a deadline (ER17). She has gone from the goal ruling everything to no goal at all, and one chosen step is the middle of that line."]]]]}}]},{"id":"IE","page":"dbtie","name":"interpersonal effectiveness","footings":27,"steps":[{"id":"IE1","title":"goals: get what you want, keep the tie, keep your respect, walk the middle","marks":[["γ","the objective"],["Q","self-respect"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why use the skills early? What does the tear that is left alone show about mending?","tree":{"t":"Zoe has let a tutor's dismissive comments pile up all term. What does she do?","o":[["She asks for a short meeting and raises one comment, calmly, before the next essay is due.",["IE1","IE5"],4,"The skills are to be used early, so that hurts do not pile up. Hers have piled up for a term, and a tear left alone gets harder to mend. So she does not bring the whole pile. She brings one comment, described as a fact, with one ask (IE5). Always putting the relationship first is how unmet needs build until a person blows up or leaves (IE4), and one calm meeting is the alternative to both. She did not cause the tutor's manner, and she is the one who can raise it."],["She sorts which comments were fair feedback and which were not, and lets the fair ones help.",["IE1","IE24"],7,"A term of comments has become one lump: \"he thinks I can't write\". Sorting breaks the lump. Some comments were curt and correct, and she can use those. Some were dismissive and wrong, and those she answers by validating herself (IE24). Validation goes only to what is valid (IE22), and that cuts both ways here. The sting eases because she is no longer carrying all of it as true or all of it as an insult."],["She accepts that the tutor is curt, and keeps her focus on the work.",["IE1","DT13"],1,"Part of the goal is keeping acceptance and change in balance. She can't rewrite the tutor's manner this term, and she accepts that he is curt (DT13). Accepting his manner is different from agreeing with what he says about her. She is not disowning the hurt. She has weighed the three aims (IE4) and decided the work matters most just now."],["She keeps quiet and dreads every class.",["IE1","G4"],6,"Keeping quiet makes sense if speaking up was punished where she grew up (G11). But every unanswered comment adds to the pile, and the dread grows with it. This is the road to one of two ends: an outburst in week ten, or dropping the module. Both are the swing the skills are meant to head off (G10)."]],"e":[["She raises one comment, calmly. The tutor leans back and says, \"I think you're being a little oversensitive\".",[["She stays on her one point, calmly, and asks for one change.",["IE1","IE5"],"\"Oversensitive\" moves the subject from his comment to her character. If she answers the attack, she hands over control of the meeting (IE5). She goes back to the point like a broken record: the comment, and one change she would like. She can come back to the word later if she still wants to."],["She validates herself: raising it was fair, whatever the reply.",["IE24"],"This is invalidation in its plainest form: the feeling is called too big for its cause (G11). She does the work the tutor did not. She checks the facts, finds her point was fair, and says so to herself as she would to a friend (IE24). How she feels about herself afterwards does not depend on getting what she asked for (IE4)."],["She lets it be for today, and writes down what was said.",["DT13","G15"],"Pushing on today would cost the relationship more than it would gain. She accepts that this meeting has gone as far as it can (DT13). She writes down what each of them said, with the date. That is a record (G15), and it will matter if she later takes it to someone who can decide (IE14)."]]],["She sorts the comments. More of them turn out fair than she expected, and she thinks, \"so he was right about me all along\".",[["She picks the one fair comment that would help most, and works on that.",["ER18","M5"],"A list of fair criticisms is a list of things to learn. It is not a verdict on her. One thing at a time (M5), and she starts with the one that pays most. Mastery is built by a hard-but-possible step (ER18), and \"fix my writing\" is not a step. \"Put the argument in the first paragraph\" is one."],["She keeps sorting, all the way through.",["IE1","IE24"],"She has stopped halfway, at the pile that hurts. \"Right about me all along\" claims every case (IE21), and the other pile exists: comments that were dismissive and wrong. She finishes sorting, and validates herself on those (IE24). The fair pile and the unfair pile are both real."],["She does something soothing, then comes back to the list.",["DT10"],"Reading fair criticism while raw turns every line into shame. A short soothing break, with a set return (DT10), brings her below the point where she can only react (ER23). The list will say the same things afterwards, and she will read them as information."]]],["She keeps her focus on the work. Then the tutor reads a sentence of hers aloud to the class as an example of \"what not to do\".",[["She asks for a word after class about how feedback is given.",["IE5","IE6"],"Acceptance was for his manner. A public example is a new fact, and it is past what she chose to accept. The problem is now how feedback happens between them, so she describes that and asks for one change: comments on her work in writing or in private (IE6). She gives no motives and no \"you should\"."],["She lets the embarrassment rise and fall, and stays in the seat.",["ER22"],"Embarrassment in front of a room peaks fast, and its urge is to walk out or go blank. It is a wave, and it falls if she does not feed it (ER22). She stays in her seat, and that keeps her options open for the conversation afterwards."],["She talks it over with a classmate she trusts.",["IE16","IE22"],"A term of comments taken alone is how the pile got built. Telling one person breaks the rule of coping alone (G11). The classmate can also tell her how it looked from outside, which is validation from a second observer (IE22). She may find she was not the only one to wince."]]]]}},{"id":"IE2","title":"what gets in the way","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is having a skill not the same as wanting to use it? How do two of the obstacles feed one another?","tree":{"t":"Mike never asks for help in office hours. What does he do?","o":[["He names the first obstacle (he doesn't know what to ask) and writes down two questions before he goes.",["IE2","IE15"],5,"Six things can get in the way: no skill, not knowing what he wants, emotions, short-term wants, worries and beliefs, and other people. He checks them in order and fixes the first that fails (IE15). His is the second: he does not know what he wants from the hour. Two written questions fix exactly that. The obstacles feed one another, so clearing one often loosens the next, and with questions in hand the worry has less to work with."],["He sees that worry and lack of skill feed each other, and stops blaming himself.",["IE2"],7,"He has been reading his silence as cowardice. The list says otherwise. He has never practised asking, so he worries. He worries, so he does not practise. That is a loop with two causes (G8), and neither is a fault in him. A skill is an ability, plus watching what it does, plus changing it to fit, and nobody has any of that before the first try. Finding the loop works better than judging himself for being in it."],["He goes once, feeling awkward, and lets the awkwardness be.",["IE2","ER22"],1,"The third obstacle is emotion, and his is plain awkwardness. He does not wait for it to clear. It is a wave, and he can carry it into the room (ER22). Going while awkward is also opposite action in a mild form (ER10). A skill used often enough runs on its own, and the first use is always the stiffest."],["He keeps working alone and falls behind.",["IE2","G4"],6,"Staying away relieves the worry today, and that relief is a short-term want beating a long-term goal, the fourth obstacle. It makes sense if he was taught to cope alone (G11), and he looks fine from outside (G10). The obstacles feed one another: falling behind makes the questions harder to ask."]],"e":[["He goes with his two questions. There are six people in the queue, all of whom seem to know the lecturer, and he feels his nerve go.",[["He stays in the queue and reads his questions again.",["ER10","IE2"],"The urge is to leave, and the fear behind it does not fit, since a queue is no threat (ER8). So he does the opposite and stays (ER10). Reading the questions again keeps the second obstacle cleared: he still knows what he wants."],["He emails the questions instead.",["IE5","ER12"],"The goal was answers, and the queue is one route to them. An email is another solution to the same problem (ER12), and a written ask can be clear and complete (IE5). He is honest with himself that he chose it and did not flee into it. He plans to go in person the next time."],["He breathes out longer than in while he waits.",["DT6"],"The obstacle is now emotion, and it is in his body. Paced breathing works on the body directly (DT6), and nobody in the queue can tell. By the time he reaches the front his heart has slowed, and the questions are still on the paper."]]],["He stops blaming himself. A week later he still hasn't gone, and a classmate says, \"just go, it's not hard\".",[["He walks the obstacles in order: skill, worry, emotion, wants, setting.",["IE15","IE2"],"\"Not hard\" is true for the classmate, whose obstacles are cleared. He checks his own in order and stops at the first that fails (IE15). He has the skill now, and he knows what he wants. The worry \"I'll look stupid\" is the one that fails. That is a belief, and a belief can be checked against one real case (IE3)."],["He makes pros and cons of going.",["DT5"],"Having a skill is not the same as wanting to use it. Four boxes put the wanting on paper (DT5): what going costs and gives, and what staying away costs and gives, with the long term weighed above this week. The decision is his, made from wise mind."],["He asks a classmate to go with him.",["IE16","IE5"],"Other people are on the list as an obstacle, and they can also be the fix. He asks the same classmate to come along (IE5). Going with someone is a step he can manage. Next time he may go alone."]]],["He goes once. The lecturer answers in two brisk sentences, glances at the queue, and says \"next\".",[["He gives the benefit of the doubt: the lecturer had a queue.",["IE17"],"He observed two sentences and a glance. \"He thought my question was stupid\" was not observed (IE17). The queue is a cause in plain sight. He holds his guess loosely, because a wrong guess here would cost him every later visit."],["He goes again next week with one question.",["ER10","G5"],"One brisk visit is one trial (G15). The skill only starts to run on its own after many. He commits again (G5), and going back to a place that stung is opposite action done all the way (ER10)."],["He notices the urge to never go back, and lets it pass.",["DT19","DT4"],"\"Never again\" arrives as if it were a decision. It is an urge with a thought attached (DT19). He stops before acting on it (DT4). \"Never\" is also a claim stronger than one brisk answer can support (IE21)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE3","title":"myths: universal claims, broken by one case","marks":[["⊥","the totally false: a claim that cannot hold","r01.html","1 · Making Expressions"]],"practice":[],"u":"A myth claims something always holds. Why is one real case enough to break it, and why is the weakest contradicting claim often enough to answer it?","tree":{"t":"Jean believes \"if I ask my teacher for help, they will think I'm stupid\". What does she do?","o":[["She asks one question this week and checks what actually happens.",["IE3","ER7"],5,"A myth claims that something always holds, and one real case where it fails is enough to break it. So she runs the case. She asks one question and watches what the teacher does. That is checking the facts by experiment (ER7). She can't observe what the teacher thinks (IE17), and she can observe what the teacher does: answers, or sighs, or thanks her. A belief learned in one classroom has to be tested in this one."],["She answers the myth with \"sometimes asking shows I care\".",["IE3"],7,"The way to answer a myth is with a claim that contradicts it and that she can believe, and the weakest such claim is often enough. \"Teachers love questions\" would be too strong, and she would not believe it. \"Sometimes asking shows I care\" needs only one case to be true (IE20). Asking why she holds the myth works better than blaming herself for it: it was true once, somewhere (G11). The fear eases because \"always\" has been taken out of it."],["She accepts that asking feels risky, and asks anyway.",["IE3","ER10"],1,"After checking the facts, the next step is to act opposite to the myth. The myth says hide, and she asks (ER10). She does not have to feel safe first. She accepts the feeling of risk as the cost of testing an old rule (DT13). The feeling changes after the act."],["She keeps the myth and asks no one.",["IE3","G4"],6,"The myth protects her from one risk and can never be tested, because she never runs the case. That is why it lasts. It makes sense as something a mocking classroom once taught her (G11). The cost is every question she does not ask, and the gaps they leave do look, in the end, like the thing she feared."]],"e":[["She asks one question. The teacher frowns, tilts her head, and says, \"sorry, what do you mean?\"",[["She rephrases the question, plainly.",["IE5"],"The teacher asked for a clearer question. That is the whole of what happened. A clear ask is a skill, and first tries are often tangled (IE5). She says it again in simpler words. The myth predicted contempt, and what she got was a request to say more."],["She checks the facts: puzzled is not \"you're stupid\".",["ER7","IE17"],"What she observed: a frown, and \"what do you mean?\". What the myth added: \"she thinks I'm stupid\". The check keeps these apart (ER7). A frown fits many causes, and the likeliest is that the teacher did not follow the wording (IE17)."],["She counts it as one case, and asks again next week.",["IE3","G15"],"One case breaks a myth, and this one did: she asked, and nothing the myth promised happened. She writes it down as a trial (G15). The new behaviour is still weak, so she plans the next trial before the old belief can retell the story."]]],["She answers the myth and asks in class. A boy at the back sniggers, and two others look round.",[["She keeps asking, and lets the snigger pass.",["ER10","DT19"],"The myth was about the teacher, and the teacher is answering her question. The snigger is one person's noise (DT19). Opposite action is done all the way (ER10), and that means finishing the question in a level voice."],["She validates herself: asking is how learning works.",["IE24","IE3"],"The snigger is invalidation from the cheap seats (G11). She answers it herself, with what a friend would say (IE24). Her new claim was \"sometimes asking shows I care\", and the snigger does not contradict it. One person sniggering is one case (IE3)."],["She asks the teacher after class instead this time.",["IE4","IE5"],"She weighs her aims (IE4). The answer is what she wants most, and she owes the back row nothing. Asking after class gets the answer at less cost (IE5). She notes for herself that this is a choice of setting, and that she is still asking."]]],["She asks anyway. The teacher explains quickly, and the answer leaves her more confused than before.",[["She asks one follow-up question.",["IE5","IE13"],"The myth now says: ask twice and they will be sure. She asks again at the same height, plainly (IE13): \"I followed up to the second step, could you say the third again?\" Naming exactly where she got lost is a good ask (IE5), and it shows she was following."],["She notes what she still doesn't get and looks it up.",["ER12"],"The problem is the gap, and there is more than one way to close it (ER12). Writing down exactly what is unclear turns confusion into a question with edges. If the book does not settle it, she has a sharper question for next time."],["She lets the confusion be, and accepts it may take two tries.",["DT13","DT16"],"Confusion after one explanation is ordinary. It is not evidence for the myth. She accepts that learning this will take more than one pass (DT13). Each time \"see, you are stupid\" comes back, she turns the mind again (DT16)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE4","title":"clarifying priorities: weigh three aims before acting","marks":[["ω⃗","the three weights taken together"],["V","values"]],"practice":["use it when :   your rights are not respected  ;   you want something done ,  changed or given  ;   you must say no\nyou want your view taken seriously  ;   there is conflict  ;   you want a relationship better","rank :   γ , γ_R , γ_Q   ↦   1 , 2 , 3 ;   note where the ranks pull against each other"],"u":"Why does how you want to feel after not depend on getting the objective? What goes wrong when the relationship always comes first?","tree":{"t":"Sarah wants her tutor to regrade an essay. What does she do?","o":[["She decides the regrade matters most, keeps it polite for the relationship, and asks with evidence.",["IE4","IE5"],4,"Before acting she weighs three aims: the objective, the relationship, and her self-respect. All three always count, and one loses weight only when it gets in the way of one she values more. The objective has to be a specific result: the essay re-marked against the rubric by Friday. With that decided, the other two aims set the manner, and she asks in a way she will respect afterwards, with DEAR MAN for the ask itself (IE5). She did not set the mark, and she is the one who has to raise it."],["She sees that her self-respect is the real aim, whatever the mark, and the pressure eases.",["IE4","IE10"],7,"Fear and shame can hide what she wants even from herself. Looking under \"I want a regrade\", she finds that what stung was feeling she had not been read fairly. So self-respect is the objective. How she feels about herself afterwards does not depend on getting the mark changed. It depends on how she acts (IE10). Finding the real aim works better than fighting for the stated one, and the pressure goes out of the number."],["She accepts the mark this time, and asks how to do better next time.",["IE4","DT13"],1,"She weighs the aims and finds the relationship heaviest: this tutor marks her for two more terms. The regrade loses weight because it gets in the way of something she values more. She accepts the mark (DT13). This is not always putting the relationship first, which piles up unmet needs. It is one weighing, done on purpose, and she can weigh differently next time."],["She complains to friends and never asks the tutor.",["IE4","G4"],6,"Complaining feels like doing something. But the objective is a result from the tutor, and friends can't give it. It makes sense as a way to be heard without risk. All three aims lose: the mark stays, the tutor may hear of it second-hand, and she knows she did not ask."]],"e":[["She asks with evidence. The tutor says, \"I don't do regrades. If I did it for you I'd have to do it for everyone\".",[["She asks what the formal appeal route is.",["IE5","ER12"],"The tutor has refused as a matter of his own rule, which means the decision may sit with someone else (IE14). She asks one clear question about the route (IE5). The first solution failed, and the problem-solving loop goes back to its list (ER12). Asking is not a threat, and she says it without edge."],["She weighs again: is the mark worth an appeal, or is the relationship worth more now?",["IE4"],"A refusal changes the weights. An appeal costs more of the relationship than a question did. She weighs the three aims again with the new facts. Extreme acts may work today and fail over time, so she looks a term ahead. Whichever way it comes out, she has decided it and not just reacted."],["She thanks the tutor, and keeps her self-respect.",["IE10","IE7"],"She asked fairly, with evidence, and was refused. FAST is already satisfied: she was fair, truthful, and did not grovel (IE10). Thanking him with an easy manner keeps the tie (IE7). She can respect how she handled it, and that was one of the three aims from the start."]]],["She sees self-respect as the aim. The mark still stings at 2 a.m., and it now says, \"you just didn't have the nerve to fight it\".",[["She lets the sting be, and aims for rest.",["ER21","ER22"],"A 2 a.m. thought is not to be solved at 2 a.m. (ER21). Can the worry be solved tonight? No. So the aim is rest. The sting is a wave (ER22). Whether she lacked nerve is a question for daylight and wise mind."],["She writes down what she did well in the essay.",["IE24","ER16"],"The night voice is invalidating her choice, and she answers as she would for a friend (IE24). The essay had real strengths, and writing them down makes them facts on paper. A small true list is a positive she can add to at will (ER16)."],["She plans the next essay with the comments beside her.",["ER18"],"If self-respect is the aim, the place to earn it is the next essay. She copes ahead: she plans it with the comments beside her and rehearses the part she finds hardest (ER18). Mastery is the answer to \"didn't have the nerve\" that she will believe."]]],["She asks how to do better. The tutor's advice is the opposite of what last year's tutor drilled into her, and he adds, \"whoever told you that was wrong\".",[["She asks which matters most for this module.",["IE5","IE19"],"Two tutors, two rules, and each may be right for its own setting. Opposites can both be true (IE19). She does not have to rule between them. She asks the question that matters now, what this module rewards (IE5), and leaves last year's tutor undefended."],["She tries both on a practice paragraph and sees which works.",["G15"],"When advice conflicts, a trial settles more than an argument does. She writes the same paragraph both ways and looks at the result (G15). She may find that each works for a different job, and then she owns two tools."],["She accepts the mixed advice, and follows the current tutor.",["DT13","IE4"],"Teachers disagree. That is how things are, and she accepts it (DT13). She weighs the aims (IE4): the mark and the tie both sit with this tutor, and her self-respect does not need her to settle who was right."]]]]}},{"id":"IE5","title":"DEAR MAN: getting what you want","marks":[],"practice":["before :   write the script for 𝖽𝖾𝖺𝗋 𝗆𝖺𝗇 ,  𝗀𝗂𝗏𝖾 ,  𝖿𝖺𝗌𝗍   →   say it aloud   →   say it in mind   →   cope ahead","after :   what was said ,  which letters were used ,  what came of it for γ , γ_R , γ_Q","look for chances to practise ,  small ones first"],"u":"Why say \"I want\" and not \"you should\"? Why does answering an attack hand over control?","tree":{"t":"Jean needs a one-week extension on her dissertation chapter. What does she do?","o":[["She emails her supervisor using DEAR MAN: the facts, her feelings, a clear ask, what is in it for him, and a new date.",["IE5"],4,"DEAR MAN, letter by letter. Describe: the chapter is due Friday, and the archive was closed for two weeks. Express: \"I'm worried about handing in something thin\". She says \"I want\", and never \"you should\". Assert: \"could I have until the 14th?\" Reinforce: he gets a chapter that needs less of his time to correct. Then the manner: mindful, so she stays on the point, appears confident, with no pile of apologies, and willing to negotiate, with five days as a fallback. Carrots work better than sticks. She did not close the archive, and she is the one who has to ask."],["She stops dreading the email by seeing it as a request, not a confession.",["IE5","ER7"],6,"The dread comes from what she has made of the email: an admission that she failed. She checks the facts (ER7). An extension request is an ordinary act with a form, and the form contains no confession. Describe gives facts, and facts are not guilt. The dread was caused by her reading, and a different reading brings a different feeling (ER5). In some worlds asking for anything was treated as weakness (G11), and that may be where the first reading came from."],["She sends the ask and accepts that it might be refused.",["IE5","DT13"],1,"The skill gives her the best ask she can make. It does not give her the answer. She accepts in advance that a no is possible (DT13), and that frees the email from having to be perfect. To assert is to ask plainly, and it can't force anything. How she feels about herself afterwards does not depend on the reply (IE4)."],["She doesn't ask, and hands in a rushed chapter.",["IE5","G4"],6,"Not asking spares her the risk of a no, and that makes sense. It is also seeming to cope when she is not, the taught end of the second line (G10). The supervisor sees a weak chapter and no reason for it. A week of work is traded away for an hour of comfort."]],"e":[["She sends the DEAR MAN email. The reply is two words: \"we'll see\".",[["She replies with a date and asks for a yes or no by Friday.",["IE5","IE12"],"\"We'll see\" is neither yes nor no, so she stays on the point: the broken record, politely (IE5). She raises the firmness one notch, from asking to asking with a deadline (IE12), because the checks support it: the ask is clear, it is within his power, and the timing is forced. She keeps her tone easy."],["She plans for both answers.",["ER18"],"She can't make him answer faster, so she copes ahead (ER18). She sketches a week with the extension and a week without it. Then either reply finds her with a plan, and the wait loses most of its grip."],["She lets the uncertainty be until Friday.",["DT13","DT11"],"Not knowing is the fact for now, and she accepts it (DT13). One thing in the moment (DT11): today's page of the chapter, which is needed under either answer. Checking her inbox every ten minutes would be a way of refusing the uncertainty."]]],["The dread eases and she sends it. By evening there is no reply, and she is sure it means no, and that he is annoyed.",[["She checks the facts: no reply yet is no answer yet.",["ER7"],"What she has observed: an empty inbox for six hours. What she added: a no, and annoyance. She can't observe his mood from silence (IE17). Supervisors answer email slowly for a dozen reasons (ER7). The fear is running ahead of the facts."],["She notices the thought as a thought.",["DT19","M1"],"\"He's annoyed\" arrived dressed as knowledge. It is a thought, and thoughts come and go (DT19). She neither argues with it nor obeys it. She holds her mind, and does not let the thought hold it (M1)."],["She does one task on the chapter while she waits.",["DT9","ER18"],"An activity that takes her whole attention gets her through the wait (DT9). This one is also a step of mastery on the thing that matters (ER18). Whatever he answers, the chapter is one section further on."]]],["She asks. The supervisor replies kindly that the committee date is fixed, and the extension is refused.",[["She asks for half: three days.",["IE5","IE13"],"Negotiate is the last letter: give to get (IE5). A no to a week is not yet a no to three days. She can ask again a step lower without it being pleading (IE13). She might also turn the problem over to him: \"what would you suggest?\""],["She accepts the no, and plans the week around the original date.",["DT13","ER12"],"The ask was good and the answer is no, and both are true. She accepts the date (DT13) and puts the energy into the new problem: the best possible chapter by Friday (ER12). Which sections matter most, and what can be cut?"],["She asks who else can grant it, if illness is the reason.",["IE14","IE11"],"He said the date is not his to move. So someone else decides, by a rule (IE14). If her grounds fall under that rule, she has a right to ask, and the checks say she may ask firmly (IE11). She goes to the right office with the facts and does not go behind his back."]]]]}},{"id":"IE6","title":"DEAR MAN on the conversation itself","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When you turn the skills on the conversation itself, why describe what is happening between you, but not why they do it?","tree":{"t":"Terry's group keeps talking over his plan in meetings. What does he do?","o":[["He describes the meeting itself: \"I've started three times and haven't finished. Can I have two minutes?\"",["IE6","IE5"],4,"When the ordinary ask keeps failing, the skills are turned on the exchange itself. He describes what is happening between them now, without saying why they do it: three starts, no finish. He leaves out motives, hate talk, shoulds and threats. Then one clear ask, two minutes (IE5). The description can't be argued with, because everyone at the table saw it (M4)."],["He stops reading it as \"they think I'm useless\".",["IE6","IE17"],7,"The rule for describing the exchange is to leave out why they do it. That rule works inside his head as well. He observed being talked over. He did not observe a thought (IE17). Groups talk over people because they are rushed, loud, or keen. The hurt came from the motive he supplied. With the motive taken out, what is left is a meeting habit, and a habit can be changed."],["He writes his plan in the group chat, and accepts the meetings as they are.",["IE6","DT13"],1,"He weighs the aims (IE4). The objective is that the plan gets seen, and the meeting is only one channel for that. He accepts that this group's meetings are loud (DT13) and uses a channel where nobody can interrupt. He is not giving up his voice. He has only moved where he uses it. Accepting the meetings does not mean he approves of them (DT14)."],["He stops contributing at all.",["IE6","G4"],6,"Going silent makes sense after three tries. It is the limit of the series (G7): the signal went unheard, and so he stops sending it. The group now loses his plan, and they will think he had none. His silence will look to them like agreement."]],"e":[["He describes the meeting. Someone says, \"we really don't have time for this\", and the talk moves on.",[["He repeats his ask, calmly: two minutes.",["IE6","IE5"],"\"No time for this\" is talking over him again, about being talked over. He does not answer the remark. He repeats the ask, same words, same tone (IE5). Two minutes is small enough to be hard to refuse twice."],["He offers to send the plan in writing tonight.",["IE4","ER12"],"The objective is the plan being considered, and it need not happen in the meeting (IE4). If time really is the obstacle, writing solves it (ER12). He gets the result and the group keeps its pace. It is negotiating: give to get."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt: they are stressed.",["IE17"],"The deadline is real, and stressed people protect their minutes. He can't see contempt (IE17), and he can see the clock. Holding the kinder guess keeps him able to try again without an edge in his voice."]]],["He stops reading it as \"useless\". At the next meeting it happens again, twice, and one of them laughs while doing it.",[["He describes the meeting again, earlier this time.",["IE6","IE1"],"The skills are meant to be used early, before the hurts pile up (IE1). He does not wait for the third interruption. At the first he says, \"I'd like to finish this sentence\". He describes the exchange with no motives in it (IE6), and the laugh is not part of the description."],["He checks the facts about the group's habits.",["ER7","IE17"],"He watches one meeting as an observer. Who gets talked over? If it is everyone, it is the group's habit. If it is only him, that is a different fact and calls for a different ask (ER7). He has to look before he knows which. The laugh tempts him to guess (IE17)."],["He asks one teammate to back him when he speaks.",["IE16","IE5"],"One ally changes a room. He asks plainly, before the meeting (IE5): \"if I get cut off, would you say you want to hear the rest?\" It is a small ask of someone near him (IE16), and it reinforces the behaviour he wants from the group (IE25)."]]],["He writes the plan in the chat. Two days later it has no replies, and the group has started on a different approach.",[["He asks the group lead to put it on the next agenda.",["IE14","IE5"],"The pair, he and the group, could not settle it, and there is someone whose role is to decide what gets discussed (IE14). He asks the lead directly for an agenda slot (IE5). He goes through the lead openly, and not around the group."],["He accepts it for now, and keeps the plan ready.",["DT13"],"The group has chosen a direction, and that is a fact (DT13). Accepting it does not mean the plan was bad or that he was wrong to offer it. He keeps it. Things change (IE19), and the first approach may run into the problem his plan solves."],["He pins it, and asks for a thumbs-up from each person.",["IE25"],"Reading the plan cost effort and paid nothing, so nobody did it. He makes the wanted behaviour tiny, one tap, and he thanks each person who does it. A small step rewarded straight away is shaping (IE25)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE7","title":"GIVE: keeping the relationship","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"\"A little\" humour and a \"soft\" sell are amounts. Some of what, and why neither none nor all the way?","tree":{"t":"Claire must tell her lab partner that his half of the report is not good enough. What does she do?","o":[["She says it with GIVE: gently, interested in his view, validating his workload, with a light touch.",["IE7","IE5"],5,"GIVE, letter by letter. Gentle: no attack, no sneer, no \"this is hopeless\". Interested: she asks how he saw the section, and she lets him finish. Validate: \"three deadlines in one week is a lot\". Easy manner: a little humour, a soft sell. GIVE is the manner, and the content still has to be said, so DEAR MAN carries the ask: the section needs redoing by Thursday (IE5). \"A little\" and \"soft\" are amounts. She does not go all the way to apologizing for raising it."],["She validates why he rushed while disagreeing with the result, and feels less angry.",["IE7","IE22"],6,"She can validate why he did it and still disagree with what he did. His rushing is understandable: it had causes, and she can name them. The section is still not good enough. Both are true together (IE19). Her anger was resting on \"he doesn't care\", a motive she never observed (IE17). With a cause in its place the anger drops to the size of the real problem, which is one section."],["She fixes the worst parts herself this time, and accepts the uneven split.",["IE7","DT13"],1,"She weighs the aims (IE4). The report is due tomorrow, and the partnership has a term to run. Tonight the objective is best served by her own hands. She accepts the unfair split for this one report (DT13). It is a single decision. If she did it every time, unmet needs would pile up (IE4), so she notes that the conversation is still owed."],["She says nothing and resents him.",["IE7","G4"],6,"Silence keeps the peace tonight, and she may think of it as kindness. Resentment wears a relationship down as surely as an attack would (IE9), only more slowly. He does not learn that the section fell short, so the next one will be the same. Her anger stays, with nowhere to go but into replaying it (ER3)."]],"e":[["She uses GIVE. He folds his arms and says, \"so I'm the problem, as usual\".",[["She validates how stressful the week was for him, and repeats her one ask.",["IE7","IE22"],"\"As usual\" tells her he has heard this as a verdict on him. She validates what is valid, that the week was heavy (IE22), and she stays gentle (IE7). Then the ask again, unchanged. Validation and the ask are not traded against each other."],["She stops and suggests talking tomorrow.",["DT4","IE18"],"Both of them are heating up, and nothing useful gets decided past a certain point (ER23). She stops, steps back, and proposes a time (DT4). She is pausing a conversation that is going badly, on purpose and from wise mind. She is not walking out on it."],["She keeps her voice gentle, and asks what he needs to fix it.",["IE7","IE8"],"She holds both halves of a helper's stance: the section must change, and he can be met where he is (IE8). \"What would help you redo it?\" hands part of the problem to him, and that is negotiating (IE5). Her tone stays gentle, so his folded arms have nothing to push against."]]],["She validates his rush. He brightens, says \"thanks for understanding\", and changes nothing.",[["She says it plainly: \"I understand the rush, and the section needs rewriting\".",["IE22","IE5"],"He took validation for agreement. Validating what is understandable does not agree with all of it (IE22). She says both halves in one sentence, joined by \"and\". \"But\" would cancel the first half. The second half is the assert of DEAR MAN (IE5)."],["She sets a clear deadline.",["IE12","IE5"],"Her first ask was too soft to register. The checks support a firmer one (IE12): the report is shared, the timing is forced, and she has done her part. She gives a date and a time, and says them without heat."],["She lets her frustration be, and asks again tomorrow.",["ER22","IE13"],"The frustration fits, and it is a wave (ER22). If she spoke at its peak she would lose \"gentle\". Tomorrow she asks again, a step firmer and without apology (IE13). Asking twice is ordinary and does not mean the first ask failed."]]],["She fixes the worst parts. The tutor marks the report down for \"uneven style\", and her partner says, \"guess we both messed up\".",[["She asks for the marks to be split by section.",["IE5","IE10"],"Self-respect is one of the three aims, and it weighs more now. FAST: she is fair to her partner, makes no apology for asking, and is truthful about who wrote what (IE10). The ask to the tutor is clear and comes with evidence (IE5). She describes the split and accuses nobody."],["She accepts this mark and plans the split earlier next time.",["DT13","ER18"],"The mark is given, and she accepts it (DT13). She chose to patch instead of talk, and this is what followed. That is a finding, not a verdict (G13). She copes ahead (ER18): next report, the division and a check-in date are agreed in week one."],["She talks to her partner about the next report, using GIVE.",["IE7","IE4"],"\"We both messed up\" is partly true: she covered for him and said nothing. The conversation she put off is still owed. She has it now, gently and with interest in his side (IE7), having weighed that the partnership matters for the rest of the term (IE4)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE8","title":"a helper's stance: three pairs held at once","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why must the helper hold all six stances at once, and not move along one line from end to end?","tree":{"t":"Randy tutors a younger student who keeps giving up on algebra. What does he do?","o":[["He says \"this is hard, and you can do the next step\", and sets one small problem.",["IE8","ER18"],3,"A helper holds three pairs at once: oriented to change and to acceptance, steady and flexible, demanding and nurturing. His sentence has both halves of the first pair. \"This is hard\" is acceptance, and \"you can do the next step\" is change. One small problem is demanding out of goodwill, sized so that she can do it, which is how mastery is built (ER18). If he dropped either half he would be scolding her or coddling her."],["He reminds himself the student is doing the best she can, and his frustration drops.",["IE8","G6"],6,"His frustration rests on \"she isn't trying\". The first assumption says people are doing the best they can, and the third says they still need to do better (G6). Those two are the helper's first pair, acceptance and change. Her giving up has causes: perhaps a history of being told she is bad at maths (G11). With the cause seen, the frustration eases, and he can be steady instead of sharp."],["He accepts slow progress, and stays steady and centred.",["IE8"],1,"The second pair is unwavering and centred, and flexible with compassion. Her pace is her pace. He accepts it without lowering the aim. Steady means he is the same tutor this week as last, whether she solved anything or not. A student who expects helpers to give up on her needs to see one who doesn't."],["He gets frustrated and does the problems for her.",["IE8","G4"],6,"Doing them for her ends the discomfort for both of them, and that is why it is tempting. It is nurturing with nothing asked of her. She learns that giving up makes the problem go away, and so giving up gets stronger (IE25). It also tells her that he does not think she can do it."]],"e":[["He sets one small problem. She pushes the paper away and says, \"I can't, I'm just not a maths person\".",[["He makes the step smaller still: just the first line.",["IE8","IE25"],"Flexible with compassion: the step was too big for today, so he shrinks it. Shaping rewards small steps straight away (IE25), and the first step has to be one she will take. When she writes the first line he says so warmly. He is still asking something of her, which is the demanding half."],["He validates how frustrating algebra feels, and waits.",["IE22","IE23"],"\"Not a maths person\" is a claim about always (IE21), and he does not argue with it yet. First he pays attention and says back what he hears (IE23): \"it feels like it never goes in\". The feeling is understandable (IE22). Then he waits, because once she feels heard she can try again."],["He tries a different kind of problem she likes.",["IE27"],"What counts as a reward depends on the person (IE27). Abstract x and y give her nothing back. A problem about something she cares about, the same algebra underneath, may pay where the worksheet did not. He watches what works for her."]]],["His frustration drops. But she has seen it on his face before, and she says, \"you think I'm stupid\".",[["He tells her plainly that he doesn't, and why he's there.",["IE10","IE5"],"She has guessed at a thought she can't observe, and he can simply tell her what it is (IE17). He is truthful, with no speech (IE10): \"I don't. I think this is hard and I think you can get it, which is why I keep coming\". It is plain and has no edge (IE5)."],["He validates the feeling: \"it makes sense to feel that way when it's hard\".",["IE22"],"The feeling is valid even though what she says about him is wrong. He validates the first and does not agree with the second (IE22). She may have been thought stupid by someone before (G11), and he hears that under what she says."],["He stays steady and centred, and goes on.",["IE8"],"She is testing whether he will get hurt, argue, or leave. Unwavering is the answer he can give without words (IE8). The next problem goes on the table in the same tone as the last. She sees that he is still there and still calm."]]],["He accepts slow progress. Her parents stop him at the door: \"it's been six weeks. Why hasn't she improved?\"",[["He describes what she can do now that she couldn't before.",["M4","IE5"],"\"Hasn't improved\" is a judgment. He answers with description (M4): six weeks ago she would not start a problem, and now she finishes two-step equations. He says it as fact, with what comes next (IE5). The record of small steps is the evidence (G15)."],["He weighs his aims: the job, the relationship, his self-respect.",["IE4"],"Three aims are in play. He wants to keep the job, keep the parents' trust, and not promise what he does not believe (IE4). Promising fast results would be selling out, and that wears self-respect down. He decides what to say with all three in view."],["He lets their worry be theirs, and keeps teaching.",["DT13","IE8"],"Their worry is real and makes sense, and he can validate it (IE22). He can't remove it by teaching faster than she can learn, and he accepts that (DT13). He stays centred under their pressure, so that she is not taught by a tutor who has caught their hurry (IE8)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE9","title":"what wears a relationship down","marks":[["D_R","behaviours that wear a relationship down"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do these behaviours wear down any relationship, whoever does them? What do many of them signal about the one doing them?","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps cancelling on her study group at the last minute. What does she do?","o":[["She commits to two sessions she can keep, and tells the group a day ahead if she can't make one.",["IE9","G5"],3,"Some behaviours wear down any relationship, whoever does them, and the first on the list is being unreliable. She makes a promise small enough to keep, two sessions, and a rule for when she can't: a day's notice. This is a fresh commitment after a drift (G5). Repair of trust is being trustworthy from here on (G13), so two kept sessions do more than an apology would. She needs to do better, and this says how."],["She sees the cancelling as a sign she is worn out, not that she is a bad person.",["IE9","ER19"],6,"Many of the wearing behaviours are signs of being worn out. She checks the body's basics, and the cause is there: five hours of sleep, meals skipped (ER19). She was run down, and cancelling followed. \"Flaky\" is a judgment, and \"worn out\" is a cause that has a fix (G6). The shame eases. What she owes the group is unchanged, and she is better able to give it."],["She accepts that the group is cooler with her, and shows up next time.",["IE9","DT13"],1,"Each cancellation lowered how far she could be relied on, and the coolness is the natural result (IE26). It fits the facts. She accepts it without resenting them and without sinking under it (DT13). A tear left alone gets harder to mend (IE1), so she shows up and tolerates the cool air."],["She keeps cancelling.",["IE9","G4"],6,"Each cancellation spares her a tired evening, and that pays at once (IE25). It makes sense, since she is worn out. But the group is one of her supports, and wearing it down leaves her more worn out. That is the loop where unstable relationships feed unstable emotion (G8)."]],"e":[["She commits to two sessions. On the morning of the second she wakes with a fever, and thinks, \"they'll never believe me\".",[["She tells the group a day ahead, as she promised.",["IE9","IE1"],"Her promise had two parts, and she can still keep the second: early notice. She messages as soon as she knows, plainly and with no long excuse (IE10). Using the skill early is the point (IE1). A cancellation with notice is a different act from a last-minute one."],["She checks the facts: being ill is not unreliable.",["ER7","IE20"],"\"They'll never believe me\" is a forecast. The facts (ER7): she kept the first session, she is ill, and she gave notice. \"I always let them down\" weakens to \"I have let them down before, and today I am ill\" (IE20). Both parts are true."],["She asks for the notes and joins online.",["IE5","ER12"],"The problem is missing the session, and there are part-solutions (ER12). She asks for one specific thing (IE5). Joining for twenty minutes online, feverish, shows the group in an act what the message said in words."]]],["She sees she is worn out. The group lead says, in front of the others, \"we all are. You're still letting us down\".",[["She says what she can promise, plainly.",["IE10","IE5"],"The lead is partly right, and she does not argue the part that is true. FAST: fair to them, no grovelling, truthful (IE10). \"You're right that I've cancelled. I can do Tuesdays and Thursdays, and I'll give a day's notice.\" It is a clear statement of what she will do (IE5)."],["She validates herself: worn out is a cause, not a flaw.",["IE24","G6"],"\"We all are\" dismisses her cause by comparison (G11). Her cause is still real. She validates it herself (IE24), and she also validates the lead's frustration, which is real as well (IE22). Causes explain, and they do not excuse: she still has to solve it (G6)."],["She works on her sleep and meals this week.",["ER19"],"If worn out is the cause, then the repair starts in the body (ER19). This is the end of the chain where vulnerability comes in (G13). A week of proper sleep will not look like an answer to the lead. It is what makes the promise keepable."]]],["She shows up. They have split the topics without her, and someone says, \"oh, we didn't think you'd come\".",[["She asks what she missed and takes a task.",["IE5","IE16"],"The sentence stings, and it is a fair summary of their evidence. She does not defend herself. She asks for a task (IE5). Joining in is how a place in a group is rebuilt (IE16), and a finished task counts for more with them than a promise."],["She lets the hurt be there, and stays.",["ER22"],"The hurt fits: she was left out, and it follows from what she did (ER8). Its urge is to leave, which would be one more cancellation. She stays, and the hurt rises and falls while the session goes on (ER22)."],["She gives it time, and keeps showing up.",["DT16","IE9"],"Trust that was worn down over weeks comes back over weeks. Each session where the air is cool is a fork, and she turns toward coming again (DT16). Reliability is a count of kept promises (IE9), and it can only be raised one session at a time (G2)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE10","title":"FAST: keeping self-respect","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does apologizing too much wear down both your mastery and the relationship? When may GIVE be set aside?","tree":{"t":"Mike apologizes three times for asking a question in seminar. What does he do?","o":[["He asks his next question once, with no apology.",["IE10"],5,"FAST: be fair, no needless apologies, stick to values, be truthful. The second letter is his: no apology for existing, asking, or having a view. A question in a seminar harms nobody, so nothing is owed for it. Apologizing too much wears down his mastery and the relationship as well, because it tells the room his questions are an imposition. The new behaviour has to be learned in the seminar, where the old one lives. He asks one question, once, and stops talking."],["He reminds himself that asking is his right, and the shame eases.",["IE10","IE24"],7,"Three apologies are shame speaking. Shame fits when he has broken his own values, and a question breaks none (ER8). So the shame does not fit, and he can say so to himself as he would to a friend (IE24). Validating his own feelings is part of FAST, and so is dropping the ashamed posture. Asking where the habit came from helps more than scolding himself for it. Somewhere, taking up room was treated as a fault (G11)."],["He notices the urge to apologize, and lets it pass without acting on it.",["IE10","ER22"],1,"The apology is an urge before it is a word. It rises just after he speaks. He lets it rise and fall like any wave (ER22) and says nothing. The silence afterwards feels rude to him, and nobody else notices it. He does not act on the urge, and he does not fight it either. It gets weaker each time it is left unanswered (IE26)."],["He keeps apologizing, or stops asking.",["IE10","G4"],6,"Both are ways of paying for a debt he does not owe. Apologizing makes sense as a way to get in first before anyone objects. It teaches the room to hear his questions as interruptions. Going silent costs him the answers. Either way his self-respect is given up without anyone having asked for it."]],"e":[["He asks without apologizing. The lecturer says, \"that's a fairly basic question\", and a few people shift in their seats.",[["He keeps to his question, calmly, until it is answered.",["IE10","IE5"],"\"Basic\" is a rating of the question. It does not answer it. He stays on the point (IE5): \"it may be, and I'd still like to understand it\". He makes no apology and takes no offence (IE10). No one can take his self-respect unless he gives it up, and he has not."],["He validates himself: basic questions are allowed.",["IE24"],"The remark invites the old shame back. He answers it himself (IE24): a seminar is for questions at every level, and others in the room were likely wondering the same thing. The lecturer may teach well and still be careless with a word (G11)."],["He lets the sting pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"\"Basic\" will replay in his head on the walk home. It is a thought now (DT19), and the sting under it is a wave (ER22). If he feeds it, he has a reason never to ask again. Left alone, it is gone by dinner."]]],["The shame eases. At the next seminar he hears himself say \"sorry, sorry, quick question\" before he can stop it.",[["He notices it, and asks the next question without one.",["IE10","DT16"],"A habit of years does not end in a week. The slip is noticed, and noticing is most of the skill. The next question is a new fork, and he takes it the other way (DT16). One clean ask after a slip counts as a trial too (G15)."],["He runs a chain: when does the apology come?",["G13"],"He describes the act exactly: two \"sorry\"s, said before the question. What started it: the lecturer paused and looked up. The links: the thought \"I'm interrupting\", a drop in his stomach, and the word out before he chose it. The key link is the thought, because without it there is nothing to apologize for. The skill that goes there is a ready first word, \"Could you\", to take the place of \"sorry\"."],["He does not judge the slip.",["M5"],"Apologizing to himself for apologizing would be the same habit turned inward. When he judges, he does not judge the judging (M5). What happened: two words came out, and he noticed. That is a description, and it has a next step."]]],["He lets the urge pass and asks plainly. Afterwards he is sure he sounded rude, and a classmate's glance seems to confirm it.",[["He checks the facts: did anyone react?",["ER7"],"What he observed: the lecturer answered, the seminar went on, and one classmate glanced at him. What he added: rudeness, and disapproval (ER7). He can't observe what a glance means (IE17). To someone used to triple apologies, a plain ask sounds rude from the inside. That is the old habit's standard."],["He asks a friend how he came across.",["IE16","IE5"],"His own gauge is off, for good reasons, so he borrows another. He asks a specific question (IE5): \"did I sound rude when I asked that?\" The answer is information from outside his head, and asking for it is a small act of friendship too (IE16)."],["He lets the doubt be, and keeps asking.",["ER22","ER10"],"The doubt is the discomfort of acting against an old rule. It does not have to be settled before the next question (ER22). Asking again without apology is opposite action, kept up until the feeling changes (ER10)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE11","title":"ten checks: four about your act, six about the pair of you","marks":[["pⱼ","pⱼ(x, y): a pair check, a relation between the one who wants and the one asked"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does refusing use the same six checks with the two of you swapped? What limits the count?","tree":{"t":"Zoe wants to ask a professor she has never met for a reference letter. What does she do?","o":[["She runs the six checks, finds the request clear and within the professor's role, and asks.",["IE11","IE12"],4,"Before asking, ten things are checked. Four are about her own act: does the aim outweigh the relationship, does it keep her self-respect, does it serve her long-term goals, is now the time. Six are about the two of them: can the professor give it, is the professor bound to, who is in charge, does it fit the relationship, who owes whom, and is the request clear. She goes through them. The professor can give it. Writing references is part of the role. The request is clear, and the timing leaves a month. It does not fit a close relationship, since there is none, and nothing is owed. Most checks favour asking, so she asks, at a middling firmness (IE12): once, clearly, taking a no if it comes."],["She sees that the professor is not bound to say yes, and lowers the stakes.",["IE11"],7,"One check asks whether the other person is bound to give. Here the answer is no. A reference is a favour within a role, and it is not a duty. That check does not tell her to stay quiet. It tells her how much rides on the answer: a no would be the professor using a right, and it would say nothing about her. Finding where her fear came from helps. She had been treating the ask as a test of her worth, and it is a request a stranger is free to refuse."],["She asks, and accepts that a stranger may decline.",["IE11","DT13"],1,"The count is only as good as the wise mind doing it, and no count guarantees a yes. She makes the ask the checks support and accepts the part she does not control (DT13). The checks told her she may ask. They did not tell her what she will hear. She can carry both the ask and the uncertainty."],["She doesn't ask, and goes without the reference.",["IE11","G4"],6,"Not asking guarantees the outcome she fears from asking. It makes sense if every ask feels like an imposition, which is a belief and not one of the ten checks (IE3). She has answered for the professor without consulting the professor. One of her own four checks was long-term goals, and that one loses."]],"e":[["She runs the checks and asks. Two weeks pass with no reply, and the application is due in ten days.",[["She sends one polite follow-up.",["IE5","IE13"],"The timing check has changed: now is more pressing than before. One more check in favour means she may be one notch firmer (IE12), so she follows up, restates the ask, and gives the date (IE5). It is the same rung said again, with nothing softened and nothing sharpened (IE13)."],["She asks a second professor too.",["ER12"],"Silence is not a no, but she has to plan as if it might be one. The problem is a reference by the deadline, and it has more than one solution (ER12). A second ask is not disloyal to the first. If both say yes, she thanks one and explains."],["She lets the wait be, and gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17","DT13"],"She can't see why there is silence (IE17). A professor in term has hundreds of emails, and that cause is far likelier than a slight. She accepts not knowing for now (DT13), and gives the follow-up a few days."]]],["She lowers the stakes and asks. The professor says yes, and needs her draft statement and CV by tomorrow at nine.",[["She asks for a different date, plainly.",["IE5"],"A yes with a condition she can't meet is the start of a negotiation: give to get (IE5). She says what she can do, both documents by Thursday, and asks if that works. The professor has shown goodwill, and a plain counter-offer respects it."],["She runs the checks again with the new facts.",["IE11"],"The facts have changed, so the count has. Is the professor bound to wait? No. Is her counter-request clear? It can be. Who owes whom now? She owes something, since a favour has been offered. That tilts her toward meeting the deadline if she can, and asking gently if she can't."],["She plans the week to meet it.",["ER12","ER18"],"The new problem is two documents by nine (ER12). She lists what exists, what needs writing, and what can be good enough. It is a hard-but-possible evening (ER18). Meeting the deadline is also the best thanks she can give a stranger."]]],["She asks. The professor replies in one line: \"I don't write references for students I haven't taught\".",[["She thanks them, and asks who might be better placed.",["IE7","IE5"],"The relationship counts for something even with a stranger (IE7). She thanks the professor without irony. Then one small ask (IE5): who would be the right person? The professor's rule points her at the answer, which is someone who has taught her."],["She accepts the no, and asks someone else.",["DT13","ER12"],"The no came from a rule and not from a judgment of her. The check \"fits the relationship\" had flagged it. She accepts it (DT13) and goes back to the list (ER12). The next ask will score better on that check."],["She lets the disappointment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"A one-line no stings even when it is fair. The disappointment fits at a small size (ER8), and it is a wave (ER22). Its urge is \"never ask a stranger again\", and one case can't support that (IE21)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE12","title":"intensity is a cardinality","marks":[["oⱼ","oⱼ(a): an own check, a property of the act a"],["𝒫","𝒫(X): all the subsets of X","r02h.html","2h · All the Subsets"]],"practice":[],"u":"If how firmly to act is the number of checks in favour, why can one more check never make you less firm?","tree":{"t":"Most of the checks favour Zoe asking firmly. A new check comes out in favour too. What does she do?","o":[["She asks more firmly: she names a date and follows up.",["IE12"],3,"How firmly to act is a count: how many of the ten checks come out in favour of holding firm. One more check never makes her less firm. So when a new one lands on her side, she moves up a rung (IE13). On the new rung the ask is put with a date, and a no is met by asking again. Wise mind may weigh one check above another, so she looks at which one changed. Firmness means clarity and persistence. Her tone stays as it was (IE7)."],["She lets the count settle her doubts.",["IE12"],7,"Her doubt asks \"am I being too much?\", and the question has an answer that does not depend on her mood. Count the checks. Seven of ten in favour is a fact about the situation, in the same way that a degree is a count of conditions (G2). The feeling of being pushy comes from an old rule about never asking (G11). The count describes this ask, with this person, now, and so it is the better guide."],["She asks firmly, and accepts whatever answer comes.",["IE12","DT13"],1,"The count sets how she asks. It does not set what she is told. She matches her firmness to it and accepts that the reply is the other person's to give (DT13). Firm asking with an open hand keeps her respect and theirs, and it is neither demanding nor timid."],["She asks timidly despite the count.",["IE12","G4"],6,"Asking softly feels safer, and it makes sense if firmness was punished somewhere. But the situation called for a clear ask, and a hint is easy to miss or to refuse. Acting helpless, even as a tactic, wears self-respect down (IE4). She may then decide that asking does not work, when the truth is that she never fully asked."]],"e":[["She asks firmly and names a date. The reply comes back in one line: \"I'll get to it when I can\".",[["She stays firm, and keeps her tone kind.",["IE12","IE7"],"A curt reply does not change any of the ten checks, so the count stands, and so does her firmness (IE12). Firmness and manner are separate dials. She keeps the date and keeps GIVE (IE7): \"thanks, I'll check in on Thursday\"."],["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"She has one line of text and no tone of voice. \"Annoyed\" is her guess (IE17). \"Busy\" fits the same words. She holds the kinder reading all the way, and that keeps her next message free of defensiveness."],["She checks the count again: is it still in favour?",["IE12","IE11"],"The count is taken at a time, and things change. She runs the ten again (IE11). If the reply told her something new, such as bad timing for them, one check may have flipped, and she eases a notch. If nothing is new, the count holds and so does she."]]],["The count settles her. Then a friend who heard about it says, \"honestly, that was a bit pushy\".",[["She checks the facts: she asked once, clearly.",["ER7","IE12"],"\"Pushy\" is a judgment. The facts (ER7): one ask, with a date, and seven checks in favour. Pushy would be firmness above what the count supports. Hers matched it (IE12). Her friend may have a lower setting of their own."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"The friend's word lands on the old sore spot. She says to herself what is valid (IE24): she checked before she asked, and she asked as the checks allowed. Feeling stung makes sense, and it does not mean the friend is right."],["She asks her friend what pushy looked like to them.",["IE6","IE17"],"She can't observe what the friend saw (IE17), so she asks, with curiosity and no heat. This is describing the exchange and asking about it (IE6). The answer may teach her something about her manner, or it may show that the friend heard the story second-hand."]]],["She asks firmly. The answer is a clear, polite no.",[["She asks once more, higher on the ladder.",["IE13","IE5"],"With this many checks in favour, the rung she is on meets a no by asking again (IE13). She asks once more, with a reason or an offer added: give to get (IE5). After that the count also tells her when to stop."],["She accepts the no.",["DT13"],"The count told her how to ask, and she did. The other person had the right to refuse, which was one of the checks. She accepts the answer (DT13). A firm ask that meets a no is the skill done correctly."],["She asks what would change the answer.",["IE5","ER12"],"This turns the problem over to them (IE5). The question treats the no as information about an obstacle. If there is a condition she can meet, she has a new solution to try (ER12). If there is none, she knows the no is final."]]]]}},{"id":"IE13","title":"the ladder: how it is put, and how a no is met","marks":[["ℓ_ask","the ladder of ways to ask"],["ℓ_no","the ladder of ways to refuse"],["⪯","is no stronger than"]],"practice":[],"u":"Each rung says two things. Why does going up a rung never soften either one?","tree":{"t":"Sarah is asked to join a fourth extracurricular during exam term. What does she do?","o":[["She says no firmly, high on the ladder, and offers to help next term.",["IE13"],4,"The ladder of refusing has rungs, and each says two things: whether she does it, and with how much grace or resistance. She runs the checks for refusing (IE11). She can't give the time, she is not bound to, it does not serve her long-term goals, and now is not the time. Nearly all favour holding firm, so she refuses high on the ladder (IE12): a clear no that she will repeat if pressed. The offer for next term keeps the tie (IE7) and softens nothing, since going up a rung never softens either part."],["She sees that saying no is not letting people down, and the guilt drops.",["IE13","IE3"],7,"The guilt comes from a myth: \"if I say no, I'm letting people down\". It claims every case, so one case breaks it (IE3). The last person who told her no did not let her down. Guilt fits when she has gone against her own values (ER8), and turning down a fourth club in exam term goes against none of them. The guilt does not fit. Her own priorities are allowed to weigh against what others ask of her (IE1)."],["She says no with grace, and accepts their disappointment.",["IE13","DT13"],1,"A no can be kind, and it can still disappoint. Their disappointment is theirs to feel, and it is valid (IE22). She does not have to prevent it or fix it. She accepts it as the cost of the no (DT13). Being able to tolerate someone else's disappointment is what makes a no possible for her at all."],["She says yes, and resents it all term.",["IE13","G4"],6,"Yes ends the awkward moment, and that is its whole appeal. Always putting the relationship first piles up unmet needs until she blows up or leaves (IE4). The club gets a tired, resentful member, and her exams get what is left over. It makes sense if a no once cost her someone's warmth (G11)."]],"e":[["She says no firmly. The organizer laughs and says, \"come on, you're the only one who can do it, don't be like that\".",[["She says no again, the same way.",["IE13","IE5"],"\"The only one\" and \"don't be like that\" are pressure, and neither changes a single check. The rung she chose meets pressure by repeating the no (IE13): same words, same tone, like a broken record (IE5). She does not explain further, because every reason she adds is something to argue with."],["She ends the conversation politely.",["IE10","IE4"],"She has said it twice. A third round would be for the organizer's benefit and at her cost. She weighs the aims (IE4): her objective is met, the tie matters a little, and her self-respect matters a lot. She closes kindly and goes, with no apology for the no (IE10)."],["She notices the guilt rising and lets it pass.",["ER22","IE3"],"\"Don't be like that\" is aimed at the myth (IE3), and the guilt rises on cue. It is a wave (ER22). She watches it without obeying it. The checks have not changed since she counted them."]]],["The guilt drops and she says no. A friend in the club stops answering her messages.",[["She asks her friend kindly if they can talk.",["IE7","IE5"],"A tear left alone gets harder to mend (IE1). She asks for a conversation, gently, with interest in the friend's side (IE7). She is asking to talk, and she is not offering to take back the no (IE5). The friendship and the refusal are separate matters."],["She gives it time.",["DT13","IE17"],"She can see that the messages stopped. She can't see why (IE17). It may be hurt, or exam term, or nothing. She accepts the quiet for now (DT13), and does not fill it with the worst story."],["She validates her own choice.",["IE24","IE13"],"The silence feels like proof that the myth was right. It is one person's reaction, and it does not make the no wrong (IE13). She tells herself what she would tell a friend (IE24): she is allowed to protect her exam term, and a friendship that is worth having can survive that."]]],["She says no with grace. They come back the next day: \"could you do just one thing? Only the poster\".",[["She says no to that too, kindly.",["IE13"],"A smaller ask is still an ask, and the checks give the same answer in exam term. Going up or down the ladder is her choice. A new request does not move her down a rung by itself. She says no with the same grace as before."],["She weighs it: is one small thing worth it?",["IE4","IE11"],"A new request deserves its own count (IE11). Can she give an hour? Does the tie matter? Would she resent it? If the checks come out differently for a poster than for a whole club, yes is a fair answer, chosen on purpose (IE4). What matters is that she weighs it. She should not just give in."],["She offers a time after exams.",["IE7","ER12"],"Their problem is a poster, and hers is time. A date after exams may solve both (ER12). It is negotiating, give to get, and it shows goodwill (IE7). If the poster can't wait, she has still said something true and kind."]]]]}},{"id":"IE14","title":"the third: self, others, agent","marks":[["Hₒ","the others: one person or any number"],["H","people"],["ag","the agent: the one who acts, perhaps on another’s behalf"],["𝒜","a set of acts, used as a model","r02e.html","2e · Membership and Same Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a third person change what an act can be? Why do the parts stay three however many others there are?","tree":{"t":"Terry asks his teacher for an extension on his friend's behalf. What does he do?","o":[["He names it as an ask for his friend, with his friend's consent, and checks the teacher's rule.",["IE14","IE5"],4,"With a third person, every act has three parts: the self it is for, the others it is toward, and the agent who does it. Here the ask is for his friend, toward the teacher, and done by Terry. He says so at the start, so the teacher knows who is who. The checks take the third place as well (IE11): on whose behalf, and by whose rule. He has his friend's consent on the first, and for the second he asks the teacher whether an extension can be requested by someone else. Then the ask itself is DEAR MAN (IE5)."],["He sees he is the agent, not the one who owes the work, and stops feeling guilty.",["IE14"],7,"He has been feeling as if the late essay were his. The three parts sort that out. The essay is his friend's, the decision is the teacher's, and his own part is only the carrying. Guilt fits when he has gone against his own values (ER8), and carrying a message well goes against none. Seeing whose problem is whose works better than taking on all of it."],["He accepts that the teacher may want to hear from the friend directly.",["IE14","DT13"],1,"An agent can carry an ask only as far as the other person's rule allows. The teacher may say that it has to come from the student. That would be a rule, and it would not be a rebuff. He accepts in advance that his part may end at the door (DT13). He is helping his friend, and that is different from standing in for him."],["He gives up and lets the friend miss the deadline.",["IE14","G4"],6,"Stepping back makes sense, because the role is awkward and unclear. Having agreed to carry the ask, he now drops it and tells no one. That is being unreliable, and it wears the friendship down (IE9). His friend thinks it is handled. He should either carry it or hand it back plainly."]],"e":[["He asks for his friend. The teacher says, \"I can't discuss another student's work with you. They'll have to come themselves\".",[["He tells his friend exactly what the teacher said.",["IE14","IE5"],"An agent carries messages both ways and carries them accurately. He reports the teacher's words, and leaves out his own reading of the tone (M4). It is a clear statement of what is needed next (IE5). His friend can now act on facts."],["He offers to go with his friend.",["IE16","IE7"],"The rule says the friend must ask. It does not say the friend must go alone. Going along is a new shape for the same three parts: now the friend is the agent, and Terry is support. It is offered gently and without pressure (IE7), and it is what a friend does (IE16)."],["He accepts the rule.",["DT13","IE14"],"The teacher answered the third-place check: by whose rule? By the school's, about privacy. It is a reasonable rule, and it is not about him. He accepts it (DT13). His part is done, and it was done properly."]]],["He stops feeling guilty. The ask fails anyway, and his friend says, \"you must have said it wrong. Thanks for nothing\".",[["He describes what he did, plainly.",["M4","IE10"],"He does not counter-attack. He describes (M4): what he said, what the teacher answered, and when. He is truthful and fair, with no grovelling and no excuses (IE10). His friend may still be angry, but the facts are now on the table and not guessed at."],["He validates his friend's frustration without taking the blame.",["IE22"],"His friend's frustration makes sense: the essay is still late, and the hope failed. He validates that (IE22). It does not follow that the failure was his doing, and he does not say it was. He can understand the feeling without agreeing with the accusation."],["He lets the hurt be.",["ER22"],"\"Thanks for nothing\" hurts, coming after a favour. The hurt fits (ER8). It is a wave (ER22), and if he answers at its peak they will have a second problem on top of the first. His friend is speaking from emotion mind, under a deadline (M3)."]]],["He accepts the rule. But his friend goes pale at the idea of asking in person, and says, \"I can't. I'll just take the zero\".",[["He helps his friend write a DEAR MAN email instead.",["IE5","IE8"],"He can't be the agent. He can still be a helper, and a helper holds both sides: you can do this, and this is hard for you (IE8). They write the email together, letter by letter (IE5). His friend sends it under his own name, and the teacher's rule is met."],["He validates the anxiety.",["IE22","IE23"],"Before any solution he listens, and says back what he hears (IE23): \"going in there feels impossible right now\". The anxiety is understandable even though the zero is not the only way out (IE22). Once his friend feels heard, he may be able to think about the email."],["He lets it be his friend's choice.",["IE14","DT13"],"The work is his friend's, and so is the decision (IE14). Terry can offer, encourage, and stay beside him. He can't choose for him. He accepts that his friend may take the zero (DT13). Respecting that is part of knowing which of the three parts is his."]]]]}},{"id":"IE15","title":"troubleshooting: the first check that fails","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why check the six obstacles in order, and stop at the first that fails?","tree":{"t":"Claire's request to switch seminar groups failed. What does she do?","o":[["She walks the six obstacles in order, finds the request was unclear, and asks again clearly.",["IE15","IE5"],4,"Troubleshooting checks six things in a fixed order and stops at the first that fails. One: did she have the skill? She reads her email back as a stranger would. \"Any chance of maybe moving groups at some point?\" has no group, no reason, and no date in it. The first check fails, so she stops there and does not go on to worries, emotions or other people. The fix belongs to that check: write the ask out in DEAR MAN form (IE5). She gives the facts, the clash on her timetable, and the exact request, Tuesday group to Thursday group from week four. She fixes the first failure and tries again."],["She stops reading the refusal as personal.",["IE15","IE17"],7,"She had gone straight to the sixth check, other people: \"the office doesn't like me\". The order matters because the early checks are about things she can see and fix, and the last is about things she can't observe (IE17). Taken in order, the failure shows up at the first check, in her own unclear wording. That is a cause, and it is not a rejection. The sting eases, because nobody refused her. They could not tell what she was asking for."],["She accepts her group for this term.",["IE15","DT13"],1,"She walks the checks and stops at the second: does she know what she wants? She wanted out of an awkward group more than she wanted into another one. Weighing it (IE4), another round with the office costs more than the switch is worth this term. She accepts the group (DT13). She decided this on purpose after checking, and that is different from giving up."],["She gives up and skips seminars.",["IE15","G4"],6,"One failed ask has become \"asking doesn't work\", a claim about every case drawn from one (IE21). She has not checked which of the six failed, so she can't know that it was a fixable one. Skipping gives relief and costs her the module. The missed seminars will be a problem of their own (G12)."]],"e":[["She asks again, clearly. The office replies that the Thursday group is full.",[["She asks to be put on a waiting list.",["IE5","ER12"],"The first check now passes: the ask was skilful. The obstacle that remains is the sixth, the setting, and no better wording moves a full room. For that obstacle the tool is problem solving (ER12): what partial result is possible? She asks for a place on the waiting list, one clear ask (IE5)."],["She walks the obstacles again: is someone else able to swap?",["IE15"],"She troubleshoots again after the new result. One to five pass. Six, other people or the setting, fails: the group is full. So the fix has to change the setting. A full group is still open to a one-for-one swap. She asks in her own group whether anyone wants Thursdays."],["She accepts it for this term.",["DT13"],"She made a good ask and the room is full. Both are true. A full room is a fact with no one behind it to persuade (DT13). She accepts it without deciding that the asking was pointless, and the clear request is on record for next term."]]],["She stops reading it as personal. Then she phones to follow up, and the administrator sighs and says, \"I've already answered this\".",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"A sigh and a short sentence are what she observed. \"She can't stand me\" is not (IE17). An office in week three of term is overloaded, and that is a cause she can see. She holds the kinder guess, all the way."],["She notes it, and lets it pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"The sigh will replay. It is a thought now, and she can let it pass (DT19). The small hurt under it is a wave (ER22). She writes down what was actually said, which was useful: her question has already been answered, and she can find that answer."],["She asks to speak to someone else there.",["IE14","IE5"],"If this person can't or won't help, the question is who decides (IE14). She asks plainly and without complaint (IE5): \"is there someone who handles group changes?\" She is finding the right door, and she is not going over anyone's head."]]],["She accepts her group. In the next seminar one of them mimics the way she speaks, and two others laugh.",[["She says plainly that it isn't funny to her.",["IE10","IE5"],"Acceptance was for the group she was assigned to. Mockery was not part of what she accepted. One fair, truthful sentence, with no apology (IE10): \"that's not funny to me\". She describes and does not attack (IE5). With someone who is being unkind, she can set gentleness aside."],["She sits with someone else.",["IE16"],"A group of twelve is not three people. Being near someone is where liking starts (IE16), and she can choose who she is near. There is likely someone in the room who did not laugh. She moves her chair. It is a small change to the situation that she can make herself."],["She tries the switch again next term.",["IE15","ER18"],"The facts have changed, so her earlier weighing can change with them. She copes ahead (ER18): she puts the date the lists open in her calendar and keeps the clear request ready. This time she knows which check failed before, and it will not fail again."]]]]}},{"id":"IE16","title":"finding people: near, alike, talking, liking, joining","marks":[["≳","is at least about as large as"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why disclose about as much as the other does? Why is praise for everyone worth nothing?","tree":{"t":"Jean has just started university and knows no one. What does she do?","o":[["She joins a society that meets weekly, and talks to the same two people each time.",["IE16"],4,"People come to like those they are often near, and those who share their interests. A weekly society gives her both: the same room, the same faces, a shared subject. Talking to the same two people each time is what turns being near into being known. The rest is conversation skill: answer and ask, say a little more than was asked, and talk about half the time. She did not cause being new. Friendship is built by repeated small contacts, and she can arrange for those to happen."],["She reminds herself that everyone is lovable to someone.",["IE16"],2,"In the first weeks loneliness turns into a belief: \"there's something about me\". The footing says otherwise. Everyone is lovable to someone, and her someones have not met her yet. Loneliness fits the facts, since she knows no one (ER8). The belief about herself does not fit, because three weeks is no evidence. She wants connection, and that want is sound. With the belief gone, what is left is ordinary loneliness, which is lighter to carry."],["She accepts that the first weeks are lonely, and keeps showing up.",["IE16","DT13"],1,"Liking grows from being near, and being near takes weeks. So for a while she will be lonely and doing the right thing, both together. She accepts the lonely weeks as the cost of the method (DT13). They do not show that it is failing. The loneliness comes in waves (ER22). She brings it with her to the society and does not stay home because of it."],["She stays in her room.",["IE16","G4"],6,"The room is safe, and every evening in it avoids one awkward hour. It makes sense. But she can only come to like people she is near, and she is near no one. Loneliness lowers her mood, and low mood keeps her in. That is the loop where emotion and relationships feed each other (G8)."]],"e":[["She joins the society. The two people she talks to turn out to be old school friends, and after each meeting they leave together.",[["She keeps going, and talks to two others.",["IE16"],"The rule is to join a group when it is open. Those two are a closed pair, and that is a fact about them. The society has other people. She tries two more, with the same skills: ask, answer, say a little more than was asked. She keeps the method and tries it on different people."],["She joins one more thing that meets weekly.",["IE16","ER12"],"One society is one pool. The problem is too few chances, and the fix is more of them (ER12). She looks for groups that meet often, think alike, and work together at something, because working side by side gives people a reason to talk."],["She lets the loneliness be there while she keeps showing up.",["ER22","DT13"],"Watching two friends leave together sharpens the loneliness. It is a wave (ER22). She accepts that tonight was not the night (DT13). Next week she will be a slightly more familiar face to everyone there, and that counts for more than it seems."]]],["She reminds herself she is lovable. Then a whole weekend passes with no plans, and her phone shows everyone else's.",[["She invites someone for coffee.",["IE5","IE16"],"She has been waiting to be asked. The skill is to do the asking: a small, specific, easy invitation (IE5). \"Coffee after Tuesday's lecture?\" is easy to accept and easy to turn down. Most people in their first term are also waiting to be asked (IE16)."],["She plans one pleasant thing alone.",["ER16"],"An empty weekend with nothing in it gets filled by the phone. One pleasant event, chosen and done with her attention on it, adds something good that depends on no one else (ER16). It makes her less vulnerable to the loneliness, and that makes Monday's hello easier."],["She lets the sadness rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Other people's photos show their best hours and none of their empty ones. Her sadness is real all the same. It rises on Saturday night and falls by Sunday if she does not feed it by scrolling (ER22)."]]],["She keeps showing up for a month. People are friendly at the society, and still no one invites her to anything outside it.",[["She invites them instead.",["IE16","IE5"],"Friendly at the society is the ground she has been preparing. The next step is hers to offer: one invitation to one person, about something near the shared interest (IE5). Saying she likes someone's company, truly and not too often, is part of the skill (IE16)."],["She weakens \"never\" to \"not yet\".",["IE20","IE21"],"\"No one ever invites me\" claims every case and every time (IE21). The facts support something weaker: not yet, in four weeks, among people who were strangers in September (IE20). \"Not yet\" has time in it, and things change."],["She accepts it takes months.",["DT13","DT16"],"Trust is earned by giving people chances, and a month is only a few chances (IE17). She accepts the pace (DT13). Each lonely evening is a fork, and she turns again toward showing up (DT16)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE17","title":"mindfulness of others: what cannot be observed cannot be described","marks":[["⊭","does not fit; is not true in","r38d.html","38d · What Is True Everywhere Is Provable"]],"practice":[],"u":"No one has observed another's thoughts. What do you do with a guess about them, and why give the benefit of the doubt all the way?","tree":{"t":"Randy is sure his tutor dislikes him. What does he do?","o":[["He treats it as a hypothesis, asks for feedback in person, and watches what happens.",["IE17","ER7"],7,"No one has ever observed another person's thoughts, motives or feelings. So \"my tutor dislikes me\" is not something he has seen. It is a guess, and a guess about another person is a hypothesis to test. The test is to ask for feedback and watch what the tutor does, with interest and open to new facts (ER7). He does not question the tutor's motives. He collects observations. Finding what is actually so works better than blaming the tutor or himself."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt, all the way, and the worry eases.",["IE17"],1,"The benefit of the doubt is given all the way, with no reserve. Everything he has observed, short comments, no smile, a quick goodbye, fits a tutor who is busy, shy, or brisk with everyone. He lets those readings stand as fully as the dark one. The tutor is doing the best they can with thirty students. The worry was resting on a single reading, and it eases when the others are let in."],["He accepts that he cannot know what the tutor thinks, and does the work.",["IE17","DT13"],6,"He wants certainty about the tutor's opinion, and that can't be had by anyone, about anyone. He accepts not knowing (DT13). The tutor's thoughts have their own causes, most of which have nothing to do with him. He stops overfocusing on himself and comes back to the present, which is the essay. He does one thing at a time."],["He decides it is true, and avoids the tutor.",["IE17","G4"],6,"Deciding makes sense if cold faces once did mean dislike (G11). But avoiding the tutor changes what the tutor sees: a student who never comes to office hours. The tutor grows more distant, and the guess seems confirmed. Each side moves the other (G7). He has made the evidence for his own belief."]],"e":[["He asks for feedback in person. The tutor answers in three clipped sentences without looking up from the screen.",[["He treats it as one observation, and asks again next week.",["IE17","G15"],"A hypothesis is tested over several trials (G15). He writes down what he observed: three sentences, eyes on the screen. He does not write \"dislike\", because he did not observe that. One short meeting on a busy afternoon is one data point."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"This is the hard case for the skill. It is easy to be generous when the tutor smiles. A screen and three sentences fit a deadline, a bad day, or a habit. He does not hold the tutor to this one afternoon."],["He asks a classmate how the tutor is with them.",["IE16","ER7"],"This is a real check of the facts (ER7): is the tutor brisk with him or with everyone? A classmate's account is evidence from outside his own head. If everyone gets three sentences, the hypothesis is answered, and he has had a conversation with a classmate too (IE16)."]]],["He gives the benefit of the doubt. Then a friend's essay, which made much the same argument, comes back eight marks higher.",[["He asks the tutor to explain the difference.",["IE5"],"Now he has a specific, observable question, and it can be asked without accusing anyone (IE5): \"I'd like to understand what would have lifted mine to that level\". He is asking about the essays. He does not ask about the tutor's feelings, which he still can't observe."],["He compares the essays plainly, without judging.",["M4","M5"],"\"Much the same argument\" is his impression. He lays the two side by side and describes the differences (M4): structure, sources, how each handles the counter-case. He leaves out \"unfair\" and \"favourite\" (M5). Eight marks often have eight visible causes."],["He lets the doubt be.",["ER22","DT13"],"Too little trust and too much trust both fail, and one mark does not tell him which he has. The doubt is uncomfortable and it passes like a wave (ER22). He accepts that he does not know yet (DT13), and waits for more evidence."]]],["He does the work. The tutor hands it back and says, \"this is excellent. Best in the group\".",[["He notes it as data against his guess.",["IE17","G15"],"A hypothesis has to be open to evidence both ways. This counts against \"dislikes me\", and he records it (G15). The pull is to explain it away: \"he's just being nice\". That would be holding the tutor to the picture he had already made."],["He thanks the tutor.",["IE7"],"A plain thank-you, with an easy manner (IE7). It also rewards the tutor for giving warm feedback (IE25). If he had gone on avoiding the tutor, this conversation would not have happened."],["He lets himself enjoy it.",["ER16","M2"],"A pleasant event counts only if he attends to it (ER16). He keeps his attention on the praise, now, without clinging to it and without waving it away (M2). The worry about when it will be taken back is a thought about the future, and he lets it pass."]]]]}},{"id":"IE18","title":"ending a relationship: from wise mind, safely","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why decide in wise mind and never in emotion mind? Why negotiate how it ends, but not whether?","tree":{"t":"Evita's study partner copies her work and leaves her to take the blame. What does she do?","o":[["She decides in wise mind to end the partnership, plans what she will say, and tells the course leader.",["IE18","IE5"],4,"A relationship is destructive when it spoils the tie or the person in it. Being copied from and blamed does both. The rule is to decide in wise mind and never in emotion mind, so she waits until the anger has passed its peak (M3). Then she plans the ending: she scripts it, rehearses it, and prepares for the replies. She is direct, and she negotiates how it ends, not whether. She tells the course leader because the blame was put on her before the person who decides (IE14). The telling is DEAR MAN, with facts and dates (IE5)."],["She tells justified guilt from unjustified guilt, and lets go of the unjustified kind.",["IE18","ER11"],7,"She feels guilty about reporting a friend. The task is to tell justified guilt from unjustified. Guilt fits when her own act goes against her own values (ER8). Telling the truth about her own work goes against none of them, and staying silent would. So this guilt is unjustified, and it is answered by doing the thing anyway (ER11). If some part is justified, perhaps that she let it go on too long, that part is answered by repair."],["She stays in the partnership for now, and makes room for herself within it.",["IE18"],1,"The rule allows staying when leaving costs more than staying, as long as she makes enough room for herself within it. The joint project is due in two weeks, and leaving now would sink it for both of them. She stays until it is handed in, and makes room: her drafts are kept separate and dated, and the division of work is in writing. She has decided this in wise mind and set a limit on it."],["She keeps covering for the partner.",["IE18","G4"],6,"Covering keeps the peace and keeps a friend, and it makes sense for someone who puts the relationship first every time (IE4). Each time she covers, the copying is rewarded, so it will happen again (IE25). The blame stays on her record. A destructive tie spoils her, and this is how that happens."]],"e":[["She ends the partnership. Within days the partner is telling people that Evita was the one who copied.",[["She tells the course leader the facts, with dates.",["IE5","IE18"],"Planning the ending included preparing for the replies, and this is one of them (IE18). She has the dated drafts. She describes what happened and attacks nobody (IE5). She puts the facts in front of the person whose decision counts, and leaves the corridor to its own talk."],["She lets the hurt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Being lied about by someone she helped is a real injury, and the hurt and anger both fit (ER8). They are waves (ER22). At their peak the urge is to answer rumour with rumour, and that would make the story a fight between two equals."],["She tells her friends the truth briefly, and leaves it.",["IE10"],"FAST: fair, truthful, no excuses, no grovelling. She adds no attack on the partner either (IE10). Two sentences to the people who matter. No one can take her self-respect unless she gives it up, and a long campaign to clear her name would cost her some of it."]]],["She lets go of the unjustified guilt. Then the partner comes to her in tears, apologizes, and asks to work together again.",[["She decides in wise mind, not in the moment.",["IE18","M3"],"Tears pull hard toward emotion mind. The rule holds in this direction too: decide in wise mind (M3). She says she will answer tomorrow. The question for wise mind is the one from the footing: does it matter, is it not destructive, and could it improve (IE18)?"],["She sets clear terms if she says yes.",["IE5","IE12"],"If she tries to mend it, the mending needs conditions, stated plainly (IE5): separate files, credited sections, and the record corrected with the course leader. The checks support holding firm (IE12). An apology that will not meet clear terms tells her what she needs to know."],["She says no kindly.",["IE13","IE7"],"She can accept the apology and still refuse the partnership. The rule for endings is not to give in to pleas. She says the no high on the ladder, so that it holds (IE13), and gently, because the relationship counts for something even while it ends (IE7)."]]],["She stays for now, with her drafts kept separate. A week later a paragraph of hers appears in the partner's section, word for word.",[["She ends it now, with a plan.",["IE18"],"Staying was conditional on there being room for herself, and that room has been broken into. The weighing has changed: staying now costs more than leaving. She has already decided in wise mind what she would do if this happened. She scripts the ending, rehearses it, and is direct."],["She tells the course leader.",["IE5","IE14"],"This is no longer between the two of them. There is a rule about copying, and a person whose job is to apply it (IE14). She brings the dated drafts and describes what happened (IE5). Making room for herself turned out to mean keeping evidence, and she has it."],["She protects her work: separate files, dated drafts.",["ER12","DT24"],"Whatever she decides about the partnership, the copying is a problem with a practical fix (ER12). She removes shared access completely, and that burns the bridge by which it happened (DT24). She can do it today, before any conversation."]]]]}},{"id":"IE19","title":"dialectics: four claims about the world","marks":[["⇝","a path of links"],["Φ","the rule that takes one round of a transaction to the next"],["∋","has as a member","r03f.html","3f · Why Infinity Is Chosen, Not Discovered"]],"practice":[],"u":"How can opposites both be true? What does it mean that change is transactional?","tree":{"t":"Mike thinks he is either a maths person or not. What does he do?","o":[["He tracks his marks over a term to see change.",["IE19","G15"],3,"Dialectics makes four claims: there is always more than one side, everything is connected, change is the only constant, and change is transactional. \"A maths person or not\" denies the third. It treats ability as a fixed kind. A record over a term tests that (G15), because a line of marks can show movement and a single mark can't. Change is transactional too: his marks move with his practice, his teacher, and his sleep. He needs to try harder, and tracking shows him where the trying pays."],["He holds both: \"maths is hard for me, and I am getting better\".",["IE19"],7,"Opposites can both be true. \"It's hard for me\" and \"I'm improving\" pull against each other, and neither cancels the other. The either–or forced him to pick one: gifted or hopeless. The both–and keeps the difficulty whole and the progress whole (IE20). That is a synthesis and not a blend. Truth here is not fixed. It evolves, a quiz at a time. The label goes, and the dread of being found out as the wrong kind of person goes with it."],["He accepts where he is now as one point on a line.",["IE19","DT13"],1,"Today's level is a fact, and he accepts it without argument (DT13). It is also one point, and a point says nothing about the slope. Every part belongs to a whole: this week belongs to a term, and the term to years. He can tolerate being at this point because he no longer reads it as the whole line."],["He decides he is not a maths person, and drops the module.",["IE19","G4"],6,"The label makes sense as a shield: if he is the wrong kind of person, the struggle is not his fault. Someone may have handed him the label early (G11). But it is an overgeneralization from where he stands today (IE21), and dropping the module makes it permanent by ending the change. He has stopped at one point on the line and called it the whole line."]],"e":[["He tracks his marks. For two weeks running they drop, and the chart he drew to show progress points down.",[["He keeps tracking: change goes both ways.",["IE19","G15"],"Change is the only constant, and that includes dips. The chart was not made to flatter him. It was made to show what is so. Two points are a short run (G15), and a skill claims \"often\", not \"always\". He adds the next mark without deciding ahead what it will prove."],["He finds the cause: a harder topic.",["G13","ER7"],"A drop has causes, and \"I'm not a maths person after all\" is not one of them. He checks the facts (ER7): the two weeks were on a new topic, and everyone's marks fell. The key link is the topic (G13). The dip tells him about the syllabus."],["He asks for help with that topic.",["IE5"],"Change is transactional: his marks move with what happens between him and his teacher. One clear ask (IE5) changes that. A \"non-maths person\" would have no reason to ask. By asking he acts as if his ability can move, and it can."]]],["He holds both. Then a classmate who finds it easy says, \"honestly, some people just aren't maths people\".",[["He keeps both truths.",["IE19","IE20"],"The classmate has restated the either–or. Mike asks what is true in it (IE20): people do start from different places. And what is true on his own side: his line is rising. Both are held whole. He does not need the classmate to give up his view."],["He lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"It is a thought the classmate had, said aloud. Once it is in Mike's head it is a thought too, and no more a fact than before (DT19). He watches it and does not adopt it. The classmate's ease is the classmate's own starting point (G7)."],["He shows the classmate his progress chart, lightly.",["IE7","M4"],"A chart is a description: marks and dates (M4). Shown with an easy manner and no point-scoring (IE7), it is one case against \"just aren't\". One real case is enough to break a universal claim (IE3)."]]],["He accepts where he is. Then he fails a quiz outright, and the old sentence is back at once: \"see? Not a maths person\".",[["He accepts this point too, and plans the next.",["DT13","ER12"],"A failed quiz is a lower point on the same line. He accepts it as a fact (DT13). Then he plans (ER12): which questions, which gaps, what before the next one. Acceptance and planning go together here (∎)."],["He checks the facts: one quiz.",["ER7","IE21"],"The sentence takes one quiz and makes it a law. That is overgeneralization (IE21). The facts (ER7): one quiz failed, others passed, a term still running. He weakens the claim until it fits: \"I failed this one\"."],["He rides the wave of disappointment.",["ER22"],"The disappointment fits a failed quiz (ER8). It is a wave (ER22), and the old label rides in on it. He lets the feeling run its length without signing the label. By evening it has passed, and the line is still there to work on."]]]]}},{"id":"IE20","title":"acting dialectically: weaken the quantifier, keep both sides","marks":[["⊻","either one or the other, not both"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why weaken \"always\" to \"sometimes\"? Why does a synthesis keep both sides whole instead of blending them?","tree":{"t":"Zoe says \"I always freeze in exams\". What does she do?","o":[["She weakens it to \"sometimes\", and plans for the times she doesn't freeze.",["IE20","ER18"],3,"To act dialectically she changes \"always\" or \"never\" to \"sometimes\". \"Always\" left nothing to plan for, since the outcome was fixed. \"Sometimes\" has two cases in it, freezing and not freezing, and each can be prepared. She copes ahead (ER18): she rehearses the exam with a freeze in it and what she does next, and she rehearses the exam going well. The weaker claim is the truer one, and it is the one she can act on."],["She finds what is true on the other side: she has finished exams before.",["IE20"],7,"She asks what is true in the other side, and argues it with equal force. The other side: she has sat perhaps thirty exams and finished nearly all of them. That is a fact, and \"always\" had hidden it. One counter-case breaks a universal claim (IE3). She keeps what is true on her own side as well, because she has frozen, and it was awful. A synthesis keeps both sides whole. The dread eases because the claim under it was false."],["She accepts that she may freeze, and has a plan for it.",["IE20","DT13"],1,"She does not swap \"always\" for \"never again\". That would be the other extreme and just as false. She may freeze, and she accepts that possibility (DT13). The plan for the freeze is short: pen down, three slow breaths (DT6), then the easiest question. A freeze with a plan is a bad two minutes. Without one it is a lost exam."],["She keeps saying \"always\".",["IE20","G4"],6,"\"Always\" makes sense as armour. If she expects the worst, it can't surprise her. But the sentence works on her. Said before every exam, it raises the fear that causes the freeze, a thought feeding a body feeding an act (G9). It is a hopeless expectancy (IE21), and she has stopped checking it against the count."]],"e":[["She plans for not freezing. She turns over the paper, reads question one, and her mind goes white.",[["She uses her plan: skip to the question she knows.",["IE20","DT11"],"This is the \"sometimes\", and she rehearsed it. One thing in the moment (DT11): she finds a question she can start and starts it. The freeze is on question one. \"Sometimes\" applies inside an exam too (IE20): frozen on this question, not on every question."],["She does paced breathing.",["DT6"],"A freeze is a body state before it is a thought. Breathing out longer than in slows the heart within a minute (DT6). When her body comes down, the words on the page start to mean something again. The minute is not lost. Without it she would have lost twenty staring at the page."],["She lets the freeze be, and waits for it to pass.",["ER22","DT13"],"Fighting a freeze feeds it with panic about the freeze. She accepts that it is here (DT13). It is a wave, and it peaks and falls (ER22). She watches the clock for one minute without demanding anything of herself, and then she picks up the pen."]]],["She finds the other side. At dinner a parent says, fondly, \"oh, you always panic. You've been like that since you were six\".",[["She weakens it again: \"sometimes\".",["IE20"],"The \"always\" was not hers to begin with. She was given it. She does the same move aloud, lightly: \"sometimes I do. Last term I didn't\". She does not argue about her childhood. She weakens the quantifier until it fits the facts."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"The parent means it warmly, and what it says is that she is a fixed kind of person (G11). She tells herself what is valid (IE24): she has frozen, she has also finished, and she is working on it. Being described as she was at six does not bind her now."],["She tells the parent what helps her.",["IE5"],"The parent may not know that the sentence does harm. She says so plainly and kindly (IE5): \"it helps me more if you say 'you've got a plan'\". She gives one fact, one ask, and what is in it for them. Change is transactional (IE19), and the family's words are part of what she is changing."]]],["She has a plan for freezing. Then it happens in an oral exam, with two examiners watching, where there is no next question to skip to.",[["She says \"may I take a moment?\" and breathes.",["IE5","DT6"],"Her plan assumed a paper, so she adapts it. A skill includes changing it to fit (IE2). The pause that was private now has to be asked for, and it is a small, clear ask (IE5). Then the same three breaths (DT6). Examiners see this often and nearly always say yes."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"She can't hide in an oral exam, and she can still change her body in plain sight. A slight half-smile and hands open in her lap (DT18): the body tells the brain there is nothing to fight. It also reads as composure from across the table."],["She accepts the pause and answers what she can.",["DT13","DT11"],"The silence feels endless, and it is five seconds. She accepts it (DT13). She does not need the whole answer. She needs the first sentence of it, one thing in the moment (DT11). The first sentence usually brings the second."]]]]}},{"id":"IE21","title":"nondialectical thinking: eight claims stronger than the facts","marks":[["≫","is much greater than","r13chem.html","13·chem · How Fast Is a Chemical Change Going Right Now?"]],"practice":[],"u":"Each nondialectical pattern claims more than the facts allow. Take one and weaken it until it fits.","tree":{"t":"After a bad mark, a student thinks \"I'll never pass anything\". What does he do?","o":[["He weakens it to \"I failed this one; here is what I can change\".",["IE21","ER12"],3,"Eight thinking patterns each claim more than the facts allow, and the remedy for all of them is to weaken the claim until it fits. His sentence holds three of them. It overgeneralizes, from one mark to every mark. It is a hopeless expectancy, predicting from his fears and not from his record. And it catastrophizes, one thing gone wrong made into disaster. Weakened step by step: \"never\" becomes \"this time\", and \"anything\" becomes \"this one\". What is left is a true sentence and a problem with edges, and that can be solved (ER12)."],["He checks the facts: he has passed other modules.",["IE21","ER7"],7,"\"Never pass anything\" is a claim about every module, past and future. One counter-case breaks it (IE3), and he has several: the modules he passed last term. The check also asks where the claim came from. It was inferred from one mark, and that is too little evidence. The first pattern on the list is arbitrary inference. The despair was built on the claim. Remove the claim, and what is left is disappointment about one mark, which fits (ER8)."],["He accepts the bad mark as a fact, and leaves \"never\" as a thought.",["IE21","DT19"],1,"Two things arrived together, and he sorts them. The mark is a fact, and facts are accepted (DT13). \"I'll never pass anything\" is a thought, and thoughts are watched (DT19). He does not argue with it. He lets it be a sentence passing through. The pain of the mark stays. The suffering that \"never\" was adding does not have to."],["He believes it and stops studying.",["IE21","G4"],6,"If it were true, stopping would be sensible, and that is the trap. The claim makes itself true, because nobody who stops studying passes. This is the far end of the first line, his own reaction turned into a verdict on himself (G10). The thought has become an act, and the act produces the evidence."]],"e":[["He weakens it and makes changes. The next mark is bad too, and the thought returns: \"told you. Never\".",[["He weakens it again: \"I failed two; here is what I can change\".",["IE21","ER12"],"Two is more than one, and it is still not \"never\". The method does not change with the count: he states exactly what the facts allow. Two failures on the same kind of task point at a cause they share, and the problem-solving loop returns to its first step with that in hand (ER12)."],["He asks for feedback on both.",["IE5"],"His own changes did not work, so he needs information he does not have. He asks one specific thing (IE5): \"what do these two have in common?\" It goes against the pattern of putting all the blame on himself, since a marker may see a cause he can't."],["He lets the disappointment be.",["ER22"],"Trying and failing again hurts more than the first time. The disappointment fits and is a wave (ER22). He does not have to weaken a feeling. Only the claim was too strong. He lets the feeling run its length, and the planning can wait until tomorrow."]]],["He checks the facts, and they are clear. The feeling of \"never\" sits in his chest regardless.",[["He notes it as a thought, not a fact.",["DT19","IE21"],"He has answered the claim, and the feeling has not caught up with the answer. That is common. He does not go back to arguing. He names what is there: the thought \"never\", and a weight in his chest (DT19). A thought can go on visiting after it has been shown to be false."],["He does one study session anyway.",["ER10"],"The feeling's urge is to stop. Checked against the facts, the hopelessness does not fit, so he acts opposite to it (ER10): one session, done fully. Action changes a feeling where argument did not. An hour of real work is also evidence against \"never\"."],["He talks to someone who knows his record.",["IE16","IE22"],"A friend who has seen his other marks can validate what is valid, that this mark was bad and that it hurts, without agreeing to \"never\" (IE22). Hearing the facts in another voice sometimes reaches where his own checking did not."]]],["He accepts the bad mark. Then he reads the scholarship terms: one more mark like it, and the funding goes.",[["He asks the scholarship office what the rules are.",["IE5","ER7"],"Now there is a real threat, and catastrophizing is close: one thing wrong, therefore disaster. He checks the facts at the source (ER7): what average, over which modules, with what appeal. He asks clearly (IE5). The real rule is nearly always more specific than the feared one."],["He plans the average he needs, module by module.",["ER12","ER18"],"With the rule in hand it becomes arithmetic: this mark here, that mark there (ER12). Each module becomes a hard-but-possible step (ER18). The fear fits, since the threat is real, and a fear that fits is answered by acting on it (ER13)."],["He accepts the risk, and works.",["DT13","DT16"],"He can't make the risk zero, and he accepts that (DT13). \"I'll lose it\" is a prediction, and each time it comes back he turns the mind to the page in front of him (DT16). He works with the risk there beside him."]]]]}},{"id":"IE22","title":"validation: understandable for every response, valid for some","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Every response makes sense given its causes, but only some fit the facts. Why validate only the valid, and why is validating not agreeing?","tree":{"t":"A friend is furious about a fair grade. What do you do?","o":[["You validate the frustration, and help her plan the next essay.",["IE22"],3,"Every response makes sense, given its causes, and only some responses fit the facts. Her frustration is understandable: she worked hard and hoped for more. Her claim that the grade is wrong does not fit the facts. You validate only the valid. So you validate the frustration and the effort, and you say nothing false about the grade. Validating lowers the push to prove who is right. With that push lower she can look ahead, and you help her with the next essay."],["You say how her anger makes sense given how hard she worked.",["IE22"],6,"This is validation at the fourth level: saying how it makes sense, given its causes (IE23). Weeks of work, high hopes, and a number below them. Anger follows from that as an effect follows a cause. Validating is not agreeing, and it is not liking. You have not said the grade is unfair. You have said her anger has a history. Heard, she is less angry, because part of the anger was the effort of making someone understand."],["You let her vent without agreeing that the grade was wrong.",["IE22"],1,"The first level of validation is paying attention (IE23), and sometimes it is all that is wanted. You listen and you do not fix. You tolerate two things: her anger in the room, and your own silence on the question she wants settled. Validating does not make the invalid valid, so you add nothing to the claim. You wait with her while it passes."],["You agree the grade was unfair to keep the peace.",["IE22","G4"],6,"It is the easy road, and it feels like kindness. But validating also reinforces, which is why only the valid is validated. Agreeing strengthens a belief that will send her to the tutor with a case that fails. It is also a lie told out of habit, and that wears your own self-respect (IE10)."]],"e":[["You validate her frustration. She seizes on it: \"so you agree it's unfair\".",[["You say kindly, \"I get why you're upset, and I think the mark is fair\".",["IE22","IE10"],"She has taken validation for agreement, and the two have to be separated aloud. You say both halves, joined by \"and\". FAST keeps you truthful (IE10). It may cost a moment of warmth. A friend whose agreement can be had by being upset at them is not much use to her."],["You ask what she wants to do next.",["IE23","IE5"],"You do not rule on \"unfair\". You treat her as an equal who can decide her own next step, and that is the sixth level of validation (IE23). One open question (IE5). It moves the talk from who is right to what she wants, and that is where validation was meant to lead."],["You let her be upset with you for a bit.",["DT13","IE7"],"She may be cross that you won't sign on. You accept that (DT13) and stay gentle (IE7). You do not chase her agreement, and you do not withdraw. Her being upset with you for an hour is a small cost for having told the truth."]]],["You say her anger makes sense. She gets louder: \"don't tell me it makes sense! You sound like a counsellor\".",[["You stop talking and listen.",["IE23"],"You offered level four when she needed level one. If you can't find the right words, pay attention is always available (IE23). You stop explaining her to herself. Silence with your eyes on her says more than a well-formed sentence."],["You reflect back exactly what she said.",["IE23","IE22"],"Level two: you say back her own words, and let her correct you. \"You worked for three weeks and got a 58.\" You add no interpretation and no \"makes sense\". You validate the facts and the experience (IE22). She will tell you if you have it wrong, and that is part of the level."],["You suggest coffee later.",["IE7","DT4"],"She is past the point where talk helps, and you may be near it too. You stop (DT4) and offer something easy and warm for later (IE7). It tells her you are not leaving, and that this conversation will go better in an hour."]]],["You let her vent. Then she says, \"come with me to the tutor. If two of us complain he'll have to change it\".",[["You say no kindly.",["IE13","IE10"],"This would be acting as if the claim were valid. Level five is for what fits the facts, and the claim does not (IE23). You refuse clearly enough that it holds (IE13), and truthfully (IE10): \"I can't say it's unfair, because I don't think it is\". You are saying no to the errand and not to her."],["You offer to help her write her own email.",["IE5","IE8"],"There is a valid thing under the request: she wants to understand her mark. You can act on that. A DEAR MAN email asking what would have earned more (IE5) is hers to send. You hold the helper's two sides, with her and honest with her (IE8)."],["You check the facts with her first.",["ER7","IE22"],"Before anyone goes to the tutor, the two of you look at the rubric and the comments together (ER7). If the mark fits them, she sees it for herself, and that is better than hearing it from you. If something really is off, then part of her claim is valid and you can back that part (IE22)."]]]]}},{"id":"IE23","title":"six levels of validation","marks":[["≅","stands level with: alike in every way that matters here","r20d.html","20d · All Three Keep Distances"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can words alone invalidate at level five? Why is level four an insult where level five holds?","tree":{"t":"A classmate says she is overwhelmed by her coursework. What do you do?","o":[["You act on it: you offer to work on the problem set together.",["IE23"],3,"Validation has six levels: pay attention, reflect back, read what is not said, say how it makes sense, act as if it is valid, and treat the person as an equal. Her being overwhelmed fits the facts, since the workload is heavy. At level five you act on what is valid, and here words alone can invalidate. \"That sounds hard\", said to someone who is going under, is level four offered where level five holds. So you offer a concrete act. You offer it, and you do not impose it, and that is level six."],["You reflect back what she said, and let her correct you.",["IE23"],7,"Level two: say back what you heard, and let her correct you. \"So it's the three deadlines in one week?\" If she says \"no, it's that I don't understand any of it\", you have learned what the real problem is. Reflecting is asking, and it works better than guessing (IE17). She feels better because someone has got it right, and getting it right took her correction."],["You pay attention and listen without fixing.",["IE23"],1,"Level one is attention, and it is harder than it sounds. You put the phone down and do not plan your reply while she talks. You tolerate not fixing it. The urge to solve is partly your own discomfort at seeing her distress. Listening treats what she says as worth hearing in full."],["You tell her everyone is busy.",["IE23","G4"],6,"It is meant as comfort: you are not alone. What it tells her is that her feeling is ordinary and so not worth attention. That is one of the worlds that do not validate, where particular feelings are called unwarranted (G11). She will stop telling you things. It usually comes from your own tiredness, and it makes sense on those grounds."]],"e":[["You offer to work on the problem set together. She shakes her head: \"no, I don't need help, I just want to talk\".",[["You listen.",["IE23"],"You chose level five, and she has told you which level she wants. Level one is no step down. It is what fits her now. You put your offer away and give her your attention. Letting her set the level is treating her as an equal."],["You reflect back what she says.",["IE23","IE22"],"\"Just want to talk\" means she wants to be heard accurately. You say back what you hear, in her words, and let her correct it. You are validating the experience (IE22). The problem set is still there, and it is not what she needs from you tonight."],["You drop the plan, and accept her choice.",["DT13","IE7"],"Your offer was a good one, and she said no. You accept that (DT13) without a trace of \"well, I tried\". An easy manner (IE7) shows her the no cost her nothing with you. The help can be offered again another day."]]],["You reflect back, \"so you're behind on the reading?\" She frowns: \"no. That's not it at all\".",[["You let her correct you, and try again.",["IE23"],"This is level two working as designed: reflect back, and let them correct you. A wrong reflection that gets corrected tells you more than a nod would have. You say, \"okay, tell me what it is\", and then you try again with her words."],["You ask a question instead.",["IE23","IE7"],"If you can't understand, say so, and keep asking. One open, gentle question (IE7): \"what's the worst part of it?\" Asking acts interested, and it replaces guessing. No one can observe another person's experience (IE17), so you ask."],["You don't judge yourself for missing.",["M5"],"A missed reflection is not a failure of friendship. It is one try. If you start to judge it, your attention goes to yourself and leaves her (M5). You note it and return to what she is saying."]]],["You listen. Halfway through a sentence her voice breaks and she starts to cry.",[["You stay, and say \"this is a lot\".",["IE23","IE22"],"Level three is reading what is not said. The tears say more than the words did. You name it in a few plain words and stay where you are. You are validating the suffering (IE22). You do not tell her it will be fine, since you do not know that."],["You offer a tissue and some water.",["IE23","IE7"],"Sometimes only an act validates (IE7). A tissue and a glass of water are level five on a small scale: you act as if her distress is real and deserves care. It is gentle, and it asks nothing of her."],["You let the silence be.",["ER22","DT13"],"Crying is a wave, and it does not need to be talked down (ER22). You accept the silence and your own urge to fill it (DT13). You stay beside her without hurrying her, and that is the first level again: you are paying attention."]]]]}},{"id":"IE24","title":"recovering from invalidation","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When does invalidation help? Why validate yourself exactly as you would someone else?","tree":{"t":"Terry's teacher called his project lazy in front of the class. What does he do?","o":[["He asks the teacher privately which parts fell short, and fixes them.",["IE24","IE5"],4,"Invalidation helps when it corrects a real mistake. It hurts when a person is ignored, misread, or disbelieved. \"Lazy\" is a misreading of him, and it may sit on top of a real point about the project. He separates the two by asking, in private and plainly (IE5): which parts fell short? Whatever comes back as specific is feedback he can use. The word \"lazy\" is not, and he leaves it with the teacher. He did not choose to be shamed in class, and he can still take from it whatever helps."],["He validates himself, exactly as he would a friend.",["IE24"],7,"The instruction is exact: validate yourself exactly as you would someone else. If a friend had spent two weekends on a project and heard it called lazy before thirty people, Terry would not say \"well, maybe you are\". He would say, \"you worked on that, and that was a harsh thing to hear\". He says it to himself. The more the invalidator matters to him, the more it hurts, and that is why it hurts so much from a teacher. All behaviour is caused, the teacher's included, and he has been doing the best he can."],["He accepts that it happened and talks about it where he is supported.",["IE24","DT13"],1,"He can't make it unsaid. The steps for recovery are to describe it where he is supported, and to accept himself radically, and the invalidator too. He accepts that the teacher said it and the class heard (DT13). Then he tells a friend who will validate what is valid. He checks the facts with them: was the project lazy? Telling it is the opposite of carrying it alone, and carrying it alone is what a world like that teaches (G11)."],["He decides he is lazy, and stops trying.",["IE24","G4"],6,"A teacher's word carries weight, so taking it on makes sense. This is the limit the series runs to (G7): he gives up his own reading and adopts the other's. It is self-invalidation, the taught end of the first line (G10). When he stops trying, the word becomes true after the fact."]],"e":[["He asks privately. The teacher says, \"I meant what I said. It was lazy work, and frankly I expected better from you\".",[["He thanks the teacher for the specific parts, and leaves.",["IE24","IE10"],"There may be one usable thing in it, \"expected better\" of which sections, and he takes that. He does not argue the word again, and he does not grovel (IE10). He thanks the teacher truthfully for the part that helps and goes. No one can take his self-respect unless he gives it up."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"The second time hurts more, because he asked in good faith. He does again what a friend would do: \"you went and asked. That took nerve, and the answer was harsh\". The teacher is doing the best they can, and may know no other way to push a student (G11). That explains it, and he still does not have to agree."],["He tells someone he trusts.",["IE16","IE22"],"Check the facts with someone who will validate the valid (IE22). He shows a friend the project and tells them what was said. He wants an outside reading and not only comfort. Twice from one voice is still one source."]]],["He validates himself. Then the mark comes back, and it really is low, and so was the one before.",[["He fixes the parts that were true.",["IE24","ER12"],"Self-validation was for what is valid: his effort, and the harshness of the word. It was never a claim that the work was good. Invalidation helps when it corrects a real mistake. Two low marks point at one, and he goes to work on it (ER12). He keeps \"not lazy\" and adds \"not yet good enough\"."],["He asks for help with one part.",["IE5"],"If the effort was real and the result is low, the missing piece is method, and someone has to show him that. He asks one clear question about one part (IE5). Asking goes against the idea that he should cope alone (G11)."],["He accepts both: he is doing his best, and he needs to do better.",["G6","DT13"],"The teacher said the second half and denied the first. He was tempted to say the first and deny the second. The assumptions hold both (G6). He accepts the marks as facts (DT13). Both halves are true together, and each is needed."]]],["He accepts it. Then two classmates start greeting him with \"hey, lazy\" in the corridor, grinning.",[["He tells them plainly to stop.",["IE10","IE5"],"Acceptance was for what the teacher said, which was over. This is new, and it is still going on. One fair sentence, with no apology and no heat (IE10): \"I don't like that. Stop\". He is asking for one thing (IE5). When someone is being unkind he can set gentleness aside."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"It is a word, borrowed from a teacher, in the mouths of two boys who want a reaction. He watches it go by like any thought (DT19). A tease that gets no reaction is not rewarded, and it tends to die out after a burst (IE26)."],["He asks the teacher for a word about it.",["IE14","IE5"],"The word began with the teacher, and the classroom is the teacher's to manage (IE14). He describes what is happening and asks for one thing (IE5). It may also show the teacher what a public word turns into, which is more than his private question managed."]]]]}},{"id":"IE25","title":"raising a behaviour: reinforce, shape, time it","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is behaviour rewarded only some of the time very hard to stop? Why does a reward not show the behaviour was done to get it?","tree":{"t":"Claire wants her younger brother to do his homework without a fight. What does she do?","o":[["She shapes it: praise for sitting down, then for ten minutes, then for the whole sheet.",["IE25"],5,"A reinforcer is any consequence that makes a behaviour more frequent. Shaping rewards small steps and then asks for a little more. \"Do your homework\" is too big a first step for him. Sitting at the table is one he will take. She rewards that right away, every time at first. When it is steady she asks for ten minutes before the praise, and then for the whole sheet. Knowing the right thing and doing it are both learned, and he learns this at the kitchen table. The reward has to be one that works on him (IE27)."],["She sees that his stalling is caused, not spite, and her anger drops.",["IE25","G6"],6,"She has been reading the stalling as defiance aimed at her. A behaviour that keeps happening is being rewarded by something, and that does not mean he does it in order to get the reward. She looks at what follows his stalling: a fight, and in the fight he has her whole attention and no homework. People reward others' behaviour without knowing it. Every behaviour is caused (G6). Her anger was at spite, and what she finds is a pattern the two of them built together (G7)."],["She accepts that it will take weeks.",["IE25","DT13"],1,"Shaping is slow by design: small steps, each made steady before the next. She accepts the timescale (DT13). She does not approve of the fights. Tonight he may only sit down, and that is tonight's whole success. A helper is unwavering as well as flexible (IE8). She tolerates the slowness so that she does not go back to shouting on a bad evening."],["She shouts at him every night.",["IE25","G4"],6,"Shouting sometimes works, and so it has been rewarded in her, some of the time, which makes it very hard to stop. It makes sense, because she is tired and it is quick. Punishment works only while the punisher is near, and it teaches him to avoid her (IE26). The homework becomes one more thing about her that he wants to get away from."]],"e":[["She praises him for sitting down. He sits, grins, folds his arms, and does not open the book.",[["She praises the next small step: opening the book.",["IE25"],"Sitting is learned, so the reward moves on one step. That is how shaping works: reward small steps, then ask for a little more. She waits for the smallest move toward the book and rewards that. His grin says he has noticed the game. A consequence works whether or not he knows it is shaping him."],["She waits a minute, then tries again.",["DT13","IE8"],"He is testing whether sitting is all it takes. She accepts the standoff for a minute (DT13) and does not fill it with nagging, which would give him attention for stalling. She is steady and she still expects the book to open (IE8). Then she makes one calm prompt."],["She asks what makes it hard.",["IE23","IE5"],"Folded arms may mean \"won't\", and they may mean \"can't\". She asks, and listens to the answer (IE23). If the sheet is beyond him, no praise will open the book, and the first step has to be a different one. She asks one plain question, and she is not accusing him (IE5)."]]],["Her anger drops. But when she reminds him, gently, he shouts, \"you're not my mum!\" and slams the table.",[["She stays calm and repeats the ask once.",["IE5","IE13"],"The shout is an attack, and answering it would hand him the fight he is used to (IE5). She stays on the point at the same height (IE13): \"I know. The sheet still needs doing\". She says it once. If she repeated it five times it would turn into the nagging that fed the pattern."],["She lets him calm down and tries later.",["DT4","IE26"],"Nothing is learned at the top of a shout (ER23). She stops and steps back (DT4). Leaving the room also takes away the audience, so the outburst pays him nothing (IE26). She comes back when he is quiet, so that the quiet is what gets her attention."],["She lets her own anger rise and fall.",["ER22"],"\"You're not my mum\" is meant to sting, and it does. Her anger rises on cue. It is a wave (ER22). If she acts on it, they are back in the nightly fight, which is the reward he knows. She lets it pass before she says anything."]]],["She accepts it will take weeks. Then she finds their mother, worn out, quietly filling in his worksheet for him to keep the peace.",[["She talks to the parent about the plan.",["IE5","IE14"],"The homework rule belongs to their mother and not to Claire (IE14). She describes what she has been doing and why, and asks for one thing (IE5). Their mother is doing the best she can at the end of a long day (G6), and may not see that stalling has just been paid in full."],["She keeps shaping when it is her turn.",["IE25","DT16"],"She can't control every evening. What is learned in one situation may not carry to another (IE27), so he may work for her and stall for their mother. She keeps going on her own nights and makes that choice again each time (DT16). A partial plan still builds something."],["She accepts what she can't control.",["DT13"],"Their mother's evenings are their mother's. Claire accepts that the plan will be undone some nights (DT13). She does not approve of it and she does not hold it against her mother. Resenting it would cost her the patience the plan depends on."]]]]}},{"id":"IE26","title":"lowering a behaviour: extinguish, satiate, punish with care","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does extinction bring a burst before the fall? Why must the alternative always be rewarded?","tree":{"t":"Jean's housemate plays music loudly the night before exams. What does she do?","o":[["She asks once, then stops rewarding the noise with attention, and thanks him when it is quiet.",["IE26","IE5"],4,"There are three ways to lower a behaviour: extinction, satiation, and punishment. None of them teaches a new behaviour, so the alternative always has to be rewarded as well. First she looks at what the music gets him. Each night she bangs on the wall, he shouts back, and they have a scene. That attention may be the reward. So she asks once, clearly (IE5), and tells him what she is doing, so that it is not arbitrary. After that the banging stops, and that is extinction. She thanks him for the quiet, and that rewards the alternative (IE25)."],["She sees the burst as a sign that it is working.",["IE26"],7,"When a reward stops, the behaviour bursts and then falls. If the music gets louder on the second night, that tells her she withheld the right reward. If no burst comes, the wrong reward may have been withheld. Knowing this changes what the loud night means to her. Before, it would have been \"he's doing it to spite me\". Now it is the expected middle of the curve. The cause of the burst is in how learning works, and blaming him would not help."],["She uses earplugs this week, and accepts the noise.",["IE26","DT13"],1,"Extinction takes days, and her exam is tomorrow. She weighs the aims (IE4): sleep tonight matters most, and a campaign can wait. She accepts the noise for this week (DT13), and earplugs solve the part she can solve. Doing what works means dealing with the night she has (M5)."],["She gives in after a few refusals.",["IE26","G4"],6,"Three nights of holding firm and then one night of banging on the wall. It makes sense, because she is tired and it is late. But giving in after a few refusals puts the behaviour on reward some of the time, and behaviour rewarded only some of the time is very hard to stop (IE25). She has taught the music that it only has to outlast her."]],"e":[["She stops reacting. The next night the music is louder, and he adds the bass.",[["She holds steady: the burst comes before the fall.",["IE26"],"This is the burst, and it comes on schedule. The reward was withdrawn, and the behaviour is pushing to get it back. It is the most dangerous moment, because a reaction now would reward the louder version. She holds firm and stays civil. The rule is to soothe while she holds firm."],["She uses earplugs through the burst.",["ER12","DT13"],"Holding steady does not mean she has to suffer through it. Earplugs solve her side of the problem (ER12), and they let her not react without gritting her teeth. She accepts that tonight is loud (DT13). The plan is working, and tonight is the price."],["She thanks him the first quiet minute.",["IE25","IE26"],"Extinction alone leaves an empty space, and something will fill it. She fills it herself. The moment the music is down she is friendly, right away (IE25). The alternative has to pay better than the noise did."]]],["She sees the burst as the plan working. But it goes on for a week, and her second exam is two days off.",[["She keeps going.",["IE26","DT16"],"A long burst means the behaviour was well rewarded for a long time. Stopping now would teach him that a week is what it takes. Each loud night is a fork, and she turns the same way again (DT16). The fall comes after the burst. It does not come during it."],["She asks once more, clearly.",["IE5"],"Extinction works on attention, and a clear ask can go alongside it. She makes it in daylight and without heat: the facts, the exam date, and one request, with what is in it for him (IE5). He may not know about the exams. She has been assuming that he knows and does not care (IE17)."],["She studies in the library this week.",["ER12"],"The plan for the housemate and the plan for the exam are two separate problems (ER12). She solves the urgent one by moving to another place. It also takes away his audience, which helps the extinction along."]]],["She uses earplugs. The bass comes through the floor regardless, and at midnight she is still awake.",[["She asks him to turn it down after 10.",["IE5"],"Acceptance was for what she could not change tonight, and she has not yet asked. The ask is specific and limited to a time (IE5): after ten, on exam nights. A small, clear request is easy to grant, and many people simply have not thought about it."],["She moves to another room.",["ER12"],"The problem is sleep, and the bedroom is one place to get it (ER12). The sofa at the far end of the house is another. It is a poor night, and it is better than no night. She solves it for tonight and leaves the larger question for after the exam."],["She accepts it for tonight.",["DT13","DT11"],"At midnight the aim is rest, not sleep (ER21). She accepts the bass as tonight's weather (DT13) and keeps to this one moment: she is lying down, and resting (DT11). Raging at the ceiling would keep her awake after the music stops."]]]]}},{"id":"IE27","title":"the consequence is relative","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is a consequence relative? Why do natural consequences work best?","tree":{"t":"Gold stars move one student and embarrass another. What does the teacher do?","o":[["She asks each student what they would work for, and uses that.",["IE27"],4,"One person's poison is another's passion. A consequence is a reward only if it makes the behaviour more frequent for that person (IE25). The star does that for one student, and for the other it works as a punishment. The method is to watch what works, or to ask what they would work to get or to avoid. So she asks each of them, privately. Context counts as well: the same star given quietly might be welcome. Natural consequences work best, so she looks first for what the work itself can give."],["She stops blaming the second student for not caring.",["IE27"],7,"She had concluded that he does not care. The footing gives another cause: for him the star was never a reward. A public star in front of classmates can cost a twelve-year-old more than it gives. So he does care, and what he cares about is not being singled out. Looking for what a consequence means to this particular person works better than judging him by what it means to someone else. Her irritation eases, and so does the pressure on him, which was part of the problem (G7)."],["She accepts that no reward works for everyone.",["IE27","DT13"],1,"She wanted one system for thirty children, because one system is manageable. Rewards do not work that way, and she accepts it (DT13). Accepting it does not mean giving up on rewards. It means some children will need something different, and she will not always find it quickly. She holds a helper's two sides: this needs to change, and this is how he is (IE8)."],["She keeps the stars for everyone.",["IE27","G4"],6,"It makes sense: the stars are simple, and they work for most. For the second student each star is a small punishment for doing well. Punishment teaches avoiding the punisher (IE26), so he learns to do a little less. She is treating his response as wrong when it is only different (G11)."]],"e":[["She asks each student. One of them shrugs, looks at the floor, and says, \"nothing\".",[["She watches what that student does choose, and uses that.",["IE27"],"The method has two halves: ask, or watch. Asking failed, so she watches. What does he do when nobody is directing him? If he draws, or helps the caretaker, or reads about engines, those are his rewards. Whatever a person does freely and often will work as a reward for the things he does less."],["She validates that school feels pointless to them.",["IE22"],"\"Nothing\" may be true. For a child who has had little success at school, nothing here pays. That is understandable given its causes, and she says so (IE22). She does not agree that school is pointless. She is acknowledging that from where he sits it has been."],["She tries a natural consequence.",["IE27","IE25"],"An arbitrary reward is only for when no safe natural one exists. She looks for work whose result pays him directly: a model that runs, or a letter that gets an answer. She shapes it in small steps (IE25). The reward is then part of the task."]]],["She stops blaming him. But the embarrassed student still does the bare minimum, and a colleague says, \"he's just coasting. Don't indulge it\".",[["She asks privately what would help.",["IE5","IE23"],"She has dropped the blame, and she has not yet asked. She asks in private, since public attention is what he avoids, and with one plain question (IE5). Then she listens for what he does not say (IE23). \"Coasting\" is her colleague's guess about a motive (IE17)."],["She uses a private reward instead.",["IE27"],"Context and amount count. The same recognition, given as a quiet word or a note in his book, takes away what made the star a punishment. She tries it and watches whether the behaviour increases. That test is the only thing that shows whether something is a reward."],["She accepts it may take time.",["DT13","IE8"],"Removing a punishment does not at once build what it was holding down. He learned to stay small, and he will unlearn it slowly. She accepts the pace (DT13). She stays steady and keeps asking a little of him (IE8), and does not catch her colleague's impatience."]]],["She accepts that no reward works for all. Then the head teacher announces that every class will use the same star chart, on the wall.",[["She asks to use stars privately for some students.",["IE5","IE4"],"She weighs the aims (IE4): the result for two or three children, her standing with the head, and her own integrity as a teacher. Then one clear ask (IE5), with what is in it for the school: the same system, with a quieter form for the few it embarrasses. She offers a small exception and does not challenge the policy."],["She follows the rule, and adds private praise.",["IE14","IE25"],"The rule is the head's to set (IE14). When she has little power, she plays by the rules (M5). Inside them she adds what works: a quiet word, right away, for the students the wall does not reach (IE25). Following the rule and looking after those students are both possible."],["She accepts the rule.",["DT13"],"She asked, or decided not to, and the chart is on the wall. She accepts it (DT13). She does not agree with it, and she keeps watching what it does to each child. Things change (IE19), and a year of her observations will make a better case than an argument today."]]]]}}]},{"id":"ER","page":"dbter","name":"regulating emotion","footings":25,"steps":[{"id":"ER1","title":"goals: name it, fewer unwanted, less vulnerable, less suffering","marks":[["🕳","vulnerability"],["ŝ","an emotion as a signal to oneself"]],"practice":["pros and cons of changing e ,  and of not changing it :\nchecked true or false :   𝔢 serves ☉ ;   ¬reg makes a new problem\nchecked true or false :   e ↓ now  ⇒  freedom ↑ ;   clinging(e) useful ;   cost(reg) too high"],"u":"How is regulating like a thermostat? Why is the aim less suffering, and not no emotion?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's nerves before a music audition are high, but useful. What does she do?","o":[["She uses the energy: one more run-through of the hard passage, then warm-ups.",["ER1","ER2"],3,"Regulating is like a thermostat: it raises the strength of an emotion when that is needed and lowers it when that is needed. The rule is to regulate only an emotion that is not working for her, and these nerves are working. An emotion readies her to act, fast, and a strong one helps her get past obstacles (ER2). Fear before a performance brings sharpness and energy. She spends that on the passage that needs it. The aim is less suffering. It was never to have no emotion."],["She turns the nerves down a notch with slow breathing, like a thermostat, until they help and no longer shake her.",["ER1","DT6"],6,"A thermostat does not switch the heating off. It sets a level. Her nerves are useful at seven out of ten and they shake her hands at nine. Breathing out longer than in lowers the body's arousal directly (DT6), and the body is one part of the emotion, so the whole comes down with it (ER5). She stops at seven, where the nerves still help. Suppressing them would bring relief now and worse trouble later, so she adjusts them and goes no further."],["She lets the nerves be there, and goes on stage with them.",["ER1","ER22"],1,"An emotion in itself is neither good nor bad. She does not have to raise the nerves, lower them, or use them. She can have them. They rise and fall like a wave when she neither blocks nor feeds them (ER22), and the audition happens on top of the wave. The fear fits, because something she cares about is at stake (ER8). She accepts that she is a nervous performer today and goes on."],["She convinces herself she will fail, and withdraws.",["ER1","G4"],6,"She has read the nerves as a forecast. An emotion is not a fact about the world (ER2). It makes sense if she believes the myth that feeling nervous means she is not ready (ER4). Withdrawing brings relief at once, and the relief rewards the withdrawing (IE25). The nerves were ready to help her, and she has used them as a reason to leave."]],"e":[["She uses the energy for a run-through. The energy tips over: her hands start to shake on the hard passage, and the next candidate is watching from the door.",[["She turns it down a notch: out-breath longer than in-breath.",["ER1","DT6"],"The thermostat works in both directions, and now it needs to come down. The emotion that was working for her has passed the level where it helps. Three or four slow breaths, out longer than in (DT6), bring her arousal back into the useful range. She changes the setting and keeps the plan."],["She tenses and releases her hands on \"relax\".",["DT7"],"Shaking is muscle tension with nowhere to go. Paired relaxation gives it somewhere to go: she tenses her hands hard and then lets go on one word (DT7). She practised the word in quiet rooms so that it would work in this one. From the door it looks like a pianist stretching."],["She lets the shaking be, and plays anyway.",["ER22","DT13"],"Fighting the shake adds fear of the shake. She accepts that her hands are shaking (DT13) and plays the passage with them as they are. The shaking is the top of a wave and it settles as she plays (ER22). Nobody watching hears it the way she feels it."]]],["She turns the nerves down. She overshoots: now she feels flat and slow, and the examiner calls her name.",[["She turns them back up a little: a brisk walk before she goes in.",["ER1","DT6"],"Regulating means raising an emotion when it is needed, and she needs it back. A minute of brisk movement raises the body's arousal (DT6). It is the same lever as before, pushed the other way. She is looking for the level that plays well, and calm was too low for that."],["She notices the flatness without judging it.",["M4","M5"],"\"I've ruined it\" is a judgment. \"I feel slow and heavy\" is an observation (M4). Flatness is a state, and states move. If she scolded herself she would be adding a second emotion on top of the first (ER5). She notes it and walks in."],["She rehearses the opening in her mind, from inside.",["ER18"],"In the thirty seconds she has, she copes ahead: she imagines the first phrase as she will play it, with the feeling it needs (ER18). Imagining the music with its emotion brings some of that emotion back. A reading of an event changes the body, and so does a picture of one (ER5)."]]],["She goes on with the nerves. Her first note cracks, and one of the panel looks down and writes something.",[["She keeps playing, one bar at a time.",["DT11","M5"],"The cracked note is in the past, and thoughts about it are happening now (M5). One thing in the moment: this bar, then the next (DT11). What works in an audition is carrying on, because panels listen for how a player recovers."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall while she plays.",["ER22"],"The crack sends a jolt of fear through her, and its urge is to stop or to rush. It is a wave on top of the wave she came in with (ER22). She plays through it. By the eighth bar it has fallen."],["She half-smiles at the next rest.",["DT18"],"She has two beats of rest. A slight half-smile there tells her brain that she is accepting what happened (DT18). She can't observe what the panel member wrote (IE17). The half-smile is for her own benefit, and to the panel it reads as composure."]]]]}},{"id":"ER2","title":"what an emotion does: moves you, tells others, tells you","marks":[],"practice":["for one e :   what it moved ☉ to do ,   the message it sent ,   its effect on h ,   what it told ☉ ,   the facts that would check it ,   the facts checked"],"u":"An emotion tells you something. Why is that a reason to go and check, and not a fact about the world?","tree":{"t":"Mike feels dread as he opens his exam results. What does he do?","o":[["He reads the results and checks the facts: which modules he passed, and what the resit rules are.",["ER2","ER7"],4,"An emotion does three things: it readies him to act, it tells others something, and it tells him something. What it tells him is \"go and check\". Dread says a threat may be here. It does not say what is on the page, because an emotion is not a fact about the world. So he does what the message asks. He opens the results, and then he checks the facts one by one (ER7): what was passed, what was failed, and what the rules allow. Each emotion organizes a person to meet one kind of problem, and dread has got him to the page."],["He reminds himself the dread is a message about how much he cares, not a fact about the results, and it eases.",["ER2","M4"],6,"He has been treating the dread as if it had already read the results. He observes it (M4): a weight in his stomach, the thought \"it's bad\". That describes him. It does not describe the page. When the message matters, the emotion is hard to change, so the size of the dread is a measure of how much the year meant to him. He takes that message and sets aside the false one, and the dread loosens."],["He opens them with the dread still there.",["ER2","ER22"],1,"The dread does not have to go before he clicks. It has done its work, which was to tell him that this matters. He lets it be a wave (ER22) and opens the page inside it. Waiting to feel ready would mean waiting for an emotion to stop doing what it is for."],["He leaves them unopened for a week.",["ER2","G4"],6,"Avoiding the page brings relief, and the relief rewards the avoiding (IE25). It makes sense. But the dread's message was \"go and check\", and when a message is not acted on, the emotion keeps sending it. He spends a week of dread on a fact that would have taken ten seconds to learn. Resit deadlines also run from the date of the results."]],"e":[["He checks the facts. He has failed one module, and his flatmates are in the kitchen opening champagne.",[["He reads the resit rules and books the resit.",["ER2","ER12"],"The dread's message was accurate in part: there was a threat, and now it has a name. An emotion organizes him to meet a problem (ER2), and this one is met by the loop: define it, find the options, act (ER12). The resit gets booked tonight while the feeling still has push in it."],["He checks the size: one module, with a resit.",["ER7"],"The champagne next door makes one fail feel like total failure. He checks again (ER7): one module of six, with a resit that has a date. Catastrophizing would fix on the worst outcome. The facts he has are much smaller than the feeling."],["He lets the disappointment come.",["ER22","DT13"],"The disappointment fits, since he wanted a clean sheet (ER8). He accepts the fail (DT13) and lets the feeling come as it is, in his own room, before he goes to the kitchen. Held in, it would wait for him (G10). Felt, it passes."]]],["The dread eases and he reads them: one fail. Then his phone rings. It is his parents, and his mother's first words are, \"well? Tell us!\"",[["He tells them plainly, and what he will do next.",["IE10","IE5"],"An emotion tells others something whether he means it to or not, and his voice will carry it. So he says it straight: truthful, with no excuses and no acting helpless (IE10). Five passes, one fail, a resit in August. Facts first and then the plan (IE5)."],["He asks for a day before he talks about it.",["IE5","DT4"],"He has only just read it, and he is not ready to manage their reaction as well as his own. He stops before answering (DT4) and asks for something clear and small (IE5): \"mostly good, one to resit. Can I ring you tomorrow with the details?\" He has told them the truth and bought himself a day."],["He lets the shame rise and fall during the call.",["ER22"],"Shame rises when he pictures their faces. He has done nothing against his values (ER8). The shame is the fear of going down in their eyes. It is a wave (ER22). He stays on the line and does not hide in a vague answer."]]],["He clicks with the dread still there. The page hangs, then shows an error: the results server is overloaded.",[["He tries again in five minutes, and does something else meanwhile.",["DT9"],"Refreshing every ten seconds feeds the dread with each failure. He sets a timer and puts his whole attention on something else for five minutes (DT9). The plan to come back is definite, so this is not the same as avoiding the page."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"He was braced for the click, and the brace has nowhere to go. His heart is running fast. Paced breathing brings his body down (DT6). With the body calmer, his thinking is less likely to fill the blank page with the worst case."],["He notices the dread climbing and names it.",["ER6","ER2"],"The delay is a new prompt, and the dread climbs. He names it exactly (ER6): dread, with the thought \"it's a sign\". Naming it lets him stand apart from it and still own it. An overloaded server tells him about the server (ER2)."]]]]}},{"id":"ER3","title":"what makes it hard","marks":[["θ*","the skills breakdown point"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does rumination keep an emotion firing? Why does worry work as an escape?","tree":{"t":"Zoe keeps replaying a presentation she fumbled. What does she do?","o":[["She writes down what to change next time, then closes the notebook.",["ER3","ER12"],3,"Rumination keeps an emotion firing. Worry pretends there is an answer just one more replay away, and it works as an escape from doing anything. Under the replay there is a real problem, and she takes it out and solves it once, on paper (ER12): slides too dense, no rehearsal, notes left at home. Three lines. Closing the notebook matters as much as the list, because the problem now has a place to be. When the replay starts again she can answer it: it is written down."],["She sees the replay as rumination that keeps the feeling firing, and steps out of it.",["ER3","M1"],6,"The replay feels like working on the problem. It is the emotion sustaining itself. Emotions narrow attention to what fits them, and then they fire again (ER5). Each replay finds the same stumble and produces the same shame. To hold her mind is to hold her attention (M1), so she notices that the film is running and puts her attention elsewhere, on purpose. Without the replay to fuel it, the embarrassment burns down by itself."],["She lets the memory come up and pass without replaying it.",["ER3","DT19"],1,"A memory arriving and a memory being replayed are different things. The first happens to her, and the second she does. She can't stop the arrival. She can decline to press play. She watches it come as a thought and go as a thought (DT19), without pushing it away and without following it. The embarrassment that comes with it is a wave (ER22)."],["She replays it all night.",["ER3","G4"],6,"It makes sense, because her mind is trying to undo something that can't be undone. Each pass feels as if the next one might find the fix. It never does. The replay lasts until three, and a tired body is more vulnerable tomorrow, which is the first thing on the list of what makes regulation hard. She has lost a night to a fumble that took four minutes."]],"e":[["She writes what to change and closes the notebook. At 1 a.m. the replay starts regardless, with a new detail: the lecturer's raised eyebrow.",[["She reminds herself the answer is written down, and lies back.",["ER3","ER21"],"At night a worry gets sorted: can it be solved now? This one has been solved, and it is in the notebook (ER21). The eyebrow is new material that rumination has offered to keep the film running (ER3). She can't observe what an eyebrow meant (IE17), and at 1 a.m. she does not try."],["She uses paired relaxation in bed.",["DT7"],"The replay is in her thinking, and it is kept going by a tense body. She works through the muscle groups, tensing and releasing on one word (DT7). It gives her attention a slow task that is not the film, and it lowers the arousal the film feeds on."],["She aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"Now the thought \"I must sleep or tomorrow is ruined\" joins the replay. She drops that aim. Rest is something she can do on purpose, and sleep is not (ER21). She lies still and rests, and she leaves anything that seems urgent at this hour for the morning."]]],["She steps out of the replay. The next day a classmate says, cheerfully, \"that presentation, though! What happened?\"",[["She describes it plainly and moves on.",["M4","IE10"],"A plain description takes ten seconds: \"I lost my place and never got it back. Next time I'll rehearse\" (M4). It is truthful and has no grovelling in it (IE10). Answered plainly, a question like that usually ends there. Answered with shame, it gets asked about again."],["She notices the replay starting and steps out again.",["ER3","DT16"],"The classmate's question has pressed play for her. The prompt came from outside this time, and the move is the same: notice, and step out. Each restart is a fork, and she turns the same way again (DT16). She is not back where she began. She has had one more round of practice."],["She lets the embarrassment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Being reminded in public brings the heat back to her face. The embarrassment is a wave (ER22). The classmate's tone was cheerful. \"What happened?\" is curiosity as far as she can observe (IE17), and anything more is something she has added."]]],["She lets the memory pass. This time it comes with a hot wave of shame, and the thought, \"they all think I'm a fraud\".",[["She checks whether shame fits: was it against her values?",["ER7","ER8"],"Shame fits when she has broken her own values or would be rejected by people who matter if they knew (ER8). She fumbled a talk in front of twenty students. No value was broken, and nobody rejected her. \"They all think\" claims to know twenty minds (IE17). The shame does not fit the facts."],["If it doesn't fit, she acts opposite: she volunteers for the next presentation.",["ER10","ER11"],"Shame's urge is to hide. When shame does not fit, the opposite of hiding is to do the thing again, in public (ER11). She puts her name down for the next slot. Opposite action works when it is done all the way and repeated (ER10), so she will stand up straight and look at the room."],["She rides the wave.",["ER22"],"Whether it fits or not, the shame is in her body now. She does not block it and does not feed it with the film. It peaks and falls within minutes (ER22). She learns that shame can be felt through to its end, and that makes the next wave less frightening."]]]]}},{"id":"ER4","title":"myths about emotion: their forms, and the one case that breaks them","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Each myth is a claim about every emotion, situation or person. Why is \"sometimes\" often enough to answer it?","tree":{"t":"Jean believes \"if I feel anxious before an exam, I am not prepared\". What does she do?","o":[["She recalls one exam she was anxious about and did well in, and prepares as planned.",["ER4","IE3"],5,"Each myth about emotion is a claim about every emotion, every situation, or every person. One real case where it fails breaks it. Hers says that anxiety always means she is unprepared. She needs one counter-case and she has one: last year's chemistry exam, when she was sick with nerves and got her best mark. The myth is broken (IE3). With it gone, the anxiety stops counting as evidence about her revision, and she goes back to the plan. The break has to be made again in this exam week, because the myth will be back for the next one."],["She answers it: \"sometimes anxiety just means I care\", and the panic drops.",["ER4"],7,"Each myth is answered with a challenge she owns, and often the weaker claim, \"sometimes\", is enough. \"Anxiety never means anything\" would be a new myth. \"Sometimes it means I care\" is true and she can believe it. The panic came from a second emotion stacked on the first, fear of what her own anxiety meant (ER5). When the meaning is taken away she has ordinary exam nerves. Those fit the facts (ER8) and are far easier to carry."],["She lets the anxiety be there, and keeps studying.",["ER4","ER22"],1,"She does not settle the argument with the myth. She declines to treat the anxiety as a message about her preparation, and she lets it be a wave (ER22) while her eyes stay on the page. An emotion is not a fact about the world (ER2). By the end of the evening she has done three hours of revision while anxious, and that is a counter-case in the making."],["She believes the myth and panics.",["ER4","G4"],6,"With the myth in place, every flutter is evidence, and the evidence produces more flutter. Emotions narrow attention to what fits them and fire again (ER5). It makes sense if she was told somewhere that calm people are the competent ones (G11). Panic then takes the study time, and the myth gets to be right."]],"e":[["She recalls the good exam. But this one is a subject she finds harder, and the myth adjusts: \"that was different. This time the nerves are real\".",[["She looks for one more case: any exam where nerves and preparation went together.",["ER4","IE3"],"The myth has retreated to a narrower claim, and a narrower claim is broken the same way (IE3). She looks for a hard subject where she was both nervous and ready, and she asks a friend for theirs. A myth that needs a new exception each time it is tested is already failing."],["She checks the facts about her preparation.",["ER7"],"She has been arguing about what the anxiety means, and she can go round it. Preparation is checkable (ER7): topics covered, past papers done, marks on them. If the count is good, the nerves are only nerves. If it is thin, she knows what to do tonight. Either way the count is what tells her."],["She prepares as planned, nerves and all.",["ER10","ER22"],"The myth's urge is to stop and panic. She acts opposite to it and carries out the plan in full (ER10), with the nerves there as a wave (ER22). An evening of real work while anxious is the counter-case for this subject."]]],["She answers the myth. At lunch her friend says, quite seriously, \"if you're that nervous, you're probably not ready. I'm never nervous when I've revised\".",[["She answers that one too: \"sometimes\".",["ER4","IE20"],"Her friend has stated the myth as a rule about every person. It may be true of the friend. Jean weakens it aloud, lightly (IE20): \"for you, maybe. I get nervous even when I'm ready\". People differ in how strongly they feel things (G7), and one rule does not cover both of them."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"It is her friend's belief, said with confidence. Once it is in Jean's head it is a thought, and she can watch it without adopting it (DT19). Her friend's calm is her friend's own nature, and it tells Jean nothing about her revision."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"The comment invalidates her in a friendly voice. She says to herself what is valid (IE24): she has revised, she is nervous, and both are true. She would say the same to anyone else who felt things strongly."]]],["She keeps studying with the anxiety. It keeps growing, until the words on the page stop making sense.",[["She takes five minutes of paced breathing.",["DT6"],"Letting it be was right while she could still read. Now the anxiety is high enough that she can't. She uses the thermostat (ER1): five minutes of slow breathing brings her body down (DT6). The earlier skill did not fail. The level changed, and a different skill fits the new level."],["She checks: is she past her breakdown point?",["ER23"],"There is a point past which ordinary skills stop working and only crisis skills do. She rates it honestly. If she is past it, studying is not what to do next. She brings the arousal down first and then goes back to the page. Knowing where that point is for her is a skill too."],["She switches to a lighter task for an hour.",["DT9","ER12"],"She can't do dense reading tonight, and she can do flashcards. Switching tasks solves the real problem, which is using the evening well (ER12), and the lighter task takes her attention off the anxiety (DT9). She plans to go back to the reading when she can read again."]]]]}},{"id":"ER5","title":"a model of an emotion: a path in 𝐗, with a loop back","marks":[["β","emotion to body"],["🔦","attention"],["⚡","a prompting event"],["🧠","brain changes"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can a change at any part of the model change the whole emotion? Why does suppressing an expression make the emotion stronger, when changing it does not?","tree":{"t":"Randy reads a red-penned essay and feels shame. What does he do?","o":[["He books a feedback meeting and makes a revision plan from the comments.",["ER5","ER12","IE5"],4,"An emotion is a path with parts. An event is attended to and read. The reading changes the brain and body, and an emotion and an urge arise. It shows in face, words and acts, and things follow. A change at any part can change the whole emotion. He changes the last part, the act. Shame's urge is to hide the essay. He books a meeting (IE5) and turns the comments into a plan (ER12). What follows is then different: he gets help, where hiding would have got him silence. The path that used to loop back into more shame now leads somewhere else."],["He rereads the comments as \"here is how to improve\", not \"I'm stupid\".",["ER5","ER7"],6,"He changes the path at its second part, the reading. The event is the same: red ink on six pages. The first reading was \"I'm stupid\", which rates his whole self, and that is what makes it shame (ER6). Checked against the facts (ER7), each comment is about a sentence or an argument. None is about him. A reading can build a complex emotion out of a simple one, and he had built shame out of disappointment. With the reading changed, the emotion that comes is closer to interest."],["He lets the shame rise and fall without hiding the essay.",["ER5","ER22"],1,"He leaves the reading alone and does not act yet. He works at the middle of the path, with the feeling in his body. Suppressing makes an emotion stronger, so he lets it be: heat in his face, the urge to shut the essay in a drawer. It rises, and since he neither feeds it nor acts on it, it falls (ER22). The essay stays on the desk, face up. Leaving it there is a small opposite action in its own right (ER10)."],["He throws the essay away and never looks at the comments.",["ER5","G4"],6,"This is the urge acted on, and it works for an hour. Then the after-effects arrive: the same mistakes in the next essay, and more red ink. What follows can set off a second emotion, and the path loops back. Hiding makes sense if red ink once meant humiliation (G11). The comments were the one part of the event that could have helped him."]],"e":[["He books feedback. The first slot is a week away, and each day the essay sits on his desk the shame grows a little.",[["He starts the revision plan now with the comments he understands.",["ER5","ER12"],"Waiting is doing nothing, and doing nothing leaves the path looping back on itself. He changes the act part today. Half the comments are clear without a meeting, and he begins with those (ER12). He will then go to the meeting with questions, which feels different from going in with a wound."],["He rereads the comments as help.",["ER7"],"Each day the unread ink has been read again by his imagination, and read as worse. He goes back to the page itself and checks the reading against it (ER7). The words are the same as on Monday. The shame grew out of what he had been adding to them."],["He rides each wave of shame.",["ER22"],"The shame comes each time his eye falls on the essay. Each time is a separate wave with a rise and a fall (ER22). It is not one week-long emotion. He does not move the essay out of sight. By Thursday the waves are lower, because none of them was acted on."]]],["He rereads them as help. Most are. One is not: \"did you even read the question?\"",[["He takes the useful part, and lets the tone go.",["IE24"],"A comment can correct a real mistake and still be harsh in how it does it. He separates the two (IE24). The useful part: his answer drifted from the question. The tone was the marker's bad moment and need not become his opinion of himself. He validates himself: he did read it, and he lost it halfway through."],["He describes the comment without adding to it.",["M4","ER5"],"What is on the page: seven words and a question mark (M4). What he added: contempt, and a verdict on his future. The reading is the part of the path he can change. Described without the additions, the comment says his answer missed the question, and that is useful to know."],["He asks the tutor what was meant.",["IE5","IE17"],"He can't observe the tutor's state of mind from ink (IE17). In the meeting he asks, plainly and without edge (IE5): \"where did I leave the question?\" He will likely get an answer about paragraph three. He will not get the contempt he imagined."]]],["He lets the shame rise and fall with the essay open on the library desk. A classmate leans over, sees the red, and says, \"ouch\".",[["He says, \"yes, lots to fix\", and leaves it.",["IE10","M4"],"An emotion shows in face and words, and things follow. A plain description (M4), said truthfully with no ashamed posture (IE10), sets the tone for what follows. The classmate takes his cue from it. He has shown that an essay covered in red ink can be talked about."],["He keeps the essay out, not hidden.",["ER10","ER5"],"The urge spikes: cover it, close it. He leaves it where it is. This is opposite action at the act part of the path (ER10), and now there is a witness, which is when it counts most. He has done nothing against his values (ER8), so there is nothing to hide."],["He lets the wave peak and fall.",["ER22"],"Being seen makes the shame jump, because shame is about other people's eyes. It is still a wave (ER22). The classmate's \"ouch\" was sympathy as far as he can observe (IE17). The peak passes in under a minute."]]]]}},{"id":"ER6","title":"describing emotions: one record for each","marks":[],"practice":["∀ ☼ :   pick one e :   the strongest ,  the longest ,  or the most painful\nrecord :   what it moved ☉ to do ,   what it told others ,   what it told ☉","S = e′   ⇒   do the record for e′ first ,  then for e   a secondary emotion is analysed after the one that set it off","|e| ∈ [0 , 100] ;   vulnerability :  the story up to the event ;   after :  memory ,  other emotions ,  body ,  acts"],"u":"How does shame differ from guilt, and jealousy from envy? Why sort which of two emotions came first only afterwards?","tree":{"t":"Claire feels sick when a classmate gets the scholarship she wanted. What does she do?","o":[["She names it envy, and uses it: she asks what made the winning application strong.",["ER6","ER13"],4,"Each emotion has a record: its words, the events that prompt it, the readings, the body changes, the expressions and the after-effects. She names hers by finding the record her experience fits best. The event is that someone else got what she wanted. The reading is \"that should be mine\". That is envy. It is not jealousy, which guards what a person already has. Envy fits the facts here (ER8). When an emotion fits, one option is to act on its urge in a useful form (ER13). The useful form of envy is working to get the thing herself, and that starts by asking."],["She fills in the record for envy, and the feeling shrinks once it is named.",["ER6","M4"],6,"\"I feel sick\" has no edges. She fills in the record one field at a time (M4). The prompting event is the few moments just before: the announcement. The reading: \"she got what I deserve\". Body: tight throat, hot face. Urge: to find fault with the winner. Observing an emotion lets her stand apart from it and still own it. Once it is named envy, and understood as wanting what another person has, it is a specific and ordinary feeling. Before that it was only a sickness."],["She lets the envy be, and congratulates the classmate.",["ER6","ER22","IE7"],1,"Envy often fits the facts and still corrodes (ER8). She does not act on its urge, and she does not pretend it away. It is a wave (ER22). The congratulation is GIVE (IE7), and it is also the opposite of envy's urge to pull the other person down (ER10). She can feel envy and say \"well done\" in the same minute. Both are true about her."],["She avoids the classmate for the rest of the year.",["ER6","G4"],6,"Avoiding makes sense, because each sight of the classmate restarts the feeling. But an unnamed emotion has no handle, and avoidance leaves it unnamed. Shame about feeling envious may be hiding the envy from her, since a secondary emotion can hide the first. She loses a classmate and also the information about what made that application win."]],"e":[["She asks what made the application strong. The classmate says, a little awkwardly, \"honestly? My uncle knows the chair of the panel\".",[["She names the envy again, and asks what else she can build.",["ER6","ER12"],"The feeling shifts, so she names it again: envy still, and now anger with it, because a goal was blocked by something unfair. With two emotions at once she fills in a record for each (ER6). The useful question still stands. She can't build an uncle, and she can build other things: a stronger statement, a referee who knows her (ER12)."],["She checks whether the envy fits.",["ER8"],"Envy fits when another person has what she wants or needs (ER8), and it still fits. The check also covers size and how long it lasts. A contact on the panel does not make her application worthless. It means one cause of this result was outside her reach."],["She accepts that part.",["DT13","DT12"],"Some people have uncles on panels. That is how things are, and she accepts it (DT13) without calling it fair (DT14). Reality acceptance is for what problem solving can't change (DT12). Her effort then goes to the parts of next year's application that she can change."]]],["She names it envy. That evening the classmate's celebration is all over her feed: balloons, family, a cake with the scholarship's name on it.",[["She lets the envy rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Each photo is a new prompting event, and the envy fires again each time. Emotions narrow attention to what fits them (ER5), which is why she keeps scrolling. She puts the phone down and lets this wave run out (ER22) without setting off another."],["She sends a kind message.",["M7","ER10"],"Envy's urge is to diminish the other person, or to turn away. A sincere good wish is the opposite act (ER10), and it is loving kindness one step outward (M7). She can write it before she fully feels it. The feeling tends to follow the act."],["She describes the record again: what the envy is telling her.",["ER6","ER2"],"An emotion tells her something, and the message is to go and check (ER2). She reads the record for what it says: she wants this badly, more than she had admitted. That tells her about her own values (ER17). The cake is not what she wants. She wants what the scholarship would have let her do."]]],["She congratulates the classmate. The classmate smiles and says, \"thanks! You'll get one next year, maybe\".",[["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"\"Maybe\" lands like a pat on the head. She can observe the word. She can't observe the intent (IE17). Someone who has just won, and feels awkward in front of someone who lost, will often say something clumsy. She holds that reading all the way."],["She lets the sting be.",["ER22","DT13"],"The sting is real whatever was meant. She accepts that it was said and that it stung (DT13). It is a small wave on top of the larger one (ER22). She does not need to answer it. The congratulation was hers to give, and she gave it."],["She plans next year's application.",["ER17","ER12"],"\"Next year, maybe\" contains a true thing: there is a next year. She brings the value down to a step she can take this week (ER17): she reads the criteria and lists what her application lacked (ER12). Envy pointed at what she wants, and this plan is how she works toward it."]]]]}},{"id":"ER7","title":"check the facts: six questions","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why look for a hidden threat when the facts are checked and the emotion stays high? What does catastrophizing add to the facts?","tree":{"t":"Terry is sure his professor's \"see me after class\" means he is in trouble. What does he do?","o":[["He goes and asks, and finds out what the professor wanted.",["ER7","IE5"],4,"The reading of an event sets off the emotion, and the emotion colours the reading. The event is four words on a slip of paper. \"I'm in trouble\" is something he added. When the fit is unsure, the instruction is to keep checking: ask others, run an experiment. The most direct check is to go, and to ask plainly if the professor does not say (IE5). People add to what they see and then react to what they added. Going to the meeting replaces the added part with the real one."],["He runs the six questions, and finds the hidden threat is \"I'll be thrown out\". Naming it shrinks it.",["ER7"],6,"The six questions, in order. What emotion? Fear. What event? A note: \"see me after class\". What reading? \"I'm in trouble.\" What threat? He has to dig here, because painful emotions nearly always assume a threat, and it is often unspoken. His is \"I'll be thrown off the course\". What catastrophe, and could he cope? Even at the worst there would be an appeal, and other courses. Does it fit? Professors write that note for a dozen reasons, and expulsion is not done by sticky note. Said out loud, the threat is too big for four words to carry."],["He puts up with not knowing until after class.",["ER7","DT11"],1,"This can't be checked for fifty minutes. When something can't be checked, the rule is to take a likely or useful reading. \"He wants to tell me something\" is both. Then one thing in the moment (DT11): this lecture, these notes. Not knowing is uncomfortable, and it is not dangerous. He puts up with it and does not try to settle it in his imagination."],["He skips class to avoid the meeting.",["ER7","G4"],6,"He is reacting to what he added to the note. Skipping makes sense as a way out of a threat, but the threat is still unchecked. Now he has done a real thing, missing a class and a meeting, and it may cause the trouble he imagined. The fear stays as it was, because the reading was never tested."]],"e":[["He goes and asks. The professor, packing up, says, \"ah, yes. Not now. Let's talk next week\".",[["He asks what it is about, briefly.",["IE5","ER7"],"A week of not knowing is a real cost, and one short question may spare him that (IE5): \"of course. Could you tell me what it's about, so I can prepare?\" It is a reasonable ask, and it carries on the check (ER7). Most professors will answer in a sentence."],["He checks the six questions about the wait.",["ER7"],"There is a new event, \"next week\", and a new reading: \"it's so bad he needs time\". He runs the questions again. What did he observe? A busy person packing a bag. What did he add? Everything else. Something urgent and serious is seldom put off for a week, so the delay points the other way."],["He lets the uncertainty be for a week.",["DT13","DT11"],"He has done the checking that can be done. The rest is waiting, and he accepts it (DT13). He takes one day at a time (DT11). Each time his mind drafts the meeting, he notices and comes back to the day he is in."]]],["He names the threat. Named, it shrinks for an hour. Then it is back: \"but it does happen. People do get thrown out\".",[["He checks the probability: how often are students thrown out after a chat?",["ER7"],"Something being possible does not make it likely. He asks the question that catastrophizing skips: how likely? How many students does he know of who were removed from a course, and what had they done? He has done nothing like it. The honest estimate is very small, and he says the number to himself."],["He copes ahead for the worst case.",["ER18"],"The fifth question asks whether he could cope with the catastrophe. He rehearses it (ER18). Suppose the worst is said: what does he do in the next minute, the next day, the next week? There turn out to be steps. A catastrophe that he has a plan for stops looking like the end of everything."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"The facts are checked and the fear is still high. Part of that is his body, still braced. Paced breathing works on that part directly (DT6). With his heart slower, the checked facts start to feel as true as they are."]]],["He puts up with not knowing. He arrives at the next class braced for the talk, and the professor is away, with a substitute and no explanation.",[["He emails to ask when they can meet.",["IE5"],"Waiting was right while there was a set time. The time has gone, so he acts. A two-line email (IE5): the note, and a request for a time. It moves him from waiting on the professor to having done his part."],["He notices the catastrophe growing and checks it again.",["ER7","DT19"],"The absence feeds the story: \"he's meeting the dean about me\". That is a thought (DT19), and it is checked like the first one (ER7). What did he observe? A substitute teacher. Professors are absent for flu, conferences, and children. He did not observe anything about himself."],["He keeps doing his work.",["DT9","ER18"],"Whatever the note meant, the coursework is due in any case. Putting his whole attention on it gets him through the wait (DT9), and it builds something real (ER18). If the talk turns out to be about his work, he will come to it with more work done."]]]]}},{"id":"ER8","title":"when an emotion fits the facts","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"How can envy fit the facts and still not work? Why can shame fit when nothing immoral was done?","tree":{"t":"Jean is angry that her group got a lower mark because of a teammate's late section. What does she do?","o":[["She raises it with the module leader, with the facts and the dates.",["ER8","IE5"],4,"Each emotion fits the facts in certain situations. Anger fits when an important goal is blocked, or when she or someone she cares about is treated unfairly. Her goal was blocked by a late section, so the anger fits. A fitting emotion is not there to be argued away. She acts on its urge in a useful form (ER13). Anger's urge is to remove the obstacle, and the useful form is a clear account to the person who can do something, with facts and dates (IE5). She did not cause the lateness, and she is the one who has to raise it."],["She checks the size: anger fits, but a week of rage does not, and she lets it come down to fit.",["ER8","ER7"],6,"Fitting has three parts: the kind of emotion, its size, and how long it lasts. The kind fits. Then she checks the size against the facts (ER7): four marks lost on one project, in one module. That calls for irritation and a conversation. A week of rage would fit a betrayal. The extra size is coming from what she has added, \"he did it on purpose\" and \"he's ruined my degree\". When she takes those out, the anger settles to the size of the event."],["She accepts the mark, and plans clearer roles for the next project.",["ER8","DT13"],1,"The anger fits, and the mark is also final. Both are true. She accepts the mark (DT13). She is not denying that it was unfair. It is recorded, and fighting the record changes nothing. The anger's message was that this should not happen again, and she acts on it where it can still count: written roles and an early deadline next time (ER18)."],["She stays furious all term.",["ER8","G4"],6,"The anger began as fitting, and that is what makes it easy to keep: she is in the right. But how long an emotion lasts has to fit too, and three months does not fit four marks. It is kept going by replaying the injustice (ER3), and it is rewarded each time it backs up her view. The teammate has forgotten the matter by now. She is the one carrying it."]],"e":[["She raises it, with dates. The module leader listens and says, \"group marks are final. Learning to manage a team is part of what we assess\".",[["She asks whether peer assessment can count next time.",["IE5","ER12"],"The first solution failed, and the problem behind it is still real, so she looks for the next one (ER12). She asks for something forward-looking that is within the leader's power (IE5). It turns her anger about one mark into a change that would protect every later group."],["She checks the size of her anger now: still fitting?",["ER8","ER7"],"There is a new event, the refusal, and so a new check (ER7). Was she treated unfairly by the module leader? The rule was stated in the handbook. Her anger at the teammate may still fit. Anger at the leader fits less. She sorts the two, because they need different actions."],["She accepts this mark.",["DT13"],"She made her case well and the answer was no. The mark will stand, and she accepts it (DT13). Accepting it does not mean the leader's reasoning persuaded her. It means she stops paying for the mark with her evenings."]]],["She lets the anger come down to size and mentions it to the teammate. He laughs: \"relax, it's one mark. You're so intense\".",[["She tells the teammate how it affected her, using DEAR MAN.",["IE5"],"He has answered a size-four complaint by calling her a size ten. She does not take up the attack (IE5). She describes: the section came two days late, and the group lost four marks. She expresses: \"I was frustrated\". She asserts: \"next time I need a day's warning\". She stays on that and does not get drawn into whether she is intense."],["She lets the anger rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Being laughed at brings the anger back above size. It is a wave (ER22). If she acted at its peak, she would prove his point. She waits until it falls, and then decides whether there is anything left to say."],["She limits contact with the teammate.",["IE18"],"A tie that blocks her goals is an interfering one (IE18). She does not need to end anything dramatically. She decides in wise mind to keep things civil and not to share a group with him again by choice. That makes enough room for herself."]]],["She accepts the mark. Then she hears that the teammate had a formal extension all along, and never told the group.",[["She asks the module leader about the extension, with facts.",["IE5","ER8"],"This is a new fact, and it changes what fits. If part of the group had an approved extension, the late penalty may have been applied in error. Anger fits again, and its useful form is one clear question (IE5): was the extension taken into account in the group mark?"],["She checks the facts before acting.",["ER7"],"She has heard it second-hand. Before she is angry at anyone, she checks (ER7): was there an extension, for which dates, and did it cover the group submission? An emotion built on a rumour is built on something added. Ten minutes of checking comes before any email."],["She accepts this too.",["DT13","DT16"],"It may turn out that nothing can be done. The acceptance she reached last week has been shaken, and she chooses it again (DT16). She accepts that he did not tell them, and that this was careless (DT13). She will be more careful about whom she works with."]]]]}},{"id":"ER9","title":"opposite action or problem solving: two questions","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Two checked answers say what to do. What do you do when both are no, and in what order?","tree":{"t":"Evita dreads a viva she has prepared well for. What does she do?","o":[["The fear does not fit the facts, so she acts opposite: she books a mock viva and answers every question.",["ER9","ER10"],5,"Two questions decide what to do with an emotion, and each is checked, true or false. Does it fit the facts? Would acting on it work here? Her fear says she will be exposed. The facts: the chapters are finished, and her supervisor has signed off. So it does not fit. Acting on it would mean postponing, and that does not work. With both answers no, the rule is to change the thoughts first, in the easiest way, and if the emotion stays, to act opposite. She has checked the thoughts and the dread is still there. So she walks toward the thing she fears and sits a full mock, all the way (ER10). The skill has to be practised in a room like the one where it will be needed."],["She checks the facts (her chapters are done, her supervisor is happy), and the dread drops.",["ER9","ER7"],6,"When the emotion does not fit, the first move is to change the thoughts, because that is the easiest way. She runs the check (ER7). What is the threat? \"They'll find out I don't know my own thesis.\" What are the facts? Three years of work, every chapter read by her supervisor, and a mock question answered well last month. The reading does not survive the facts. The dread was resting on the reading, and it comes down with it. A fear that drops when it is checked needs nothing more done to it."],["She lets the fear ride along into the viva.",["ER9","ER22"],1,"She is not required to change an emotion, and the first step is always to be sure she wants to. A viva matters, and some fear before it is fitting in kind even if hers is oversized (ER8). She decides to carry it and not to work on it. It rises and falls (ER22), and she takes it in with her like a heavy coat. She does not act on its urge to cancel. Carrying it without acting on it is as much as tolerating asks of her."],["She postpones the viva.",["ER9","G4"],6,"Postponing is the fear's own urge, and acting on a fear that does not fit teaches the fear that it was right. The relief is immediate, and that rewards the postponing (IE25). It makes sense, since the dread is real even though the danger is not. In three months she will have the same viva and a stronger dread."]],"e":[["She books the mock viva. It goes badly: she stumbles on a basic question about her method, and the colleague playing examiner looks worried.",[["She asks what went wrong and practises those questions.",["ER12","ER18"],"The mock has turned up a real problem, and she has time to fix it. She answers the two questions again with the new fact. Does some fear fit now? Yes, about the method section. Does acting on it work? Yes, by preparing. When both are yes, she solves the problem (ER12) and rehearses those answers aloud (ER18)."],["She does another mock.",["ER10","ER9"],"Opposite action is repeated until the emotion changes, and falling off the horse means getting back on (ER10). One bad mock, followed by avoiding mocks, would be acting on the fear. A second mock, after preparation, is the opposite done properly. She books it before she can talk herself out of it."],["She checks the facts: a mock is a practice.",["ER7"],"\"If the mock went badly, the viva will\" is an inference from one case (IE21). The facts (ER7): a mock is there to find weak spots, and this one found a single weak spot. Her colleague's worried face is an observation, and its meaning is a guess (IE17). Rehearsals often go worse than the performance."]]],["She checks the facts and the dread drops. Then her supervisor mentions, in passing, \"your external is known for being very strict\".",[["She asks what the examiner usually asks.",["IE5","ER9"],"There is a new fact, so she asks both questions again. A strict examiner is a real demand, and some fear now fits. Acting on it works if the act is preparing. She asks her supervisor one specific thing (IE5): what does this examiner push on? Strictness that she can describe is something to prepare for."],["She copes ahead.",["ER18"],"She imagines the hardest moment in detail: the examiner frowning and saying \"I'm not convinced\". She rehearses her answer, and her breathing while she gives it (ER18). She rehearses coping with it. She does not rehearse the catastrophe. When the frown comes, she will have met it before."],["She lets the fear rise and fall.",["ER22"],"The remark sends the fear back up. Part of it now fits, and a fitting fear is still a wave (ER22). She does not have to bring it to zero again before she sleeps. It tells her what to prepare, and after that it can pass."]]],["She lets the fear ride along. In the viva the external asks her opening question, and her mind goes completely blank.",[["She asks for a moment and breathes.",["IE5","DT6"],"A blank is fear in the body shutting down recall. She asks plainly (IE5): \"may I take a moment to think?\" Examiners expect this. Then three slow breaths, out longer than in (DT6). Her body comes down, and the thesis she has known for three years comes back."],["She says what she knows, one point at a time.",["DT11","M5"],"She does not need the whole answer. She needs the first true sentence, one thing in the moment (DT11). Doing what works (M5) means she starts anywhere solid: \"the central claim of the thesis is…\". One point usually brings the next one with it."],["She lets the blank pass.",["ER22","DT13"],"Panic about the blank is what keeps it going. She accepts that her mind is blank for now (DT13) and waits, as she would for any wave (ER22). Five seconds of silence feels like a minute to her and looks like thinking to them."]]]]}},{"id":"ER10","title":"opposite action: all the way, until it changes","marks":[["🏷","words"]],"practice":["∃ a :  avoided because of e ,  listed   |e| before ,  |e| after","if it fails ,  checked :   e ⊭ F ;   ⊖ of my own u ;   all the way :  voice ,  eyes ,  posture ;   long enough to learn"],"u":"Why must opposite action go all the way, and why does halfway not work?","tree":{"t":"Mike avoids the maths help centre out of embarrassment. What does he do?","o":[["He goes, all the way: shoulders back, asks a full question, stays the whole hour.",["ER10","IE5"],5,"Opposite action is for an emotion that does not fit, or that fits and does not help, or that keeps him from what needs doing. Embarrassment at a help centre is all three. The rule is all the way: acts, body, words and thoughts. Acts: he walks in and stays the hour. Body: shoulders back, head up, eyes on the tutor. Words: a full question in a clear voice (IE5), not a mumble with an apology. Thoughts: \"this room exists for this\". Halfway does not work. Going and then hiding at the back, hunched over, would still be the embarrassment acted out."],["He keeps going each week until the embarrassment falls even a little.",["ER10"],2,"Opposite action is repeated until the emotion changes, and an old habit takes much practice. One visit will not do it. The instruction is to keep on until it falls even a little, and \"even a little\" is the mark to look for (G2). He does not suppress the feeling. He lets the action do the work, with his senses open, so that he learns by experience that nothing bad follows. He rates the embarrassment before and after each visit (G15), and the numbers show the slope."],["He goes once, feeling embarrassed, and lets it be.",["ER10","ER22"],1,"The embarrassment does not have to be changed for him to go. He takes it with him. It rises at the door and peaks when he sits down, and since he neither leaves nor fights it, it falls (ER22). Going once while embarrassed is already opposite to the urge. He tolerates an hour of feeling exposed, and he comes out with an answer he would not have got at home."],["He keeps avoiding it.",["ER10","G4"],6,"Each time he turns away from the door the embarrassment stops, and that relief rewards the avoiding (IE25). It makes sense, and it is why avoidance grows. The emotion never learns that it is not justified, because he is never in the room long enough to find out. Meanwhile the maths goes unlearned, which gives him more to be embarrassed about."]],"e":[["He goes, all the way. The tutor on duty explains once, fast, and when he asks again says, \"I just told you\".",[["He stays the whole hour anyway.",["ER10"],"This is the moment the urge was waiting for: proof, and a reason to leave. All the way includes staying when it gets hard. If he fell off the horse, he gets back on. He stays, works the problem himself, and asks someone else. Leaving now would teach him the old lesson twice as strongly."],["He gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"He observed a short sentence. He did not observe \"you're stupid\" (IE17). A tutor at the end of a shift with a queue is likely to be curt with everyone. He holds that reading all the way, and that keeps the embarrassment from getting evidence it has not earned."],["He asks another tutor next time.",["ER12"],"The goal is help with maths, and this tutor is one route to it (ER12). He comes back at a different hour. He has changed which tutor he sees and kept the visit, so it is problem solving and not avoidance."]]],["He goes each week. After three weeks his embarrassment rating is exactly where it started, and he wonders if he is just built this way.",[["He troubleshoots: is he going all the way?",["ER24","ER10"],"When a skill does not work, he checks in order and fixes the first thing that fails (ER24). Is he using the skill as written? He looks at what he actually does. He goes, but he sits by the door, speaks quietly, and leaves as soon as he has an answer. That is halfway: his body and words are still acting out the embarrassment. He fixes that first."],["He keeps going.",["DT16","ER10"],"An old habit takes much practice, and three visits against years of avoiding is a short record (G15). Each week is a fork, and he turns toward the door again (DT16). The rule is to keep on until it falls even a little, and it gives no number of weeks."],["He checks whether embarrassment fits.",["ER7","ER8"],"If an emotion will not move, the facts may not be as he assumed, so he looks again (ER7). Has anyone there laughed at him, or treated him as lesser? No. So it does not fit (ER8), and opposite action is the right tool. The check also asks about a hidden threat, and he finds one: \"they'll see I shouldn't be on this course\"."]]],["He goes once. As he sits down he sees a classmate from his lecture two tables away, who looks up and sees him too.",[["He says hello normally.",["ER10","IE7"],"The urge is to look away and pretend he is there for something else. He nods and says hello in an ordinary voice, with an easy manner (IE7). That is opposite action with his face and words (ER10). He is acting as if being here is normal, and it is."],["He lets the embarrassment rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Being seen is what he feared, and it has happened. The embarrassment spikes. It is a wave (ER22), and nothing follows from it. The classmate goes back to their own page. He learns that the moment he feared most lasts a few seconds."],["He checks the facts: the classmate is there for help too.",["ER7"],"What does it mean that the classmate saw him at the help centre? It means the classmate is at the help centre (ER7). Whatever he fears the classmate thinks of him, he could think the same of the classmate, and he doesn't. One real case has broken the myth that only failures come here (IE3)."]]]]}},{"id":"ER11","title":"the opposite of each urge","marks":[["⚔","attack"],["🕊","peace; gentleness"],["🔨","destroy"],["🔒","control; guard"],["🔓","let go of control"],["🙈","hide"],["🙇","bow down; make up for"],["🧍","stand up straight"]],"practice":[],"u":"For shame and guilt, why does the opposite action depend on which of the two fits?","tree":{"t":"Zoe feels guilty for skipping a group meeting on purpose. What does she do?","o":[["Guilt fits, so she repairs it: she apologizes and takes on an extra task.",["ER11","ER13","IE10"],4,"Each emotion has its own urge and its own opposite. For shame and guilt, which action to take depends on which of the two fits. Guilt is about one act, and it fits when the act went against her own values. She promised to come and chose not to, so it fits. A fitting emotion is not opposed. She acts on its urge (ER13), and guilt's urge is to repair. To repair, she first finds what was harmed: the group's time and their trust. She mends that, with an apology that has no grovelling in it (IE10) and an extra task. Then she lets the guilt go."],["She checks whether it fits (she broke a promise, so it does), and the guilt settles to the right size.",["ER11","ER8"],6,"She sorts two questions. Is this guilt or shame? Guilt rates one act, and shame rates the whole self (ER6). Hers is about the skipped meeting, so it is guilt. Does it fit? She broke a promise, so yes. Then size (ER8): one meeting, one evening of the group's time. The guilt had been running at the size of a betrayal because shame had mixed in with it: \"I'm unreliable\". Once that is separated out, the guilt is small and exact, and it points at one repair."],["She accepts the guilt, and lets it guide her next week.",["ER11","DT13"],1,"The meeting is over and can't be attended now. She accepts that she skipped it (DT13), without excuses and without flogging herself. Guilt that fits is information about her own values. A moral code says what not to do, and values say what is worth doing. She carries the guilt for a week as a reminder of what she wants to be, and next Tuesday she is in the room."],["She avoids the group to escape the guilt.",["ER11","G4"],6,"This is shame's action used on guilt. Hiding answers shame. It does nothing for guilt, because the harm stays unrepaired, and avoiding the group adds a second harm to the first. It makes sense, since seeing them brings the feeling up. But guilt that fits ends only when the repair is made."]],"e":[["She apologizes and takes the extra task. The group says \"fine\", and the next meeting is polite and cool.",[["She keeps the repair going: she does her task well and early.",["ER11","IE9"],"Some repairs take long. Trust is mended by being trustworthy from then on (G13), so the apology only began the repair. Reliability is rebuilt one kept promise at a time (IE9). Work done well and early is the repair made in a form they can see."],["She accepts that trust takes time.",["DT13"],"She can't make them warm on her own schedule. The coolness is the natural result of what she did, and it is in proportion. She accepts it (DT13), with no resentment that her apology did not buy instant forgiveness. An apology offered in order to get relief is not quite an apology."],["She gives the benefit of the doubt.",["IE17"],"\"Cool\" is her reading of a quiet meeting. She can't observe whether they are holding a grudge or are just tired (IE17). She takes \"fine\" at its word and behaves as someone who has been forgiven. That is the easiest person for them to forgive."]]],["The guilt settles to size. Then a different feeling creeps in: \"what kind of person skips on purpose? Maybe I'm just selfish\".",[["She checks: shame fits values; guilt fits acts.",["ER6","ER11"],"A secondary emotion has followed the first (ER5). She fills in a new record (ER6). This one rates her whole self, so it is shame. Shame fits if she would be rejected for who she is. One skipped meeting, apologized for and repaired, does not make her a selfish person. The guilt fitted and has been dealt with. The shame does not fit."],["She validates herself: one skipped meeting is one act.",["IE24","IE21"],"\"Selfish\" is name-calling, and it turns one act into a character (IE21). She says to herself what she would say to a friend (IE24): \"you skipped one meeting, you owned it, you made up for it\". A selfish person would not be lying awake over it."],["She rides the wave.",["ER22"],"The shame is in her body whether it fits or not: the urge to curl up and to avoid the group. She does not act on it, because hiding would undo the repair. It is a wave (ER22), and she lets it peak and fall. Tomorrow she posts in the group chat as usual, and that is the opposite of hiding."]]],["She accepts the guilt and goes the next week. A week after that, it still has not faded.",[["She checks whether repair is complete.",["ER11","ER13"],"Guilt that fits and persists is still sending its message. She asks what was harmed and whether it has been mended (ER13). She turned up again, but she never said anything to the group, and the missed work was done by someone else. The repair is unfinished, and that is why the guilt is still there."],["She asks a group member if things are okay.",["IE5"],"She can't observe whether the group still minds (IE17), so she asks one person, plainly (IE5): \"I skipped that meeting and I'm sorry. Is there anything I should pick up?\" The question is itself part of the repair. The answer tells her whether the guilt still has work to do."],["She lets it be.",["ER22","DT13"],"If the repair is made and the guilt lingers, what remains is only the feeling. Its length no longer fits (ER8). She accepts that it is there (DT13) and lets it fade at its own pace (ER22). She does not go on apologizing in order to get rid of it."]]]]}},{"id":"ER12","title":"problem solving: a loop with two returns","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is the loop's goal what must happen for you to feel all right? Why does a new problem go back to the start?","tree":{"t":"Randy fails a lab practical. What does he do?","o":[["He runs the loop: the problem, a goal he can reach, three solutions, his pick, and back to the start if it fails.",["ER12"],4,"Problem solving is a loop. Describe the problem, and check the facts: he failed the titration practical because his readings were inconsistent, and he had done the technique only twice. What makes it a problem: the practical is needed to pass the module. Set a goal that can be reached, the thing that must happen for him to feel all right: pass the resit in three weeks. Think up many solutions without judging them: extra lab sessions, a technician's demonstration, practising with water at home, asking a classmate to watch him. Rank them by how likely they are to work and how possible they are. Pick one and act. If it does not work, go back. A first try may only improve things."],["He sees the failure as one practical at one time, not a verdict on him, and the shame drops.",["ER12","IE20"],6,"A problem is specific to the person and the time. \"I failed this practical on Tuesday\" is a problem. \"I'm a failure\" is not one, because it has no time or place, and nothing can be done about it. He weakens the claim until it fits the facts (IE20): this one, this once. The loop's first step is to describe and check the facts, and doing that already removes the verdict. The shame was resting on the verdict."],["He accepts the fail, and books the resit.",["ER12","DT13"],1,"The fail is recorded, and problem solving does not reach backward to change it. He accepts it (DT13). Booking the resit is the smallest forward act, and he can do it while he still feels bad. Acceptance clears the way for the loop: he will solve the problem that actually exists, the resit, and not the one he wishes he had (∎)."],["He gives up on the module.",["ER12","G4"],6,"One failure has become a hopeless expectancy (IE21). Giving up makes sense as relief from trying and failing again. But he has not described the problem, so he does not know that it is a small and fixable one, a technique done twice. Mastery guards against helplessness (ER14), and he has walked away from it."]],"e":[["He runs the loop and picks extra lab sessions. The sessions are fully booked until after the resit.",[["He goes back to the start of the loop.",["ER12"],"The loop has two returns. One is for a solution that failed, and the other is for a new problem. This is the first. He goes back and describes the problem again, since the obstacle has taught him something: the real need is supervised practice, in a lab or anywhere else. With the problem described better, different solutions turn up."],["He picks the next solution on his list.",["ER12","G15"],"He brainstormed several solutions so that one closed door would not end it. Next in the ranking is asking a technician for a demonstration. He treats each solution as a trial (G15): he tries it, records what happened, and keeps or drops it. The failed booking is one entry in that record."],["He asks someone for a solution he didn't think of.",["IE5","IE16"],"Brainstorming alone is limited to what he already knows. He asks a classmate who passed (IE5): \"how did you get your readings consistent?\" Other people's solutions can go on his list too. Asking them also goes against the pull to sort it out alone (G11)."]]],["He sees it as one practical. His lab partner, who passed, says, \"mate, you're just not a practical person. Stick to theory\".",[["He weakens it: \"this one\".",["IE20"],"His partner has turned one event into a type of person. He does the same weakening aloud, lightly: \"I failed this one. I'd done it twice\". He changes \"never\" to \"this time\", and \"a kind of person\" to \"a technique I haven't practised\"."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"The remark is meant kindly, and it still tells him to give up. He says what is valid (IE24): the fail was real, the cause was lack of practice, and practice can be had. He would not tell a friend to drop half of chemistry over one titration."],["He lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"\"Not a practical person\" is his partner's thought. He watches it go by and does not take it home (DT19). If he had accepted it, it would have ended the loop before it began."]]],["He accepts the fail and books the resit. The date comes back: it is the same morning as his organic chemistry exam.",[["He tells the department and asks for another date.",["IE5","ER12"],"A new problem goes back to the start of the loop. He describes it: two assessments, one slot. The goal is to sit both. The first solution is to ask the people who made the clash (IE5), with the facts and a clear request. Departments fix timetable clashes all the time."],["He makes pros and cons of each order.",["DT5"],"If the dates can't move, he has a choice to make, and stress pushes him toward a quick one. He writes four boxes for each option (DT5): which to sit and which to defer, and what each costs now and later. He decides from the page, in wise mind."],["He accepts a heavy week, and plans it.",["DT13","ER18"],"It may be that both stay where they are, back to back. He accepts the week as it is (DT13) and copes ahead (ER18): what he revises on which day, and when he sleeps. A hard week that he has planned is manageable."]]]]}},{"id":"ER13","title":"fitting emotions: act on the urge, solve, or avoid","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When an emotion fits, the solutions come in an order. Why does acting on the urge come first, and why may it bring only short relief?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's fear of failing a placement exam fits the facts. What does she do?","o":[["She acts on the urge in a useful way: she studies the weak topics hard.",["ER13","ER12"],4,"When an emotion fits, the solutions come in an order: act on its urge, change the situation, avoid or leave it, change how she reads it. Her fear fits, because the exam is hard and her scores are below the line. Fear's urge is to get away from the threat. Here the way to get away from failing is to get better, so acting on the urge usefully means preparing. That is also the second solution, changing the situation: the situation is weak topics, and she changes it one topic at a time (ER12). Opposite action would be the wrong tool. It is for fear that does not fit."],["She reads the situation in the kindest way the facts allow: she has six weeks and a list of topics.",["ER13","ER7"],6,"The last solution in the order is to put on rose-coloured glasses: read the situation in the kindest way the facts allow. That is different from pretending. The facts stay: the exam is hard and she is behind. Within those facts there is a harsh reading, \"I'm going to fail\", and a kind one, \"I have six weeks and I know exactly what to study\". Both fit (ER7). The kind one is at least as true, and it leaves her able to work."],["She accepts the fear as fitting, and keeps preparing.",["ER13","ER22"],1,"A fitting fear is not a malfunction. She does not try to talk it down, because it is right. She lets it be there as a wave (ER22) and prepares alongside it. Acting on the urge may bring only short relief, and the fear will be back tomorrow. She tolerates studying while afraid, for six weeks."],["She doesn't sit the exam.",["ER13","G4"],6,"Avoiding or leaving the situation is on the list of solutions, so this is not absurd. It is the third option taken before the first two were tried. The threat was failing to get the placement, and not sitting the exam guarantees that outcome. Acting on the urge in this form defeats the urge's own purpose."]],"e":[["She studies the weak topics hard for two weeks. Her practice scores move by one mark.",[["She changes how she studies: past papers instead of notes.",["ER13","ER12"],"The fear still fits, and the solution is not working, so the loop returns (ER12). She was changing the situation with the wrong lever: rereading notes feels like work, and it does not train her to answer questions. She changes the method and keeps the hours as they are. A first try may only improve things, and one mark is information about the method."],["She asks for help with one topic.",["IE5"],"Two weeks of solo effort with no movement suggests there is a gap she can't see from inside. She asks one clear, small thing (IE5): \"could you go through one question on this topic with me?\" The fear, used well, is pushing her toward the people who can help."],["She records each score, and looks at the trend.",["G15"],"One mark in two weeks looks like nothing, and it may be noise or the start of a slope. A record of each paper will tell her which (G15): the date, the score, and what she did differently. She decides on the trend over several papers."]]],["She reads it kindly. Then a friend who sat it last year says, \"honestly? It's brutal. Half of us failed\".",[["She checks the facts: past pass rates.",["ER7"],"\"Half of us\" is one person's memory of a bad day. The pass rate is published (ER7). If it is 50 percent, the fear fits at that size, and she needs to know. If it is 75, her friend has rounded down. The kindest reading the facts allow depends on having the facts."],["She copes ahead for a hard paper.",["ER18"],"Suppose it is brutal. She rehearses that paper (ER18): a first page of questions she can't do, and what she does next. She finds the ones she can do, breathes, and keeps moving. She will have met a hard paper once already when the real one comes."],["She lets the fear rise and fall.",["ER22"],"The word \"brutal\" sends the fear up. It fitted before, and it fits a little more now. It is still a wave (ER22). She lets it peak before she opens a book, so that tonight's revision is done by her and not by the panic."]]],["She accepts the fear and keeps preparing. The night before, it is 1 a.m. and she is wide awake.",[["She aims for rest, not sleep.",["ER21"],"\"I must sleep or I'll fail\" is a new threat, and it keeps her awake. She drops the demand. Rest is something she can do, and sleep is not (ER21). She lies still with her eyes closed. A night of rest carries a person through an exam better than a night spent fighting for sleep."],["She uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"A fitting fear the night before sits in the body as tension. She goes through the muscle groups, tensing and releasing on one word (DT7). It does not argue with the fear, which is right. It lowers the arousal that the fear is riding on."],["She accepts a tired morning, and plans breakfast.",["ER19","DT13"],"She may sleep four hours. She accepts that (DT13). Less sleep leaves her more vulnerable tomorrow. It does not make the exam any harder (ER15). She plans the things still in her control: a proper breakfast (ER19), and leaving early."]]]]}},{"id":"ER14","title":"lowering vulnerability: A, B, C, and PLEASE","marks":[],"practice":["each morning :   plan  𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼  by the hour ;   each night :  what was done ;   mood at start and end ,  0 to 100","over the weeks :   done / planned ↑   ⇒   ν ↓"],"u":"Why does each of A, B, C and PLEASE lower vulnerability on its own, and more of them lower it further?","tree":{"t":"Terry is going into exam term. What does he do?","o":[["He puts all four in place: one pleasant thing a day, one hard task a day, a cope-ahead plan, and sleep and meals.",["ER14","ER16","ER18","ER19"],3,"Vulnerability to emotion mind is lowered by four things: Accumulate positives, Build mastery, Cope ahead, and PLEASE for the body. Each one in place lowers it, and the more in place, the lower. They do different work. Pleasant events build a life worth living (ER16). Mastery guards against helplessness and hopelessness (ER18). Coping ahead means the hard moment has been met once already. The body's basics keep the thresholds high (ER19, ER15). He sets all four before the term starts, while it is still easy to plan. That is trying harder in a form that prevents trouble."],["He reminds himself that each one helps on its own, and the term feels less daunting.",["ER14"],2,"A list of four can read like a test with four ways to fail. The footing says otherwise: each one in place lowers vulnerability, and one more never raises it. It is a count (G2), and it is not a pass mark. With one in place he is better off than with none. He wants to do the term well, and he can now see that as something he adds to. Before, it looked like something he could fall off."],["He accepts that not all four will hold every week.",["ER14","DT13"],1,"Exam term will knock some of them out. There will be a week with no time for anything pleasant, and a night with four hours of sleep. He accepts this ahead of time (DT13), so that a missed week is expected and does not look like a collapse. Whatever holds in a given week still lowers the hill. He tolerates the plan being imperfect, and that lets him keep it."],["He puts none of them in place.",["ER14","G4"],6,"It makes sense: all four take time, and time is what exam term has least of. But vulnerability lowers both thresholds (ER15). The same bad mark will hit harder, and his skills will fail sooner. He saves an hour a day and loses days to moods he had no protection against."]],"e":[["He puts all four in place. In week two his grandmother is taken to hospital, and sleep and the pleasant things go first.",[["He keeps the two that still hold.",["ER14","G2"],"Two of four is a degree. It is not zero (G2). He can still do one hard task a day, and he can still cope ahead for the hospital visits. Each one that stays in place lowers vulnerability on its own. He protects those two and does not write off the whole plan."],["He tells his tutor about the crisis.",["IE5"],"A family crisis is exactly what tutors need to be told about, and early. He describes it and asks clearly for what he needs (IE5). It goes against the pull to seem fine (G10). It also changes his environment, which is half of what makes him vulnerable (G9)."],["He accepts a harder week.",["DT13"],"This week will be worse than he planned, and he accepts that (DT13). The sadness about his grandmother fits (ER8), and it is not something to regulate away. He lowers what he expects from the week. He does not lower his opinion of himself."]]],["He knows each one helps. A week into term he has started none of them, and he says to himself, \"I can't even look after myself\".",[["He picks the smallest: one meal at a regular time.",["ER19","ER18"],"Four things were too many to start at once. He picks one small enough that he can't fail at it: lunch at one o'clock (ER19). Doing it for three days is a small piece of mastery (ER18), and mastery is what answers \"I can't even\"."],["He runs a missing-links check.",["G14"],"He takes the four questions in order. Did he know what to do? Yes. Was he willing? Yes. Did it come to mind at the right time? No. The days went by, and he remembered at midnight. He stops at that first no (G14). The fix is a cue: an alarm at one that says \"lunch\". Willpower was never the missing piece."],["He asks a friend to join him for one.",["IE16","IE5"],"A pleasant event planned with someone is harder to skip (ER16). He asks plainly (IE5): a walk on Thursdays. It puts one of the four in place and adds a second person to his exam term. Friends are also on the list of what makes a life worth living."]]],["He accepts that not all four will hold. By week three none of them does, and he is living on coffee and four hours' sleep.",[["He starts again with one.",["ER14","DT16"],"None is where he is. It does not have to stay that way. He turns the mind (DT16) and puts one back: sleep, because the body comes first. He does not need to rebuild all four tonight. One in place already lowers the hill."],["He checks what knocked them out.",["G13"],"He writes a short chain. What was the first thing to go? Sleep, in week two, for an essay. What followed? He was too tired for the hard task, then too low for anything pleasant, and then he had no plan. The key link was the first late night (G13). He protects that link from now on."],["He lets the week be what it was.",["M5","DT13"],"The three weeks have happened. Rating them a failure adds nothing (M5). He accepts them (DT13). He was doing the best he could under a load that was too heavy, and he needs to do it differently from tonight (G6)."]]]]}},{"id":"ER15","title":"vulnerability lowers the hill: two thresholds","marks":[["θ₀","the strength at which an emotion fires"],["odds","chance it happens over chance it does not"],["∝","grows in step with","r06stat.html","6·stat · How Do You Pick a Fair Sample?"]],"practice":[],"u":"Vulnerability lowers both thresholds. Why does it not make the event any stronger?","tree":{"t":"Terry slept four hours before a chemistry exam. What does he do?","o":[["He eats breakfast and arrives early to give himself margin.",["ER15","ER19"],4,"An emotion fires when the push of an event gets over a threshold, and above a second, higher threshold, skills break down. Vulnerability does not make the event stronger. It lowers both thresholds, as a catalyst lowers the hill a reaction must climb. Four hours' sleep has lowered his. He can't raise them by will. He can stop them dropping further and keep the pushes small: breakfast puts back one of the body's basics (ER19), and arriving early removes one event, the rush. He did not choose the short night. The morning is still his to arrange."],["He reminds himself the exam is no harder; he is only more reactive today.",["ER15"],6,"Vulnerability does not change what the event is. The paper is the paper it would have been after eight hours' sleep. What has changed is how easily things tip him over. So when the first hard question brings a surge, he can read it correctly: the surge is the short night, and it says nothing about the question. The cause is in his thresholds. Knowing that keeps him from adding a second fear, that the exam is impossible, on top of the first (ER5)."],["He accepts he will feel more fragile today.",["ER15","DT13"],1,"The four hours are a fact, and no skill adds sleep before nine o'clock. He accepts that today he will be closer to the edge (DT13): quicker to panic and slower to settle. Because he has accepted it ahead of time, the fragility will not take him by surprise. He goes in knowing how he is today."],["He skips the exam.",["ER15","G4"],6,"With the thresholds lowered, his fear of the exam fires more easily, and the fear says to run. That makes sense as a reading from inside a tired body. But the exam is no harder than it was. He would be turning one bad night into a failed module because of a feeling the night itself produced."]],"e":[["He eats and arrives early. Then the invigilator announces a forty-minute delay, and the corridor fills with people comparing what they revised.",[["He uses the wait for paced breathing.",["DT6"],"A corridor of anxious students is a row of small pushes, and his threshold is low today. He steps aside and breathes out longer than in (DT6). It keeps his arousal below the first threshold, so he walks in with some room to spare."],["He reviews his one-page summary.",["ER18"],"He prepared the page for this kind of gap. Reading it is a small act of mastery (ER18), and it holds his attention on what he knows. The corridor talk would put it on what other people know."],["He accepts the delay.",["DT13","ER15"],"A delay is an event of ordinary size. On four hours' sleep it feels like an outrage, and that is the lowered threshold talking (ER15). He recognizes it and accepts the forty minutes (DT13). Being indignant would use up energy he needs for the exam."]]],["He reminds himself he is more reactive. Outside the hall a friend makes a harmless joke about his hair, and he snaps, \"just leave me alone!\"",[["He apologizes briefly.",["IE10","ER11"],"The snap went against his values, so a little guilt fits, and guilt's action is repair (ER11). He makes one sentence of apology and does not grovel (IE10): \"sorry, I slept badly. That wasn't about you\". The repair takes ten seconds, and then it is not on his mind in the exam."],["He notices the vulnerability, and takes it slower.",["ER15","M4"],"The snap is data. It shows him how low the threshold is this morning. He observes that without commentary (M4). Then he adjusts by giving himself a wider margin: fewer conversations, and slower answers. He is treating himself as a tired person would sensibly be treated."],["He does not judge the slip.",["M5"],"\"I'm a horrible friend\" would be a second emotion set off by the first (ER5), and he has no threshold to spare for it. What happened is that a tired person snapped. He leaves it as a description and does not rate it (M5)."]]],["He accepts feeling fragile. He turns over the paper, and question one is on his weakest topic.",[["He skips it and comes back.",["DT11"],"On a normal day this would be an annoyance. Today it is a push toward the second threshold, where his skills break down. He steps away from it and does one thing in the moment (DT11), a question he can do. He will come back to the hard one when he is further from the edge."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"The surge has arrived in his body. He takes three slow breaths (DT6) before he reads anything else. He is bringing his arousal back below the line while his skills still work. Half a minute later it might have been too late for that."],["He lets the jolt pass.",["ER22"],"He expected to be fragile, and here is the jolt. It is a wave, and today it is a tall one (ER22). He watches it peak without believing what it says about the whole paper. It falls, and question two is still there."]]]]}},{"id":"ER16","title":"accumulate positives now","marks":[],"practice":["∀ ☼ :   a⁺ planned ,  a⁺ done ,  mindful 0 to 5 ,  worries let go 0 to 5 ,  pleasant 0 to 100"],"u":"Why drop \"deserving\" and \"not deserving\"? Why do small pleasant events add up only when there are many, varied, over time?","tree":{"t":"Claire's revision weeks have no pleasant events at all. What does she do?","o":[["She plans one pleasant thing a day for a week: a walk, a call, a meal out.",["ER16","ER14"],3,"The practice is to do one pleasant thing each day, be mindful of it while it happens, and not worry about when it will end, whether she deserves it, or what is expected next. She picks from a long list and plans the week ahead, with someone if she can. Planning matters, because in revision weeks nothing pleasant happens by accident. Small events add up only when there are many of them, varied, over time, so seven different ones are better than one big one. This is the A of ABC, and it lowers her vulnerability for everything else (ER14)."],["She drops \"I don't deserve a break\".",["ER16","M5"],7,"The footing is blunt about this: drop deserving and not deserving, because that is judging. \"Deserve\" is a rating (M5), and it has been keeping every pleasant thing out of her weeks. The causes are plain enough. Somewhere, rest had to be earned by results (G11). A pleasant event is not a prize. It is maintenance, like sleep. Once the judgment is gone the guilt goes too, and she can take the walk without having to pay for it afterwards."],["She takes one pleasant moment and is mindful of it, worries and all.",["ER16","M2"],1,"She does not wait for a clear mind before she has something good. She takes one moment, a coffee in the sun, and puts her attention on it on purpose (M2). The worries come along. When the mind goes to the negative she brings it back, even if feeling better feels like giving in. She tolerates having a nice minute while there is revision waiting. Even a hard life has room for a pleasant event, and a little helps."],["She keeps grinding with no breaks.",["ER16","G4"],6,"It feels like the responsible choice, and in the short run more hours do mean more pages. But a life with nothing pleasant in it is a vulnerable one. Her mood sinks and her focus goes, and the hours produce less. Avoiding painful things can mean avoiding pleasant ones too. She has cut out everything that was not revision, and what is left is not working either."]],"e":[["She plans a pleasant thing each day. On the first walk she spends the whole forty minutes running through what she has not yet revised.",[["She brings her attention back to the walk, again.",["ER16","M2"],"A pleasant event only counts if she is there for it. The instruction covers this: when the mind goes to the negative, bring it back. She notices the list in her head and returns to her feet and the trees and the air, on purpose and now (M2). She may do that thirty times in one walk, and each return is the skill."],["She notices the worry and lets it pass.",["DT19"],"\"You should be revising\" is a thought (DT19). It will keep coming, because it has been in charge for weeks. She does not argue with it or obey it. She lets it go past like a cyclist on the path and carries on walking."],["She keeps the plan.",["DT16","G15"],"One worried walk is one trial (G15). It does not show the skill has failed. The plan is seven days, and the effect comes from many and varied events. She turns the mind back to the plan (DT16). Tomorrow's is a phone call, which leaves less room for the list."]]],["She drops \"I don't deserve it\" and takes an afternoon off. A course-mate sees her in town and says, \"must be nice to have time off\".",[["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"The comment is the judgment she had just put down, coming back in someone else's voice. It is their thought (DT19), and it may be envy or tiredness. She does not take it home with her. Whether the afternoon was right for her was never theirs to rule on."],["She validates herself.",["IE24","ER16"],"She says to herself what is valid (IE24): rest is part of studying well, and she planned this. Deserving does not come into it (ER16). The course-mate lives by the rule Claire has just dropped, and so the afternoon looks like cheating to them."],["She says, kindly, \"it helps me study\".",["IE10"],"One truthful sentence, with no apology and no edge (IE10). She does not owe a defence, and she does not need to pretend she was on an errand. Said kindly, it may also give the course-mate permission she didn't know she wanted."]]],["She takes a pleasant moment: tea on the step, in the sun. Her phone buzzes: a deadline has been moved forward two days.",[["She finishes the moment, then reads the message.",["M5","DT11"],"One thing at a time (M5). The message will say the same thing in four minutes. She finishes the tea with her attention on the tea (DT11). She is not avoiding the news. She is refusing to let every buzz own the minute she is in."],["She deals with the deadline, then takes another moment.",["ER12","ER16"],"The deadline is a real problem, and it gets solved (ER12): she re-plans the week tonight. Then the pleasant event is put back in. It is not cancelled as the first casualty of the new plan (ER16). A tighter week is when she needs it most."],["She lets the jolt be.",["ER22"],"The buzz sends a jolt of alarm through the calm she had found. It is a wave (ER22). She lets it rise and fall on the step, in the sun, before she does anything. If she acts once it has fallen, she plans better than she would in the first ten seconds."]]]]}},{"id":"ER17","title":"accumulate positives long term: a value down to one step now","marks":[],"practice":["avoid avoiding :   r ≔ how much ☉ has avoided this ,  0 to 100 ,  in the past and now\nwhy :   hopeless ,  wilful ,  too hard ;   then cope ahead to stop avoiding","∀ ☼ :   v ,  g ,  the act done today ,  the next act"],"u":"How does a value differ from a goal? How can you tell a value is really yours?","tree":{"t":"Jean values learning for its own sake. What does she do?","o":[["She picks one goal, reading one paper outside her syllabus a week, and reads the first today.",["ER17"],2,"A value is a way of living, with a direction and no end. A goal is reached and done. The method is to pick a value, then goals that make it part of her life, then break one goal into small steps and take the first one now. Value: learning for its own sake. Goal: one paper a week outside the syllabus. First step: today's paper, open on the desk. A step that feels too big is broken smaller, and if there are too many steps she keeps to one. She starts from a value already present in her life, so this is carrying on something she has. It is not a new start."],["She checks that the value is hers, not her parents', and feels steadier in it.",["ER17"],7,"A value counts only if it is really hers and not held for someone's approval. The test is whether she would live by it even if no one could know. She imagines reading the paper with nobody ever finding out: no credit, and no one to be proud of her. Would she still want to? Yes. Then it is hers. Finding where a value comes from works better than assuming, and the answer steadies her, because a value she holds for herself does not depend on anyone's praise."],["She accepts that grades will compete with it, and keeps a little room for it.",["ER17","M10"],1,"A value she lives by has to compete with her other aims, and she accepts that (DT13). A balanced life holds pairs without leaning all the way to either end (M10). Here the pair is need and want, grades and learning. She does not demand the whole week for curiosity. She keeps one hour that grades can't claim. A lasting life worth living rests on living her values, and one hour a week is enough to keep this one alive."],["She drops it for grades alone.",["ER17","G4"],6,"Grades are counted, and curiosity is not, so it makes sense to feed the one that is counted. But a goal is reached and done, and then what? The value was what the grades were for. Dropping it leaves her with achievements and less and less reason to want them. That is how a life goes flat (M8)."]],"e":[["She reads one paper outside the syllabus. She understands the abstract and almost nothing after it.",[["She picks an easier one for next week.",["ER17","ER18"],"A step that feels too big is broken smaller. The value is learning, and it is not served by suffering through pages she can't follow. She finds a review article at her level. Mastery wants hard-but-possible (ER18), and the first paper was only hard."],["She asks a lecturer for a reading suggestion.",["IE5"],"Someone already knows the path from where she is to that paper. She asks plainly (IE5): \"I tried this and got lost. What should I read first?\" Most lecturers are delighted by this question. Asking is also a step taken in the direction of the value."],["She accepts that it was hard, and keeps the value.",["DT13","ER17"],"Not understanding is what learning feels like from inside. She accepts that the paper beat her this week (DT13). A value has a direction and no end, and one hard paper does not disprove it. She would still want to learn if nobody were watching (ER17)."]]],["She checks that the value is hers. Over dinner her parents say, \"that's lovely, but it won't get you a job. Focus on your marks\".",[["She keeps it, kindly.",["IE10","ER17"],"FAST says to stick to her values (IE10). She does not need to win the argument or hide the reading. She tells them one truthful thing, warmly: \"I'll keep my marks up, and I'm keeping this too\". The value passed its test, and it is hers whether or not they approve."],["She validates their worry.",["IE22"],"Their worry makes sense. They want her to be safe, and jobs are a real concern. She can say so and mean it (IE22), without agreeing that the reading has to go. Validating them lowers the need on both sides to prove who is right."],["She lets the disagreement be.",["DT13"],"They may never see it her way. She accepts that (DT13). Two people can love each other and hold different values (IE19). She does not need the dinner table to agree before she opens the next paper."]]],["She keeps room for it. At mid-term her grades have dipped by a few marks, and she wonders if the reading is to blame.",[["She sets a time limit for outside reading.",["M10","ER12"],"A goal must be possible, and it must not make her life worse if she meets it. If the reading has grown past its hour, she puts it back inside one (ER12). She is balancing the pair, and she is not giving one side up (M10). An hour a week is still a value lived."],["She checks the facts: is the reading the cause?",["ER7","G13"],"\"The reading did it\" is a guess. She checks (ER7): which marks dipped, and what else changed in those weeks? A short chain on the lowest mark (G13) may point at a missed lecture or a bad week of sleep. She finds the cause before she cuts anything."],["She accepts a small dip for something she values.",["DT13","ER17"],"Suppose the reading does cost her two marks. She rated her values for importance, and this one came high (ER17). A small cost that she has chosen on purpose is a trade she can accept (DT13). It would be different if the dip were large, or if she had not chosen it."]]]]}},{"id":"ER18","title":"build mastery; cope ahead by rehearsal","marks":[["ϖ","a plan"],["W̃","an imagined world"]],"practice":["∀ ☼ ,  hour by hour :   planned ( 𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼 )   ,   done ( 𝖺 ,  𝖻 ,  𝖼 )","cope ahead :   helped ,  true or false"],"u":"Why make the hard thing harder as you succeed, and easier when it is too hard? Why rehearse in the present tense, from inside?","tree":{"t":"Evita has a mock interview for medical school next week. What does she do?","o":[["She does one hard-but-possible prep task each day: a timed answer, a mock with a friend, a reading.",["ER18","ER14"],3,"Mastery is built by doing something each day that is hard, but possible. She makes it harder as she succeeds and easier when it is too hard. Mastery is feeling competent and in control, and it guards against helplessness (ER14). She plans for success: Monday's task is one timed answer, and she does not start with a full mock. She raises the level through the week. Seven tasks, each a little past the last, mean that she arrives at the interview with seven experiences of having managed something hard."],["She rehearses in the present tense, from inside, with the nerves that are likely to come, and feels readier.",["ER18"],5,"Coping ahead has steps. She names the emotions and urges likely to get in the way: fear, and the urge to gabble. She writes the plan in detail. Then she imagines the interview now, in the present tense and from inside her own eyes, not watching herself. She rehearses acts, thoughts, words, and how she says them. She rehearses coping with the hard moment, including the nerves. Then she relaxes. A skill has to be learned in the setting that needs it, and rehearsal brings that setting to her a week early."],["She accepts that some nerves will stay.",["ER18","ER22"],1,"No amount of preparation takes all the fear out of an interview that matters. She accepts that (DT13). The fear fits, because something she wants is at stake (ER8). If rehearsal leaves fear, the footing says to ask again what the threat is. She asks. The threat is \"they'll decide I'm not good enough\". That is a real possibility, and she can live with it. The nerves are a wave she will bring into the room (ER22)."],["She doesn't prepare.",["ER18","G4"],6,"Not preparing protects her from one thing: trying her hardest and still failing. If she does not try, a bad result proves nothing about her. It makes sense as armour. But it gives up feeling competent and in control, which is what mastery is, and it leaves her to meet the hard moment for the first time in the room."]],"e":[["She does a hard task each day. On day four it is a full mock with a friend, and she falls apart on the ethics question.",[["She makes the next task easier.",["ER18"],"The rule has two directions: harder as she succeeds, easier when it is too hard. Day four was too hard, and that tells her about the task. It does not mean anything is wrong with her. Tomorrow's is smaller: three ethics questions, untimed, one at a time. Hard but possible is a range, and she has just found its top."],["She does not judge the miss.",["M5"],"\"I'm hopeless at ethics\" is a rating. \"I had no structure for that question\" is a description, and it has a fix in it (M5). She keeps the judgment that tells one thing from another and drops the one that scores her."],["She keeps going.",["DT16","G15"],"Three good days and one bad one make a record of four trials (G15). The plan was for seven. She turns the mind back to it (DT16). Mastery fades if it is never rewarded, so she counts the three as well as the one."]]],["She rehearses thoroughly. On the day, the panel's second question is one she never rehearsed: \"tell us about a time you were wrong\".",[["She takes a breath and answers honestly.",["DT6","IE10"],"She takes one slow breath first (DT6), which looks like thought and steadies her. Then the truth: FAST says be truthful, no excuses (IE10). She tells a real story of being wrong and what she did about it. That is what the question was asking for."],["She says what she does know.",["DT11","ER18"],"She rehearsed more than answers. She rehearsed how she speaks under pressure: slowly, one point at a time (ER18). That carries over to a question she has never seen (G1). One thing in the moment (DT11): she gives the first sentence, and then the next."],["She lets the jolt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"The surprise sends a jolt through her. It is a wave (ER22), and it passes in the time it takes to say, \"that's a good question\". Because she is not fighting the jolt, her attention is free for the answer."]]],["She accepts the nerves. On the day they are far bigger than she expected, and in the waiting room her leg won't stop shaking.",[["She uses paced breathing outside the room.",["DT6"],"Accepting the nerves never meant leaving them at any height. They have gone above the useful level (ER1). She breathes out longer than in, for two minutes, in the corridor (DT6). Her body comes down a notch, and that is enough to walk in on."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"Her hands are clenched in her lap. She opens them on her knees and lets her face soften into a slight half-smile (DT18). The body tells the brain that she is willing to be here. It is a skill she can use in a waiting room without anyone noticing."],["She lets them ride along.",["ER22","DT13"],"The shaking leg is what the fear is doing in her body. She accepts it as it is (DT13) and stops trying to hold it still. The nerves rise and fall in waves (ER22), and she will walk in on one of them. Interviewers have seen a great many nervous candidates."]]]]}},{"id":"ER19","title":"the body: PLEASE","marks":[["🩺","a physical illness, treated"],["🍽","eating"],["🛌","sleep"],["🏃","exercise"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a body out of balance raise vulnerability? Why does looking after it count as building resilience?","tree":{"t":"Mike runs on energy drinks, skips meals and sleeps little during finals. What does he do?","o":[["He fixes sleep first: lights out at midnight every night.",["ER19","ER21"],3,"PLEASE has five parts: treat physical illness, balance eating, avoid mood-altering substances, balance sleep, get exercise. The more of the five hold, the less vulnerable he is. He is failing three: eating, substances (the caffeine counts), and sleep. He starts with one, and sleep is the one the others lean on. The method is a regular pattern, the same time every night (ER21). A body out of balance lowers his thresholds (ER15). The same exam question will tip him further on four hours than on seven."],["He sees the crash is caused by his body, not his ability, and the self-blame lifts.",["ER19"],6,"At 3 p.m. each day his focus collapses, and he has been reading that as \"I can't do this degree\". A body out of balance raises vulnerability, and his has had no lunch, five hours' sleep, and three cans of stimulant followed by their comedown. The crash has a full physical explanation. Every state is caused (G6), and this one was caused by Tuesday. The self-blame was aimed at his ability, and the cause was in his habits. Habits can be changed."],["He accepts finals will be tiring, and keeps one meal fixed each day.",["ER19"],1,"He will not live well this fortnight, and he accepts that (DT13). He does not let all five parts go because he can't keep all five. He holds one: lunch, at one o'clock, every day. One of five is a degree (G2), and it gives the day one fixed point. He puts up with being tired and under-slept, and he keeps a small anchor in place."],["He keeps going as he is.",["ER19","G4"],6,"It works, for a while. The drinks buy hours, and the hours feel like progress. That makes sense when time is short. But each can borrows from the next day, and the debt comes due around exam three. He gets more hours and gets less out of each one."]],"e":[["He sets lights out at midnight. His roommate revises aloud until two, with the lamp on.",[["He asks the roommate for quiet after midnight.",["IE5"],"Sleep depends on the room, and the room is shared. He asks clearly, in daylight (IE5): the facts, his exam dates, and one request. He adds what is in it for the roommate, an offer to trade for mornings. The roommate is also in finals, and he is doing the best he can (G6)."],["He uses earplugs and an eye mask.",["ER12","ER19"],"The problem is light and noise, and he can solve part of it alone (ER12). The conditions for sleep are dark, quiet and cool, and he can come close to them with earplugs and a mask. The roommate's revision does not have to end for him to sleep."],["He accepts one bad night.",["DT13"],"Tonight may not work. He accepts that (DT13) and does not lie there furious, which would cost him more sleep than the lamp does. If he can't sleep, he does not catastrophize (ER21). One short night in a regular pattern does him far less harm than he fears."]]],["He sees the crash as his body. But at 3 p.m., staring at the same page, the feeling of being a failure is as strong as ever.",[["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"He understands the cause, and the feeling has not caught up with the understanding. He says to himself what he would say to a friend (IE24): \"you've been running on empty for a week. Anyone would feel like this\". The feeling makes sense as an effect of the crash. It is still not a fact about him."],["He checks the facts.",["ER7"],"What is the evidence for \"failure\"? One unread page at 3 p.m. (ER7). What is the evidence against? Two years of passed exams. The feeling arrives at the same hour every day, and that timing points to blood sugar. His prospects do not change at three o'clock."],["He lets the feeling pass.",["DT19","ER22"],"\"I'm a failure\" is a thought that the low produces (DT19). He does not argue with it or build on it. The heaviness that comes with it is a wave (ER22). He eats something, waits twenty minutes, and looks at the page again."]]],["He keeps lunch fixed at one. On Thursday a lab overruns to half past two, and he has eaten nothing since seven.",[["He keeps a snack in his bag.",["ER19","ER12"],"The fixed meal has a weak point, which is days he does not control. He solves for it (ER12): something to eat in his bag, every day. Balanced eating does not need a table. It needs him to have thought ahead."],["He doesn't judge the miss.",["M5"],"One missed lunch does not mean the plan has failed. \"I can't even keep one meal\" is a judgment (M5). What happened is that a lab ran late. He eats at half past two, and the fixed lunch is back tomorrow."],["He tells the lab demonstrator he needs a break next time.",["IE5"],"He can make a small, reasonable ask (IE5): \"if we run past one, can I step out for five minutes?\" It is no imposition to ask. Looking after his body sometimes means saying what it needs out loud (IE10)."]]]]}},{"id":"ER20","title":"the nightmare protocol","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why change the nightmare before the harm happens? Why start with an easier one?","tree":{"t":"Zoe dreams every night that she is late for an exam she hasn't studied for. What does she do?","o":[["She writes the dream down, changes it before the harm (she arrives early, and knows the answers), and rehearses it.",["ER20"],5,"The protocol has an order. She writes down the recurring nightmare, as it happens. She chooses a change, and puts it before the harm happens. In her dream the harm is the moment she sees the clock, so the change goes before that: she wakes in good time. The change can be outlandish. She writes the new dream out in full, with a peaceful ending. Then she rehearses the changed dream and relaxes afterwards, every night and every day. This is coping ahead for something that happens in her sleep (ER18). The dream is hers, so she can only do the work on it herself."],["She gives the new dream a peaceful ending, and wakes less shaken.",["ER20"],6,"The instruction is to end the dream with peace, and it matters, because she has been waking each morning at the worst point with her heart pounding. The rehearsed version does not stop at \"I arrived on time\". It goes on: she hands in the paper, walks out into the sun, and feels done. The change can also reach beliefs about herself. In the new dream she is someone who is prepared. Mornings that begin from that ending are calmer."],["She accepts the dream for now, and starts with an easier one.",["ER20","DT13"],1,"The protocol says to start with an easier nightmare and work up. With exams two weeks off, the exam dream is her hardest. She accepts that it will keep coming for now (DT13). It is a dream. It tells her about her fear and nothing about her revision. She practises the method on a milder dream first. She puts up with the bad nights while she learns the tool."],["She stays up to avoid sleeping.",["ER20","G4"],6,"If sleep is where the dream is, staying awake keeps it away. That makes sense for one night. But lost sleep raises her vulnerability (ER19, ER15), and that makes the fear behind the dream stronger. She ends up with less sleep and a worse dream when sleep does come."]],"e":[["She rehearses the new dream at bedtime. That same night the old one comes back, clock and all.",[["She keeps rehearsing every night and day.",["ER20"],"The protocol asks for rehearsal every night and every day, because one rehearsal is set against months of the old dream. One night back in the old version is one trial (G15). She rehearses again at breakfast and again at bedtime. The new dream has to be more practised than the old one."],["She makes the change easier.",["ER20","ER18"],"Perhaps the change was too large a jump for the dream to take. She tries a smaller one, placed earlier: in the dream she looks at her phone, and the exam is tomorrow. It is the rule of mastery again: easier when it is too hard (ER18)."],["She lets the fear pass after waking.",["ER22"],"She wakes with her heart racing. The fear is a wave left over from the dream (ER22). She lies still and feels it fall. She does not get up and start revising at 4 a.m., because that would teach the dream that it was right."]]],["She gives it a peaceful ending, and the dream softens. But she still wakes at 3 a.m. each night, wide awake.",[["She aims for rest.",["ER21"],"The dream is improving, and the waking is a separate problem. At 3 a.m. the aim is rest and not sleep (ER21). She stays in bed, lets her thoughts drift, and does not catastrophize about the lost hours. Thoughts in the middle of the night read differently in the morning."],["She uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"Her body has woken into alertness. She works through the muscles, tensing and releasing on her one word (DT7). The protocol itself says rehearse and then relax, so this is a step she already knows."],["She writes the dream down, and goes back to bed.",["ER20","ER21"],"If a fragment of the dream is what woke her, two lines on paper get it out of her head. That is step one of the protocol, and it is also what she does with any solvable worry at night (ER21). She can work on the changed version tomorrow. Tonight she goes back to bed."]]],["She accepts the dream for now. In the week before exams it gets worse: now she is late and has also lost her voice.",[["She checks her body: sleep, food.",["ER19"],"Dreams get worse when vulnerability rises. She checks the basics (ER19). In exam week she has been drinking coffee at nine at night and skipping dinner. The dream may be telling her about her body as much as about the exam."],["She tells someone.",["IE5","IE16"],"A nightmare kept to herself gets larger. She tells a friend, in plain words (IE5). Saying it aloud in daylight often shrinks it, and the friend may have the same dream. She was carrying it alone because that was her habit (G11)."],["She starts the protocol on an easier dream.",["ER20"],"She accepted waiting, and she still has the method. She picks a milder recurring dream and works it through: she writes it down, changes it before the harm, gives it a peaceful ending, and rehearses it. Success on an easy one builds the skill, and it is evidence that her dreams can be changed."]]]]}},{"id":"ER21","title":"sleep: what to do instead of ruminating","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why keep the bed only for sleep? Why aim for rest and reverie, and not getting up for the day?","tree":{"t":"Randy lies awake before a deadline. What does he do?","o":[["The worry is solvable, so he writes a two-line plan, then lies down again.",["ER21","ER12"],4,"What to do when awake depends on which of three states he is in: calm, anxious, or worrying about something solvable. He sorts first. His worry is the deadline, and it has a solution, so the rule is to solve it. He does not need the whole plan (ER12). Two lines are enough to let his mind put it down: \"conclusion first thing, references after lunch\". Then he goes back to bed, because a bed is only for sleep. He has taken the problem out of his head and put it on paper."],["He doesn't catastrophize about the lost sleep, and the worry about sleep fades.",["ER21","ER7"],6,"He now has two worries, the deadline and the sleeplessness: \"if I don't sleep I'll ruin it\". The second is the one keeping him awake. The rule is that if he can't sleep, he does not catastrophize. He checks the facts (ER7). He has worked on short sleep before, and one bad night does not ruin a day's writing. When the second worry is taken away, his arousal drops, and sleep has a chance."],["He aims for rest, not sleep, and stays in bed.",["ER21"],1,"The footing says to aim for rest and reverie, and not to get up for the day. Sleep can't be commanded, and trying to command it is effort, which wakes him up. Rest can be chosen. He lies still and lets his mind wander. He accepts being awake (DT13) and does not give up on the night."],["He gets up and works until dawn.",["ER21","G4"],6,"Getting up feels productive: if he is awake anyway, why not use it? It makes sense. But the bed stops being a place for sleep, and the next night his body expects the same. Work done at 4 a.m. has to be redone at ten. He gives up the night and writes the next day tired."]],"e":[["He writes the plan and lies down. Ten minutes later the worry is back, turning over the same two lines.",[["He reminds himself the plan is written, and returns to rest.",["ER21","ER3"],"The worry has been solved, so what is left is rumination. It pretends there is still an answer to find (ER3). He answers it once: \"it's on the paper\". For looping thoughts, the instruction is to put attention on the body sensation under the worry (ER21). He finds the tightness in his chest and stays with that."],["He uses paired relaxation.",["DT7"],"A looping mind sits in a tense body. He goes through the muscle groups, tensing and releasing on one word (DT7). It gives his attention a slow, dull task and lowers his arousal. The loop can't run as easily on a relaxed body."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"The two lines keep presenting themselves. They are thoughts now, and they are not tasks (DT19). He watches them come round and go by. He does not get up to check the paper. Thoughts in the middle of the night read differently in the morning."]]],["He doesn't catastrophize. He also only sleeps three hours, and wakes at six feeling it.",[["He plans tomorrow's essentials only.",["ER12","DT11"],"The problem has changed, so he solves the new one (ER12): what must be done today, on three hours? The conclusion and the references. Everything else is cut. One thing at a time (DT11). A short list is one a tired person can finish."],["He has breakfast and a short walk.",["ER19"],"Sleep was one of the five body basics, and he has lost it for today. He puts two of the others in place, food and movement (ER19). The more of them hold, the less vulnerable he is. He goes easy on the coffee, because it would make tonight's sleep worse."],["He accepts a tired day.",["DT13"],"He will be slower today and things will tip him more easily (ER15). He accepts that (DT13). He did the right things last night and still slept three hours. Both are true, and neither makes the essay impossible."]]],["He stays in bed resting. His mind will not drift: it races, faster and faster, from the essay to his degree to his whole future.",[["He does cold water on his face, then back to bed.",["DT6"],"He has moved from the calm state to the anxious one, and that state has its own instruction: cold water and paced breathing. He splashes cold water on his face, which slows the heart (DT6), and then gets straight back into bed. It takes two minutes, and it is not getting up for the day."],["He gets up for twenty minutes, then tries again.",["ER21"],"If lying there has turned into a fight, the bed is becoming a place for fighting. He leaves it for a quiet, dull task in low light and comes back when he is sleepy. This is for the calm-and-awake state. He comes back to bed and does not sit down at the laptop."],["He lets the racing be.",["ER22","DT13"],"Trying to stop a racing mind makes it race faster. He accepts that it is racing (DT13) and watches it as he would a wave (ER22), with his attention on his breath and not on the content. \"My whole future\" is a 3 a.m. thought, and he leaves it unanswered."]]]]}},{"id":"ER22","title":"mindfulness of the current emotion: a wave","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why does a wall built to keep an emotion out keep it in? How does accepting an emotion leave the pain and drop the suffering?","tree":{"t":"Sarah's panic rises as she turns over the exam paper. What does she do?","o":[["She starts with the question she knows best, so marks go on the board while the panic settles.",["ER22","DT4"],5,"She is not her emotion, and she need not act on it. The panic's urge is to freeze or to bolt. She stops (DT4), steps back for one breath, and then does something that is neither: she writes an answer she knows. The wave goes on rising and falling under her pen. She has not blocked it, and she has not obeyed it. Met without acting on them, emotions turn out not to be catastrophic. She learns that in the exam hall, with marks on the page as proof."],["She reminds herself she is not her emotion, and the panic loosens its grip.",["ER22","M1"],6,"For a moment the panic was everything: she was panic. Observing it makes a gap. There is panic, and there is someone noticing it. She holds her mind, and the emotion does not hold her (M1). She stays with the body sensations, curious: a fast heart, cold hands, a tight throat. Described like that it is an event in her body with a cause, the exam paper. It is no longer a verdict on how the exam will go. The grip was the fusion, and it loosens when she sees the panic as something she has."],["She lets the panic be there and rides the wave for a minute, without blocking it.",["ER22"],1,"The instruction is to watch the emotion rise and fall like a wave, and not to block it, push it, hold it, or make it bigger. A wall built to keep an emotion out keeps it in. So she puts her pen down and gives it one minute. It climbs, peaks, and begins to drop, as it always does. Accepting it leaves the pain and drops the suffering. That one minute costs her less than twenty minutes of fighting would."],["She fights the panic until time runs out.",["ER22","G4"],6,"Fighting makes sense: the panic is in the way, so she tries to get rid of it. But suppressing an emotion increases suffering, and the fight is a second struggle on top of the exam. Always having to control an emotion is a way of being controlled by it. The panic lasts as long as the fight does, and that is the whole exam."]],"e":[["She starts with the question she knows. Two answers in, she reaches question three, and the panic comes back as strong as before.",[["She moves to another question she knows.",["DT11","ER22"],"The wave has come round again, and that is what waves do. She uses the same move: one thing in the moment (DT11), a question she can start. She does not have to act on the panic, and she does not have to finish question three now. She will come back to it when the wave is lower."],["She breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"A second wave on top of a tired body can climb higher than the first. She lowers the body directly: four slow breaths, out longer than in (DT6). She is not blocking the emotion. She is lowering the arousal it rides on, so that this wave is one she can ride."],["She notices it, and rides it again.",["ER22","DT16"],"\"It's back, so the skill didn't work\" is a thought, and a wrong one. The skill was never to end the panic. It was to ride each wave. This is a new fork, and she turns the same way (DT16). She gives it thirty seconds of watching, lets it peak, and picks up her pen."]]],["She reminds herself she is not her emotion. The panic keeps growing anyway: her vision narrows, and the words on the page swim.",[["She checks: past the breakdown point?",["ER23"],"Past a certain strength of emotion, ordinary skills break down. Swimming words and a narrowing view are signs of it. She checks honestly. If she is past that point, observing will not be enough, and crisis skills come first. Knowing which side of the line she is on tells her which skill to use."],["She does paced breathing.",["DT6"],"The emotion has gone too high for mindfulness alone, and the body is where she can still reach it. Slow breathing, out longer than in, for two full minutes (DT6). It is a crisis skill, to be used and then set down. When the words stop swimming, she goes back to the paper."],["She lets it peak.",["ER22"],"Every wave has a top. The fear now is of the panic itself: \"what if it never stops?\" It always comes down. She stays with the sensations and does not add to them. She feels the peak pass, and that weakens her fear of the next one."]]],["She lets the panic be, and it takes longer to fall than she hoped. When she looks up, there are twenty-five minutes left for three questions.",[["She answers what she can, fast.",["DT11","ER12"],"The panic is lower, and the problem now is time. She solves that (ER12): she finds the parts worth most marks and writes them first, in note form if she has to. One thing in the moment (DT11). Twenty-five minutes of clear work can earn a lot."],["She accepts a lower mark.",["DT13"],"The lost minutes are gone. \"I should have started sooner\" is a fight with the past, and it would take more of her time (DT13). She accepts that this paper will not be her best. That frees all twenty-five minutes for writing."],["She runs STOP: pen down, breath, then write.",["DT4"],"Seeing the clock sets off a new surge, and the urge is to scribble anything. She stops, takes one breath, and observes: three questions, twenty-five minutes, eight minutes each (DT4). Then she proceeds on purpose. Two seconds of stopping gets her a plan."]]]]}},{"id":"ER23","title":"past the breakdown point: a region where skills are not defined","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why work out your breakdown point while calm? Why check first that you really are past it?","tree":{"t":"Terry can't think at all ten minutes into a final. What does he do?","o":[["He checks he is past his breakdown point, uses TIP (paced breathing), then goes back to the paper.",["ER23","DT6"],4,"Past a certain strength of emotion, skills break down. The first step is to check that he really is past it: he reads the same line four times, his hands shake, and he could not say what question one asks. Yes. In that region, problem solving and checking the facts do not work, so he stops trying to use them. Crisis skills come first, to bring him back below the line: he puts the pen down and breathes out longer than in for two minutes (DT6). Then the rest. The order matters, and he was not given this state by choice. He still has to be the one who brings himself back."],["He checks it isn't just that the skills feel too hard, and finds he can still think a little.",["ER23"],6,"The footing asks for a second check: is he past the point, or do the skills only feel too hard? The two feel alike from inside. He tests it with something small: can he read one sentence and say it back? He can. So he is below the line, though close to it. That changes what it means. He is frightened, and he has not broken down. He can still think, and a frightened person who can think can start with the easiest question."],["He sits with it, knowing it always comes down.",["ER23","DT2"],1,"Sometimes the instruction is just to sit with it, because it always comes down. He does not fight, and he does not flee. Crisis skills are working if things are not made worse, even when he feels no better (DT2). For three minutes he makes nothing worse: he stays in his seat and writes nothing wrong. The peak passes, because the body can't keep it up."],["He walks out.",["ER23","G4"],6,"Past the breakdown point, the urge is all there is, and the urge says leave. It makes sense. He can't think, so he can't weigh it. Walking out turns ten bad minutes into a failed paper. It is the one move that makes things worse, and not making things worse is what crisis skills are for (DT2)."]],"e":[["He uses paced breathing for two minutes. He still can't think: the words are just shapes.",[["He tries cold water in the break, if allowed.",["DT6","ER23"],"TIP has more than one part, and he has tried one. Cold water on the face slows the heart faster than breathing does (DT6). He asks for a toilet break, which invigilators allow, and uses the tap. He is still past the point, so he is still using crisis skills only."],["He tenses and releases muscles.",["DT7"],"He can do this in his seat without anyone seeing: fists, shoulders, thighs, tensed hard and released on a breath out (DT7). The panic has the body braced, and this gives the bracing somewhere to go. It is paired relaxation, which is the P of TIP."],["He waits it out, knowing it falls.",["DT2","ER22"],"Two minutes was not enough, and this is still not permanent. The test for a crisis skill is whether things have been made worse, and they have not (DT2). He watches the clock for three more minutes, riding it (ER22). It always comes down."]]],["He checks, carefully. He really is past the point: he can't hold a sentence in his head at all.",[["He uses crisis skills only.",["ER23","DT2"],"The check has told him which toolbox to use. He does not try to reason with it, plan, or talk himself round, because those skills do not work here. He uses TIP, and then a single point of attention: his feet on the floor (DT2). When he can read a sentence again, he will switch back."],["He tells the invigilator he feels unwell.",["IE5"],"It is true, and it is the right person to tell. One plain sentence (IE5). Invigilators have procedures for this: a break, some water, a note on his paper. Asking goes against the pull to sit there looking fine while he goes under (G10)."],["He accepts a lost section.",["DT13"],"Some of this paper will not get done. He accepts that now (DT13), and that takes away one source of the panic, the demand that he rescue all of it. He has gone from needing to save the whole paper to needing only to come back for part of it."]]],["He sits with it. It does come down, as promised. When his mind clears, there are twenty minutes left.",[["He answers the questions worth most.",["ER12","DT11"],"He is below the line, so the ordinary skills work again, and he uses them at once. The problem is twenty minutes (ER12): which questions carry the most marks for the least writing? Then one at a time (DT11)."],["He doesn't judge the lost time.",["M5"],"\"I wasted an hour\" is a judgment, and it would raise the emotion he has just ridden down (M5). What happened is that his body went past its limit and came back. He does what works now and leaves the verdict out."],["He plans for next time with his tutor.",["ER18","IE5"],"The footing says to know his own breakdown point, worked out from past episodes while calm. Now he has an episode to work from. Afterwards he tells his tutor what happened (IE5), and they plan ahead (ER18): the early signs, and what he does at the first of them."]]]]}},{"id":"ER24","title":"troubleshooting: the first check that fails","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why fix the first check that fails, and not the one that seems biggest?","tree":{"t":"Claire tried opposite action for her fear of speaking in seminar, and it didn't work. What does she do?","o":[["She walks the checks: she did it halfway. She does it all the way next time.",["ER24","ER10"],3,"When a skill does not work, she goes down a list of checks in order and fixes the first one that fails. One: is her body looked after? She slept and ate, so yes. Two: was it the right skill, done to the letter? She stops here. She did speak, but in a whisper, looking at the desk, with \"this is probably wrong, but\" in front of it. Opposite action is all the way: acts, body, words and thoughts (ER10). Hers was the act without the rest. The second check fails, so she does not go on to the others. Next time she lifts her head, uses a clear voice, and leaves out the disclaimer."],["She sees one try as one trial, not proof that it fails, and the frustration drops.",["ER24","G15"],7,"\"It didn't work\" is a conclusion from one seminar. A skill claims \"often\", not \"always\", and one miss does not break it (G15). Troubleshooting assumes that a failed try has a cause that can be found. It is a list of six possible causes, and \"the skill is useless\" is not on it. Looking for which one it was works better than blaming the skill or herself. The frustration came from reading one trial as a verdict."],["She accepts that it takes many tries.",["ER24","DT16"],1,"Opposite action is repeated until the emotion changes, and an old habit takes much practice (ER10). She accepts that this fear is years old and one seminar is one seminar. Each week is a fork where she chooses again to speak (DT16). She tolerates the fear still being there at week three. Its being there does not mean she is failing. She is partway through."],["She stops speaking in seminar.",["ER24","G4"],6,"She tried and it did not help, so stopping makes sense. But the fear now has new evidence: \"I even tried, and it was awful\". Avoiding gives relief, and the relief rewards the avoiding (IE25). She has drawn her conclusion at the first failed check, and has not looked to see which check it was."]],"e":[["She does it all the way: head up, clear voice, no disclaimer. Afterwards the fear is exactly as strong as before.",[["She checks the next item: has she repeated it enough?",["ER24"],"The second check passes now, so she goes on down the list. Three: is the emotion being rewarded somewhere? Four: was she willing, or only going through it? Five: was the emotion too high for the skill? Six: is a myth in the way? She finds one at six: \"if I'm still afraid, I did it wrong\". That is a myth (ER4). Opposite action does not promise that the fear is gone after one try."],["She keeps going.",["ER10","DT16"],"Once, all the way, is one repetition. The rule is to repeat it until the emotion changes, and it comes down slowly at first (ER10). She commits to next week's seminar now, while the fear is telling her not to (DT16)."],["She checks whether the fear fits.",["ER7","ER8"],"If a fear will not move, perhaps it fits, and then a different tool applies (ER13). She checks (ER7). Did anyone laugh, or dismiss her? No. The tutor nodded. So the fear does not fit (ER8), and opposite action is the right skill. The check confirms the tool she is using. It also turns up the hidden threat: \"they'll see I don't belong here\"."]]],["She sees one try as one trial. Then her tutor says, in her feedback meeting, \"you really need to contribute more in seminars\".",[["She tells the tutor she is working on it.",["IE5","IE10"],"The tutor sees silence and can't see the work behind it (IE17). She says so, truthfully and without apology (IE10): \"speaking up is hard for me, and I'm practising. I spoke last week\". The tutor now knows something new, and may help by asking her a direct question next time."],["She sets a goal: one comment per seminar.",["ER18"],"\"Contribute more\" is too vague to act on. She turns it into a step that is hard but possible (ER18): one comment, every week, all the way. When that is easy, she will make it two. It also gives her trials something to count."],["She lets the pressure be.",["DT13"],"The comment adds pressure, and pressure feeds the fear. She accepts that the tutor wants more than she can give this week (DT13). She is doing the best she can and she needs to do better, and both hold (G6). She keeps to her own pace."]]],["She accepts it will take many tries. Then term ends, and there are no more seminars until January.",[["She practises in another setting: a society meeting.",["G1","ER10"],"A skill works the same whichever way the situation was reached (G1), and it has to be practised in more than one setting for that to hold. The fear is of speaking in a group, and a society meeting is a group. She does one comment there, all the way (ER10). When January comes she will have kept up the practice."],["She copes ahead for next term.",["ER18"],"She rehearses the first seminar of January, in the present tense and from inside (ER18): the pause, her hand going up, the first sentence, her voice. She rehearses it going reasonably well, and she rehearses a stumble and carrying on."],["She records her progress so far.",["G15"],"Six weeks of tries will fade from memory into \"it didn't really work\". She writes them down (G15): the dates, what she did, and the fear before and after. On paper there may be a slope she could not feel. The record is where she starts from in January."]]]]}},{"id":"ER25","title":"every skill, placed on the model","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Each skill acts at one node of the model in ER5. Pick two skills and name the node each one acts on.","tree":{"t":"A student is using skills the night before an exam. What does she do?","o":[["She places them on the model: cope ahead at vulnerability, check the facts at the reading, opposite action at the act.",["ER25","ER5"],3,"Each skill of this series acts at one node of the model of an emotion (ER5). She lays the evening out along it. Vulnerability comes before any event, and that is where ABC and the body skills act: she eats, and copes ahead for the morning. The event is the exam, and problem solving acts there: her bag is packed and her route is checked. The reading is \"I'll fail\", and checking the facts acts there. High arousal is where the crisis skills and riding the wave act. The urge is to stay up cramming, and opposite action acts there: she goes to bed. Placing the skills tells her which one to use at which moment."],["She sees that any node is a way in, and feels less stuck.",["ER25","ER5"],6,"A change at any part can change the whole emotion (ER5). She had been stuck at one node, trying to argue herself out of \"I'll fail\" and losing. The map shows five other doors. If the reading will not move, the body will: a meal, slow breathing. If the body will not, attention will. She felt stuck because she thought there was one way in and it was locked. There were always several."],["She picks one skill and accepts the rest can wait.",["ER25"],1,"The map has a dozen skills on it, and it is ten at night. She does not need all of them. She picks one node and one skill, sleep (ER19), and accepts that the fear will still be there in the morning (DT13). One thing at a time (M5). She tolerates going to bed with the emotion unsolved, because one skill used properly is worth more tonight than six used badly."],["She uses none.",["ER25","G4"],6,"The night before an exam, skills can feel like one more thing to do. Using none makes sense, since she is tired. But the emotion then runs its own path: the reading feeds the body, the body feeds the urge, and she is up until two rereading notes. Having a skill is not the same as wanting to use it (ER3)."]],"e":[["She places the skills and starts with checking the facts. She checks them, they are fine, and the dread does not move at all.",[["She picks a skill at another node.",["ER25","ER5"],"The model shows that the reading is one node of several. If changing the reading has not changed the emotion, she goes in somewhere else (ER5). The dread is in her body now, so she works at the body node (ER25): food, a shower, slow breathing."],["She troubleshoots the one she used.",["ER24"],"She goes down the checks in order (ER24). Is her body looked after? She has not eaten since noon. That is the first check, and it fails, so she stops there. She can't check the facts well on an empty stomach, and the fix is dinner. Then she tries the skill again."],["She accepts the night.",["DT13"],"Sometimes the dread before an exam does not come down, and the facts still stand. She accepts that she will feel this tonight (DT13). The exam does not require her to feel calm. It requires her to turn up having slept."]]],["She sees that any node is a way in. But she is so tired that every skill on the map looks like work.",[["She picks the body node: sleep.",["ER19","ER25"],"Tiredness is information about which node needs attention. Vulnerability is the first node on the map, and sleep is what repairs it (ER19). The most useful skill tonight is to stop. It acts earlier on the path than any of the others (ER25)."],["She lets herself rest.",["ER21"],"She does not have to get to sleep. She has to lie down and rest (ER21). Rest is the aim, and she does not catastrophize about how many hours she will get. For a tired person, lying down is a skill used correctly."],["She does not judge it.",["M5"],"\"I should be doing more\" is a judgment (M5). What is actually so is that she has revised, she is tired, and the exam is tomorrow. Going to bed does not mean she has given up. She notes the \"should\" and lets it go."]]],["She picks one skill, sleep, and uses it well. The exam the next morning is far harder than anyone expected.",[["She uses a crisis skill in the hall.",["DT6","DT4"],"The emotion jumps to the high-arousal node, and that node has its own skills (ER25). She stops and puts her pen down (DT4), then breathes out longer than in (DT6). When arousal is high the crisis skills come first, and the others work again once she is back below the line (ER23)."],["She answers what she can.",["DT11"],"A hard paper is hard for everyone in the hall. One thing in the moment (DT11): she finds the question she can start, and starts it. Marks on a hard paper come from the parts that can be done, and she slept well enough to find them."],["She accepts the paper as it is.",["DT13"],"This is the paper she has. \"It shouldn't be this hard\" is a fight with the page (DT13), and the page does not change. Once she accepts it, her attention goes back to the questions. The sleep she chose last night is why she has that attention to give."]]]]}}]},{"id":"DT","page":"dbtdt","name":"distress tolerance","footings":27,"steps":[{"id":"DT1","title":"goals: survive it, accept it, be free of the urge's demand","marks":[["χ","a crisis"],["🩹","pain"],["😖","suffering"],["⊒","at least as good as"],["#","the number of"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why does living as if life were all crisis make more crises? Why does freedom need both crisis survival and radical acceptance?","tree":{"t":"Jean treats every quiz as a crisis. What does she do?","o":[["She sorts the term: two real crises (the midterm and the lab report), and the rest ordinary work with a weekly plan.",["DT1","DT2","G15"],3,"Life is not all crisis, and living as if it were makes more crises. A crisis is highly stressful, short, and presses for a fix now (DT2). She puts each item in the term to that test. Two pass. The weekly quizzes do not: they are small, they repeat, and they can be planned for. Crisis skills are for the two. The rest gets an ordinary plan and a record of how it goes (G15). The all-nighters she pulled for quizzes were crisis behaviour used on something that was not a crisis, and they caused the exhaustion that made the next quiz feel like one."],["She drops the reading \"every quiz decides my future\" and puts \"each quiz is a few percent\" in its place.",["DT1","DT8","G1"],6,"The crisis is in the reading. The quiz is the same quiz either way (G1). \"Every quiz decides my future\" is what she tells herself, and that sentence raises the arousal. She puts an effective statement in its place (DT8). She has checked the facts first, and the handbook says three percent. The new sentence is true, and her body believes it after some repetition. To be free is to be at peace whatever the circumstances, and for her that starts with the circumstances being described correctly."],["She lets next week's quiz be stressful, and gets through it without making it worse: no all-nighter, no skipped meals.",["DT1","DT2"],1,"The first goal of the series is to survive a crisis without making it worse. She may not be able to stop feeling that the quiz is a crisis yet. She can decline to act on that feeling. The skills are working when time passes and she has not made things worse, even if she feels no better (DT2). She goes to bed at midnight feeling stressed, and that is the win. She is free of having to do what the urge demands."],["She keeps panicking before every quiz.",["DT1","G4"],6,"Each panic gets rewarded: she crams, she passes, and the panic takes the credit (IE25). That makes sense. But one crisis after another is one end of the third line (G10), and it uses up what she needs for the two things that really are crises. By the midterm she will have nothing left."]],"e":[["She sorts the term. Then, in a week she had marked \"ordinary\", her laptop dies with the only copy of the lab report on it, due tomorrow.",[["She uses crisis skills for this one: STOP, then a plan.",["DT1","DT4"],"This passes the test: stressful, short, and pressing for a fix now. Sorting was never meant to say crises don't happen. It was meant to save the crisis skills for a moment like this one. She stops first (DT4), because the urge is to do six frantic things at once. Then she steps back, observes, and proceeds: what can be recovered, and who needs to know."],["She asks the IT desk and her tutor at once.",["IE5","ER12"],"A crisis that can be solved gets solved. Crisis skills are for surviving the ones that can't be fixed fast (DT2). She makes two clear asks in the first ten minutes (IE5): can the drive be read, and can the deadline move a day. That is problem solving under pressure (ER12), and the early ask is the one that works."],["She accepts the lost work, and starts again.",["DT13"],"The drive may be gone. If it is, the report as she wrote it no longer exists, and no skill brings it back. She accepts that (DT13). \"It's not fair\" is true, and it does not write a single page. She has done the report once, and a second draft goes faster than the first."]]],["She drops the reading. Then a teacher hands back the quizzes and says to the class, \"remember, every mark counts\".",[["She checks the facts: a few percent each.",["ER7","DT1"],"The teacher has said her old sentence in a voice of authority. She checks it again (ER7). Every mark does count, for exactly what it counts for, and here that is three percent. The teacher's sentence and her new one are both true. Only the old reading, \"decides my future\", was false."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"It is a thing teachers say to a room. It was not aimed at her, and in her head it is a thought like any other (DT19). She watches it arrive and leave. She does not rebuild the crisis around it."],["She keeps her plan.",["DT16"],"The old reading has been offered back to her, and this is a fork (DT16). She turns the mind to the plan she made when calm: ordinary work for ordinary quizzes. She was in wise mind when she decided that. One remark at the front of a classroom is not a reason to change it."]]],["She gets through the quiz without making it worse. The next week's timetable has two quizzes, a day apart.",[["She does the same again: one at a time.",["DT11","DT2"],"Two quizzes are two small things. They are not one big crisis. She takes them one at a time, with only the next one in view (DT11). The test is the same as last week: when the time has passed, has she made anything worse (DT2)? She has done it once and knows it can be done."],["She plans the week, one block each.",["ER12"],"Something she can see coming a week ahead is not a crisis, and an ordinary problem gets an ordinary plan (ER12): Monday evening for one, Wednesday for the other. Crisis skills used for every pain turn into avoidance, and the planning never gets learned (DT2)."],["She keeps sleep and meals fixed.",["ER19"],"What made last term's quizzes feel like crises was partly her body: short sleep and skipped meals lowered her thresholds (ER15). She holds the basics steady through a two-quiz week (ER19). With her body looked after, a quiz feels the size it is."]]]]}},{"id":"DT2","title":"when crisis skills apply, and when they do not","marks":[],"practice":["for each crisis :   who ,  what ,  when ,  where  set it off ;   r_before ,  r_after   of distress ∈ [0, 100] ;   skills used ,  and how","effect ∈ {1, …, 5} :   1  «could not stand it one more minute»   …   3  «it helped somewhat»   …   5  «used skills ,  tolerated it ,  resisted the urge»"],"u":"When are crisis skills working, even if you feel no better? Why do they turn into avoidance when used for every pain?","tree":{"t":"Two cases: a missing essay draft the night before it is due, and a dull weekly reading. What does a student do?","o":[["She solves the reading with an ordinary plan (twenty pages a day), and saves crisis skills for the missing draft.",["DT2","G15"],4,"Crisis skills are for when pain is high and can't be fixed fast, or when acting on the urge would make things worse. They are not for everyday problems, or for building a life worth living. She sorts the two cases. The reading is dull, it repeats, and it can be solved, so it gets a plan and a tally (G15). The missing draft at 11 p.m. is short, severe, and presses for a fix now, so it is a crisis. Crises come in two kinds, a strong destructive urge or a big demand she feels too overwhelmed to meet. This is the second kind."],["She names the reading as boring, not a crisis, and the dread drops to a shrug.",["DT2","M4"],6,"She has been dreading the reading as if it were dangerous. She describes it (M4): forty pages, dry, due Monday. The word for that is boring. Boredom is an everyday discomfort, and it calls for none of the alarm that a crisis does. Used for every pain, crisis skills turn into avoidance, and so does crisis feeling. Named for what it is, the reading is a chore, and nobody panics about a chore."],["For the missing draft she uses crisis skills to get through the night, and does not make it worse: no panicked email at 3 a.m.",["DT2","DT4"],1,"The skills are working when time passes and she has not made things worse, even if she feels no better. The urge at 3 a.m. is to send her tutor four paragraphs of apology and excuse. She stops (DT4). That email would be the thing that makes it worse. She brings the pain down to a bearable level with breathing and cold water (DT6), writes what she can, and leaves the email for wise mind in the morning."],["She treats both as crises.",["DT2","G4"],6,"If everything is a crisis, the alarm never goes off, and it makes sense that she is exhausted. The dull reading gets panic it did not need. The real crisis gets what is left over. One crisis after another is one end of the third line (G10), and here she is making some of the crises herself."]],"e":[["She plans the reading at twenty pages a day. Then the missing draft takes the whole week, and by Friday she has read nothing.",[["She catches up the reading in small blocks after.",["DT2","ER12"],"The crisis was real and it came first, which was the right order. Now it is over, and the footing says what comes next: bring the pain down, then use the longer-term skills. The reading is still an ordinary problem with an ordinary answer (ER12): the same pages, spread over next week."],["She asks for the reading notes from a classmate.",["IE5","IE16"],"A week lost to a crisis is a fair reason to ask for help. She asks plainly (IE5), and offers her notes in return another week. This is how study friendships work (IE16), and it keeps one crisis from causing a second."],["She accepts one week behind.",["DT13"],"She is a week behind on a dull reading because she dealt with something urgent. That is the fact, and she accepts it (DT13). Being behind is not a new crisis. It is an everyday problem again, and it gets everyday handling."]]],["She names the reading dull, and the dread drops. By Thursday, with none of it done, it is starting to feel like a crisis again.",[["She describes it again: dull, forty pages.",["M4","DT2"],"The feeling has grown, and the reading has not. She describes what is in front of her (M4): the same forty pages. She puts it to the test for a crisis (DT2). Is it short and severe? No. Does it press for a fix now? It is due Monday. It is still boring, and four days of avoiding it have been added on top."],["She does twenty pages and stops.",["DT11"],"The dread is about the whole forty. One thing in the moment (DT11): twenty pages, tonight, and then she closes the book. Dread that came from avoiding the reading is answered by doing a set amount of it."],["She checks the facts: due Monday.",["ER7"],"What is the threat? \"I'm hopelessly behind.\" What are the facts (ER7)? Forty pages and three and a half days. That is about twelve pages a day. The feeling of crisis came from not looking at the numbers."]]],["She gets through the night without making it worse and hands something in. It is thin, and she knows it.",[["She asks for feedback and a short extension to improve it.",["IE5"],"The crisis has been survived, and now comes the ordinary work. In daylight and with a clear head she writes the email she did not send at 3 a.m. (IE5): what happened, what she handed in, and one ask. It is shorter and better than the one she would have sent in a panic."],["She accepts it's a weak draft.",["DT13"],"Crisis skills promised that things would not be made worse. They never promised a good essay. She kept her side: she handed something in, and she burned no bridge with the tutor. The draft is weak, and she accepts that (DT13). A weak draft handed in is something she can improve."],["She sleeps first, then decides.",["ER19","DT4"],"After a night like that, every decision looks worse than it is. She stops before doing anything (DT4), and looks after her body first (ER19). The effects of crisis skills are short. Sleep is what carries her from the crisis to ordinary life again."]]]]}},{"id":"DT3","title":"targets: on the side of life, then the quality of life","marks":[["Q_life","quality of life"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why do behaviours on the side of life come before the quality of life? Why does whoever helps save a life take on helping to make it worth living?","tree":{"t":"After failing finals, a student writes: \"thinking life isn't worth it, skipping classes, arguing with my parents\". What comes first?","o":[["He tells someone today about the first item (a parent, a doctor, a counsellor, or a crisis line), and leaves the rest for later.",["DT3","IE5"],4,"There is an order of targets. Anything on the side of life comes first, and behaviours that wear down the quality of life come next. Thoughts that life is not worth it are in the first group. The skipped classes and the arguments are in the second. So the list is not three equal problems. It is one that comes first and two that wait. The act that fits the first is telling someone today, in plain words (IE5). While he is alive, life can still get better, and death rules that out in every case. He did not choose to feel this, and telling someone is the part that only he can do."],["He reminds himself that while he is alive life can still get better, and a failed exam can be retaken.",["DT3","DT11"],2,"Suffering is suffering, whatever caused it, and his is real. The claim he holds on to is small and exact: while he is alive, life can still get better. A failed final feels like the end of everything. It is one event, and it has a resit. He does not have to see the whole way forward tonight. He takes one moment at a time (DT11). This does not replace telling someone. It is what he says to himself on the way to doing that."],["He gets through tonight without making it worse, and keeps someone with him.",["DT3","DT2","DT6"],1,"Tonight the aim is the first goal of this series: get through it without making it worse (DT2). He does not stay alone, and that is the main thing. If the feeling surges, he changes the body: cold water on his face, slow breathing (DT6). The skills are working if time passes and nothing has been made worse, even if he feels no better. Tomorrow he tells someone who can help."],["He keeps the first item to himself.",["DT3","G4"],6,"Keeping it to himself makes sense. He may come from a world where feelings were to be coped with alone (G11), and he is ashamed of the failed finals. But this is the one item on the list that should not be carried alone. Carried in silence it gets heavier, and no one near him knows to help. Telling one person today changes that."]],"e":[["He tells a parent. The parent says, \"everyone feels low after exams, you'll be fine\", and changes the subject.",[["He tells someone else today: a doctor, a counsellor, or a crisis line.",["DT3","IE5"],"The parent did not hear it. That is a fact about this one conversation, and it does not make what he said any less serious. A feeling that calls for help was seen and got none (G11). The target has not changed, so he takes it to someone whose work is to hear it (IE5), today."],["He says it again, plainly: \"I've been thinking life isn't worth it\".",["IE5","IE10"],"He may have said it sideways the first time. He says it straight, with no softening (IE10): the exact words. He stays on it like a broken record (IE5). His parent is doing the best they can, and may need to hear it without a cushion to understand."],["He stays with someone tonight.",["DT2","DT3"],"Whatever the parent understood, he does not spend tonight alone. That is getting through without making it worse (DT2). He can sit in the same room without explaining anything further. Tomorrow he tells someone who can help."]]],["He reminds himself that life can get better. At 2 a.m., with the house asleep, the thoughts come back stronger.",[["He calls a crisis line.",["DT3","IE5"],"A crisis line exists for this hour. He does not have to know what to say. \"I'm having thoughts that life isn't worth it\" is enough (IE5). Middle-of-the-night thoughts read differently in the morning (ER21), and the call is how he gets to morning with someone on the line."],["He uses cold water or paced breathing to get through the next hour.",["DT6"],"The aim is the next hour. It is not the rest of his life. Cold water on the face, or breathing out longer than in, brings the body down fast (DT6). The effect is short, so it is followed by the next step, which is reaching a person."],["He wakes someone in his home.",["DT3","IE16"],"This is worth waking someone for. Shame says not to be a burden. The order of targets says this comes first, ahead of anyone's sleep. He knocks on the door. He does not need a speech. \"I need you to sit with me\" is enough."]]],["He gets through tonight, with someone near. In the morning the weight is exactly the same.",[["He tells someone today about the first item.",["DT3","IE5"],"Getting through the night was a crisis skill. It did what it does, which is not to make things worse. It was never meant to lift the weight. That takes help, and help starts with telling someone who can give it (IE5): a doctor, a counsellor, a crisis line. Today."],["He gets through one hour at a time.",["DT11","DT2"],"A whole day at this weight is too much to look at. One hour is not (DT11). He does the next small thing: a shower, some food, a phone call. The test for each hour is that nothing has been made worse (DT2)."],["He keeps someone with him.",["DT3","IE16"],"He does not have to be good company. He only has to not be alone while it is this heavy. He asks someone to stay near, and says why if he can. Having someone there is on the side of life, and that is what comes first."]]]]}},{"id":"DT4","title":"STOP","marks":[["↤","is turned back toward"]],"practice":["the act to stop ;   which of  𝗌 , 𝗍 , 𝗈 , 𝗉  were used ;   r_before ,  r_after  of distress ;   effect 1 to 5"],"u":"Why does stopping come first? What does acting on impulse take away?","tree":{"t":"Mike gets a harsh comment in a group chat and wants to reply at once. What does he do?","o":[["He runs STOP: freezes, steps back, observes, and replies the next day from wise mind with what he actually needs from the group.",["DT4","IE5","M3"],5,"STOP, letter by letter. Stop: he does not act, and his thumbs come off the screen. Take a step back: he puts the phone down and takes one breath. Observe, inside and outside and the people there: he notices heat in his chest, the exact words on the screen, and who else is in the chat. Proceed mindfully, from wise mind (M3): what does he want from this group in a month? That question gives a different reply from the one his anger had ready. The next day he sends a clear ask (IE5). Acting on impulse leaves no time to use a skill, so stopping comes first. He has to learn it where the impulse arrives, with the phone in his hand."],["He notices \"I am angry\" is something he has, not something he is, and the urge to fire back loosens.",["DT4","M1"],6,"The footing says he is not his emotion, and he is in charge of it. \"I am angry\" sounds like an identity. \"There is anger\" describes something passing through him, with a cause: the comment. He holds his mind, and the anger does not hold it (M1). The urge to fire back belonged to the anger. Once he sees the anger as a state he is in, the urge is one option among several."],["He puts the phone face down and lets the anger burn for ten minutes without typing.",["DT4","ER22"],1,"This is the S of STOP, held for ten minutes. The anger fits, since the comment was harsh (ER8), and he does not try to cool it. He lets it rise and fall (ER22), and his hands stay off the keyboard. An emotion readies the body to act (DT6), and he is declining the act. After ten minutes the reply he would have sent no longer seems worth sending."],["He replies in anger.",["DT4","G4"],6,"The reply is fast and feels good for about ninety seconds. It makes sense, because the anger fits. But what an act brings about is not what it was meant to do (IE4). The group now sees two harsh comments, and the second is his. He had no time to use a skill, because he never stopped."]],"e":[["He runs STOP and sleeps on it. In the morning the thread has fifty new replies, and people have taken sides.",[["He replies once, from wise mind, to what matters.",["DT4","M3"],"Fifty replies are fifty invitations to react. He observes before he proceeds (DT4): what was the original issue, and what does he need from the group? He writes one message about that (M3). He does not answer the forty-nine others. Some of the group will read a single calm message more closely than the pile."],["He mutes the thread for a day.",["DT24"],"Every buzz is a new prompt and a new urge. Muting the thread removes the cue (DT24). He will read it once, tomorrow, at a time he chooses. This is taking a step back and making it last a day."],["He lets the jolt rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Seeing his name argued over by fifty messages sends a jolt through him. It is a wave (ER22). He does not have to do anything about the wave. He lets it pass before he decides whether to do anything about the thread."]]],["He sees the anger as a state. Then the same person posts again, sharper this time, with a laughing emoji.",[["He runs STOP again.",["DT4","DT16"],"There is a new prompt and a new urge, stronger than the first. The skill is the same four letters. Each provocation is a fork, and he stops again (DT16). Nobody runs out of chances to use STOP."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"The second jab has reached his body: his jaw is tight and his heart is fast. An emotion readies the body to act, and calming the body makes the act easier to hold back (DT6). He takes four slow breaths with the phone face down."],["He asks the group admin to step in.",["IE14","IE5"],"Twice, with mockery, is past what he should settle one-to-one in public. The chat has someone whose role is to keep it civil (IE14). He sends them a private message with the facts and one request (IE5). That is proceeding mindfully."]]],["He lets the anger burn without typing. A friend messages him privately: \"aren't you going to say anything? Everyone's waiting\".",[["He tells the friend he will reply tomorrow.",["IE10","IE5"],"His friend has added social pressure to the urge. He answers the friend, and leaves the thread alone. He is truthful and brief (IE10): \"I'm angry, so I'm waiting. I'll answer tomorrow\". He does not owe anyone a reply on their timetable (IE5)."],["He keeps to his plan.",["DT4"],"\"Everyone's waiting\" is information about them. It gives him no reason to act. He observes it as part of what is going on outside him, and proceeds as he had decided: no reply while the anger is at its peak. He made the plan when he was calmer than he is now."],["He makes pros and cons of replying now.",["DT5"],"The pressure has made it a real question, so he treats it as one. He fills four boxes (DT5): reply now, or reply tomorrow, with the gains and costs of each. He weighs the long term over the next ten minutes. If replying now wins on paper, he replies from the page."]]]]}},{"id":"DT5","title":"pros and cons, written before the urge","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why write the pros and cons before the crisis, and fill all four boxes? Why weigh the long term above the now?","tree":{"t":"Zoe has the urge to quit her degree after one bad term. What does she do?","o":[["She fills all four boxes (pros and cons of quitting, pros and cons of staying), weighs the long term above the now, and sees her adviser about what can change.",["DT5","IE5"],3,"Pros and cons has four boxes, and the urge only ever fills one: the pros of quitting. She fills all four, and she lists what does follow each choice, leaving out what does not. Relief by Friday goes in one box. Two finished years and the work she wants go in another. Then she weighs the long term above the now. Asked to choose between a good day and a good life, she chooses the good life. The page also shows her what is wrong with staying: one course and one timetable. Those can be changed, so she takes them to her adviser with a clear ask (IE5). She wants to improve, and that is why the bad term hurts (G6)."],["She sees that a bad term and a bad degree are different things, and the urge to run eases.",["DT5","IE20"],7,"The urge speaks in absolutes: \"this degree is wrong for me, it always was\". She weakens it until it fits the facts (IE20). One term went badly. Four went well. \"Sometimes this degree is hard for me\" is true, and it does not tell her to leave. An urge is a crisis when it is strong and acting on it makes things worse in the long run. This one loses strength when the sentence under it shrinks. Her fear of failing is real and has causes (G6). It tells her about this term. It does not tell her about the whole degree."],["She holds off any decision until the end of next term, and lets the doubt ride along.",["DT5","DT11"],1,"She does not solve the doubt and she does not argue it down. She sets a date and carries the doubt to it. One term at a time is one thing in the moment, stretched to fit a degree (DT11). The pros and cons list is written before the urge decides, and she has given herself a term to write it in. People who decide in the worst week of the year tend to decide for that week. In June she will have two terms of facts to go on, and the doubt can come to every lecture until then."],["She quits tomorrow.",["DT5","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The relief would be real and it would arrive tomorrow. That is the pros-of-acting box, and it is the only box the urge has read. A crisis behaviour harms her interest in the short run, the long run, or both. Here the short run feels good and the long run pays for it. She has chosen the good day. The degree might in fact be wrong for her, but she has not weighed that question on paper."]],"e":[["She fills in the four boxes, and the cons of staying look bigger. Her flatmate reads the page over her shoulder and says \"well, that settles it\".",[["She weighs the long term again, carefully.",["DT5"],"The length of a list does not settle anything. Six cons that last a term can weigh less than one pro that lasts a lifetime. She goes through each line and marks it short term or long term. The footing says to weigh what comes long term above what comes now. Her flatmate counted the lines, and she is weighing them."],["She sees her adviser about what could change.",["IE5","ER12"],"Most of the cons of staying turn out to be about two things she could change: a course and a part-time job. So the list has handed her a problem to solve (ER12). She brings the page to her adviser and asks for one specific change (IE5). If the cons shrink, the boxes get filled again."],["She waits a week before deciding.",["DT4","DT11"],"\"That settles it\" is a push to act now. She stops and takes a step back (DT4). The page will read the same in a week, and she may read it differently. She takes the week one day at a time (DT11). A decision this size can wait seven days."]]],["She sees one bad term, and then a second bad week starts. A classmate says \"told you this course eats people\".",[["She checks the facts: one week.",["ER7","IE21"],"The classmate's sentence is stronger than the evidence. She checks: what happened, and how much of it is there (ER7)? There was one bad term and now one bad week. \"The course eats people\" gets weakened to \"this week went badly\" (IE21). The smaller claim is the true one, and she can act on it."],["She does one pleasant thing.",["ER16"],"A second bad week on top of a bad term leaves her with little in reserve, and with little in reserve everything reads worse (ER14). She adds one pleasant thing today, on purpose (ER16). It will not fix the week. It will mean she judges the degree with a little more in reserve than she has now."],["She lets the doubt ride along.",["ER22","DT13"],"The doubt came back with the bad week. She had not expected it to stay away. It rises, and she lets it (ER22). She accepts that this is a week with doubt in it (DT13). She goes to the Thursday lecture with the doubt and takes notes."]]],["She holds off the decision, and her parents push her to decide now. Her father says \"we're paying for this, we need an answer\".",[["She tells them plainly when she will decide.",["IE5","IE10"],"Her father's worry is fair and so is her timetable. She describes the plan, says what she needs, and gives a date: \"I'll decide after the June results, and I'll tell you that week\" (IE5). She is fair to them and to herself, and she does not apologise for taking the time (IE10). They get a date to wait for."],["She validates their worry.",["IE22"],"They are paying, and they are frightened for her. Both of those make sense, and she says so first (IE22). Validating the worry does not mean agreeing to the deadline. Once he has heard \"of course you want to know\", her father finds it easier to hear \"June\"."],["She keeps her plan.",["DT5","DT16"],"She wrote the plan when she was calm. Now there is pressure, and this is the kind of moment the plan was written for. Her father's sentence puts her at a fork, and she turns back toward the plan (DT16). There will be more forks before June. She rehearses the reasons, so that they come to mind when they are needed (DT5)."]]]]}},{"id":"DT6","title":"TIP: change the body to change the emotion, fast","marks":[["β̂","body to emotion"],["♥","heart rate"],["🧊","cold water"],["°","degrees","r05j.html","5j · Ratio, Proportion and Units"],["⚠","caution"]],"practice":["r_before ,  r_after   for   arousal ∈ [0, 100]   and   can stand it ∈ [0, 100] :   0  «I can't stand it»   …   100  «I can surely survive»","distress ∈ [0, 100] :   0  none   …   100  the most ever felt ;   it cannot go past 100"],"u":"How does TIP change the emotion by changing the body? Why follow it with another skill?","tree":{"t":"Randy is too overwhelmed to think in the hour before an exam. What does he do?","o":[["He does paced breathing in the hall, breathing out longer than in, then runs STOP and walks in with a plan for the first question.",["DT6","DT4"],4,"He can't think, so he does not start with thinking. TIP is for when you are stuck in emotion mind and too overwhelmed to think. It works on the body, and it is fast. He breathes deep into the belly, and out for longer than in. Nobody in the hall can see him doing it. Calming the body makes the act easier to hold back, and the act his body is ready for is running. The effect is short, so the footing says to follow it with another skill. He follows it with STOP (DT4): he steps back, looks at what is in front of him, and proceeds with one plan, to read question one twice. He is below the breakdown point now, so his other skills work again (ER23). The exam is his to sit, whoever set it (G6)."],["He splashes cold water on his face beforehand, if nothing makes cold water unsafe for him, and feels his heart slow.",["DT6","ER19"],6,"Cold water on the face calms the body: cheeks and eyes, up to the temples, for thirty to sixty seconds. He knows his own body first (ER19). Cold water is not safe for everyone, and he would ask a doctor before using it if he had a heart condition. He has none. His heart slows. The exam is the same exam, and his arousal has changed. The emotion had readied his body to act, and the water has calmed his body. Every act is caused (G6), and he has just changed one of the causes."],["He sits with the overwhelm, breathing slowly, and goes in anyway.",["DT6","DT2"],1,"He does not wait to feel ready. Crisis skills are working if things are not made worse, even when he feels no better (DT2). He breathes slowly, and the overwhelm stays. He walks in with it. Being overwhelmed and being able to sit an exam are both true of him this morning (IE19). Staying is tolerating, and the only thing he does about the feeling is breathe."],["He leaves before the exam.",["DT6","G4"],6,"It makes sense. His body is ready to run, and leaving ends the feeling within a minute. An emotion readies the body to act, and he has done the act. The relief rewards the leaving (IE25), so the next exam hall will bring the same urge, and it will be stronger. He still has to sit the exam, on a later date."]],"e":[["He does paced breathing, and the hall is too crowded to hear himself think. Two classmates beside him are quizzing each other on a topic he skipped.",[["He steps outside for two minutes.",["ER12","DT6"],"The problem is the noise, and he can solve it (ER12). He walks out of the door and breathes there (DT6). He has not left the exam. He has picked a better place to do the skill. He sets two minutes on his watch and comes back in when it ends."],["He tenses and releases his hands.",["DT7"],"He can't get away from the quizzing, but he can use muscles nobody sees. He makes fists under the desk, holds them, and lets go on the out-breath with the word \"relax\" (DT7). Paired relaxation was practised for a room like this. His hands let go, and his shoulders follow."],["He focuses on one thing: his summary sheet.",["DT9","M5"],"The classmates' quiz is filling his head with a topic he can do nothing about now. He turns his attention to something else on purpose (DT9). He picks one thing, the top line of his own sheet (M5). Short-term memory holds only a little, and he fills it with material he knows."]]],["He splashes cold water, and still feels sick. A friend at the next sink says \"you look awful, are you sure you should sit this?\"",[["He follows it with STOP and a plan for question one.",["DT6","DT4"],"TIP has a short effect, and the footing says to follow it with another skill. His friend's question is a prompt to act on impulse. He stops, steps back and observes (DT4). His heart is slower and his stomach is not. He proceeds with a plan small enough for a sick stomach: read question one."],["He sips water and breathes.",["DT6","ER19"],"The sick feeling comes partly from his body: he ate no breakfast and he is dry. He looks after the body first (ER19). He sips water and breathes out longer than in (DT6). He can still do two letters of TIP at a sink with a friend watching."],["He lets the sick feeling be.",["ER22","DT13"],"The water did what it does, and the nausea is what is left. He accepts that he will sit this exam feeling sick (DT13). The feeling will rise and fall on its own if he neither fights it nor feeds it (ER22). He tells his friend \"I'm sure\", and it is true."]]],["He goes in overwhelmed, and the first question is one he can't do. The student beside him is already writing fast.",[["He skips it.",["DT11"],"He takes one thing in the moment (DT11). Question one is not this moment's thing. He turns the page to a question he can start. He only has to get through this minute, and for this minute he has question two."],["He breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"The blank on question one has pushed his arousal back up, and his neighbour's pen is pushing it further. He takes four breaths with the out-breath longer (DT6). Nobody can see it, and it takes twenty seconds. He reads the paper again with a slower heart."],["He accepts losing it.",["DT13"],"Question one is worth ten marks, and they may be gone. Refusing to accept that would cost him the next twenty minutes as well. He accepts it all the way (DT13). The ten marks still hurt. He stops spending time on them, and he puts the time into the ninety marks that are left."]]]]}},{"id":"DT7","title":"paired relaxation: from sixteen muscles to one word","marks":[],"practice":["practise many times a day   until   out-breath  ⇒  muscles let go   on its own ;   then less often ,  but keep it up","each day :   times practised ;   relaxation  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 100] ;   times used when tense ;   single ,  groups ,  or all at once"],"u":"Why pair each release with the word relax? Why move from single muscles to the whole body?","tree":{"t":"Sarah tenses before every presentation. What does she do?","o":[["She rehearses the talk three times in the room itself, so the room and the words are both familiar.",["DT7","ER18"],5,"Paired relaxation is practised first in a quiet place and then in many places, and the footing gives the reason: a skill has to be learned where it will be needed. She applies the same rule to the talk. She copes ahead (ER18) and rehearses the hard scene in the room where it will happen, three times. The tension had a cause (G6): a strange room and words she had only said to herself. She removes both causes. This changes the situation, and her body has less to brace against. Each rehearsal is also a small piece of mastery, hard but possible."],["She practises the muscle groups at home, then the single word \"relax\", and at the lectern releases on it, and the nerves drop a notch.",["DT7"],6,"This is the skill written out. At home she sits comfortably with nothing crossed. She tenses one muscle group hard, never to a cramp, and releases it on the out-breath with the word \"relax\". When single muscles are easy she moves to groups, and then to the whole body at once. With practice the word alone brings the release. At the lectern she tenses only what others cannot see, her stomach, and lets go on the word. The nerves drop a notch. They do not drop to zero, and the skill does not promise that. Her body learned the pairing over two weeks of quiet practice (G6: new behaviour has to be learned in every setting it is needed)."],["She lets the tension be there, and presents with it.",["DT7","ER22"],1,"She leaves the tension alone. When judgments about it come (\"I shouldn't be this nervous\"), she lets them go, as the footing says to do in practice. The tension is a wave, and it peaks in the first minute and falls (ER22). She speaks through the peak. Being tense and giving a good talk are both possible in the same ten minutes (IE19). Most of the room cannot see what she feels."],["She skips the presentation.",["DT7","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The tension is unpleasant, and skipping ends it at once. The relief teaches her body that the tension was right to sound the alarm (ER10: acting on a fear keeps it). The next presentation will bring more tension. The mark for this one is gone, and she never finds out that the first minute is the worst."]],"e":[["She rehearses in the room, and on the day there are twice as many people. Her tutor has invited the other seminar group.",[["She focuses on three friendly faces.",["DT9","M5"],"Forty faces are too many to attend to. She turns her attention to a part of the room on purpose (DT9). She picks three faces she knows and speaks to one at a time (M5). The rehearsed talk is the same talk, and she is giving it to three people while thirty-seven others listen."],["She releases on \"relax\".",["DT7"],"Her stomach has tightened at the sight of the room. She practised for that. She tenses it a little further on the in-breath and lets go on the out-breath with the word (DT7). Nobody sees it. The word alone brings the release because she paired the two at home."],["She lets the nerves be.",["ER22"],"A bigger room brings a bigger wave. She does not block it or feed it (ER22). She notices it in her chest as she walks to the front, and lets it be there while she opens her notes. It crests about a minute into the talk."]]],["She releases on \"relax\", and it doesn't work this time. Her hands shake as she clips on the microphone, and the technician notices.",[["She keeps practising it at home.",["DT7","G1"],"One failure in the hardest setting is information about the practice. It says nothing against the skill. She had practised in a quiet room, and the footing says to practise in many places after that. A skill has to work in the new setting (G1), so she adds harder settings: standing, then with someone watching. She repeats today's attempt with more practice behind it."],["She uses paced breathing instead.",["DT6"],"The footing says what to do when relaxation raises anxiety: switch to paced breathing. She breathes in for four and out for six (DT6). It is another way of calming the body, and she needs one that works in the next thirty seconds. The technician sees her take a slow breath."],["She presents tense.",["DT13","ER22"],"The word did not work, and her hands are shaking. She accepts that this talk will be given with shaking hands (DT13). The shaking will rise and fall if she lets it (ER22). She holds the lectern and starts on her first line."]]],["She presents with the tension, and loses her place. The room goes quiet, and someone in the second row coughs.",[["She looks at her notes, and goes on.",["DT11"],"She takes one thing in the moment (DT11). Right now she has to find the line, and she does not have to rescue the talk. She looks down, finds it, and says it. The pause felt like a minute to her, and it lasted four seconds for the room."],["She says \"let me find my place\".",["IE10","M4"],"She describes what is happening and says nothing more about it (M4). She does not apologise or joke at her own expense (IE10: no needless apologies). The plain sentence keeps her self-respect, and it tells the room that nothing is wrong. Audiences relax when a speaker stays calm about a small slip."],["She lets the jolt pass.",["ER22"],"Losing her place sends a jolt of heat through her. It is a short, steep wave (ER22). She neither fights it nor reads meaning into it. It passes while she is turning the page, and she picks up the next sentence."]]]]}},{"id":"DT8","title":"rethinking, paired with relaxing","marks":[],"practice":["fear of it happening again ∈ [0, 5] ,  after rethinking ;   relaxation  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 100]"],"u":"When you are this upset, why is there something you are telling yourself? Why say the new statement on the in-breath and relax on the out-breath?","tree":{"t":"Terry tells himself \"if I fail this test my life is over\". What does he do?","o":[["He checks what the test covers (10% of his grade) and books two sessions on his weakest topics.",["DT8","ER7","G15"],4,"The footing says to describe the event and check the facts first. The event is a test on three topics, worth ten percent (ER7). \"My life is over\" does not survive that check. Then he changes the situation. He books two sessions on the topics he is weakest in, and he notes his practice scores before and after (G15), so that he has a record to go on. When he is this upset there is something he is telling himself, and it can be found. He found it and checked it, and then he did something about the part that was true: he is behind on two topics. He wants to improve (G6), and the fear showed that long before he booked anything."],["He writes \"one test is one test\", says it on the in-breath, and relaxes on the out-breath, and the arousal comes down.",["DT8"],5,"This is the skill written out. What he tells himself about the test raises his arousal. He puts an effective statement in its place: \"one test is one test\". He writes as many as he can for the one stressful thought, and picks the one he believes. He says it on the in-breath and relaxes on the out-breath. He practises away from the test, inside the scene with the desk and the paper, and then uses it there. This is coping ahead (ER18). His body needs repetition before it accepts the new sentence, because new behaviour has to be learned (G6)."],["He lets the thought pass without arguing with it, and studies.",["DT8","DT19"],1,"He does not replace the thought and does not believe it. A thought is a sensation of the brain. It is not a fact (DT19). \"My life is over\" arrives, and he notices that it has arrived. He opens the book. It comes back on page three, and he notices it again. He is studying with the thought in the room. The rethinking skill would change the sentence. Here he leaves the sentence alone and changes how he stands toward it."],["He believes it.",["DT8","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The thought feels true because the fear is strong, and a strong emotion makes its own thoughts convincing (ER2). Believing it keeps the arousal high, and he can't study at that level. The test then goes badly, and the thought seems to have been proved right. The handbook, which says ten percent, was never opened."]],"e":[["He books two sessions, and the first one is cancelled. The tutor's email says \"sorry, try again after the test\".",[["He rebooks, and studies alone in the slot.",["ER12"],"The solution failed, so he goes back to the start of the loop (ER12). The problem is the same: two weak topics. He takes the next free session, and he keeps the cancelled hour for the first topic on his own. He still does an hour's work on the topic."],["He asks a classmate to study with him.",["IE5","IE16"],"The tutor is gone, and other people know the topic. He asks one classmate, clearly, for one hour on one topic (IE5). Working beside someone is also how friendships start (IE16). He gets the help, and he may get someone to study with for the next test as well."],["He accepts the change.",["DT13"],"The email is final, and being angry at it changes nothing. \"This shouldn't have happened\" is a second thought, and it raises arousal like the first one did. He accepts that the session is gone (DT13). He loses the hour and nothing more."]]],["He says the new statement, and the old one shouts back. His brother, passing his door, says \"you'd better not fail, Mum will lose it\".",[["He says it again on the next in-breath.",["DT8","DT16"],"The old thought is practised and the new one is a day old. He will have to choose the new one many times before it comes on its own (DT16). He says it again on the next in-breath and relaxes on the out-breath (DT8). His brother has supplied a rehearsal with a real distraction in it."],["He lets the old thought pass.",["DT19"],"He stops fighting it. \"My life is over\" is a sound his brain makes when it is frightened (DT19). His brother's sentence is his brother's fear. He watches both go by. The thought does not have to leave before he can work."],["He checks the facts again.",["ER7"],"His brother has added a new claim: Mum will lose it. He checks that one too (ER7). Last time he did badly, she was worried and she helped him. Both statements, his own and his brother's, are stronger than the facts, and he has the facts on a card."]]],["He lets the thought pass, and it's still there the next morning. At breakfast a friend messages: \"ready for the test that decides everything? lol\".",[["He lets it pass again.",["DT19","DT16"],"The thought was never going to leave after one night. Each time it comes he is at a fork again, and he turns the same way (DT16). The friend's joke is the same thought from another mouth. He notices it and does not argue (DT19). He finishes his toast."],["He writes the new statement on a card.",["DT8"],"Letting it pass has worked for a day, and now he adds the other skill. He writes \"one test is one test\" on a card, with two more effective thoughts under it (DT8). He will say them on the in-breath on the bus. He writes the card before the test so that he has it in the test."],["He does an hour of study.",["DT9","ER18"],"The thought needs attention to keep going, and an activity takes the attention (DT9). He chooses the activity that also builds mastery: one hour on the hardest topic, hard but possible (ER18). At the end of the hour he knows more, and he has not been thinking about his life being over."]]]]}},{"id":"DT9","title":"distract with wise mind ACCEPTS","marks":[["≷","compared with, either way"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is distraction for a crisis and not a habit? Why must a sensation used in a crisis be followed by coming back to the cue on purpose?","tree":{"t":"Claire can't stop crying in the library before a seminar she must attend. What does she do?","o":[["She distracts with an activity (a crossword), goes to the seminar, and comes back to what upset her tonight.",["DT9","DT2"],4,"Distraction is for a crisis, and this is one: the pain threatens to overwhelm her, the seminar is in twenty minutes, and the problem cannot be solved now (DT2). An activity fills short-term memory, so the thoughts that keep firing the emotion stop firing it. A crossword does that. The crying slows because the fuel has stopped. Then she does the second half of the skill, which is the half people leave out: she comes back to the problem later. She sets a time, tonight at eight. Without the return, distraction becomes a habit of avoiding painful emotions, and that is the experiential avoidance that keeps emotions stuck (G10). She uses the skill once, with a plan to come back. The seminar still needs her this afternoon (G6)."],["She compares this with a worse time she got through, and the crying slows.",["DT9","ER2"],6,"Comparison is the C of ACCEPTS. She compares now with a worse time: the week her grandmother died, which she got through. The comparison does not say today's pain is small. It says she has survived more. The emotion is a message to check and it is not a fact about the world (ER2). The message was \"I can't bear this\", and her own history answers it. The situation has not changed, and her reading of whether she can stand it has. Every emotion has a cause (G6), and one cause of this one was the thought that it could not be borne."],["She pushes the upset away for an hour, on purpose, knowing she will return to it.",["DT9"],1,"Pushing away is the P of ACCEPTS. She imagines putting the upset in a box on a shelf for a while. The footing puts limits on this move: it is not the first resort, and it is not for often. She uses it because the seminar starts in twenty minutes and nothing else is quick enough. The upset is on the shelf. It is still hers, and she has neither solved it nor shrunk it. She has put it off for an hour, and she knows she will take the box down. Without that knowledge this would be the inhibited grieving of the biosocial pattern (G10)."],["She goes home and misses the seminar.",["DT9","G4"],6,"It makes sense. She is crying in public and home is private. Leaving is a kind of pushing away, but with no plan to return and with a cost. The seminar is assessed, and her group presents without her. Tomorrow the original upset is still there, and guilt about the seminar has been added to it. A crisis behaviour harms her interest in the short run, the long run, or both (DT5)."]],"e":[["She distracts and goes, and in the seminar the topic brings it back. The tutor asks her directly what she thinks.",[["She focuses on writing notes.",["DT9","M5"],"The topic is a cue, and she can't leave the room. She picks another activity that is available at a seminar table: writing down what is said, word for word (DT9). She does one thing, with all her attention (M5). She answers the tutor from the notes in front of her."],["She steps out briefly and splashes water.",["DT6"],"The crying is close, and it is a state of her body as much as a thought. She says \"back in a minute\" and goes out. She puts cold water on her cheeks and eyes for thirty seconds (DT6). It works fast and it does not last, which is long enough for forty more minutes of seminar."],["She lets it be, and stays.",["ER22","DT13"],"She has distracted once already today, and the footing says not to make it a habit. This time she lets the feeling be in the room with her (ER22). Her eyes are wet and she accepts that they are (DT13). She tells the tutor \"I'm thinking about it\". The feeling passes its peak while someone else is talking."]]],["She compares with a worse time, and it makes her feel worse. Now she is crying about both, and a librarian is glancing over.",[["She switches to a different ACCEPTS move: an activity.",["DT9"],"ACCEPTS has seven moves because no single one works every time. The comparison gave her a second sad memory to think about. She drops it and takes the first letter, an activity (DT9). She re-shelves the six books on her desk in order. Her hands and eyes are busy, and the memories get less of her attention."],["She soothes with one sense.",["DT10"],"Distraction did not work, so she moves to the next crisis skill. She soothes one sense (DT10): the hand cream in her bag, slowly, noticing the smell. This is being gentle with herself. The comparison had felt like a telling-off, and this is the opposite. The librarian sees a student putting on hand cream."],["She lets the crying come for five minutes.",["ER22"],"She has tried to stop it twice. She goes to the stairwell and lets the crying come, with a limit of five minutes on her phone (ER22). Crying that is not blocked rises and falls like any wave. At four minutes it is done, and she has time to wash her face."]]],["She pushes it away for an hour, and comes back to it at night, and it's huge. Her flatmate knocks and asks if she's all right.",[["She writes it down and makes one plan.",["ER12","ER21"],"It is huge partly because it is late and it is all in her head at once. She writes it down. On the page it is three separate things, and one of them can be solved (ER12). She writes a two-line plan for that one, for tomorrow (ER21). The other two wait until morning."],["She calls someone she trusts.",["IE16","IE5"],"She came back to the box as she said she would. She does not have to open it alone. Her flatmate is at the door and has asked. She says what happened and what she needs: \"can you sit with me for a bit?\" (IE5). Being near someone and talking is how closeness is built (IE16), and it is also how a huge thing gets carried."],["She lets it be, and rests.",["ER22","ER21"],"It can't be solved at midnight. She tells her flatmate \"I'm upset, and I'll be okay, thanks\". In bed she is aiming for rest and lets sleep come if it does (ER21). She lets the upset rise and fall without feeding it more thoughts (ER22). She did come back to it tonight, as she had planned."]]]]}},{"id":"DT10","title":"self-soothe with the five senses","marks":[],"practice":["body scan :   Θ moves through the body ,  part by part ;   alone ,  or guided","r_before ,  r_after   for   can stand it ,   negative |e| ,   positive |e|"],"u":"Why balance soothing with the task? Why bring a wandering mind back without harshness?","tree":{"t":"Jean is frazzled after a long day of labs. What does she do?","o":[["She soothes each of the five senses for twenty minutes, then does an hour of her report.",["DT10","ER14"],3,"Self-soothing is one kind thing for each of the five senses. She has soft light for her eyes, one album for her ears, tea for taste, the smell of the tea, and a warm jumper for touch. She is mindful with each, and she notices her body loosen. The footing then says to balance it with the task: soothe, then get to work. Twenty minutes, then one hour of the report. Soothing lowers her vulnerability to emotion mind (ER14), and it lowers the sense of being deprived that makes a long day feel like a punishment. With that lowered, the report is one hour of work. The report is still hers to write (G6). She makes it possible to write it, and then she writes it."],["She is kind to herself, not harsh, about being tired, and the frazzle eases.",["DT10","M5"],7,"Half the frazzle is the labs and half is what she says about herself for being frazzled: \"other people cope, I'm weak\". That sentence is a judgment (M5). She drops it and describes the day: eight hours standing, two failed runs. Self-soothing is being comforting, nurturing, gentle and kind to yourself. For years the people around her answered tiredness with \"stop complaining\", and she learned to say it to herself (G8, G9). Tonight she answers her tiredness the way she would answer a friend's. The tiredness has causes, and her tiredness makes sense (G6)."],["She does a short body scan, one part at a time, and lets the day settle.",["DT10","M1"],1,"The body scan changes nothing. She lets go of past and future, and moves her attention part by part: feet, calves, knees. She brings a child's curiosity to each, and imagines the breath flowing there. Her mind wanders to the failed run. She brings it back without harshness, and the footing calls that the heart of it. This is mindfulness practice (M1): she is paying attention to her body as it is. She has little time, so she scans three parts. The day is still the day it was. She has stopped carrying it clenched."],["She scrolls until 2 a.m.",["DT10","G4"],6,"It makes sense. She is worn out and the phone asks nothing of her. It feels like soothing. But she is not mindful with any sense, and she is not being kind to her body. She is numbing it. At 2 a.m. she is more tired, the report is untouched, and tomorrow's lab starts at nine. Short sleep raises her vulnerability for the whole of the next day (ER19)."]],"e":[["She soothes her senses, and then can't get started on the report. Her lab partner messages: \"sent you my half, where's yours?\"",[["She does five minutes only.",["ER18","DT10"],"An hour is too big to start. Five minutes is hard but possible, which is how mastery is built (ER18). She keeps the tea beside her and the soft light on (DT10). She writes the heading and two sentences. Starting was the hard part, and after the five minutes she carries on."],["She runs STOP and decides.",["DT4"],"The message is a jolt, and her impulse is to reply with an excuse or to shut the laptop. She stops and steps back. She observes: she is tired, the report is due Friday, and her partner is waiting. She proceeds mindfully (DT4) with a true reply: \"mine by tomorrow noon\". She has made the decision on purpose."],["She accepts a lighter evening.",["DT13"],"Some evenings the hour is not there. She accepts that tonight is one of them, without calling herself lazy (DT13). She tells her partner when her half will come. The soothing was not wasted. It was all she had the strength for tonight, and tomorrow she starts rested."]]],["She is kind to herself, and a labmate calls her lazy for leaving early. Two others hear it and say nothing.",[["She validates herself.",["IE24","DT10"],"\"Lazy\" is the sentence she grew up with, said aloud by someone else. She does what she would do for a friend who was called that (IE24). She tells herself the facts: eight hours, and she left at the scheduled end. Her tiredness is valid. She keeps being gentle with herself (DT10), which is harder now and matters more."],["She lets the comment pass.",["DT19"],"The word plays again in her head on the walk home. It is a thought, and thoughts come and go (DT19). She notices \"there's 'lazy' again\" and does not argue with it or believe it. It was her labmate's opinion at six o'clock. It tells her nothing about what she is."],["She tells the labmate she needed rest.",["IE10"],"She is fair, she does not apologise for something that needs no apology, and she is truthful (IE10). \"I left at the end of the session because I needed rest.\" She does not excuse herself or attack. The two who said nothing hear it too. She has kept her self-respect in front of the same people."]]],["She does a body scan, and finds every part tense. Her flatmate puts loud music on in the next room.",[["She tenses and releases each part.",["DT7"],"The scan found the tension, and paired relaxation is the skill that works on tension. She tenses each part a little more, then lets go on the out-breath with the word \"relax\" (DT7). She starts with her hands. The music stays on, and her shoulders come down anyway."],["She has a warm drink.",["DT10"],"Every part is tense and now there is noise. She goes back to the simplest soothing: one sense, one kind thing (DT10). She makes a warm drink and holds it in both hands, noticing the heat. She is choosing to be gentle with herself, and she can do that with music coming through the wall."],["She lets the tension be.",["ER22"],"The scan was for noticing, and she has noticed that her body is tense after a hard day. That fits the facts. She lets the tension be there without adding \"I can't even relax properly\" (ER22). Tension that is left alone tends to ease, and she lets it take its time."]]]]}},{"id":"DT11","title":"IMPROVE the moment","marks":[],"practice":["sensory awareness :   «can you feel …?»  ,  one question at a time ;   𝗈(felt  or  not felt) ;   no right or wrong answer ;   brief :  2 or 3 questions"],"u":"Why is the only pain to survive this moment's? How does each of the seven moves make the emotion less?","tree":{"t":"Evita faces three weeks of back-to-back deadlines. What does she do?","o":[["She lays out every deadline on one page, then takes one thing in the moment: today's deadline only.",["DT11","G15"],3,"The footing says the only pain to survive is this moment's, and remembered and expected pain is extra. Three weeks of deadlines felt at once is nearly all expected pain. She puts every deadline on one page, so that the page holds the three weeks and she does not have to (G15). Then she works on one line of it: today's. Improving the moment is for long-lasting stress, when distracting and soothing do not work, and three weeks is too long to distract through. She is solving the problem as well, because a plan on paper is the problem made smaller. She has to do the work whoever set the deadlines (G6), and she does it a day at a time."],["She finds meaning: each deadline is a step toward her degree.",["DT11","ER17"],2,"Meaning is the M of IMPROVE: find or make a purpose in the pain, and recall your values. Her value is the work she wants to do after the degree, and the degree is how she gets there (ER17). Each deadline is one step toward it. The deadlines are the same. Now each one is a piece of something she chose. She wants to improve (G6), and remembering that changes three weeks of things done to her into three weeks of things she is doing. The tiredness stays, and the resentment goes down."],["She accepts that the three weeks will be hard and tiring, and stops fighting that they are.",["DT11","DT13"],1,"She has been saying \"this is ridiculous, nobody should have this many\". Each time she says it she is tired and angry as well as busy. Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering (DT13). She accepts the fact: three hard weeks, and she will be tired. Two of the IMPROVE moves are acceptance and letting go, and this is one. Accepting does not mean she approves of the timetable (DT14). She has stopped spending energy on the argument, and the weeks need that energy."],["She tells herself it will never end.",["DT11","G4"],6,"It makes sense. On day two of twenty-one, the end is out of sight. But the footing names this sentence: rethink when you tell yourself it is hopeless, will never end, or that you cannot do it. \"Never\" is stronger than the facts, which say three weeks (IE21). Believing it adds despair to tiredness, and despair makes each day's work slower."]],"e":[["She takes today's deadline only, and two land on the same day. Her project partner says \"just pull an all-nighter like everyone else\".",[["She asks for an extension on one.",["IE5"],"Two in one day is a problem, and someone has the power to move one of them. She writes to the tutor: the facts, what she is asking for, and why it helps the work (IE5). She asks for two days. Tutors hear these requests all the time, and she asks early."],["She takes one at a time in the day.",["DT11"],"One thing in the moment still works when a day holds two things. She works on one in the morning and the other in the afternoon, and never both at once. When her mind jumps to the second, she brings it back to the first. The all-nighter would cost her tomorrow's deadline (ER19)."],["She accepts a long day.",["DT13"],"The two deadlines are real and neither will move. She accepts that this will be a twelve-hour day (DT13). Having accepted it, she plans it: food, two breaks, and a finish time. She tells her partner \"I'll do a long day, I won't do a night\"."]]],["She finds meaning, and then a deadline feels pointless: a reflective log nobody reads. A classmate says \"it's just box-ticking, copy last year's\".",[["She finds a smaller meaning: done is done.",["DT11"],"This task will not carry a big meaning. A small one will do. The footing says find or make a purpose, and she makes one: finishing this clears a line off the page. She keeps her own values and does not copy (IE10). She writes it honestly in forty minutes."],["She takes a short break.",["DT11","ER16"],"The pointless feeling is partly tiredness. The V of IMPROVE is a vacation: brief, harmless, with her duties covered (DT11). She takes twenty minutes outside with a coffee, and counts it as one pleasant event for today (ER16). She comes back, and the log still looks dull, but she can do it."],["She lets the pointless feeling be.",["ER22"],"She does not have to feel that the log matters in order to write it. She lets \"this is pointless\" sit beside her while she types (ER22). The feeling comes and goes a few times. The log gets done and the feeling goes with it."]]],["She accepts the three weeks, and gets ill in week two. A housemate says \"you can't afford to be ill right now\".",[["She tells her tutors and asks for time.",["IE5"],"Illness changes the facts, and her tutors can't act on facts they don't have. She tells them today: what is wrong, which deadlines it touches, and what she is asking for (IE5). She asks before she is late. Asking for time is not weakness, and tutors usually allow for illness."],["She rests.",["ER19"],"Treating physical illness is the first letter of PLEASE (ER19). Working through a fever gives poor work and a longer illness. She rests for two days on purpose. Her housemate is right that the timing is bad. She was going to be ill either way, and the two days of rest make it shorter."],["She accepts it.",["DT13","DT12"],"\"I can't be ill now\" is a fight with a fact. She is ill now. She can't solve that by trying, so it calls for reality acceptance (DT12). She accepts it all the way, with the bad timing (DT13). She is left with the illness and has dropped the argument about it."]]]]}},{"id":"DT12","title":"reality acceptance: living a life that is not the one you want","marks":[["↻","turn the mind again"],["½🙂","half-smile"],["🤲✋","willing hands"]],"practice":[],"u":"When is acceptance the skill to use instead of problem solving?","tree":{"t":"Mike missed the cut for the programme he wanted, and appeals are closed. What does he do?","o":[["He solves what is left open: he applies to the next intake, and asks which modules would strengthen his application.",["DT12","IE5"],4,"Reality acceptance is for when problem solving is not working. So he first sorts what can be solved from what can't. This year's place is closed. Next year's intake is open. He asks the admissions tutor plainly which modules would strengthen his application (IE5), and he enrols in two. To change something he first has to accept that it is (DT13). He could not have written that email while he was still arguing that the cut was unfair. The aim of acceptance is less suffering and more freedom, and one freedom is seeing which door is still open. He did not set the cut, and the next application is his to write (G6)."],["He uses half-smile and willing hands when it stings, and the bitterness softens.",["DT12","DT18"],6,"The fourth acceptance skill works through the body. When the sting comes, he relaxes his face and lets the corners of his mouth lift a little. He opens his hands on his knees (DT18). The body tells the brain, and a clenched jaw and fists are how his body refuses the news. Bitterness is a sign of not accepting (DT13). He does not argue with it. He changes the posture that goes with it, and it softens. The rejection is the same. He is in pain and he is not in crisis, which is when the footing says to use acceptance."],["He turns to acceptance for the part that can't be changed, and goes on with the term.",["DT12","DT13"],1,"The footing is about living a life that is not the one he wanted. The appeals are closed, and that part can't be changed. He accepts it completely, in mind, heart and body (DT13). He acknowledges the fact and stops fighting it. He does not say it was fair and he does not say it doesn't hurt. Then he goes to Tuesday's lecture. He will have to turn the mind again, because one choice does not make the next (DT16). Wanting the programme and living well without it are both true of him now (∎)."],["He keeps refusing that it happened.",["DT12","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The loss is large, and saying \"it can't be right\" keeps the sadness away for a while. Fear of sadness is often at the core of not accepting (DT13). But refusing reality does not change it. He rereads the letter, drafts appeals that can't be sent, and misses the deadline for the next intake. He has the pain of the rejection, and the refusing adds to it."]],"e":[["He applies to the next intake, and is turned down again. His father says \"maybe it's time to be realistic\".",[["He asks for feedback on the application.",["IE5"],"Two rejections tell him there is something he doesn't know. He asks the one office that knows: what was weak, and what would a strong application have had (IE5)? He asks once, clearly and politely. Then his next decision rests on what they tell him, and neither his hope nor his father's worry has to settle it."],["He looks for another route.",["ER12"],"The goal was the work the programme leads to. When one solution fails, he goes back to the start of the loop (ER12). He lists other routes to the same work: a different university, a conversion course, a job that trains. Two of them are open now. His father asked him to be realistic, and a list of open routes is realistic."],["He accepts this too.",["DT12","DT13"],"The second no is a second loss, and it needs its own acceptance (DT12). He lets the sadness come, since acceptance of great losses takes time (DT13). He does not have to decide anything this week. He is accepting a fact. He has not given up on the work he wants (DT14)."]]],["He uses half-smile, and a friend asks why he's smiling. The friend has just been complaining about his own bad week.",[["He says he's had bad news, and is coping.",["IE10"],"He is truthful, and he does not need to apologise or explain the skill (IE10). \"I've had some bad news and this helps me cope. I am listening.\" The friend learns two true things. Being honest about it keeps his self-respect and also keeps the friendship."],["He keeps the half-smile private next time.",["DT18"],"The skill says no one else need see it (DT18). A half-smile is slight: his face is relaxed and the corners of his mouth are barely lifted. He had overdone it. With friends he uses willing hands under the table, which do the same job out of sight."],["He lets the moment pass.",["ER22"],"He feels a flush of embarrassment at being caught. It is a small wave (ER22). He says \"just thinking\", and asks his friend to go on. The embarrassment has faded by the next sentence."]]],["He accepts it, and each acceptance letter his friends get stings again. They celebrate in the group chat, and someone tags him by mistake.",[["He turns the mind again at each one.",["DT16","DT12"],"Each letter is a new fork, and \"it should have been me\" is the road he took before. He chooses acceptance again (DT16). The more painful the event, the more turns it takes. This is reality acceptance working as described (DT12), and it does not mean he failed to accept the first time."],["He congratulates them.",["M7","IE7"],"Kindness can be acted before it is felt (M7). He writes \"well done, you earned it\", and he is gentle and interested about it (IE7). The sting is still there when he presses send. He would rather keep these friends than stay away from them for a year, and the kindness gets easier with each message."],["He lets the sting rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Envy and sadness fit the facts: he wanted what they got. He mutes the chat for an hour and lets the sting be what it is (ER22). He does not add \"I'm a bad friend for feeling this\". The sting falls, as it did the last time."]]]]}},{"id":"DT13","title":"radical acceptance: all the way","marks":[["≡","means the same as","r43a.html#0a68e6","43a · Clock Arithmetic"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is pain plus refusing to accept it suffering? What may be accepted, and what may not?","tree":{"t":"Zoe got a bad grade back. Even with a perfect score on the next test, the last essay, and the final, she will cap out at a B+. What does she do?","o":[["She plans the next test, the last essay and the final to the mark, so the B+ she can still reach is the one she gets.",["DT13","ER12","ER18"],4,"To change something, first accept that it is: the plan starts from the cap, not from the A she wanted. From there problem solving (ER12) and mastery (ER18) work together, each test a hard-but-possible step. This is the last footing in small: change standing on acceptance (∎). She did not choose the cap, and the B+ is still hers to earn."],["She accepts that she needs to try harder, do better, and be more motivated, even for a less good result than she hoped for.",["DT13","G6","ER17"],3,"Radical acceptance all the way: of the fact, and of the work it still asks of her. Two assumptions hold at once (G6): she was doing the best she could, and she needs to do better. The feeling changes because the effort is tied to a value (ER17), learning the subject, and no longer to a letter that is gone."],["She stops fighting \"it should not be B+\", and the suffering drops.",["DT13","M5","DT16"],6,"Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering. \"It should not be\" says causes should not have effects, and the grade had causes. Dropping the judgment (M5) leaves the pain and removes the rest. The fight will come back, so this is a turn of the mind she will make again (DT16), not something she does once."],["She gives up because an A is gone.",["DT13","G4","G10"],6,"Giving up makes sense: the goal she built the term around is gone, and the loss wants grieving. But this is a jump to one end of a line (G10): grief held in, shown as not caring. It costs her the B+ too, and the grief waits for her."]],"e":[["The plan works. Her tutor praises her progress and her study group starts asking her for help. Then she is ill the week of the last essay, and even the B+ is out of reach. The praise stops, and the group stops asking.",[["She turns the mind each time \"it should not be\" comes, many times a day at first, and lets the grief come.",["DT16","DT13","G10"],"She can't stay in wise mind all day, and out of it the loss arrives as grief. Grief held in is one end of a line (G10); held long enough, it swings to the other end, one crisis after another. Radical acceptance lets it move: sadness, then calm. One turn does not make the next, so she turns again."],["She drops the rating \"I am nothing to them now\" and describes it: \"they valued my progress, and the progress stopped\".",["M5","M4","G7"],"Worth is a rating in the observer's mind, and any rating can flip (M5): valued today, valued at nothing tomorrow, by a hiccup she did not cause. Described plainly, the silence is a transaction (G7), her sensitivity meeting a setting that pays only for progress. It is not a fact about her."],["She tells one person in the group what happened, and asks to keep studying together.",["IE5","IE16","G11"],"Mastery was carrying the friendship, and mastery is knocked out, so the interpersonal skill has to carry it alone now. A world that goes quiet when you fail teaches you to cope alone (G11). Asking anyway works against that lesson, and one person who answers is enough to begin."]]],["She works harder for the lesser result, and her parents, who boasted about her A average, stop mentioning her grades at all.",[["She validates herself, exactly as she would a friend: \"I am working harder for less, and that is hard\".",["IE24","G11","IE22"],"Their silence is invalidation: the effort is there and gets no answer (G11). Her own validation does not depend on theirs. It keeps the acceptance from turning into self-invalidation, the far end of the first line (G10)."],["She tells them what she is doing and why it matters to her, without asking for praise.",["IE10","IE5","ER17"],"Self-respect is an aim of its own, whatever they say back (IE4). Naming the value puts the effort on her own ground, where a grade average cannot take it away."],["She lets the sadness about their silence rise and fall, and keeps the study plan.",["ER22","DT13","ER18"],"Fear of sadness is often at the core of not accepting. The sadness is a wave. The plan is mastery, and it goes on beside the sadness: acceptance and change held together (∎)."]]],["She stops fighting it. A week later her friend gets the A, and \"it should have been me\" is back, with shame at feeling it.",[["She turns the mind again at this fork, and does not count the return as failure.",["DT16","DT14","M5"],"The more painful the fact, the more turns it takes. Accepting is not approving, and it is not done once (DT14). Judging herself for the return would add a second refusal on top of the first."],["She names both emotions: envy at the A, and shame at the envy. She checks each against the facts.",["ER6","ER8","ER5"],"The shame is a secondary emotion (ER5): it follows the envy and hides it. Named apart, the envy fits (her friend has what she wanted), and the shame does not (she has done nothing against her values). So there are two emotions and two different actions."],["She congratulates her friend and means as much of it as she can.",["M7","ER10","IE7"],"This is opposite action for envy that does not help her (ER10), done as loving kindness one step outward (M7). It keeps the friendship (IE7) at the moment a rating could have ended it, and she finds she is more than the comparison."]]]]},"tree2":{"t":"Terry is lead programmer on the robotics team, getting better every week, and the team is heading for regionals. Then the school cuts the club's funding, and the entry is withdrawn. The mentor moves to another team, and the teammates who praised him stop messaging. What does he do?","o":[["He accepts that the regional entry is gone, then looks at what is still open: he asks the mentor whether two of them can enter the open online round next term.",["DT13","IE5","ER12"],4,"A goal with others, built together with mastery, can become unattainable through a hiccup he did not cause. That is his position exactly. The goal was shared (IE1), the mastery was real and growing (ER18), and the funding cut was nobody on the team's doing. To change something he first has to accept that it is. So he says it as fact, no bigger and no smaller: \"the regional entry is withdrawn, and this team is over\". Refusing reality is a cloud around the pain, and behind the cloud he would have gone on trying to solve the wrong problem, which is how to get the old team back. With the fact accepted, he can see what is still open, and he works on that (ER12). He asks the mentor clearly for one thing (IE5). He did not cause the cut, and what happens next is still his to work out (G6)."],["He drops the rating \"I am worth nothing to them now\" and describes what happened: \"they valued my progress, and the progress stopped\". The worth was their rating, and the shame eases.",["DT13","M5","M4"],7,"Those who valued him for his progress may then value him at nothing. Valued can always become valueless, because the value was a rating in their minds, and a rating can move from good to bad when circumstances change (M5). He had taken their rating as a fact about himself. He accepts only facts, and a judgment is not a fact. So he describes what can be observed (M4): the messages stopped the week the entry was withdrawn. The teammates were there for the regionals, and the regionals are gone. That is a cause. It passes no verdict on him (G6: finding causes works better than judging and blaming). He can program as well as he could a month ago. Undue shame is a sign of accepting what is not a fact, and it eases when the rating is put back where it came from. He is still sad, and the sadness is about a real loss."],["He lets the grief come, and turns the mind toward \"the team is over\" each time \"it should not be\" returns.",["DT13","DT16","ER22"],1,"Not judging gains mindfulness, but nobody is in wise mind all the time. He manages to describe the loss without rating himself, for an afternoon. By evening he is out of wise mind (M3), and the grief comes. Grief held in must resolve, and holding it in is one side of a known pattern: inhibited grieving, paired with one crisis after another (G10). So he does not hold it in. He lets it rise and fall (ER22). The way through is radical acceptance, turning the mind at every fork (DT16). \"It should not be\" comes back many times a day, and each time he turns toward \"the team is over\", in mind, heart and body. Fear of sadness is often at the core, and for great losses acceptance takes a long time. Acceptance brings sadness first and calm afterwards. He changes nothing and solves nothing. He is doing the best he can (G6), and for now that is grieving."],["He holds the grief in, tells everyone he never cared about robotics, and picks a fight with the mentor about the funding.",["DT13","G4","G10"],6,"It makes sense. The loss is large, the people who praised him have gone quiet, and \"I never cared\" keeps the sadness out for a while. Fear of sadness is often at the core of not accepting. But grief held in does not go away. It comes out as crisis (G10), and the fight with the mentor is the first one. Refusing reality does not change it. The entry is still withdrawn, and he now has bitterness and a damaged tie with the mentor as well. Every part of this is caused (G6), and he can turn the mind at the next fork."]],"e":[["The mentor says yes to the online round. Two weeks in, the other teammate leaves for the football season, and the mentor says \"maybe leave it this year, Terry\".",[["He asks the mentor plainly for one thing: a signature so that he can enter alone.",["IE5","IE13"],"The second goal with others has gone the way of the first, through another hiccup he did not cause. Part of it can still be done by one person. He asks for exactly that, clearly (IE5), and he does not soften the ask when the mentor hesitates (IE13). He needs a signature, and he does not need the mentor to be enthusiastic about it."],["He checks which part of the goal was his own: the programming he was getting better at.",["ER18","ER17"],"The team gave him two things, belonging and mastery. The team is gone, and the mastery is his own (ER18). He names the value under it, which is building things that work (ER17). Then he takes one step that needs nobody's approval: he finishes the navigation routine he had started. Nobody is watching his progress now, and he goes on making it."],["He turns the mind again: the second team is over too.",["DT16","DT13"],"This is a new loss on top of the old one, and it needs its own acceptance (DT13). \"It should not be\" is louder the second time. He turns toward the fact again (DT16). The more painful the event, the more turns it takes. He has not failed at accepting. This is what accepting a large loss is like."]]],["He holds the description for a week. Then he sees that the team photo has been taken down from the corridor display, and \"I was nothing to them\" is back at full strength.",[["He notices that he is out of wise mind, and does not count that as a failure.",["M3","M5"],"Nobody is in wise mind all the time. The empty space on the wall put him in emotion mind in a second (M3). He notices where he is. He does not add a second judgment, \"I can't even do this right\" (M5). Noticing that he has left wise mind is how he starts back toward it."],["He validates himself, as he would a friend.",["IE24","G11"],"Nobody else is going to say that this loss was real. For years the people around him answered hurt with \"don't be so sensitive\", and he learned to say it to himself (G11). He says the other thing (IE24): \"I gave that team a year. Seeing the photo gone hurts, and that makes sense\". The hurt fits the facts."],["He lets the grief rise, without rating himself for having it.",["ER22","DT13"],"\"I was nothing to them\" is the grief speaking in the form of a rating. He lets the rating go and keeps the grief (ER22). The photo is down, and that is a fact he can accept (DT13). He stands in the corridor for a minute and feels it, and then he walks to class."]]],["The grief comes in waves for a fortnight. His father says \"it was only a club, time to move on\", and a former teammate walks past him without a word.",[["He turns the mind at this fork too.",["DT16","DT14"],"His father's sentence offers a shortcut: decide it never mattered. That would be refusing the loss while calling it acceptance. Accepting does not mean making light of it (DT14). He turns toward the real fact again: it mattered, and it is over (DT16). He does not need his father to agree."],["He tells his father what the team was to him, without asking him to agree.",["IE10","IE5"],"He is truthful and does not apologise for grieving (IE10). \"It was the best thing in my year. I'm sad about it, and I'm still doing my work.\" He asks for one thing: \"please don't tell me to move on\" (IE5). His father may still think it was only a club. He has now been told what it was."],["He says hello to the teammate anyway.",["M7","ER10","IE7"],"The teammate walking past brings shame, and shame says look down. The shame does not fit, since he did nothing wrong. He acts opposite, all the way (ER10): he lifts his head and says \"hey\", easily (IE7). Kindness can be acted before it is felt (M7). The teammate valued his progress and may not be able to offer more than that. Terry still treats him as a person."]]]]}},{"id":"DT14","title":"what radical acceptance is not; what gets in its way","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is accepting not approving, and not giving up on change? When would accepting turn you passive?","tree":{"t":"Randy fears that accepting a bad mark means he has given up. What does he do?","o":[["He accepts the mark, and plans the next essay with his tutor's comments beside him.",["DT14","IE24"],3,"The footing says acceptance alone changes nothing, but makes change possible. He takes both halves. First he accepts: the mark is 48, and the essay had the faults the tutor listed. What cannot be changed is the past and this present moment. Then he changes what can be changed, the next essay, with the comments open beside him. He could not read those comments while he was still arguing with the mark. He also validates himself (IE24): the disappointment makes sense, because he worked on it. He would turn passive only if he also refused to accept that he dislikes the mark, that change is possible with effort, and that trying is worth it. He accepts all three. He is doing the best he can, and he needs to do better (G6)."],["He sees that accepting is not approving, and the fear lifts.",["DT14","M5"],7,"The fear rests on one belief, that accepting means approving. The footing names it as one of the three things that get in the way. Accepting is not approving, liking, being passive, or giving up on change. He can say \"the mark is 48\" and \"I don't like it\" together. The first is a fact and the second is how he feels, and neither is a judgment of him (M5). People fear that accepting makes light of a thing. It doesn't. The mark has causes, and his fear makes sense given what he believed (G6). The mark is the same, and he is no longer afraid to look at it."],["He practises accepting a little at a time, each time the mark comes to mind.",["DT14","DT16"],1,"Acceptance is a skill, and it gets better with practice. He does not expect to manage it in one go. Each time the mark comes to mind he is at a fork, and he turns toward \"it is 48\" (DT16). Some turns are only a few degrees. Lack of skill is the first thing that gets in the way, and practice is the answer to lack of skill. He changes nothing about the mark or about his plans. He is practising the turn, and he gets many chances in a day."],["He refuses to accept it, and stays angry.",["DT14","G4"],6,"It makes sense. Strong emotion is the third thing that gets in the way, and anger feels like loyalty to his own effort. Anger may also be hiding sadness (DT15). But refusing changes nothing about the mark. The comments stay unread, and the next essay will have the same faults. He has avoided giving up on the mark, and the cost is that he gives up on the next essay."]],"e":[["He plans the next essay, and feels like he's given up anyway. A classmate who got the same mark is appealing, and asks why he isn't.",[["He checks: accepting is not giving up.",["DT14"],"He puts the feeling to the footing's test. Does he dislike the mark? Yes. Does he think effort can change the next one? Yes. Is it worth trying? Yes. So he is not passive. He is working on the next essay. If he thought the marking was wrong, he could accept the mark as a fact and appeal as well."],["He keeps the plan.",["DT16"],"His classmate's question is a fork: back to fighting the mark, or on with the plan. He turns toward the plan (DT16). He made the choice with the comments in front of him, and nothing in the comments has changed. He tells his classmate \"I think the mark was fair, so I'm fixing the next one\"."],["He lets the feeling be.",["ER22"],"The feeling of having given up is a feeling. It does not report what he is doing, and what he is doing is planning an essay. He lets it rise and fall (ER22) while the plan stays open on the desk. He can write an outline with the feeling there."]]],["He sees accepting isn't approving, and his parent says \"don't just accept it\". She adds \"your sister would have gone straight to the office\".",[["He explains: accept the mark, change the next.",["IE5","DT14"],"His mother has the same belief he had this morning. He describes what he is doing in a way she can hear: \"I'm not happy with it. I've read the comments and I'm meeting my tutor on Thursday\" (IE5). Accepting has not made him passive (DT14), and he can show her the meeting in his diary."],["He validates the parent's worry.",["IE22"],"\"Don't just accept it\" means \"I'm afraid you'll stop trying\". That worry makes sense in a parent, and he says so (IE22). He does not take up the comparison with his sister. Once she has been heard, his mother can listen to the plan."],["He lets it be.",["DT13"],"His mother thinks what she thinks, and tonight he can't change that. He accepts this too (DT13). He doesn't need her to understand acceptance before he practises it. He accepts the mark, and he accepts his mother's view of it."]]],["He practises accepting, and the mark keeps coming to mind. It is pinned on the department noticeboard by student number, and people are gathered round it.",[["He accepts again each time.",["DT16","DT14"],"The noticeboard brings the mark back every time he passes it. Each pass is a turn (DT16). Acceptance is a skill that gets better with practice (DT14), and the corridor gives him a lot of practice. By Friday the turn takes about a second."],["He does something else for an hour.",["DT9"],"Practice needs breaks. When the mark has come to mind ten times in one morning, he gives his attention something else on purpose: an hour in the gym (DT9). He does it once and comes back afterwards. He has not started avoiding the mark. He is resting between rounds of practice."],["He lets it come and go.",["M2"],"He stops counting the returns. The mark comes to mind, and he notices it without judging and without holding on to it (M2). His attention goes back to what he was doing. He has stopped treating each return as something he has to deal with."]]]]}},{"id":"DT15","title":"practising radical acceptance","marks":[],"practice":["list :   two things that matter most  ,   two smaller things   that ☉ must accept","r ≔ acceptance :   0  denial or rebellion   …   5  at peace","refine :   each item  ⊨ F ;   no reading ,  no J ;   rewrite until it is a plain fact","pick one from each list ;   practise ;   rate again","writing an item down at all   ⇒   acceptance > 0","keep the pros and cons of accepting where they can be read when it is hard"],"u":"Why can understanding a cause not excuse it? What do you do when the mind shouts No!?","tree":{"t":"Sarah must repeat a year. What does she do?","o":[["She meets her adviser and builds the repeated year: which modules to retake, which to improve, and a study plan for each.",["DT15","IE5","G15"],4,"To change something she first has to accept that it is (DT13). The meeting with her adviser is acceptance acted out. The practice footing says to act as if you had accepted, and a person who has accepted a repeat year plans it. She asks clearly for what she needs (IE5): which modules to retake, and which to improve. She writes a study plan for each and keeps a record of how each goes (G15). Understanding the causes is not excusing, and consequences can still follow. The repeat year is the consequence, and she looks at the causes with her adviser so that the year is a different one. The year changes because she rebuilds it. She has to solve this herself, whatever caused it (G6)."],["She talks it over with a friend who repeated a year and did well, and the shame loosens.",["DT15","IE16"],6,"The fact is \"I will repeat second year\". The shame comes from what she has added to it: \"only failures repeat\". The footing says to describe what must be accepted as fact, without blowing it up or shrinking it. Her friend is evidence against the blown-up version. She repeated and did well. Talking with someone who has been there is how closeness forms (IE16), and it shows her that she is one of many. Shame grows when a thing is hidden and shrinks when it is told to someone who does not reject her (ER11). The year is the same, and she feels less ashamed of it."],["She says \"it is as it is\", describes it as fact (\"I will repeat second year\"), acts as if she accepts it, and enrols.",["DT15","DT13"],1,"This is the practice written out. She notices that she is fighting reality: her jaw is tight, and her mind says \"this can't be happening\". She reminds herself that it is as it is, and that it was caused. She describes it as fact, no bigger and no smaller: \"I will repeat second year\". She uses an accepting statement. She notices her chest, stomach and shoulders with a curious mind, and lets the tension go. When her mind shouts \"No!\", she starts again. She lets the sadness rise, because anger that comes first may be hiding it. Then she acts as if she accepts, which is opposite action (ER10): she fills in the enrolment form. Acceptance is all the way, in mind, heart and body (DT13). Her mind and heart may not have accepted it yet when she signs the form."],["She tells no one, and hides it.",["DT15","G4"],6,"It makes sense. Shame says hide, and hiding avoids every hard conversation this week. But hiding is acting as if it were not so, the reverse of the practice. She spends the summer keeping stories straight. The shame is acted on every day and grows (ER10). In September her classmates find out anyway, and they also find out that she hid it."]],"e":[["She plans the year, and finds one module she needs isn't offered. The timetable office says \"that's just how it falls this year\".",[["She asks her adviser for a substitute.",["IE5","ER12"],"This is a new problem inside the plan, and it can be solved (ER12). She goes to the person who can decide. The office can't. She asks her adviser plainly: \"which module can stand in, and who signs that off?\" (IE5). Problems like this come with repeating a year, and she is prepared for them."],["She accepts a gap.",["DT13"],"If no substitute exists, the timetable is as it is. She accepts a lighter term (DT13) and does not treat it as one more injustice. She can use a lighter term: she puts the freed hours into the modules she is improving."],["She checks the facts about requirements.",["ER7"],"\"I need it\" is what she assumed. She reads the regulations (ER7). The requirement turns out to be credits at that level, and no particular module is named. Her fear had been bigger than the regulations justify."]]],["She talks to a friend who repeated, and the friend says it was awful. \"Honestly? Everyone looked at me like I was thick.\"",[["She lets that be the friend's year, not hers.",["DT15","IE17"],"She is to accept facts, and a forecast is not a fact (DT13: fearful thoughts about the future are not facts). Her friend's year happened to her friend. Her friend was also guessing what others thought, and nobody can observe another person's thoughts (IE17). Sarah describes her own year as it is so far: it has not happened yet."],["She asks what helped.",["IE5"],"The friend got through an awful year and did well, so the friend knows something. She asks directly: \"what got you through it?\" (IE5). The answer is specific: a study partner in the new cohort, and telling people early. She came for comfort, and she leaves with two things to do."],["She lets the fear pass.",["ER22"],"\"Awful\" sends fear through her, and the fear fits: it may be hard. She lets it rise and fall without building a picture of the whole year on it (ER22). When it has passed she can still enrol. The fear did not make that decision."]]],["She enrols, and on the first day she sees her old classmates in the year above. One of them calls across the corridor, \"wrong floor, Sarah!\"",[["She half-smiles and walks on.",["DT18","DT15"],"Her face wants to drop and her hands want to clench. She relaxes her face, turns the corners of her mouth just up, and opens her hands (DT18). This is the body's part of practising acceptance (DT15). The half-smile is serene, and it is nothing like a tense grin put on as a mask. She walks to her own floor."],["She says hello.",["ER10","IE7"],"Shame says look away and hurry. She does the opposite, all the way: she looks up, smiles, and says \"right floor, actually. I'm repeating second year. How's third?\" (ER10). She keeps an easy manner (IE7). It is not a secret any more, so the shame has less to feed on."],["She lets the shame rise and fall.",["ER22"],"The call across the corridor lands, and shame floods in. She does not block it and does not replay the moment (ER22). It peaks on the stairs and is lower by the time she finds her seat. She had expected this to happen sooner or later, and now it has."]]]]}},{"id":"DT16","title":"turning the mind: a choice made again at every fork","marks":[],"practice":["acceptance  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 5] ;   what was hard to accept ;   how the commitment was made ;   the plan to catch the drift"],"u":"Why is choosing acceptance a turn and not an arrival? Why does one turn not make the next?","tree":{"t":"Terry accepted his low mark on Monday, but is fighting it again by Wednesday. What does he do?","o":[["He turns the mind again at this fork, and checks in each night, so the next fork finds him ready.",["DT16"],3,"Choosing acceptance is a turn. It is not an arrival. Monday's choice was real, and one choice does not make the next. On Wednesday he is at a new fork, with acceptance one way and \"it should not be\" the other. He notices which road he is on, and that noticing is the first step. He makes the inner commitment to accept, and he knows the commitment is only the choice and that acceptance comes after it. Then he adds the part that changes his week: he checks in at a set time each night, to see whether he needs to turn. With the check-in, the fighting is caught the same day. The fighting has a cause, and he needs to do better at catching it (G6). A nightly check is how he does better."],["He sees that turning again is normal, not failure, and the frustration drops.",["DT16","M5"],7,"He was frustrated because he believed \"I accepted it Monday, so fighting it Wednesday means I failed\". The footing says otherwise. The more painful the event, the more turns it takes, sometimes many in a minute. Turning again is the skill. \"Failure\" is a judgment he added (M5). Without the judgment, Wednesday is another fork, and he had been told to expect forks. He is doing the best he can (G6). Returning to the path over and over is also what mindfulness practice consists of (M2). His frustration came from the judgment, and it drops when the judgment goes."],["He makes a turn of a few degrees today, and leaves the rest for tomorrow.",["DT16","DT11"],1,"A turn may be a few degrees, or a full turn back to the path. Today he has only a few degrees in him. He moves from \"it's outrageous\" to \"it's a low mark and I hate it\". That is a turn. He does not demand the full turn of himself, and he takes only today's part (DT11). He keeps most of his dislike of the mark, and the footing allows for a turn that small."],["He stops turning.",["DT16","G4"],6,"It makes sense. Turning again and again is tiring, and \"I tried acceptance, it didn't work\" sounds reasonable. But acceptance was never going to arrive after one choice. With no turns, every fork goes the old way by default. By Friday he is where he was before Monday, and he now also believes that he can't do the skill."]],"e":[["He turns the mind, and by Friday he's fighting it again. A classmate says \"you're still on about that mark?\"",[["He turns again.",["DT16"],"Friday brings another fork. The footing says so you turn the mind again, every time, and \"every time\" includes the third time in a week. He notices the fight and chooses again. His classmate's remark showed him he had drifted, which did his nightly check-in for him."],["He writes down why he chose to accept.",["DT16","DT5"],"At the fork, the reasons for accepting are hard to remember and the reasons for fighting are loud. He writes the pros and cons of accepting and of fighting on a card (DT5). The next time he is at a fork he reads the card. Rehearsing makes the long-term reasons come to mind when they are needed, and they help him turn (DT16)."],["He lets the fight be there.",["ER22"],"Wilfulness can't be beaten by more wilfulness (DT17). He does not fight the fighting. He lets the anger at the mark rise and fall (ER22). When the wave is lower, the turn is easier to make, and he makes it then."]]],["He sees turning again as normal, and still feels weak. His older brother says \"I'd have got over it in a day\".",[["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"His brother has compared him with a made-up version of himself. Terry says to himself what he would say to a friend (IE24): \"it mattered to me, so it takes a while, and that makes sense\". Feeling weak after being called slow is understandable. It is a feeling, though, and it does not show that he is weak."],["He checks the facts: painful events take more turns.",["DT16","ER7"],"\"Weak\" is a reading, and he checks it (ER7). The footing gives the fact: the more painful the event, the more turns it takes (DT16). So the number of turns tells him how much the mark mattered. It does not measure his strength. His brother did not have this mark."],["He lets it pass.",["DT19"],"\"I'm weak\" is a thought, in his brother's voice. He watches it come and go like any other (DT19). He does not need to refute it or act on it. It passes, and he goes on turning the mind when he needs to."]]],["He turns a few degrees, and a new low mark arrives. It is in the module he thought was his best.",[["He turns again, for both.",["DT16","DT13"],"Now there are two facts to accept and one skill for both. He names each as fact: 52 in one, 49 in the other (DT13). He turns toward each (DT16). The second mark hurts more, so it will take more turns. He knows how to make them after a week of practice on the first."],["He asks for help with the next essay.",["IE5"],"Two low marks in a row point to something he can't see himself. Accepting them lets him ask about them. He writes to his tutor with a clear ask: twenty minutes, both essays, what to change first (IE5). He needed to accept the marks before he could ask, and the asking is how the next mark changes."],["He lets it be.",["ER22"],"The new mark arrives while he is still part-way through the old one. The disappointment is strong, and it fits. He lets it be strong tonight (ER22) and does not demand a turn from himself straight away. His nightly check-in comes round again tomorrow."]]]]}},{"id":"DT17","title":"willingness against wilfulness","marks":[],"practice":["acceptance and wilfulness ,  each  r_before ,  r_after ∈ [0, 5] ;   one willing act toward G ;   one place ☉ held back from what was needed"],"u":"Why can wilfulness not be beaten by more wilfulness? What usually stands behind fixed wilfulness?","tree":{"t":"Claire refuses to start a resit because she should not have to sit it. What does she do?","o":[["She accepts the wilfulness, then turns toward willingness and books the resit.",["DT17","DT16"],3,"Wilfulness cannot be beaten by more wilfulness. If she orders herself to \"just get on with it\", she adds a second refusal on top of the first. The footing gives the order of steps: accept it, then turn toward willingness. So first she notices it and names it without judging: \"I am being wilful. I am refusing\". She does not fight it. Then she turns the mind (DT16) toward willingness, which is doing what works, wholeheartedly, from wise mind (M3). Booking the resit is what works. \"I should not have to\" may even be true, and willingness does not ask her to give it up. It asks her to take part in what is. She may not have caused the resit, and she still has to sit it (G6)."],["She finds the threat behind it, fear of failing again, and the wilfulness loosens.",["DT17","ER7"],6,"Behind fixed wilfulness there is usually a threat. She asks what hers is. \"I should not have to\" is covering \"what if I fail twice?\" The footing says the threat is seldom a catastrophe, and other skills can meet it. She checks the facts (ER7): the resit covers the same syllabus, she failed by four marks, and she has six weeks. The fear fits a little and is far too big. Once the fear has a name and a size, the refusal is no longer needed to guard it. The refusing had a cause (G6), and the cause was fear. She had taken it for pride."],["She plays the hand she holds: she sits the resit as it is.",["DT17","DT13"],1,"Life deals hands like cards: play the hand you hold as well as you can, then let it go for the next. Her hand has a resit in it. She accepts that card completely (DT13). She does not ask for a different exam, a different marker, or an apology. Life keeps pitching like a machine, and refusing does not stop the balls. She can be hit, let them pass, or swing, and she swings. Willingness is saying yes to the hand she holds, and she can say it while still thinking the deal was unfair."],["She sits on her hands.",["DT17","G4"],6,"It makes sense. \"I should not have to\" feels like standing up for herself, and she may be right about the first paper. But wilfulness sits on its hands when action is needed, and the booking deadline is Friday. The refusal does not hurt whoever marked the paper. The resit window closes, and the fail stays on her record."]],"e":[["She books the resit, and wilfulness comes back the night before. A friend messages: \"honestly I'd boycott it, the first paper was a joke\".",[["She notices it, and turns to willingness again.",["DT17","DT16"],"The refusal is back, and it has an ally. She follows the same steps: notice it, name it, accept that it is there, and turn (DT17). One turn did not make this one (DT16). She tells her friend \"maybe it was. I'm sitting it anyway\". She can agree about the first paper and still sit the second."],["She makes pros and cons of sitting it.",["DT5"],"The boycott now sounds attractive, so she puts it on paper. She fills four boxes: sit it or skip it, with the gains and costs of each (DT5). She weighs the long term. The skip box has one item, tonight's satisfaction. The page gives her an answer when the wilfulness speaks up again."],["She half-smiles and opens her hands.",["DT18"],"Her arms are crossed and her jaw is set, which is how wilfulness sits in the body. She relaxes her face, turns her lips just up, and turns her palms up on the desk (DT18). The body tells the brain. It is hard to keep refusing in that posture."]]],["She names the fear, and it gets bigger. Her mother says \"well, you'd better not fail it twice\".",[["She copes ahead for failing again.",["ER18"],"The fear is of one scene, opening a second fail. She rehearses that scene on purpose, from the inside, and rehearses coping with it (ER18): who she tells, what the third attempt involves, and what she does that evening. A threat she has rehearsed is seldom a catastrophe (DT17). It shrinks to something with steps."],["She checks the facts about the resit.",["ER7"],"Her mother's sentence makes failing twice sound likely and final. She checks both (ER7). The pass rate for resits is on the department page, and so is the rule about third attempts. The facts are milder than her mother made them sound."],["She lets it rise and fall.",["ER22"],"Naming a fear often makes it louder for a while, because she has stopped holding it down. She lets it be loud (ER22). She does not go back to \"I shouldn't have to\" to quieten it. It rises, peaks while she is washing up, and falls."]]],["She sits the resit as it is, and it's harder than the first one. Around her, people are sighing and one walks out.",[["She does what she can, one question at a time.",["DT11"],"She takes one thing in the moment (DT11). She can't sit a hard paper all at once, and she can read question three. She does the part she can, and moves on. The sighing is about the whole paper, and she keeps to the question in front of her."],["She plays the hand she has.",["DT17"],"This is a worse hand than she expected. She plays it as well as she can and then lets it go for the next (DT17). Someone has walked out, and that is one way to meet the paper. She swings at each question, and marks the ones to come back to."],["She accepts the paper.",["DT13"],"\"This is unfair, it's harder than the first\" is true, and it is using up her time. She accepts the paper as it is (DT13), with its hard questions. The paper is still hard. She stops arguing with it and has the full two hours to write."]]]]}},{"id":"DT18","title":"half-smile and willing hands: the body tells the brain","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"How does the body tell the brain? Why need no one else see the half-smile?","tree":{"t":"Jean waits outside an exam room with clenched fists. What does she do?","o":[["She opens her hands, palms up, and uses the last minutes to go over her one-page summary.",["DT18","DT4"],5,"Emotions show in the body, and the body feeds back to the emotion. Her fists are the anger, and they also keep the anger going. She unclenches her hands and turns them palms up on her knees. It is hard to stay furious with open hands. Then she has room to stop, step back and observe (DT4): there are eight minutes left, and a summary sheet is in her bag. She proceeds mindfully and reads it. The willing hands are for accepting that the exam is happening, and the summary changes how ready she is for it. Acceptance comes first and makes the change possible (DT14). She has to learn the new behaviour in the setting where it is needed (G6), and that setting is this corridor."],["She half-smiles where no one can see, and feels the anger soften.",["DT18"],6,"This is the skill written out. She relaxes her face from the forehead down: forehead, eyes, cheeks, jaw. She turns her lips just up, so slightly that nobody in the corridor would notice. No one else need see the half-smile. It is a message to her own brain. A tense grin would signal a mask, and this is serene. Her face has been telling her brain \"this should not be\". Now it tells her brain something milder, and the anger softens. The footing says to practise often, when calm and when upset, but not while anger is overwhelming. Hers is strong and not overwhelming, so she can use it here."],["She notices that clenched hands mean she is angry at the exam, and lets the anger be there while she waits.",["DT18","M4","ER22"],1,"She reads her own body and describes what she finds (M4): \"my fists are clenched. I am angry\". Anger clenches the hands, and anger says what is should not be. So she knows what she is feeling and what it is saying: this exam should not be happening to her. She does not open her hands or argue with the anger. She lets it rise and fall while she waits (ER22). She has noticed and named the anger, which is different from being run by it. It stays, and she can see what it is."],["She stays clenched.",["DT18","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The exam is hard, she is tired, and the anger fits a little. But the body feeds back to the emotion. Ten minutes of fists gives her ten minutes of rising anger, and she walks in with a tight jaw and shallow breath. She has built up the anger while waiting, and she starts the first question with less attention than she had."]],"e":[["She opens her hands and reviews, and the invigilator calls her in early. \"Bags at the front, now please.\"",[["She keeps her hands open as she walks in.",["DT18"],"The summary has to go in the bag. The willing hands can go into the room with her. She walks in with her palms loose and open at her sides (DT18). The sharp tone brings a flick of anger, and her open hands give it nothing to hold on to."],["She breathes out longer than in.",["DT6"],"Being hurried has pushed her arousal up. On the walk to her desk she breathes in for four and out for six (DT6). She takes three of those breaths before she sits down. She lost the review minutes, and she arrives at the desk calm."],["She accepts the change.",["DT13"],"She had eight minutes and now she has none. \"I wasn't finished\" is true, and it does not help her. She accepts the early start (DT13). She loses a little time and nothing else, and she begins the exam without a quarrel with the invigilator."]]],["She half-smiles, and the anger stays. The student next to her is loudly telling someone how easy the mock was.",[["She keeps the half-smile a little longer.",["DT18"],"The body feeds back, and it does so slowly. Thirty seconds is short. She checks that her forehead and jaw are loose, because a half-smile over a clenched jaw is a mask (DT18). She holds it through the next two minutes of boasting."],["She names the anger.",["ER6"],"The half-smile hasn't moved it, so she looks at it directly. She names it and what set it off (ER6): \"anger, and now irritation at him\". They are two emotions with two causes. Naming them puts a little distance between her and them, enough to stop listening to her neighbour."],["She lets it be.",["ER22"],"Anger before a hard exam, next to a boaster, fits the facts. It does not have to go. She lets it be there, neither clenching around it nor pushing at it (ER22). It will fall once she has a question to think about."]]],["She lets the anger be, and it turns to dread. Someone comes out of the earlier sitting in tears.",[["She checks the facts about the exam.",["ER7"],"Dread is fear, and fear makes a prediction. She checks it (ER7). One person crying tells her about that person's exam. Her own facts are these: she passed the mock, and she knows four of the five topics. The dread shrinks toward the size of one weak topic."],["She uses paced breathing.",["DT6"],"The dread is in her stomach and her breath. She slows the breath, out longer than in, deep into the belly (DT6). An emotion readies the body to act, and dread readies her to flee. The breathing calms the body enough for her to stay in the queue."],["She rides the dread.",["ER22"],"Anger was covering the dread, and now she can see it. She does not cover it again. It is a wave, and she rides it (ER22): it rises as the door opens and falls when she turns over the paper and sees an ordinary exam."]]]]}},{"id":"DT19","title":"mindfulness of current thoughts","marks":[["ξ","a change of response"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why is a thought not a fact, and not you? Why can an event be catastrophic when a thought about it is not?","tree":{"t":"Evita keeps thinking \"everyone here is smarter than me\". What does she do?","o":[["She asks her question in tutorial anyway, and joins a study group to see how others are really doing.",["DT19","IE5","IE16"],5,"A thought is not her and not a fact, and it need not be acted on. The act this thought asks for is silence. She does not obey it. She asks her question, clearly (IE5), with the thought still present. Then she changes her situation: she joins a study group (IE16). We react to thoughts in place of the facts they stand for, and in a study group she meets the facts. Other people are confused about week four too. She did not argue with the thought. She went where the evidence is and looked. New behaviour has to be learned in the setting (G6), and the setting is a room full of the people she compares herself with."],["She sees the thought as a sensation of the brain, firing and passing, and it loses its grip.",["DT19","M1"],6,"A thought is a sensation of the brain: it fires and passes. She had been treating \"everyone here is smarter than me\" as a report on the room. She watches it as an event in her head, the way she would watch a sound (M1). Thousands of thoughts have come and gone, and she remains. This one has come before every seminar this term and gone after it. Years of being told her views were wrong taught her to distrust her own mind (G8, G9). She is aiming for a new relation to her thoughts. She does not need new thoughts or an empty mind. The thought is the same, and she no longer takes orders from it."],["She lets it flow without checking it or changing it.",["DT19"],1,"The footing says this is not checking the facts, and not changing the thought. She does neither. The thought comes, and she watches it come and go like a wave. Walling thoughts out keeps them coming back, and letting them flow is freedom. She does not ask why she thinks it. At a crevasse she needs a way across, and a reason for the crevasse would not help her. She watches the pleasant and neutral thoughts too, such as \"nice window\" and \"what's for lunch\", so that this one is one of many. It goes on coming, and she lets it."],["She believes it and stops asking questions.",["DT19","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The thought is persistent, and keeping quiet protects her from being found out. But she is reacting to a thought in place of the facts it stands for. With no questions asked, she understands less each week. In time the thought comes true, because she acted on it."]],"e":[["She asks her question, and someone answers it before the tutor can. He adds \"that was in the first chapter\".",[["She notes the thought \"they're smarter\" and lets it pass.",["DT19"],"The thought fires at once, as she expected. She labels it, \"there's the thought\", and watches it go (DT19). His remark was an event. The thought about it is a sensation of her brain. She has her answer, which is what she asked the question for."],["She thanks them, and asks a follow-up.",["IE7","IE5"],"She stays gentle and interested (IE7): \"thanks. So does that also hold for the second case?\" She asks it clearly (IE5). A follow-up shows that she understood the answer. It also puts her in the conversation on the same footing as him."],["She checks the facts: they had done the reading.",["ER7"],"Here checking is the right tool, because there is a fact to find (ER7). He knew because it was in chapter one, and she had read chapter two. That is a difference in reading, and it tells her nothing about minds. She can fix it tonight, and it supports no conclusion about everyone here."]]],["She sees the thought as a sensation, and it comes back every seminar. A classmate mentions that she already has a paper under review.",[["She lets it pass every time.",["DT19","DT16"],"The aim was never to stop it coming. Each seminar brings the thought back and gives her another chance to choose how she stands toward it (DT16). She watches it arrive with the classmate's news, and watches it go (DT19). She has had weekly practice at this for a whole term."],["She keeps a record of what she contributes.",["G15"],"The thought makes a claim about a whole term, and she has no data. She starts a record: each seminar, one line on what she said or asked (G15). After five weeks the page shows eleven contributions. The record is not there to argue with the thought. It keeps the facts where she can see them."],["She talks to someone about it.",["IE16","IE5"],"A thought that is kept private stays large. She tells one classmate over coffee: \"I often feel like the slowest one here\" (IE5). The classmate laughs and says \"me too, every week\". Telling it brought the two of them closer (IE16), and showed her the thought is common in that room."]]],["She lets it flow, and it starts to make her stay quiet. The tutor writes on her feedback: \"would like to hear more from you\".",[["She acts opposite: one question per seminar.",["ER10"],"Letting the thought flow is fine, and obeying its urge is where the trouble is. The urge is to hide, and the fear behind it does not fit the facts. She acts opposite, all the way (ER10): one question per seminar, said aloud, early. She repeats it weekly until the fear changes."],["She checks the facts.",["ER7"],"Mindfulness of thoughts leaves the thought alone. Now she chooses a different skill, because the thought is costing her marks. She checks it (ER7): her grades are in the middle of the group, and the tutor wants more from her. \"Everyone is smarter\" does not fit either fact."],["She lets it flow and speaks anyway.",["DT19","ER10"],"She keeps the acceptance and adds the act. The thought can be in the room while she talks (DT19). A thought need not be acted on, and that includes the urge to stay quiet. She speaks with it there (ER10). The tutor hears her question, and nobody in the room knows about the thought."]]]]}},{"id":"DT20","title":"when the crisis is an addiction","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"When are you addicted? Why can an addiction be any repeated act, and why work on one at a time?","tree":{"t":"Mike games until 4 a.m. every night despite failing modules. What does he do?","o":[["He sees that it meets the test (he cannot stop despite effort and harm), and starts dialectical abstinence: no gaming on school nights, with a plan for a slip.",["DT20","DT21"],4,"The footing's test has two parts, and he puts his gaming to both. Effort: he has tried to stop at midnight four times this month and failed. Harm: two modules are failing. He cannot stop despite effort and harm, so the word addiction applies. An addiction can be any repeated act, and it does not have to be a substance. The skills run D, C, B, A, and he starts at D: dialectical abstinence (DT21). He aims for never on school nights, and at the very back of his mind he keeps a plan for a slip: console off, bed, recommit in the morning. He works on one addiction at a time, so he leaves the energy drinks for later. He has to solve this himself, whatever led to it (G6)."],["He names it as an addiction without judging himself, and the shame drops.",["DT20","M5"],7,"He had two names for it before. Sometimes it was \"just a hobby\", which was denial. Sometimes it was \"I'm pathetic\", which was a judgment. Neither helped him stop. The footing gives a third name with a test attached: he cannot stop despite effort and harm. That is a description (M5). It says what is happening and passes no verdict on him. The gaming has causes (G6): it was the one place this year where he was good at something and the reward came quickly. Shame drops when a thing is named accurately, because shame had been attached to \"pathetic\". He games the same hours tonight, but he sees the gaming differently, and that is where D, C, B, A starts."],["He accepts that stopping will take more than willpower, and tells a friend.",["DT20","DT23"],1,"He has been running on \"tomorrow I'll just be stronger\" for months. He accepts the fact that willpower is not enough (DT23). The footing says so plainly, and his four failed attempts say the same. This is acceptance of a limit, with the evidence in front of him (DT13). Telling a friend follows from it. If willpower is not enough, other people have to be part of it, and his friend can notice and reward each night he stops. He has not changed his gaming yet. He has stopped pretending he can do it alone and in secret."],["He keeps gaming.",["DT20","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The game pays tonight, and the modules cost him in June. The high now outweighs costs that come later, and that keeps an addiction going (DT23). As the gaming grows, other things shrink: he sees fewer people and does less else that feels good. So the game is tied ever closer to the only reward he has left. He may yet stop on his own, but it has been getting harder each month."]],"e":[["He starts abstinence, and slips on Wednesday. His squad messages at 11 p.m.: \"one match, we need a fourth\".",[["He recommits at once.",["DT21"],"One slip does not mean all the rest. After a slip the pull is to give up, because he feels guilty and out of control. He fights that pull (DT21). He turns the console off at the end of the match, and on Thursday morning he recommits to 100%, as if this slip were the last. He limits the harm and gets back on the wagon."],["He runs a chain on the slip.",["G13"],"He writes the chain (G13). The exact act: he joined at 11:05 and played until 2. Vulnerability: a bad lab that day, and no dinner. The starting event was the message. The links were \"they need me\", then \"one match\", then the headset going on. To find the key link he imagines each one gone. Without the notification there would have been no slip. So the skill goes there: squad chat muted on school nights. The repair is to tell them his new hours."],["He tells his friend.",["IE5","DT23"],"Addict mind hides slips (DT22). He does the opposite and tells the friend who knows about the plan: \"I slipped last night. I'm back on it today\" (IE5). The friend says \"good that you told me\". That reply rewards the honesty and the recommitment (DT23). The slip is no longer a secret, and a secret slip is easier to repeat."]]],["He names it without judging, and his friends say \"it's just gaming\". One adds \"you're being dramatic, we all play\".",[["He keeps his name for it.",["DT20"],"The test is about him and not about the game. Can he stop when he tries? Is it doing harm? His friends may pass that test, and he does not. The name fits his facts (DT20). He does not need them to agree with it. He needs it so that he can use the skills that go with it."],["He validates himself.",["IE24"],"\"You're being dramatic\" tells him his reading of his own life is wrong. He answers it the way he would for a friend (IE24): two failed modules and four failed attempts to stop are not drama. His concern is valid, and it remains valid when the others dismiss it."],["He tells them what it costs him.",["IE10","IE5"],"He is truthful and does not exaggerate or apologise (IE10). \"For me it's every night till four, and I'm failing two modules. So I'm off on school nights.\" Then he asks for one thing: \"don't ping me after ten\" (IE5). They may still think he is being dramatic, but they know what he is asking."]]],["He accepts it will take more than willpower, and his friend can't help this week. \"Sorry mate, I'm away till Monday.\"",[["He uses the library to study at night.",["DT23","ER12"],"The help he counted on is gone for five days, and that is a problem he can solve (ER12). Community reinforcement means changing the people and places around him (DT23). The library is open until midnight and has no console. He spends the evenings there, where the gaming is not possible and other people are working."],["He removes the console from his room.",["DT24"],"With no one to check on him, he makes the gaming harder to do. He burns a bridge (DT24): the console goes to a flatmate's cupboard until Monday. One bridge left standing is enough for a relapse, so the handheld goes too. Then he does not have to refuse the console every night, because it is not in his room."],["He finds another person to tell.",["IE16","IE5"],"One supporter was never going to be enough. He thinks about who else is near and safe, and tells his lab partner over lunch (IE16). He asks clearly: \"can I message you at eleven each night this week to say I'm off?\" (IE5). Now two people know."]]]]}},{"id":"DT21","title":"dialectical abstinence: aim for never, plan for a slip","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Abstinence and harm reduction each fail one way. How does aiming for never while planning for a slip keep the gain of both?","tree":{"t":"Zoe commits to no phone during study blocks. What does she do?","o":[["She commits 100%, out loud, from tomorrow, and has a plan for a slip: phone in another room, back to work at once.",["DT21"],3,"Dialectical abstinence holds two plans that seem to contradict each other. The first is abstinence: commit 100%, out loud and in public, with a set date. She tells her flatmate \"from tomorrow, no phone in study blocks\". There is no \"just this once\". A vegetarian never makes the exception, and neither does she. The second is harm reduction, kept at the very back of her mind so that it never weakens the resolve: if she slips, the phone goes to the other room and she returns to work at once. Abstinence alone keeps her off longer, but she would come back slower after a slip. Harm reduction alone brings her back faster, but she would slip sooner. Held together, she gets the long stretch and the quick return. This is a synthesis of opposites (IE19, ∎). She needs to try harder and be more motivated to change (G6), and saying it in public does both."],["She tells herself one slip does not mean all the rest, and the guilt shrinks.",["DT21","IE20"],6,"After a slip her mind says \"I always do this, I've ruined it\". The footing says one slip does not mean all the rest, and a slip need not become a slide. She weakens \"always\" to \"this time\" (IE20). One slip is one event in one block. The guilt was the size of \"always\", and it shrinks to the size of five minutes. This matters because of what guilt does next: after a slip, the pull is to give up because she feels guilty, ashamed and out of control. She is doing the best she can (G6). When the guilt is smaller, the pull to give up is smaller. The slip happened, and she reads it as a single slip."],["She accepts that slips will come, and lets a slip be a slip without a speech to herself.",["DT21","DT13"],1,"There is a line to keep here. The footing says do not accept a slip before it happens, so before each block she aims for never. After a slip she accepts it completely as a fact of the past (DT13). She gives herself no speech and no verdict, and she does not replay it. The speech is the dangerous part, because it is how one slip turns into an hour. She notes \"I picked it up\", and that is all she says about it. She makes no new rule and no new promise. She has left out the self-attack."],["She gives up after the first slip.",["DT21","G4"],6,"It makes sense. She committed to 100%, and now it is not 100%, so the commitment feels broken. That is abstinence held without its other half. With no plan for a slip, the first slip ends everything. She feels guilty and out of control, and giving up stops the guilt. The phone comes back to the desk, and the study blocks return to what they were."]],"e":[["She commits, and slips in the first block. Her flatmate, who heard the promise, raises an eyebrow from the doorway.",[["She puts the phone back in the other room, at once.",["DT21"],"This is the plan she kept at the back of her mind, used as it was meant to be: limit the harm and return at once. She gets up, puts the phone in the other room, and sits down again. She recommits to 100%, as if this slip were the last. Her flatmate saw the slip and also saw her get back to work."],["She does not judge it.",["M5"],"The raised eyebrow invites a verdict, and she has one ready: \"can't even last an hour\". She drops it and describes what happened (M5): \"I picked it up at minute twenty\". That is a fact, and facts can be used. The verdict would only make her feel bad, and feeling bad is what leads to giving up."],["She makes the next block shorter.",["ER18"],"A fifty-minute block was too hard for day one. Mastery is built with steps that are hard but possible (ER18). She sets the next block at twenty-five minutes and completes it. A success she can repeat is worth more this week than a long block she fails. She will lengthen it on Thursday."]]],["The guilt shrinks, and she slips three times in a day. A group chat about the weekend is buzzing through the wall.",[["She troubleshoots: where is the phone?",["ER24","DT24"],"Three slips in a day mean something in the setup is failing, and she checks in order (ER24). Body: fine. Did she use the skill to the letter? No. The plan said another room, and the phone is on the shelf behind her, on loud. That is the first failed check, so she fixes that one. She puts it in the kitchen, on silent, which removes that bridge (DT24)."],["She recommits.",["DT21","DT16"],"Each slip is a fork, and she has gone the wrong way three times. The fourth fork is in front of her now (DT16). She recommits to 100% as if the last slip were the last (DT21). She makes no special bargain for a bad day. The commitment is the one she made on the first day."],["She asks a friend to hold her phone.",["IE5","DT23"],"Willpower is not enough (DT23), and today has shown it. She asks her flatmate clearly: \"will you keep my phone until six, and not give it back if I ask?\" (IE5). That brings another person into the plan. Her flatmate now has a job in it and will notice when she succeeds."]]],["She accepts that slips will come, and one slip becomes an hour of scrolling. She surfaces to a message from her study partner: \"where are your notes?\"",[["She recommits now.",["DT21"],"A slip need not become a slide, and this one already has. The rule is the same at minute sixty as at minute one: get back on the wagon. She does it now. She does not wait for the end of the video or for tomorrow. The phone goes to the other room, and she answers her partner from her laptop."],["She lets the guilt pass.",["ER22"],"The guilt fits. She lost an hour, and someone was waiting. She lets it rise and fall (ER22) and does not act on its urge, which is to hide or to write the whole day off. It fits one lost hour and no more than that."],["She runs a chain on it.",["G13"],"An hour is worth analysing (G13). The exact act was picking up the phone at 3:10 to check the time. Vulnerability: she was tired after lunch, on a dull chapter. The links were a notification on the lock screen, then \"just this one\", then the feed. To find the key link she imagines each one gone. With a clock on the desk, she would not have picked the phone up at all. She buys a clock. The repair is to send the notes with an honest line."]]]]}},{"id":"DT22","title":"clear mind: between addict mind and clean mind","marks":[["𝔞","addict mind"],["𝔠","clean mind"],["𝔨","clear mind"]],"practice":["clean mind acts ,  each to be swapped for a clear mind act :\nacts that seem beside the point but led to ⛓ before  ;   «I've learned my lesson»  ;   «I can control it»\n«I'm not addicted»  ;   stopping a medicine that helps  ;   places ,  friends ,  housemates still using\nkeeping the gear  ;   carrying spare money  ;   careless with bills  ;   skipping meetings  ;   isolating\n«I can do this alone»  ;   ignoring what feeds it  ;   «willpower is enough»  ;   «I needn't tell anyone»  ;   «I can't stand this»"],"u":"Why are both addict mind and clean mind dangerous? What does clear mind know that clean mind forgets?","tree":{"t":"Randy has stopped late-night energy drinks and is sure he never will again. What does he do?","o":[["He moves to clear mind: he plans for the night before a deadline, when the urge will come back.",["DT22","DT5"],5,"There are three minds here. Addict mind is ruled by the urge. Clean mind is clean, and sure it cannot happen again. Clear mind is clean, knows it could happen, and plans for it. He is in clean mind, and the footing says both ends are dangerous. Clean mind lowers its guard and skips the plan, so the urge hits unprepared. He moves to clear mind, which is wise mind where addiction is concerned (M3). He names the night the urge will return, the one before the next deadline, and he plans it. He writes his pros and cons now, before the urge decides (DT5), and carries the card. He puts water and food on the desk, and sets a stop time. He is coping ahead (ER18). He changes that night before it comes."],["He notices the small acts that set up a slip (the vending machine route), and feels less at their mercy.",["DT22","DT24"],6,"Acts that seem irrelevant set up a slip. Each looks unrelated, but together they lead there. He lists his own. He walks home past the vending machine, he keeps coins in his jacket, and he starts work at 10 p.m. No single one is a decision to drink. Together they put him in front of the machine at midnight, tired, with change in his hand. Every act is caused (G6), and now he can see the causes lined up. Each of those acts is a bridge (DT24). He had felt that slips came from nowhere. Now he knows where they come from. He has changed nothing yet, but the urge no longer seems to arrive from nowhere."],["He accepts that the urge may return, and does not panic when it does.",["DT22","ER22"],1,"Clear mind knows that without skills an urge can come back at any moment. He accepts that as a fact about himself. A returning urge is not a relapse, and it does not prove he was never clean. When it comes he recognises it as the urge, back as he had been told to expect, and he lets it rise and fall (ER22). Urges do not last forever (DT24). He has made no plan and changed no route. He has dropped the certainty, which was what made him panic when the urge appeared."],["He stays in clean mind.",["DT22","G4"],6,"It makes sense. He has done something hard, and being sure of it feels like the reward. But clean mind skips the plan. On the night before the deadline the urge arrives, and he has no card, no water, and coins in his jacket. He is surprised, and addict mind works by surprise. He told himself \"never again\", and it does not hold up at midnight."]],"e":[["He plans for the night, and it comes sooner than he thought. A surprise quiz is announced for tomorrow, and his flatmate offers him a can.",[["He uses his plan.",["DT22"],"This is what the plan was for. It has come eleven days early. Clear mind planned for an urge at any moment. He takes out the card and reads his own reasons. He fills the water bottle and sets the stop time. He says \"no thanks\" to the can. The date has changed and the plan has not."],["He rides the urge.",["DT24","ER22"],"The can is a foot from his hand, and the urge spikes. He watches it rise and shift without acting (DT24). It is a wave, and waves fall (ER22). Each urge he watches and does not act on teaches his brain that an urge need not be acted on. After ten minutes it has dropped, and the can is still on the table."],["He calls a friend.",["IE16","DT23"],"His flatmate is offering the can, so he looks for support elsewhere. He messages the friend who also quit: \"urge is here, talk to me for five minutes\" (IE16). Being with people who don't use is what makes not using pay (DT23). The five minutes get him past the peak."]]],["He notices the small acts, and finds he has done three already. He is standing in the corridor with coins in his hand, and a classmate at the machine says \"your usual?\"",[["He stops at the fourth.",["DT22","DT4"],"Three steps toward a slip are not a slip. The fourth would be putting the coins in. He stops (DT4): he freezes, takes a step back from the machine, and observes where he is and how he got here. He proceeds from clear mind (DT22), which today means buying water with the same coins."],["He burns that bridge: a different route home.",["DT24"],"The corridor is a bridge to the old behaviour, and one bridge left standing is enough for a relapse (DT24). From tonight he takes the stairs at the other end of the building. The coins go in the charity tin. He has stopped relying on resisting the machine and no longer walks past it."],["He doesn't judge it.",["M5"],"\"Three already, I'm hopeless\" is addict mind's opening, because someone who is hopeless might as well drink. He describes what happened (M5): he took the old route, he had coins, and it was late. Those are three facts, and he can act on them. He tells the classmate \"not any more, actually\"."]]],["He accepts the urge may return, and it returns very strongly. It is 1 a.m., the essay is half done, and the shop downstairs is open.",[["He rides the wave.",["DT24","ER22"],"An urge can be strong and still pass. He watches it as a wave (ER22). He notices where it sits in his body, how it climbs, and how it shifts. He does not act (DT24). Urges do not last forever. This one peaks at about eight minutes and then falls."],["He uses cold water.",["DT6"],"The urge is very strong and he is tired, so he changes his body quickly. He holds cold water on his face for thirty seconds at the sink (DT6). It calms him and wakes him up at once. A good part of the urge was tiredness, and the drink had been his way of treating it."],["He makes pros and cons.",["DT5"],"With the shop downstairs, it is a live choice, and he puts it on paper. He fills four boxes (DT5). The pros of drinking are two hours of alertness. The cons are no sleep, a wrecked tomorrow, and being back where he started. He weighs the long term above the next two hours. He decides to finish the essay in the morning."]]]]}},{"id":"DT23","title":"community reinforcement","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why is willpower not enough? Why does sampling not using work as an experiment?","tree":{"t":"Sarah wants to stop cramming all night with friends who pull all-nighters. What does she do?","o":[["She makes not cramming pay: a morning study group, and a reward each early night.",["DT23","IE27"],4,"Willpower is not enough. The all-nighter pays her at once, with company, drama and the feeling of effort. The cost comes later, as a fogged exam. What pays now outweighs costs that come later, and that keeps it going. So she changes what pays. She changes the people and the place by starting a morning study group, with people who sleep. She adds a reward for each early night, and she chooses one that works for her, since what rewards one person does not reward another (IE27). For her it is a good coffee on the way in. She also asks one friend to notice each early night and say so. She is using the principles of behaviour on herself (IE25). She needs to be more motivated to change (G6), and she gets there by arranging rewards."],["She notices how good her clear mornings feel after an early night, and the pull of the all-nighters fades.",["DT23"],6,"If unsure, try out not using for a while and notice what comes. The footing calls this sampling. It is an experiment with no long promise and a set number of days. She sets five early nights, and she watches for the extra good things. She can follow the nine o'clock lecture. She remembers what she read. She is not shaking by noon. These rewards were always there, and she had never been awake to collect them. Once she has noticed them, they compete with the all-nighter. She has not confronted her friends or made a rule. She tried it for five days and paid attention, and the pull of the all-nighters has faded."],["She accepts that her friends may tease her, and goes to bed anyway.",["DT23","IE13"],1,"The footing warns about people who are uneasy when you don't use. Her friends may tease her because her early night makes them wonder about their own all-nighter. She accepts that this may happen and that she can't prevent it (DT13). She says her no at the height it needs, without softening it (IE13): \"I'm going to bed. See you tomorrow\". She does not explain or bargain. She has changed nothing about them, and they may tease her. She goes to bed with that possibility accepted."],["She keeps cramming.",["DT23","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The all-nighters are where her friends are, and belonging is a strong reward. As the cramming grows, her other ways of studying and of seeing people shrink, so the habit is tied ever closer to its reward. She goes on getting the company, and she goes on sitting her exams exhausted."]],"e":[["She sets up the morning group, and no one comes. One of the all-night friends says \"told you, nobody's awake at eight\".",[["She asks one person personally.",["IE5","IE16"],"A general invitation is easy to ignore. She picks one classmate who is always at the nine o'clock lecture and asks directly: \"would you do Tuesday at eight with me, in the café?\" (IE5). Two people are a group. Working near each other regularly is also how friendships form (IE16), and she needs friends who sleep."],["She studies alone in the morning and rewards herself.",["DT23","IE25"],"The group was one way to make mornings pay. She has another: she does the morning session alone and gives herself the reward straight after (IE25). The coffee still counts when she is the only one there. The behaviour she is rewarding is the early start (DT23), and she can do that without anyone else."],["She accepts a slow start.",["DT13"],"The empty room is a fact about week one. It is not a verdict on the idea. She accepts that this may build slowly (DT13). \"Nobody's awake at eight\" is stronger than the facts, since half the lecture hall is awake at nine. She puts the invitation up again for next week."]]],["She enjoys the mornings, and her friends stop inviting her out. She sees photos of a dinner she was not told about.",[["She invites them to something in the day.",["IE16","IE7"],"The all-nighters were how this group spent time together. She was leaving the hours, and the friends are being lost along with them. She offers another way of being near them (IE16): lunch on Friday. She asks with warmth and an easy manner (IE7). She wants the friends and does not want the all-nighters."],["She lets the hurt be.",["ER22"],"Being left out hurts, and the hurt fits. She lets it rise and fall (ER22) without sending a sharp message and without going back to the all-nighters to buy her place. Her early nights have cost her this, and she feels the cost."],["She keeps one late night a week with them.",["M9","DT23"],"She looks for a middle path (M9). She does not want all-nighters before exams, and she does not want to drop her friends. She keeps Friday evening with them, when no exam follows. The late night is not what harmed her. Cramming through the night before a paper did. She keeps the people and changes the pattern (DT23)."]]],["She accepts the teasing, and it gets meaner. \"Off to bed, grandma? Enjoy your B-minus.\"",[["She tells them plainly to stop.",["IE10","IE5"],"Accepting that teasing may come does not mean accepting whatever is said. She is fair and truthful, and she does not apologise for her bedtime (IE10). \"The jokes about my grades aren't funny to me. Please stop\" (IE5). She says it once, calmly, to the one who said it."],["She spends less time with them.",["IE18"],"The footing says to stay away from people who are uneasy when you don't use. She weighs this from wise mind, as she would any tie that has started to harm her (IE18). She does not end the friendships. She sees these friends less during exam weeks and sees others more."],["She validates herself.",["IE24"],"The jab was meant to make her doubt her choice. She answers it herself, as she would for a friend (IE24): wanting to sleep before an exam is sensible, and being hurt by the joke makes sense too. Her decision still rests on her clear mornings, and her friends' approval was never part of it."]]]]}},{"id":"DT24","title":"burning bridges, building new ones","marks":[],"practice":["r ≔ commitment to  |A_⛓| = 0 ,  0 to 100 ;   then go to 𝔴 and rate it again","list A_⛓  →  check off what is gone ;   list what would make ⛓ impossible  →  check off what is done","the images to call up when a craving comes ;   which were used :  new images ,  moving images ,  new smells ,  𝗈(wave(u_⛓))"],"u":"Why is one bridge left standing enough for a relapse? What does each urge watched and not acted on teach?","tree":{"t":"Terry wants to stop betting on match odds during lectures. What does he do?","o":[["He burns bridges: deletes the app, cancels the card, unfollows the tipsters.",["DT24"],4,"Burning bridges is radical acceptance, commitment and action together. The acceptance is that he can't bet a little (DT13). The commitment is absolute. The action is removing everything that makes betting possible. The footing asks for a list made with full honesty, so he writes one: the app, the saved card, the second account he told nobody about, the tipster accounts he follows, and the group chat where odds are posted. One bridge left standing is enough for a relapse, so the second account is on the list. Then he deletes each one, with the list in front of him, in one sitting. This changes the situation itself. In tomorrow's lecture a bet would take twenty minutes and a new card, and the urge would have to last that long. He needs to do better (G6), and he has made doing better the easiest thing to do."],["He builds new bridges: when the craving comes, he pictures the money staying put for the summer trip, and the craving fades.",["DT24"],5,"A craving arrives as an image: the win, the notification, the rush. Moving images and new smells compete with a craving, so he builds one to compete. He makes it vivid and moving: the train pulling out, the coast, the balance in his account still whole. He practises it when no craving is there, so that it is ready (ER18). When the odds flash in his mind during a lecture, he runs the trip. The craving image and the trip image can't both hold his attention, and the trip is the better picture. He can still place a bet, since the app is on his phone. He has changed what he sees when the craving comes, and it fades."],["He rides the wave of each urge, watching it rise and fall.",["DT24","DT19"],1,"Urge surfing means watching the urge rise and shift without acting. The urge is a sensation: a pull in his chest, his thumb wanting the phone, and the thought \"just check the odds\". He watches the thought as a thought (DT19), and it is not an instruction. He notes where the urge is and how strong, and watches it change. Urges do not last forever. Each urge watched and not acted on teaches the brain that an urge need not be acted on, which is extinction (IE26). He should expect a burst before it falls. He has deleted nothing and built nothing. He sits in the lecture with the urge and lets it go by."],["He keeps one account just in case.",["DT24","G4"],6,"It makes sense. Deleting everything feels extreme, and \"just in case\" sounds sensible. But the footing was written about this: one bridge left standing is enough for a relapse. The spare account is the harm-reduction plan moved from the back of his mind to the front (DT21). He has made the decision to bet already, and he has left open only the date."]],"e":[["He burns bridges, and a friend offers to place a bet for him. \"Give me a tenner, I'll put it on, you don't even need the app.\"",[["He says no clearly.",["IE13","DT24"],"The friend has offered him a bridge he had not listed: other people's accounts. He refuses at full height, with no softening and no \"maybe later\" (IE13). \"No. I've stopped.\" The refusal burns that bridge as well (DT24). A hesitant no would leave the offer standing for next week."],["He tells the friend why.",["IE10","IE5"],"He is truthful and does not make excuses (IE10). \"I was betting in every lecture and couldn't stop, so I've cut it off completely.\" Then he asks for one thing: \"please don't offer again\" (IE5). After that the friend knows about it, and someone who knows about it is less likely to tempt him."],["He rides the urge.",["ER22"],"The offer has set off a strong urge, because betting has become easy again. He lets the urge rise and fall without acting (ER22). He says \"not today\" to his friend and watches the pull in his chest climb and drop. He can decide what else to say once the wave has passed."]]],["He pictures the summer trip, and the craving is stronger than the picture. It is the cup final, and the whole row in front of him has the odds open.",[["He adds another image: the savings total.",["DT24"],"One image was not enough for a cup final. The footing speaks of images and smells in the plural. He adds a second image: his banking screen with the total, and the same screen in June, larger. He runs both pictures one after the other. Two pictures compete with the craving better than one did."],["He uses cold water.",["DT6"],"The craving is past what imagery can meet, and it is in his body: fast heart, restless hands. He leaves for two minutes and puts cold water on his face (DT6). TIP is for when he is too stirred up to think. He comes back calmer, and the picture works again."],["He calls someone.",["IE16","DT23"],"Willpower is not enough, and tonight a picture is not enough either. At the break he messages a friend who does not bet: \"cup final, craving badly, walk with me?\" (IE16). Time spent with people who don't bet makes not betting pay (DT23). They walk for ten minutes, and by then the kick-off has passed."]]],["He rides each wave, and they come every hour. His lab partner says \"you keep zoning out, are you even here?\"",[["He keeps riding.",["DT24","DT16"],"Hourly urges in the first week are the burst before the fall (IE26). Each one is a fork, and he chooses again (DT16). Each one watched and not acted on is one more lesson for his brain (DT24). He tells his lab partner \"sorry, back now\". That is true each time he says it."],["He changes the setting: library, not phone.",["DT23","ER12"],"Surfing every hour is costing him the lab. It is a problem, and he solves it (ER12). The urges come with the phone, so during labs the phone stays in his locker. He changes the place around him (DT23). There are fewer cues, so there are fewer waves. He keeps the surfing for the ones that still come."],["He talks to a doctor or a support group.",["IE5","DT23"],"Urges every hour, for an act he could not stop on his own, are reason enough to get help. He asks for it plainly (IE5), from the student health service or a gambling support group. People who have stopped are the community that makes stopping pay (DT23). He needs help and he asks for it."]]]]}},{"id":"DT25","title":"alternate rebellion; adaptive denial; just for now","marks":[],"practice":[],"u":"Why only ask yourself to hold out for now? How does alternate rebellion still serve your goal?","tree":{"t":"Claire wants to skip a compulsory class to rebel against a pointless rule. What does she do?","o":[["She rebels in a way that does less harm: she writes to the course committee about the rule.",["DT25","IE5"],4,"Alternate rebellion keeps the rebellion and changes how it is carried out. The footing says to honour it creatively, without suppressing it, judging it, or giving in mindlessly. Her objection is real, and the rule may indeed be pointless. Skipping would be cutting off her nose to spite her face: the rule would stand, and she would fail attendance. So she rebels in a way that does less harm and still serves her goal. She writes to the committee with the facts, what she is asking for, and why it would help the course (IE5). This is the only one of her options that could change the rule. The wish to push back is valid (G11). She needs a better way of acting on it (G6), and the letter is one."],["She writes a private parody of the rule on the bus, laughs, and the urge to skip has less force.",["DT25"],6,"The rebellion may be against authority, convention, rules or boredom, and hers is against a rule. It wants an outlet. The parody gives it one that costs nothing: \"Regulation 14b: students shall attend, and shall be seen to be attending, attendance\". She laughs. She has honoured the rebellion creatively. She did not suppress it and she did not judge herself for it. The urge to skip was the same urge wanting somewhere to go. It has gone into the parody, so it pushes less. The rule stands and she will be in class, feeling differently about it."],["She holds out just for today, honouring the urge without acting on it, and goes to class.",["DT25","DT11","M1"],1,"The footing says to ask yourself to hold out only for now, and not forever. \"I must attend this stupid class every week until June\" is too heavy, and she would rebel against the weight of it. \"I'll go today\" is small enough to carry. It is one thing in the moment (DT11). She notices the urge to skip, names it, and lets it be there (M1). She does not argue with herself about whether the rule is fair, and she does not promise anything for next week. She changes nothing and settles nothing, and she goes to class today."],["She skips and fails attendance.",["DT25","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The rule is irritating, and skipping feels like the honest response. This is giving in mindlessly. The committee never hears her objection, and the rule is untouched. The only person the protest reaches is Claire, as a failed attendance mark. Rebelling this way is cutting off your nose to spite your face."]],"e":[["She writes to the committee, and gets no reply. A classmate says \"they never read those, you wasted your time\".",[["She follows up once.",["IE5","IE13"],"Silence is not a no. She asks again, a notch more firmly, without softening the request (IE13): \"I wrote on the 3rd about the attendance rule. Could you tell me when it will be considered?\" (IE5). She follows up once and politely. After that it is for the committee to answer."],["She gets classmates to sign.",["IE16","IE12"],"One letter is easy to overlook, and twelve signatures are harder to. She asks the people who grumble about the rule to sign (IE16). More signatures are another check in favour of asking firmly (IE12). The rebellion now has company, which is more satisfying than skipping alone would have been."],["She accepts the rule this term.",["DT13","DT25"],"The committee may not answer before June. She accepts that the rule stands for this term (DT13). She keeps her objection and stops being angry about it every Tuesday. She attends just for now, a week at a time (DT25). The letter stays on file for the next review."]]],["She writes the parody, and a classmate posts it online. It gets shared, with her name on it, and the course leader is in that group.",[["She asks them to take it down.",["IE5"],"The parody was private, and she can ask for what she wants. She describes what happened, says how it is for her, and asks plainly: \"that wasn't meant to be public. Please delete it today\" (IE5). She asks without an accusation, so her classmate has no need to defend the post and can simply take it down."],["She accepts it's out.",["DT13"],"Once something has been shared it can't be recalled. She accepts that the parody is public (DT13). Wishing it back would only add to her distress. The rebellion was meant to be harmless, and it now carries some risk. She accepts that as well, and thinks about what, if anything, to do."],["She tells the teacher first, if needed.",["IE10","IE14"],"The course leader will see it. She thinks about who decides what happens next, and by whose rules (IE14). She chooses to speak first and truthfully (IE10): \"I wrote it as a joke for myself. It was posted without my asking. I do think the rule deserves a look\". She does not grovel and she does not deny writing it."]]],["She holds out for today, and tomorrow the urge is stronger. Half the class has skipped, and they are messaging from the café.",[["She holds out for tomorrow too.",["DT25","DT11"],"\"Just for now\" is renewed each day, and she never extends it to forever. Today's \"now\" is one class (DT11). She does not ask herself whether she can do this until June. She asks whether she can do it for this hour. In adaptive denial she is adamant and does not argue with herself (DT25)."],["She plans another harmless rebellion.",["DT25"],"The urge is stronger because the rebellion has had no outlet since yesterday. She gives it one: she will sit in the front row in her loudest shirt and ask the lecturer, politely, what the attendance rule is for. This honours the rebellion creatively. She will be in the room, as the rule requires."],["She makes pros and cons.",["DT5"],"Half the class skipping makes it a real question, so she writes it out (DT5). There are four boxes: skip or attend, with the gains and costs of each. The café goes in one box. The attendance threshold goes in another, and she is one absence from it. The long term outweighs the café, and she goes in."]]]]}},{"id":"∎","title":"acceptance and change commute","marks":[["α̂","accept the self, leave the world as it is"],["κ̂","leave the self, change the world"],["↪","sits inside: each thing on the left has its own place on the right","r35c.html","35c · More Parts Than Members"]],"practice":[],"u":"Why can accepting yourself and changing the situation be done in either order? Which series stand on each side, and what is the synthesis?","tree":{"t":"Jean failed her first university essay. What does she do?","o":[["She changes first: she books a writing-centre session, then accepts the grade.",["∎","G4","G15"],4,"The last footing says that accepting yourself and changing the situation can be done in either order. She starts on the change side. Interpersonal effectiveness and regulating emotion are the change side, and she uses both: she asks for help, and she solves the problem. She books the session and takes the essay with her, and she will track the next three marks (G15). Solving the problem is the first of the four ways to meet any problem (G4). The acceptance comes second, and it comes more easily. When she sits down with the grade she already has an appointment, so \"this is a fail\" no longer sounds like \"this is the end\". Some people can't accept until they have done something. She is one of them, and the footing says that order is valid. She has to change it herself, whoever set the essay (G6)."],["She accepts first, then changes how she plans, and the panic lifts before the planning begins.",["∎","DT13"],6,"She starts on the acceptance side, which is mindfulness and distress tolerance. She reads the grade and says it as fact: \"I failed this essay\". She stops fighting it, all the way (DT13). Pain plus refusing to accept it is suffering, and her panic was the refusing. The fail is still a fail. The panic lifts. Then she plans, with a clear head, and the plan is better for it. To change something she first has to accept that it is. This is the other order, and it leads to the same result as changing first: she has accepted the grade and she has a plan. That is what it means to say the two commute. She is doing the best she can, and she needs to do better (G6). She says the first before the second."],["She holds both at once: \"this grade is what it is, and the next one can be better\".",["∎","IE19"],1,"Dialectics is the synthesis of the two. She does not take acceptance and change in turn. She holds them in one sentence, joined by \"and\". \"This grade is what it is\" is the acceptance. \"The next one can be better\" is the change. Opposites can both be true (IE19), and \"and\" keeps both whole where \"but\" would cancel the first half. The six assumptions make the same move: she is doing the best she can, and she needs to do better (G6). She neither acts nor resolves anything yet. She holds both sides, and the walking the middle path skills aim at the same position (M9). She can move to either side from there."],["She does neither.",["∎","G4"],6,"It makes sense. The grade hurts, and both accepting and changing mean looking at it. Without acceptance she keeps saying \"it shouldn't have been a fail\", and suffers. Without change, the second essay is written the same way as the first. This is the fourth of the four ways: stay miserable (G4). She can leave it at any time, in either order."]],"e":[["She books the session, and the advice doesn't fit her essay. The adviser has read the wrong brief and tells her to add sources she already has.",[["She asks a second tutor.",["IE5","ER12"],"This solution failed, so she goes round the loop again (ER12). She asks her own seminar tutor for fifteen minutes, with the essay and the marking comments (IE5). This is the change side at work again. One poor session tells her nothing about whether help exists."],["She takes the parts that fit.",["IE24"],"She trusts her own reading of her essay (IE24). The adviser was wrong about the sources and right about her paragraph openings. She keeps the second point and drops the first. She can find what is valid in advice without swallowing all of it, and she can do the same with a grade."],["She accepts the grade, and changes first anyway.",["∎"],"The session was meant to make acceptance easier, and it did not. She accepts the grade now, without that help. Then she carries on with the change by rewriting her plan for essay two. The two commute (∎). The order she tried did not work this time, so she takes them in the other order."]]],["She accepts first, and the planning feels heavy. Her flatmate, who got a first, says \"I just wrote mine the night before\".",[["She plans one step.",["ER18"],"The whole plan is too heavy to pick up. Mastery is built one hard-but-possible step at a time (ER18). She plans only the first step: read the question for essay two and underline its verbs. That takes ten minutes. Her flatmate's method is her flatmate's. Jean has a small step and does it."],["She changes first next time.",["∎","G4"],"She has learned something about herself: after accepting, she sinks a little before she acts. The footing says either order is valid (∎), so next time she will act first and accept second. Choosing how to meet a problem is always open (G4). She is still learning which order suits her."],["She lets the heaviness be.",["ER22"],"Acceptance took away the panic, and under the panic there was sadness. That is the heaviness. It fits a failed essay. She lets it rise and fall (ER22) and does not treat it as a sign that accepting was a mistake. She can plan when it lifts, and it does lift."]]],["She holds both, and the next essay is also weak. A classmate says \"maybe this just isn't your subject\".",[["She holds both again.",["∎","IE19"],"The sentence still works with two grades in it: \"these grades are what they are, and the next one can be better\" (∎). Her classmate's remark takes one side and drops the other. She keeps both (IE19). Two points are not the whole line, and things keep changing."],["She asks for help.",["IE5"],"Holding both includes the change half, and two weak essays show that she can't change this alone. She asks her tutor directly (IE5): \"two essays, both weak. What is the one thing I should work on first?\" Asking for help is one of the things she can do to improve the next essay."],["She lets it be.",["DT13"],"The second weak grade is a fact, and so is the remark. She accepts both as things that happened (DT13). She does not accept the forecast, because fearful thoughts about the future are not facts. For tonight she leans on the acceptance half, and she will come back to the change half tomorrow."]]]]}}]}],"ways":["solve the problem","feel better about the problem","tolerate the problem","stay miserable"],"axioms":["people are doing the best they can","people want to improve","they still need to do better, try harder, be more motivated","they may not have caused their problems, but they have to solve them","a new behaviour must be learned in every context it is needed","every action, thought and emotion is caused","finding and changing causes works better than judging and blaming"]};
